Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(24)
                        Stability: The singled dose vial is preservative free; hence, unused drug should be dis-
                        carded. Some have noted that the product is stable for 24 hours after dilution at room
                        temperature. The colorless solution may become slight pink or light purple following
                        puncture of stopper, but this does not affect NAC activity.(2)
                        Compatibility: Incompatible when administrated via Y-site with cefepime hydrochloride
                        and ceftazidime.(24)
                        Delivery system issues: Contact with rubber, copper, iron, and cork may inactivate NAC.(24)

IV push                 Not administered by this method

Intermittent infusion   To reduce the potential for anaphylaxis, a loading dose should be given over 60 minutes.
                        One report noted the safe use of a 15-minute infusion rate in patients between 15 and
                        83 years of age.(24) (See the Comments section.)

Continuous infusion     First (50 mg/kg) and second (100 mg/kg) maintenance doses should be given over 4 hours
                        and 16 hours, respectively.(24) Infusions of 0.8–5.6 mg/kg/hr have been used safely in
                        newborns.(18)

Other routes of         Oral
administration

Comments                Anaphylaxis: Some recommend that infusing the loading dose over 60 minutes may
                        reduce the likelihood of anaphylaxis.(24) Once the anaphylactoid reaction has been treated
                        (e.g., antihistamines, H2 blockers, and in severe cases epinephrine), NAC can be reinsti-
                        tuted cautiously, but it should be discontinued if signs or symptoms recurs.(2) There was
                        a significant association between low serum acetaminophen concentrations and NAC-
                        associated cutaneous anaphylactic reactions (but no other reported adverse events).(25)
                        Another report noted that delayed time to initiation of NAC was a risk factor for develop-
                        ing anaphylactic reactions, suggesting early NAC infusion may be protective against this
                        type of event.(26)
                        Monitoring—serum concentrations: The need for NAC may be influenced by serum
                        acetaminophen concentration. Concentrations that are obtained within 4 hours of ingestion
                        are not predictive of severity.(2) Similarly, an acetaminophen level drawn within 8 hours of
                        the ingestion of an extended-release preparation, may be misleading, and a second level
                        should be drawn 4–6 hours after the initial level.(2) Undetectable serum concentrations, in
                        a patient who delayed seeking medical care, may not reflect the ingestion of a potentially
                        lethal dose.(2) Despite low or undetectable concentrations, NAC should be given if any
                        signs of hepatotoxicity are evident.(2)
                        Monitoring—Rumack-Matthew nomogram (Appendix F): This nomogram is used
                        to estimate the potential for hepatotoxicity due to acetaminophen overdose and to guide
                        therapy with NAC. Ideally, NAC should be administered within 8 hours of acetaminophen
                        ingestion to prevent or lessen hepatic injury; however, it may be effective when given
                        24 hours or longer after ingestion. The nomogram is less predictive in a chronic ingestion
                        or in an overdose with an extended-release product. The nomogram also does not take
                        into account patients at high risk of acetaminophen toxicity (e.g., alcoholics, malnour-
                        ished patients) or individuals ingesting larger than recommended acetaminophen doses
                        for extended periods of time.(2)
                        Other: A study comparing the 20-hour IV NAC and the 72-hour oral NAC protocols for
                        acetaminophen overdose favored the IV protocol in patients presenting early (within
                        12 hours of ingestion) and the oral protocol in patients presenting late (greater than
                        18 hours after ingestion) with respect to the risk of hepatotoxicity.(27)




