administration

Comments                            Significant adverse effects: Nonherpetic vesicular eruptions occurred in a child receiv-
                                    ing acyclovir for presumed herpes simplex encephalitis.(20)
                                    Pharmacokinetic considerations: A pharmacokinetic study of IV acyclovir (15 mg/
                                    kg/day) in patients 9 months to 20 years of age with malignancy showed that those with
                                    good renal function and/or lower body weight could possibly be underdosed.(21)
                                    A population pharmacokinetics study in neonates and infants found 20 mg/kg q 8 hr may
                                    underdose infants 36–41 weeks postmenstrual age and recommended increasing the
                                    dose to 20 mg/kg q 6 hr.(25)
                                    Drug interactions: Coadministration with probenecid has been shown to increase the
                                    mean half-life and the area-under-the-concentration time curve for acyclovir.(2) Consult
                                    appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before combining probenecid with
                                    acyclovir.
                                    Other: A 28-week premature infant has been successfully treated with IV acyclovir for
                                    21 days after the mother presented with eczema herpeticum at the time of delivery.(22)


