Brand names         Adenocard IV, Adenoscan, generic


Medication error    High-alert medication (anti-arrhythmic) that has an increased risk of causing significant
potential           patient harm if it is used in error.(1)


Contraindications   Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to adenosine or any components of the
and warnings        formulation.(2) Adenosine should not be given in second- or third-degree atrioventricular
                    (AV) block or sinus node disease (e.g., sick sinus syndrome, symptomatic bradycardia),
                    unless a functioning artificial pacemaker is placed.(2) Adenosine should also not be used
                    in patients with Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome or wide complex tachycardia.(3) Although
                    not listed in the prescribing information, some suggest that adenosine should be contra-
                    indicated in heart transplant recipients as it may cause prolonged asystole.(4)
                    Warnings
                        Arrhythmias, postconversion: A variety of new rhythms (e.g., premature ventricu-
                        lar contractions, atrial premature contractions, sinus bradycardia, sinus tachycardia,
                        skipped beats, and varying degrees of AV nodal block) may appear on the ECG at the
                        time of conversion to normal sinus rhythm; however, these generally last only a few
                        seconds and resolve without intervention.(2)
                        Bronchoconstriction: Patients with a history of reactive airway disease may expe-
                        rience bronchospasm and respiratory failure.(2,5-7) Aminophylline has been used to
                        relieve bronchospasm associated with adenosine.(7)
                        Heart block: Adenosine decreases conduction through the AV node and may pro-
                        duce first-, second-, or third-degree heart block, which is generally self-limiting due
                        to adenosine’s short half-life. Additional doses should not be given if high-level block
                        develops after one dose.(2) Transient or prolonged episodes of asystole have been fatal.
                        Rarely, ventricular fibrillation has been reported following adenosine administration,
                        including both resuscitated and fatal events.(2)


Infusion-related    Although adenosine can be administered via peripheral, it may be more effective if given
cautions            close to the heart via central line administration.(3) Normal saline flushes should be 5–10 mL
                    for infants and children and 20 mL for adults.(28)


Dosage              Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia
                        Initial dosing: 0.05–0.1 mg/kg(2-4,12-17,28) up to a maximum 6 mg for the first dose.(3,28)
                        Infuse over 1–2 seconds, then immediately and rapidly flush the catheter with normal
                        saline. (See the Infusion-Related Cautions section.)(2,3) One report noted that only 9%
                        of infants responded to a dose of 0.05 mg/kg and that the median effective dose was
                        0.15 mg/kg.(12) The maximum single initial dose is 0.3 mg/kg in neonates, 0.5 mg/kg
                        in children,(18) and 6 mg in adults.(3) One report suggests that a larger initial dose
                        of 0.2–0.25 mg/kg in both infants and children may reduce the risk of unsuccessful
                        cardioversion by 35%.(19)
                        Subsequent dosing: Supraventricular tachycardia is terminated in 60% to 80% of
                        patients treated with 6 mg of adenosine and in 90–95% of those treated with 12 mg.(4)
                        If conversion does not occur in 30 seconds, double the dose and immediately and
                        rapidly flush the catheter with normal saline.(28) This process should continue until
                        sinus rhythm is established or a maximum single (NOT cumulative) dose of 0.3 mg/kg
                        (maximum of 12 mg) has been given.(2,3,29) One study reported a single dose as large
                        as 0.4–0.6 mg/kg for successful conversion of a 7-week-old with refractory SVT.(30)
                        Pretreatment in children undergoing cardiac surgery (prevention of ischemia
                        and reperfusion injury): Adenosine was infused through a central venous catheter at
                        0.05 mg/kg/min for the first minute, then increased by 0.05 mg/kg/min each minute
                        up to 0.35 mg/kg/min (maximum dose maintained for 4 additional minutes). The total
                        dose of adenosine was 2.45 mg/kg over 10 minutes. There was a 5-minute interval
                        between the completion of adenosine and initiation of cardiac bypass surgery.(20)




