                    able to albumin 4% for acute resuscitation of patients with severe traumatic brain injury
                    (improved mortality and neurologic outcomes).(7)
                    Hypoalbuminemia: Albumin deficits have been replaced by intermittent infusions of up
                    to 1 g/kg of albumin 25%.(8) Manufacturers recommend that doses should not exceed
                    2 g/kg/day(34,35,37) or 25 g/day (in children) in the absence of ongoing albumin losses.(33)
                    Continuous infusion of albumin results in a more sustained increase in serum albumin
                    concentration.(9) The albumin deficit can be estimated with the following equation:
                      g albumin = weight (kg) × 3 dL/kg × (3.5 – observed serum albumin in g/dL)(10,11)
