administration

Comments                Rare adverse effects: Aminoglycosides accumulate in renal cortical tissue and may dam-
                        age proximal tubule cells leading to oliguric renal failure. Risk of nephrotoxicity may be
                        influenced by type of aminoglycoside, dose, duration (cumulative dose), frequency of
                        therapy, and elevated serum concentration.(2,3) It is also increased by advancing age,
                        preexisting renal or hepatic dysfunction, decreased renal perfusion, hypoalbuminemia,
                        and dehydration.(2,3) Concurrent administration of nephrotoxic medications may increase
                        the risk of toxicity and should be avoided.(2)
                        Cochlear and/or vestibular ototoxicity has been associated with aminoglycoside antibi-
                        otics.(4) Total AUC may be a better indicator of ototoxic risk than either peak or trough
                        serum concentration.(47,48) Use cautiously with other drugs known to cause ototoxicity.
                        Aminoglycosides may potentiate the curare-like effects on the neuromuscular junction and
                        should be used with caution in patients with neuromuscular disorders because they may
                        aggravate muscle weakness.(2,72) Aminoglycosides may enhance the respiratory depressant
                        effect of neuromuscular-blocking agents and may prolong blockade.(2,6) Neuromuscular
                        blockade may be reversed by administration of calcium salts.(2)
                        Monitoring: Because large variability exists in patient response to therapy, individualize
                        dosage based on serum concentrations, clinical response, and renal function. Recom-
                        mended peak and trough serum amikacin concentrations are ≤35 mg/L and ≤10 mg/L,
                        respectively.(2) Although serum concentration monitoring has become routine practice in



                                                                                                           
                        many institutions, not all patients require monitoring.(73,74)



