potential
                       USP reports that Amicar has been confused with amikacin. No patient harm resulted.
                       Amicar has been confused with Omacor; patient harm resulted.(1)


Contraindications      Aminocaproic acid should not be used when there is an ongoing intravascular clotting
and warnings           process.(2)
                       Due to the risk of thrombosis resulting in intrarenal obstruction, aminocaproic acid should
                       not be used for hematuria of upper urinary tract origin unless possible benefits outweigh
                       the risks.(2)


Infusion-related       Hypotension, bradycardia, and cardiac arrhythmias may occur with rapid or undiluted
cautions               administration.(2)
                       Thrombophlebitis may occur.(2)


Dosage                 Continuous infusion: Loading dose of 100–200 mg/kg not to exceed 5 g followed by
                       continuous infusion at 10–33.3 mg/kg/hr or 1 g/m2/hr.(3-14)
                       In high-risk infants on extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO), aminocaproic acid
                       infusion has been continued up to 72 hours during ECMO or until decannulation.(3)
                       In infants and children 5–20 kg undergoing cardiac surgery, 75 mg/kg over 15 minutes
                       at induction followed by 75 mg/kg/hr until chest closure with additional 75 mg/kg at the
                       start of cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB) was superior in reducing postoperative blood loss
                       and transfusion requirements compared to 100 mg/kg doses over 15 minutes given at
                       induction, at start of CPB, and after protamine administration.(25)
                       In infants 6–24 months old undergoing craniofacial surgery, a pharmacokinetic dose-finding
                       study concluded that a loading dose of 100 mg/kg over 10 minutes followed by 40 mg/
                       kg/hr throughout surgery is expected to maintain steady-state plasma concentrations of
                       130 mcg/mL (the concentration at which fibrinolysis is completely inhibited).(26)
                       In children and adolescents undergoing spine surgery, a pharmacokinetic dose-finding
                       study made the following recommendations to attain target steady state plasma concen-
                       trations of 130 mcg/mL (the concentration at which fibrinolysis is completely inhibited)(27):

                         Weight (kg)           Loading dose       Continuous infusion (mg/kg/hr)
                                          (mg/kg) over 10 minutes       throughout surgery
                                <25                     100                                 40
                             25–49                      100                                 35
                                ≥50                     100                                 30

                       Bolus dosing: 100 mg/kg or 3 g/m2 not to exceed 5 g q 4–6 hr or PRN.(15-19) For repair
                       of congenital heart defects, boluses of 75 mg/kg at the beginning and end of cardiopul-
                       monary bypass along with the addition of 75 mg/100 mL of bypass priming solution have
                       been used.(10-12)
                       In infants undergoing congenital diaphragmatic hernia repair during ECMO, 100 mg/kg
                       was given 30–60 minutes prior to surgery and after surgery for 24–48 hours to maintain
                       an activated clotting time of 200–220 seconds.(15)


Dosage adjustment      Clearance is markedly reduced in patients with severe renal dysfunction; specific dosing
in organ dysfunction   adjustments are not established.(2)


Maximum dosage         24 g/day in children or in adults 30 g/day(20)
                       36 g/day was given to a 12-year-old with acute lymphoblastic leukemia.(21)




