Additives                          Some products (multidose vials) contain benzyl alcohol. Single-use vials are preservative-
                                   free.(22) (See Appendix C for specific information about potential benzyl alcohol toxicity.)


Suitable diluents                  NS, D5W, R(2)


Maximum                            20 mg/mL(2)
concentration

Preparation and                    Should not be administered undiluted.(2)
delivery

IV push                            Not recommended.(2) (See the Infusion-Related Cautions section.)


Intermittent infusion              Concentration of 16–20 mg/mL is recommended by manufacturer (2) over 15–60
                                   minutes.(2,4,5,8,13,14)


Continuous infusion                Concentration of 16–20 mg/mL is recommended by manufacturer.(2) May be infused
                                   continuously.(2-14,23)


Other routes of                    Not indicated
administration

Comments                           Prophylactic aminocaproic acid in children undergoing cardiac surgery reduced intraopera-
                                   tive blood loss but did not significantly decrease blood product transfusions.(23) Periop-
                                   erative blood loss was reduced in patients during posterior spinal fusion and segmental
                                   spinal instrumentation.(4,5)
                                   Reports on the ability of aminocaproic acid to decrease the incidence of intracranial hem-
                                   orrhage and other hemorrhagic complications in neonates on ECMO are conflicting.(3,6,7)
                                   Adverse events: A 12-year-old child with refractory thrombocytopenia after bone mar-
                                   row transplantation developed myopathy after 98 days of treatment with doses ranging
                                   from 4–36 g/day.(21)
                                   Serum creatine phosphokinase concentrations, particularly in patients with myalgia, may
                                   be a useful indicator of muscle injury. Myopathy is usually reversible on discontinuation
                                   of therapy.(2,21)
                                   Pharmacokinetic considerations: Molecular weight 131.17.(22) To attain concentrations
                                   above the therapeutic threshold, children require larger loading and maintenance doses
                                   than adults.(24)
                                   Other: Comparison to tranexamic acid: A prospective, randomized, double-blind trial com-
                                   paring tranexamic acid (10 mg/kg over 15 minutes then 1 mg/kg/hr), aminocaproic acid
                                   (100 mg/kg over 15 minutes then 10 mg/kg/hr) and placebo (saline) in 125 adolescents
                                   undergoing spine surgery found that both antifibrinolytics reduced intraoperative blood
                                   loss and that tranexamic acid was superior at reducing postoperative drain losses.(28)


