Brand names         Generics


Medication error    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Aminophylline may be confused with ampicillin and
potential           amitriptyline.(1) Failure to correctly convert between aminophylline and theophylline can
                    result in medication error.


Contraindications   Contraindications: Patients with a history of hypersensitivity to theophylline or other
and warnings        components in the product including ethylenediamine.(2)
                    Warnings: Use with extreme caution as theophylline may exacerbate active peptic ulcer
                    disease, seizure disorders, hypothyroidism, and/or cardiac arrhythmias (not including
                    bradyarrhythmias).(2)


Infusion-related    Rapid administration may result in toxic serum concentrations(3) that may be associated
cautions            with circulatory failure.(4) Avoid extravasation. (See Appendix E.)


Dosage              Aminophylline products contain varying amounts of theophylline (check manufacturers’
                    information). All of the doses in this monograph are reported as THEOPHYLLINE unless
                    otherwise noted. Regardless of disease, therapy should be individualized using serum
                    theophylline concentrations.
                    Apnea and bradycardia of prematurity (usual targeted serum concentration
                    range of 6–12 mg/L)
                        Loading dose (LD): 4–6 mg/kg over 20–30 minutes(3-10)
                        If the patient is already receiving theophylline, a serum concentration must be obtained
                        and the LD based on that concentration(11) using the equation:
                        LD (mg/kg) = (desired concentration − measured concentration)/2
                        (See the Comments/Other section for more information about the equation.)
                        Initial maintenance dose: 2–4 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr(3-6) or 3.3–7.5 mg/kg/
                        day divided q 8 hr(7-10)
                    Diuretic: Adjunctive therapy in critically ill children unresponsive to furosemide or in
                    those who are diuretic-dependent. Low-dose aminophylline as a single loading dose of
                    3 mg/kg over 30 minutes(12) or a loading dose of 3 mg/kg over 20 minutes followed by
                    0.5 mg/kg/hr.(13)
                    Severe acute asthma (usual “therapeutic” range 5–15 mg/L): The role of IV amino­
                    phylline in acute asthma is controversial. Many adult(14-17) and pediatric(18-20) patients
                    who respond to beta-agonist and systemic glucocorticoid experience no additional ben-
                    efit from aminophylline.(21-23) A retrospective study of 47 children (3–18 years of age)
                    with severe asthma who were admitted to the intensive care unit and who had failed
                    systemic corticosteroids and inhaled albuterol received aminophylline. Patients receive
                    6 mg/kg loading dose of aminophylline followed by a continuous infusion of 1–1.2 mg/kg/
                    hr. Thirty-one of 49 children had serum theophylline concentration >10 mg/mL. Use of
                    aminophylline was associated with a longer length of hospital stay and time to resolution
                    of symptoms when compared to those not receiving aminophylline.(41) However, studies
                    suggest that the addition of aminophylline may be beneficial in critically ill children with
                    severe acute asthma and impending respiratory failure who are unresponsive to aggres-
                    sive beta-agonist and systemic glucocorticoids.(14-17)
                    Loading(24,25) and maintenance(26) doses should be based on ideal body weight in children
                    who are overweight or obese. (See Appendix B.)
                        LD (no theophylline in the past 24 hours): 5–6 mg/kg over 20–30 minutes(2,27,28)
                        LD (theophylline in the past 24 hours): A theophylline serum concentration must
                        be obtained and the LD estimated based on the following equation (see the Comments
                        section):
                            LD (mg/kg) = (desired concentration – measured concentration)/2(11)




