Brand names            Cordarone IV, Nexterone, generic

Medication error       ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential              if it is used in error.(1)
                       Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Amiodarone may be confused with inamrinone,
                       amantadine and AMILoride.(2,5) Corarone may be confused with Cardura or Cordran.

Contraindications      Hypersensitivity to amiodarone, iodine, or any component of the formulation (i.e., corn or
and warnings           corn products).(3) Should not be used in those with severe sinus-node dysfunction causing
                       marked sinus bradycardia; second- and third-degree heart block (except in patients with
                       a functioning artificial pacemaker); bradycardia causing syncope (except in patients with
                       a functioning artificial pacemaker) and/or cardiogenic shock.(3)

Infusion-related       Because dosing units can be variable (mcg/kg/min and mg/kg/day), caution should be
cautions               used to validate dose and dosing. May cause hypotension and bradycardia that is associ-
                       ated with the rate of infusion; hence, the dose rate should be slowed if these develop.(3)
                       The likelihood of phlebitis increases with peripheral infusion of concentrations >3 mg/mL
                       in D5W.(3) Concentrations ≤2.5 mg/mL may be less irritating. Use of an inline filter during
                       continuous infusions may reduce the incidence of phlebitis.(3)

Dosage                 Dosing units for regimens can vary (mcg/kg/min and mg/kg/day); therefore, use caution
                       to ensure appropriate dose is given. Taper infusion as soon as clinically possible, and if
                       necessary switch to oral therapy. (See the Comments section.)
                       Perfusing tachycardias: Give an IV loading dose of 5 mg/kg (maximum: 300 mg/dose)
                       over 20–60 minutes. Dose may be repeated twice up to a maximum total dose of 15 mg/kg
                       during acute treatment.(4,5)
                       Shock refractory ventricular fibrillation (VF) or pulseless ventricular tachy-
                       cardia (VT): Amiodarone is recommended for cardiac arrest with pulseless VT or VF
                       that is unresponsive to defibrillation, CPR, and vasopressor administration and control
                       of hemodynamically stable monomorphic VT or wide-complex tachycardia of uncertain
                       ­origin.(5,7) May be given IO if an IV cannot be established.(6) Give 5 mg/kg (maximum:
                        300 mg/dose) by rapid bolus.(4,5,16) This may be repeat twice up to a maximum total dose
                        of 15 mg/kg during acute treatment.(4,5,16) In the event of breakthrough episodes give
                        5 mg/kg (150 mg) over 10 minutes.
                       Tachyarrhythmia, including junctional ectopic tachycardia, paroxysmal supra-
                       ventricular tachycardia: Although limited information is available, amiodarone is
                       recommended for supraventricular tachycardia that is unresponsive to vagal maneuvers
                       and adenosine.(4)
                       Loading dose of 5 mg/kg (maximum: 300 mg) given over 20–60 minutes (not to exceed
                       0.25 mg/kg/min unless clinically indicated).(4,5,8-10,12,16) Initial dose may be repeated up to
                       a maximum of 15(4,16)–20 mg/kg.(11-13) Some have suggested dividing the loading dose of
                       5 mg/kg into 1-mg/kg aliquots that are each given over 5–10 minutes.(13,14) If after 30
                       minutes additional therapy is needed, give a dose of 1–5 mg/kg in the same manner.(13,14)
                       Continuous IV infusion has also been used. The initial dose of 5 mcg/kg/min is followed
                       by incremental increases as clinically needed. The usual required dose is 10 mcg/kg/min
                       (range: 5–15 mcg/kg/min).(5,9,12,15,16)

Dosage adjustment      No adjustment necessary in renal or hepatic dysfunction.(3) Although no specific recom-
in organ dysfunction   mendations are available, dosage adjustment may be necessary in those with significant
                       hepatic impairment.

Maximum dosage         300 mg/dose. Although doses of 20 mg/kg/day have been used,(11-13) current guidelines
                       suggest 15 mg/kg/day as a maximum dose.(4) Up to a maximum total 24-hour dose of
                       15 mg/kg/day, 20 mg/kg/day(4,5) or 20 mg/kg(11-13) during acute treatment or(13) 10(5)–15
                       mcg/kg/min.(5,12)

Additives              Each milliliter contains 100 mg of polysorbate 80 and benzyl alcohol 20.2 mg in water
                       for injection. Commercially prepared, premixed solutions do not contain benzyl alcohol.
                       Nexterone does not contain polysorbate 80 or benzyl alcohol.(3) (See Appendix C for spe-



                                                                                                                   
                       cific information about benzyl alcohol potential for toxicity.)



54
                                                                                        Amiodarone
Suitable diluents       D5W

Maximum                 Concentrations >2 mg/mL are associated with increased phlebitis are generally discour-
concentration           aged unless given via a central venous catheter.(3,5) Commercially premixed solutions
                        are available (1.5 and 1.8 mg/mL).

Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(17)
                        Stability: Store undiluted vials and premixed solutions (Nexterone) at 20°C to 25°C.(17)
                        Excursions are permitted between 15°C and 30°C.(3,17) Protect from freezing.(3)
                        Solutions containing <0.6 mg/mL in D5% are unstable.(17)
                        Compatibility: Should only be added to D5.(17) Amiodarone may precipitate immediately
                        or on standing when diluted. At concentrations between 0.0025 mg/L and 45 mg/mL in
                        phosphate buffer (pH 7.4), the drug concentration may exceed the solubility of amiodarone
                        hydrochloride in the mixture. Precipitation may occur when the drug enters the blood-
                        stream, contributing to the phlebitis associated with peripheral infusion of amiodarone.
                        Delivery system issues: Solutions that will infuse for >2 hours must be prepared in a
                        non-polyvinyl chloride (PVC) container (e.g., glass or polyolefin).(17) An inline filter has been
                        recommended during administration for continuous infusions to reduce the incidence of
                        phlebitis. PVC tubing is recommended for administration regardless of infusion duration.
                        Amiodarone leaches diethylhexyl phthalate (DEHP) plasticizer from PVC tubing.(17) The
                        degree of plasticizer leaching depends on the concentration and rate of administration
                        with higher concentrations and slower administration rates leach more plasticizer.
                        Photosensitivity: Protect from light during storage. There is no need to protect solutions
                        from light during administration.(17)

IV push                 Pulseless VT or VF via rapid IV bolus. Adjust rate to urgency of the situation.(4,5)

Intermittent infusion   Administer loading dose over 20–60 minutes. Must be infused via volumetric infusion
                        device.

Continuous infusion     Reported dosing units for regimens may vary (mcg/kg/min and mg/kg/day); hence, ensure
                        appropriate dose and dosing units are used.

Other routes of         Intraosseous infusion(6)
administration

Comments                Conversion to oral therapy: Although conversion to oral therapy has not been formally
                        evaluated, some experts recommend a 1–2 day overlap when converting from IV to oral
                        therapy, especially when treating ventricular arrhythmias.(3)
                        Overdose: Intoxication with amiodarone necessitates ECG monitoring. Because bra-
                        dycardia may be resistant to atropine, IV isoproterenol or cardiac pacemaker may be
                        required. Amiodarone is not dialiazable. Hypotension, cardiogenic shock, heart block, QT
                        prolongation and hepatotoxicity may occur. The patients should be monitored for several
                        days following overdose due to long half-life IV product is used for acute treatment.(3)
                        Monitoring: Heart rate and rhythm, blood pressure, and ECG. Amiodarone can be pro-
                        arrhythmic and may worsen or precipitate new arrhythmias (e.g., torsades de pointes).(3)
                        If QRS widens more than 50% of baseline, discontinue amiodarone.(4) If hepatic enzymes
                        exceed 3 times normal or double in a patient with an elevated baseline, consider decreas-
                        ing the dose or discontinuing amiodarone.
                        Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources before combining any drug with
                        amiodarone. Amiodarone inhibits CYP1A2, CYP2A6, CYP2C9, CYP2D6, CYP3A4 and
                        P-glycoprotein.(3) Should be used cautiously in patients receiving other agents known to
                        prolong the QT interval or depress the sinus or AV node.(3)




