Brand names            Fungizone, generic


Medication error       Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
potential
                       ISMP reports that amphotericin B has been confused with Abelcet and AmBisome.(1)
                       USP reports that amphotericin B has been confused with amphotericin B liposome; patient
                       harm resulted. USP also reports that Fungizone has been confused with AmBisome; no
                       patient harm resulted.(2)


Contraindications      U.S. boxed warning: Reserve treatment with amphotericin B for progressive and poten-
and warnings           tially life-threatening fungal infections; do not use for noninvasive fungal infections in
                       immunocompetent patients.(3)
                       Do not give a dose >1.5 mg/kg. Overdose may result in potentially fatal cardiac arrest
                       or cardiopulmonary syndrome.(3)
                       Verify the product name, dosage and preadministration, especially if the dose is >1.5 mg/kg.(3)
                       Contraindications: Amphotericin B therapy is complicated by many dangerous side
                       effects. It is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity to amphotericin B or any of its
                       components, unless benefit of treating life-threatening disease outweighs potential risk.(3)
                       Amphotericin B formulations are not interchangeable. Confusion over these products
                       has led to fatal overdoses and subtherapeutic dosing. The ISMP recommends the use of
                       proprietary names when communicating orders, and the inclusion of indication, mg per
                       kg dose and final dosage calculation for individual patients.(4)


Infusion-related       Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, have been reported.(5–7) Other infusion-
cautions               related symptoms include fever, chills, nausea, vomiting, headache, malaise, hypotension
                       and arrhythmias.(8) Patients have been premedicated with acetaminophen, codeine, diaz-
                       epam, diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine, meperidine, prochlorperazine,(9–11) or ibuprofen.(12)
                       Corticosteroids have been added to amphotericin B solutions to decrease the incidence
                       and severity of febrile response during infusion.(9,13)
                       Thrombophlebitis and injection pain may also occur; adding 1 unit of heparin per mL of
                       solution may reduce pain at the injection site and phlebitis.(14)
                       Arrhythmias have been reported during infusion in a neonate.(15)
                       Although cardiovascular collapse has occurred in animals after rapid injection,(16,17) it has
                       been infused over 45–60 minutes without problems in adults.(18-20)


Dosage                 Test dose: A single dose of 0.1 mg/kg, up to 1 mg, infused over 20–60 minutes is often
                       used.(3,21-24) Vital signs should be monitored every 30 minutes for 2 hours. If a test dose
                       is done, therapy should commence the same day with the remainder of a recommended
                       therapeutic dose.(16) However, many clinicians view the test dose as unnecessary, recom-
                       mending close monitoring during the first 1–2 hours of the initial dose.(8)
                       Initial and maintenance
                           Neonates: 1 mg/kg/dose q 24 hr infused over 2–6 hours(8)
                           Infants and children: 0.25–0.5 mg/kg q 24 hr infused over 2–6 hours. Increase
                           daily dose as tolerated by 0.25–0.5 mg/kg up to 0.5–1.5 mg/kg q 24 hr.(8,13,25-31)


Dosage adjustment      Dose adjustment is not necessary in patients with renal insufficiency(8,32); however, if renal
in organ dysfunction   insufficiency occurs during therapy, the dosing interval should be increased or alternate
                       day dosing should be done prior to a decrease in the total daily dose.(8,32) Alternately,
                       therapy may be changed to an amphotericin B lipid product in patients who experience
                       significant toxicities during therapy.(8)
                       Poorly dialyzable. No dosage adjustments or supplemental doses are necessary.(32)




58
                                                                                                                    
                                                                                                  Amphotericin B
Maximum dosage                    Total daily dose should not exceed 1.5 mg/kg.(3,8)
                                  Larger doses (>1 mg/kg) may be associated with increased risk of nephrotoxicity, which
                                  can present as oliguria, azotemia, increased SCr, potassium wasting, and renal tubular
                                  acidosis. GFR was reduced permanently in adults who received >4 g over the course of
                                  therapy.(16,17,33) Pediatric patients have received total cumulative doses of 30–52 mg/kg
                                  without evidence of “permanent” nephrotoxicity.(34-36) Evidence does not support serum
                                  drug concentration or cumulative dose monitoring as a marker of toxicity.(37) (See the
                                  Comments section.)
                                  Three children who inadvertently received doses of 3.8, 4.6, or 5 mg/kg and two premature
                                  neonates who received 25 and 40.8 mg/kg experienced cardiac arrest during or shortly
                                  after the infusion. Only one child survived.(38)


Additives                         None reported


Suitable diluents                 D5W, D10W, D15W, or D20W(39)


Maximum                           0.1 mg/mL.(3,39,40) The osmolality of this solution (0.1 mg/mL in D5W) is 256 mOsm/kg.(39)
concentration                     Concentrations as high as 0.5–1 mg/mL have been found to be chemically stable for up
                                  to 120 hours when stored at 4°C.(41)


Preparation and                   Filtration: If inline filtration is indicated, use filters with a pore size ≥1 micron.(39)
delivery

IV push                           Not reported. Rapid infusion should be avoided.(3)


Intermittent infusion             0.1 mg/mL in D5W, D10W, D15W, or D20W(39,42) over 2–6 hours.(16,39) Shorter infusion
                                  times (1–2 hours) have been tolerated in older children and adults and may improve
                                  drug delivery.(8)


Continuous infusion               Not reported


Other routes of                   Amphotericin B has also been given via IT route as well as intra-articularly, intrapleurally,
administration                    intraventricular,(43) aerosolized,(44) and by irrigation.(39)


Comments                          Significant adverse effects: Concomitant nephrotoxic drugs may increase the potential
                                  for nephrotoxicity.(32) Hydration and sodium repletion, either through sodium loading with
                                  NS over 30 minutes prior to administration or with sodium intakes of >4 mEq/kg/day,
                                  may reduce the risk of nephrotoxicity.(8,32,45) Because rifampin acts synergistically with
                                  amphotericin B, they have been given concomitantly to reduce the amphotericin B dose
                                  and, hence, the risk of nephrotoxicity.(46)
                                  Hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, and renal tubular acidosis may occur.(8)


