                    Human antihemophilic factor (AHF) products are prepared from human pooled plasma
                    and may carry a risk for transmission of infectious agents, despite viral attenuation pro-
                    cesses.(2-6) Hepatitis A and B vaccination are recommended in all hemophilia patients.(5)
                    Progressive anemia and hemolysis may occur when large or frequent doses of human
                    AHF are administered to patients with blood groups A, B, and AB, due to trace amounts
                    of blood group A and B isohemagglutinins.(1,4)
                    Patients treated with AHF products should be carefully monitored for the development of
                    Factor VIII inhibitors by appropriate clinical observations and laboratory tests. Inhibitors
                    have been reported following administration of these products, predominantly children <5
                    years of age. If expected plasma Factor VIII activity levels are not attained, or if bleed-
                    ing is not controlled with an expected dose, an assay that measures Factor VIII inhibitor
                    concentration should be performed.(1-5)


