IV push                             Hemofil M may be administered at a maximum rate of 10 mL/min.(2)
                                    Monoclate-P may be administered at a maximum rate of 2 mL/min.(3)
                                    Koate-DVI may be administered over 5–10 minutes; adjust administration rate based on
                                    patient response.(4)


Intermittent infusion               No recommendation


Continuous infusion                 Continuous infusion has been used in the treatment of moderate and severe bleeds in
                                    hemophilia. It has been shown that using a continuous infusion of factor after initial
                                    homeostasis is achieved can result in less overall factor consumption by avoiding the peaks
                                    and troughs associated with bolus dosing.(10-13) It appears that adjusted-dose continuous
                                    infusion, where the dose is increased or decreased to meet a target steady-state factor
                                    level, may be preferred over fixed-dose continuous infusion.(12)
                                         Fixed-dose continuous infusion: Initial bolus of 25–50 international units/kg,
                                         followed by 2–4 international units/kg/hr to maintain activity of 50% to 100%.(12-15)
                                         Adjusted-dose continuous infusion: Initial bolus of 25–50 international units/kg
                                         followed by infusion of 2–5 international units/kg/hr to maintain Factor VIII levels
                                         as below(14,16):
                                               At start of surgery: 0.7–1 international units/mL
                                               Postoperative days 1–3 (steady-state): 0.5–1 international units/mL
                                               Postoperative days 4–7 (steady-state): 0.4–0.8 international units/mL


Other routes of                     Not recommended
administration

Comments                            Inhibitor development: Patients with mild hemophilia appear to be at greater risk of
                                    inhibitor development after treatment with recombinant factor products (e.g., Advate,
                                    Helixate FS, Kogenate FS, Recombinate)(17,18) Continuous infusion of Factor VIII does not
                                    seem to induce inhibitor formation, although this remains controversial.(14,19)


