                                                                    Antithrombin III
Dosage (cont.)   Extracorporeal membrane oxidation (ECMO): The Extracorporeal Life Support Orga-
                 nization Anticoagulation Guideline(3) states that in patients with escalating unfractionated
                 heparin (UFH) requirement, UFH >35–40 units/kg/hr, and/or clinically subtherapeutic anti-
                 coagulation, acquired antithrombin deficiency may be a contributing factor. Some ECMO
                 centers routinely administer antithrombin replacement when ATIII activity <30% to 80%,
                 while some centers only replace when there is also evidence of reduced UFH effect clini-
                 cally or if it is based on low activated clotting time (ACT), low Anti-Xa levels, or minimal
                 UFH effect on kaolin and heparinase thromboelastogram (TEG) samples. Most centers
                 that give ATIII replacement target levels of 50% to 100%.(3-6) If giving ATIII replacement,
                 consider decreasing the UFH infusion before ATIII administration due to the potential for
                 augmentation of UFH effect.(3)
                     An ECMO center using a product not available in the United States (ATIII Immuno,
                     Baxter, Canada) reported good outcomes (doubling of ATIII activity level after
                     supplementation, increased UFH Anti-Xa level from 0.22–0.26 units/mL before to
                     0.35–0.43 units/mL after supplementation, reduction of UFH dose from approximately
                     30 to 18 units/kg/hr before/after supplementation, and no adverse bleeding, throm-
                     botic, or allergic events within 1 week of administration). Dosing strategy: One vial
                     (approximately 1000 units ATIII) as a bolus dose when ATIII activity levels were low
                     (reported as <0.5 units/mL) and with evidence of reduced UFH effect. Mean dose
                     given was 241 units/kg (199–283 units/kg).(7)
                     ATryn: One ECMO center used equation 2 (a and b) to calculate ATIII dosage and
                     checked ATIII activity level 2 hr after start of infusion and q 2 hr after rate changes
                     and adjusted rate per manufacturer recommendations (see Monitoring, ATryn in the
                     Comments section). Two of five patients never reached the target ATIII level (80%).
                     In the other three patients, the median time to achieve target ATIII level was 11 hr
                     (10–17 hours). Median infusion rate was 480 units/kg/day (104–520 units/kg/day).
                     Authors concluded that the delay in achieving target was likely due to the dosing
                     equation not accounting for the added volume provided by the ECMO circuit.(5)
                     Thrombate III: Two retrospective studies reported no beneficial effects of ATIII
                     supplementation.(6,8) The first used UFH targeting goal ACT 180–220 seconds and ATIII
                     supplementation was given (target 100% ATIII activity) using the following equa-
                     tion: ATIII units = [100 – ATIII activity level]/1.4 + circuit factor (based on circuit’s
                     volume). ATIII supplementation had no clinically meaningful impact on daily coagu-
                     lation measures, no difference in UFH rates, and was independently associated with
                     an increased risk of circuit change (Hazard Ratio 3.15 [95%CI, 1.21–8.16]). Authors
                     acknowledged that UFH Anti-Xa levels were subtherapeutic (0.19–0.25), which may
                     have led to the lack of benefit.(6) The second study reported using UFH targeting goal
                     ACT 180–220/160–180/140–160 seconds based on bleeding risk stratification and
                     supplementing ATIII if ATIII activity levels were <80% if ACT was subtherapeutic on
                     higher UFH dose. Dosing strategy: Per provider discretion and rounded to vial size
                     sometimes. Median ATIII dose 83 units/kg (6.5–295 units/kg). Mean ATIII activity levels
                     (reported as Units/mL) increased from 62 before to 97 at 8 hours and 92 at 24 hours
                     after supplementation. Neither UFH dose nor ACT measurements were impacted.(8)
                 Respiratory distress syndrome in preterm neonates: A Cochrane review concluded
                 that preterm infants are unlikely to benefit from antithrombin and may be harmed.(9)
                 Shock or severe sepsis: The Surviving Sepsis Campaign recommends against the use of
                 antithrombin therapy for severe sepsis and septic shock (grade 1b recommendation).(10)
                 Treatment of thrombosis: Two studies report on the use of ATIII supplementation in
                 combination with enoxaparin(11) and with UFH.(12) Sixty infants with thrombosis were stud-
                 ied; enoxaparin with versus without ATIII supplementation (Thrombate III, dosing per
                 equation 1). Mean time to achieve goal Anti-Xa was longer (P=NS) and bleeding events
                 were more often and more severe in the group that received ATII supplementation.(11) In
                 51 patients (age 1–18 months) with acute thrombosis treated with UFH, ATIII supple-
                 mentation (Thrombate III) was considered if ATIII activity level <70%. Usual dose was
                 50 units/kg, sometimes rounded to vial size. Median dose was 50 units/kg (IQR, 33–50
                 units/kg). Median number of doses given was 3 (IQR, 1–6). ATIII doses were associ-
                 ated with increase in ATIII activity level (for each unit/kg of ATIII given, 0.5% to 0.8%
                 increase) but not with change in Anti-Xa level or with more frequent achievement of goal
                 anticoagulation.(12)




