Brand names         Generic


Medication error    ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error.(1)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
                    ISMP and USP report that argatroban has been confused with Aggrastat; no patient harm
                    resulted.(2,3) ISMP also reports that argatroban has been confused with Orgaran.(2)


Contraindications   Use with caution in patients with diseases that have an increased risk for hemorrhage.(4)
and warnings

Infusion-related    Allergic reactions have been reported, and they were primarily in patients receiving
cautions            thrombolytic therapy or contrast media.(4)


Dosage              See Dosing Adjustment in Organ Dysfunction section for initial dosing suggestions in
                    patients with hepatic impairment.
                    Dosing adjustment for obesity: One retrospective trial in adults receiving infusions
                    for prophylaxis or treatment of thrombosis found that activated partial thromboplastin
                    time (aPTT) responses were not different between obese (BMI >30 kg/m2) and nonobese
                    patients, and that infusions of 1 mcg/kg/min provided therapeutic aPTTs.(5) Another trial
                    in adults undergoing percutanous coronary intervention concluded that dosing should be
                    based on actual body weight up to BMI 50.9 kg/m².(14)
                    Infants and children(4,6-10)
                        Prophylaxis or treatment of thrombosis, treatment of heparin-induced throm-
                        bocytopenia (HIT) or suspected HIT: 0.75 mcg/kg/min initially, then titrated in
                        increments of 0.1–0.25 mcg/kg/min to goal aPTT of 1.5–3 × baseline (not to exceed
                        100 seconds).(4,9,10) Infusion rates have ranged from 0.1–13 mcg/kg/min.(7,10)
                        Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) or ventricular assist device
                        (VAD): A multicenter study used the following protocol: circuit prime of 50 mcg/750
                        mL, initial bolus of 100 mcg/kg, initial infusion of 2 mcg/kg/min titrated to activated
                        clotting time (ACT) 180–220 seconds.(10) A review reported that 12 patients received
                        varying regimens; infusions ranged from 0.1–24 mcg/kg/min (usually <6 mcg/kg/min).(7)
                        Hemodialysis or continuous venovenous hemofiltration (CVVH): A multicenter
                        study used the following protocol: initial bolus of 250 mcg/kg and initial infusion of
                        2 mcg/kg/min titrated to ACT 180–220 seconds.(10) A review reported that five patients
                        on hemodialysis received infusions of 0.1–2 mcg/kg/min (one received an initial bolus
                        of 65 mcg/kg).(7)
                        Cardiac catheterization: A multicenter study used the following protocol: bolus of
                        250 mcg/kg and initial infusion of 15 mcg/kg/min titrated to ACT 200–300 seconds.(10)
                        A review reported that five patients received a bolus of 150 or 250 mcg/kg followed by
                        infusion rates of 7.5–15 mcg/kg/min titrated to an ACT of 300.(7) A case report described
                        successful use of 1.45 mcg/kg/min titrated to aPTT 55–80 seconds in an infant.(15)
                        Cardiopulmonary bypass: A 2.5-month-old infant received 2–5 mcg/kg/min with
                        boluses of 20 mcg/kg PRN to achieve ACT ≥400 seconds. No loading dose or pump
                        prime was used.(6) A multicenter study used the following protocol: circuit prime of
                        50 mcg/750 mL, loading dose of 250 mcg/kg, and initial infusion of 20 mcg/kg/min
                        titrated to ACT 400–500 seconds.(10)
                    Adults with HIT and HIT with thrombosis syndrome(4)
                        Prophylaxis or treatment of thrombosis: 2 mcg/kg/min with the infusion adjusted
                        up to 10 mcg/kg/min to steady-state aPTT of 1.5–3 × baseline value (≤100 seconds)
                        Percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI): Bolus 350 mcg/kg via large bore IV
                        line over 3–5 minutes followed by infusion of 25 mcg/kg/min. Titrate to goal ACT
                        (refer to package insert for full recommendations).




