Atropine Sulfate
Preparation and         Storage: Atropine sulfate injection should be stored at controlled room temperature.
delivery (cont.)        Freezing should be avoided.(21)
                        Stability: Atropine (salt form unspecified) 1 mg/mL repackaged in polypropylene syringes
                        showed little or no change in concentration after 4 weeks of storage at room temperature.(21)
                        Atropine sulfate 0.4 mg/mL repackaged in polypropylene and glass syringes were evalu-
                        ated for physical and chemical stability over 4 days of storage at room temperature. There
                        was no change in physical appearance, but HPLC analysis showed losses of 44% and 28%
                        in the polypropylene and glass syringes, respectively, in 1 day. The losses were attrib-
                        uted to sorption to the rubber plunger tip since no decomposition peaks were observed.
                        Analytical difficulties should be considered as a possible influence on these results, since
                        these results are not consistent with other studies.(21) The FDA reported that select lots
                        of AtroPen (Meridian Medical Technologies) are stable for years (about 3 years) beyond
                        their labeled expiration date when stored per manufacturer recommentations.(24)


IV push                 Infuse rapidly.(1) If administered into a peripheral vein during CPR, follow dose with a
                        normal saline flush to facilitate drug delivery to the circulation.(3)


Intermittent infusion   Not recommended


Continuous infusion     Continuous infusion was used in a 68-year-old adult with intentional ingestion of an
                        organophosphate insecticide.(22)


Other routes of         May be given IM, sub-Q (preoperatively), ET, or IO.(10) If injected in an extremity, the limb
administration          should be elevated for 10–20 seconds. In preanesthesia induction, a faster onset of heart
                        rate acceleration was reported following submental glossal injection than with deltoid or
                        vastus lateralis injection.(23)


