Brand names            Zithromax


Medication error       None reported
potential

Contraindications      Contraindications: Should not be used in patients with known hypersensitivity to azithro-
and warnings           mycin, erythromycin, any macrolide, or ketolide antibiotic.(1)
                       Warnings: Serious allergic reactions, including angioedema, anaphylaxis, and dermato-
                       logic reactions (e.g., Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis) have been
                       reported.(1) If an allergic reaction occurs, the drug should be discontinued and appropriate
                       therapy should be instituted.(1) (See the Infusion-Related Cautions section.)
                       Despite discontinuation of azithromycin and successful management of the allergic reac-
                       tion, symptoms may recur after treatment is complete without further azithromycin expo-
                       sure.(1) Patients required prolonged periods of observation and symptomatic treatment.
                       Prolonged use may cause superinfection and/or Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea
                       (CDAD), which has been reported and may range in severity from mild diarrhea to fatal
                       colitis.(1) If CDAD is suspected or confirmed, appropriate fluid and electrolyte manage-
                       ment, protein supplementation, antibiotic treatment of C. difficile, and surgical evaluation
                       should be instituted as clinically indicated.(1)


Infusion-related       Local IV site reactions have been reported.(1) The incidence and severity of these reac-
cautions               tions were the same when 500 mg of azithromycin were given over 1 hour (2 mg/mL as
                       a 250-mL infusion) or over 3 hours (1 mg/mL as a 500-mL infusion).(1)


Dosage                 Very limited dosing information is available for IV use in pediatric patients.
                       Neonates: Doses of 10 mg/kg daily for 7 days, then 5 mg/kg daily for up to 5 weeks,
                       have been used in <72 hours old and <1000 g neonates for an anti-inflammatory effect.
                       These patients were switched to oral therapy when tolerating full enteral feeds.(2)
                       One source recommends 10 mg/kg/day divided q 24 hr for all neonates <28 days of age.(6)
                       Infants and children
                           Mild to moderate infection: IV therapy is not recommended.(6)
                           Severe infections: 10 mg/kg/day divided q 24 hr(6)
                       Community-acquired pneumonia (moderate to severe)
                           Pediatric patients: 10 mg/kg daily for 2 days and then switch to oral dosing when
                           possible(3)
                           Adolescents: 500 mg IV q 24 hr for a minimum of 2 days followed by 500 mg/day
                           orally for a total of 7–10 days(4,5)
                           Pelvic inflammatory disease: 500 mg IV for 1–2 days, followed by 250 mg/day
                           orally to complete 7 days of therapy(1)


Dosage adjustment      Caution should be used in patients with impaired hepatic function.(1) No adjustment for
in organ dysfunction   renal impairment is recommended.(11)


Maximum dosage         Little information is available, but generally 10 mg/kg/dose(1) up to 500 mg.(1) Higher
                       doses may be recommended due to drug interactions.(1) Although serum concentrations
                       have persisted for >10 days, a large IV dose (4 g) has been well-tolerated in healthy
                       adult males.(7)


Additives              Each 500-mg vial contains 114 mg (4.96 mEq) sodium.(1)




