                                                                                                 Baclofen
Dosage (cont.)                       If spasticity is of cerebral origin doses as large as 1000 mcg/day have been
                                     used.(3,10,17) Doses up to 1400 mcg/day have been given to children.(10,18)
                            Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS): IT baclofen has been used successfully
                            to treat NMS in adults and one pediatric patient.(22)
                            Status dystonicus: IT baclofen has been used for this disorder, but reports are lim-
                            ited and results have been conflicting.(50)
                            Tetanus: IT baclofen has also been used successfully in treating muscle rigidity
                            associated with tetanus in adults.(18-21)

Dosage adjustment       Baclofen is primarily eliminated unchanged by the kidneys (>70%). Patients with renal
in organ dysfunction    impairment may require smaller doses.

Maximum dosage          Although doses up to 1500–2000 mcg/day have been given,(12) there is limited experience
                        with dosages >1000 mcg/day.(3)

Additives               9 mg/mL (0.39 mEq/mL)(3,23)

Suitable diluents       Sterile, preservative-free NS for injection(3,23)

Maximum                 50 mcg/mL (commercially available) for test dose. For maintenance dosing the com-
concentration           mercially available strengths must be diluted if the patient requires strengths other than
                        0.5 mg/mL or 2 mg/mL.(3) IT refill kit concentration 0.5 mg/mL in 20-mL ampules or 2 mg/
                        mL in 5-mL or 20-mL ampules.(3)

Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(23)
                        Delivery system issues: Catheter complication may cause a sudden requirement for a
                        substantial increase or decrease in dose.(3-6) Pump malfunction can also potentially lead
                        to an overdose.(24)
                        Stability: Because the product is preservative free, each vial is intended for single use
                        only and any unused solution should be discarded. It does not require refrigeration and
                        is stable at 37°C; however, storage temperature should not exceed 30°C, and it should
                        not be frozen or heat sterilized.(3,23) Appears to be stable in an implantable pump with
                        morphine sulfate or clonidine without significant loss of either drug; however, <4% loss
                        of morphine concentration occurred during a 30-day infusion course.(23)

IV push                 Not intended for IV administration(3)

Intermittent infusion   Not applicable

Continuous infusion     Administered intrathecally via implantable pump device. Total daily dose in mcg divided
                        by 24 hours = infusion rate.(3) Do not discontinue abruptly.(3) (See U.S. boxed warning in
                        the Contraindications and Warnings section and see the Comments section.)
                        A complex (flex) dosing schedule has been described in the literature in both children and
                        adults to allow for dosing intrathecally at varying rates to correspond with variation in muscle
                        tone that occurs in a typical day (i.e., nighttime spasms in multiple sclerosis, etc.).(25,26)

Other routes of         Not intended for IM, IV, sub-Q, or epidural administration.(3) Intraventricular administra-
administration          tion has been reported as an alternative for patients with dystonia; may not be effective
                        for spasticity.(27,28)




                                                                                                                  129
Baclofen
Comments                             Significant adverse effects: Baclofen withdrawal syndrome can develop quickly into
                                     an emergent, life-threatening situation if not recognized and treated.(29) It can be difficult
                                     to diagnose because symptoms can be similar to sepsis,(30) meningitis, autonomic dysre-
                                     flexia,(4,29) neuroleptic malignant syndrome,(31) and serotonin syndrome.(32) Patients can
                                     present with fever, tachycardia, seizures, and spasticity.(29-31,33,34) Even with early diagnosis
                                     and treatment patients can become refractory to therapy, and withdrawal can lead to
                                     morbidity and mortality(33,34) including rhabdomyolysis, elevated plasma creatine kinase,
                                     elevated liver function tests, organ failure, disseminated intravascular coagulation, and
                                     death.(29,31,33,35) This syndrome has been reported in adults and pediatric patients.(31,36,37)
                                     A 10-year-old exhibiting dystonic-dyskinetic movements along with generalized tonic–clonic
                                     seizure activity (resembled status epilepticus) following a kink in the catheter essentially
                                     disrupting delivery of IT baclofen.(38)
                                     A pump malfunction in a 12-year-old delivered a near-fatal dose of baclofen.(24) Reinitiate
                                     baclofen as soon as possible if withdrawal syndrome occurs.(3,29,33) Other treatment options
                                     include oral baclofen replacement,(3) benzodiazepines,(29,32,33,37) dantrolene sodium,(39) and
                                     cyproheptadine.(32,40) Withdrawal symptoms have persisted despite oral baclofen replace-
                                     ment therapy.(41)
                                     Rare adverse effects: A study with adult patients found that IT baclofen increased
                                     seizures in patients with multiple sclerosis.(42,43) A pediatric study reported no change in
                                     the incidence/frequency of seizures in patients with spasticity of cerebral origin receiving
                                     IT baclofen.(10)
                                     Monitoring: Careful assessment of the infusion system, refill schedule and procedures,
                                     and attention to pump alarms is required.(3) If a patient develops unexplained tolerance
                                     or symptoms of withdrawal despite continued infusion consider catheter leakage,(4,5,34)
                                     catheter dislodgement,(33) or pump failure.(6,33,44) One study recommends that cerebral
                                     spinal fluid (CSF) pressure should be measured prior to implantation to decrease risk of
                                     postoperative CSF leaks in patients with elevated CSF pressures.(45)
                                     Complications: Infection of the implanted pump or catheter has been reported to occur
                                     in 4% to 12% of pediatric patients with spasticity.(46-48) Overall complications requiring
                                     surgery have been reported at a rate of 31% over a 3-year treatment period in pediat-
                                     rics.(47) Children with dystonia experience a higher rate of complications than do those
                                     with spasticity.(49)


