Brand names         Claforan


Medication error    None reported
potential

Contraindications   Contraindications: Should not be used in those with a known allergy to cefotaxime or
and warnings        another cephalosporin antibiotic.(2)
                    Warnings: Individuals who have a type I reaction to penicillin may have cross-sensitivity
                    to cephalosporins. Cefotaxime should be given cautiously to these patients.(2) (See Appen-
                    dix C for specific information.)
                    A potentially life-threatening arrhythmia has been reported in six patients who received
                    cefotaxime by rapid bolus injection (<60 seconds) through a central venous catheter.(2)
                    Prolonged use may cause superinfection and/or Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea
                    (CDAD), which has been reported and may range in severity from mild diarrhea to fatal
                    colitis.(2) If CDAD is suspected or confirmed, appropriate fluid and electrolyte manage-
                    ment, protein supplementation, antibiotic treatment of C. difficile, and surgical evaluation
                    should be instituted as clinically indicated.(2)


Infusion-related    If a decision is made to give this medication to a patient with known penicillin hypersen-
cautions            sitivity, the patient should be closely observed for allergenicity. Although rare, anaphylac-
                    toid reactions may require immediate emergency treatment with epinephrine and other
                    emergency management.(2)


Dosage              Neonates: The manufacturer indicates that it is not necessary to differentiate between
                    premature and normal gestational age infants(2); however, others have recommended the
                    following dosing regimens:


                       PNA              ≤1200 g                 1200–2000 g                   ≥2000 g
                     <7 days      100 mg/kg/day             100 mg/kg/day divided       100–150 mg/kg/day
                                  divided q 12 hr(2,3,5)*   q 12 hr(2-5)                divided q 8–12 hr(2,4)
                     ≥7 days      150 mg/kg/day             100–150 mg/kg/day           150–200 mg/kg/day
                                  divided q 8 hr(2)         divided q 8–12 hr(2-5)      divided q 6–8 hr(2,4,5)
                    *Until 4 weeks of age

                    Doses as small as 50 mg/kg/day given as a single dose or divided q 12 hr may provide
                    effective serum concentrations for non-CNS infections in very low birth weight infants.(6,7)
                    Neonatal gonococcal infection: 50 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr for 7 days or for 10–14
                    days if meningitis is suspected(19)
                    Infants and children
                        Mild-to-moderate infections: 50–180 mg/kg/day (up to 3–6 g/day) divided
                        q 6–8 hr(4)
                        Severe infections: 200–300 mg/kg/day (up to 8–10 g/day) divided q 6–12 hr(4,8)
                        The manufacturer recommends 50–180 mg/kg/day divided q 4–6 hr if <50 kg(2)
                    Adolescents and adults: Usual dose 2 g/day given as a single dose or divided q 12 hr.(2)
                    Depending on type and severity of infection, may be treated with 2 g/dose q 6, 8, or
                    12 hr up to 12 g/day.(2)
                    Meningitis: For neonates, see maximum doses in above table. 200–300 mg/kg/day
                    divided q 6–8 hr.(4,9-12)




