                                                                                   CefoTEtan Disodium
IV push                          ≤182 mg/mL over 3–5 minutes(6,10)


Intermittent infusion            ≤40 mg/mL over 20–60 minutes(6)


Continuous infusion              No information is available to support cefotetan administration by this method; however,
                                 other beta-lactams have been given by continuous infusion.(8)


Other routes of                  400–500 mg/mL in SW, BW, NS, 0.5, or 1% lidocaine by deep IM injection.(6,10) The IM
administration                   route is not recommended for patients with septicemia, bacteremia, or life-threatening
                                 infections.(2)


Comments                         Rare adverse effects: The risk of cefotetan-induced hemolytic anemia is 3 times higher
                                 than that reported with cephalosporins.(2) If anemia develops anytime within 2–3 weeks
                                 of receiving cefotetan, the diagnosis of a cephalosporin-associated anemia should be
                                 considered.(2)
                                 Cephalosporins may inhibit vitamin K-dependent clotting factors. Cefotetan may inhibit
                                 to a greater extent than other cephalosporins.(9)
                                 Patients with renal or hepatobiliary impairment or poor nutritional status, and those with
                                 cancer have an increased risk for cefotetan inhibition of vitamin K-dependent clotting
                                 factors.(2)
                                 Nephrotoxicity may be potentiated by concomitant administration with aminoglycosides.(2)
                                 Monitoring: Prothrombin time (especially if on warfarin) and administer exogenous vita-
                                 min K as needed.(2) Monitor for signs and symptoms of hemolytic anemia and Clostridium
                                 difficile-associated diarrhea.
                                 Drug interactions: Disulfiram-like reaction (flushing, sweating, headache, tachycardia)
                                 may occur when alcohol is consumed within 72 hours of administration of cefotetan.(2)
                                 Laboratory interference: Cefotetan may cause false-positive urinary glucose results
                                 when cupric sulfate solution-based tests (Clinitest, Benedict’s solution, or Fehling’s solu-
                                 tion) are used.(2) It is recommended that glucose tests based on enzymatic glucose oxidase
                                 reactions be used. When the Jaffe reaction is used to determine creatinine, high concen-
                                 trations of cefotetan may cause falsely elevated serum or urinary creatinine values.(2)
                                 Osmolality: 200 mg/mL has an osmolarity of 800 mOsm/L.(6) Concentrations used for
                                 IM injection are very hypertonic and have osmolarities >1500 mOsm/L.(6)


