Brand names            Zinacef


Medication error       None reported
potential

Contraindications      Contraindications: Should not be used in those with a known allergy to cefuroxime or
and warnings           another cephalosporin antibiotics.(2)
                       Warnings: Individuals who have a type I reaction to penicillin may have cross-sensitivity
                       to cephalosporins. Cefuroxime should be given cautiously to these patients.(2) (See Appen-
                       dix C for specific information.)
                       Prolonged use may cause superinfection and/or Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea
                       (CDAD), which has been reported and may range in severity from mild diarrhea to fatal
                       colitis.(2) If CDAD is suspected or confirmed, appropriate fluid and electrolyte manage-
                       ment, protein supplementation, antibiotic treatment of C. difficile, and surgical evaluation
                       should be instituted as clinically indicated.(2)


Infusion-related       If a decision is made to give this medication to a patient with known penicillin hypersen-
cautions               sitivity, the patient should be closely observed for allergenicity. Although rare, anaphylac-
                       toid reactions may require immediate treatment with epinephrine, and other emergency
                       management.(2)
                       Thrombophlebitis may occur.(2)


Dosage                 Neonates*(5):

                          PNA                      ≤2000 g                                  >2000 g
                        ≤7 days     100 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr               100 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr
                        >7 days     100–150 mg/kg/day divided q 8–12 hr         150 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr
                       *Until 4 weeks of age

                       Infants ≥3 months and children
                           Mild-to-moderate infections: 50–100 mg/kg/day divided q 6–8 hr(2,5-8)
                           Severe infections: 100–200 mg/kg/day divided q 6–8 hr.(5) Cefuroxime should not
                           be used to treat bacterial meningitis. (See the Comments section.)
                       Surgical prophylaxis (clean-contaminated or potentially contaminated proce-
                       dures): 50 mg/kg within 60 minutes before surgery to allow sufficient time to achieve
                       effective antibiotic concentrations in the wound tissues during the procedure.(20) The dose
                       should be repeated intraoperatively every 2 hours during the surgical procedure.(20)
                       Meningitis: Cefuroxime is not recommended for the treatment of meningitis.(21)


Dosage adjustment      Adjust dosage in patients with renal dysfunction.(2,11) If GFR is 10–29 mL/min/1.73 m2,
in organ dysfunction   give a normal dose q 12 hr, and if GFR is <10 mL/min/1.73 m2, give q 24 hr. Supplemen-
                       tal dose for intermittent hemodialysis and peritoneal dialysis is 25–50 mg/kg q 24 hr.
                       Supplemental dose for continuous renal replacement therapy is 25–50 mg/kg q 8 hr.(11)


Maximum dosage         300 mg/kg/day,(12,13) not to exceed 6 g/day in children(5) and 3 g/dose or 9 g/day in adults
                       with meningitis(2)


Additives              Contains 2.4 mEq sodium/g of cefuroxime.(2,14)


Suitable diluents      D5W, D10W, NS, LR, D5NS, D5¼NS, D5½NS, R, 1/6 molar sodium lactate(2,14)




190
                                                                                                                 
                                                                                       Cefuroxime Sodium
Maximum                           90 mg/mL for IV push and 225 mg/mL for IM administration.(2) 137 mg/mL in SW
concentration                     (489 mOsm/kg) resulted in a maximum recommended osmolality for peripheral infusion
                                  in fluid-restricted patients.(14,15)


Preparation and                   Delivery system issues: Cefuroxime should not be admixed with an aminoglycoside.(2)
delivery
                                  Stability: Stable for 24 hours at room temperature and 2–7 days if refrigerated depend-
                                  ing on formulation.(2,14)
                                  Compatibility: See Appendix D for PN compatibility information.(16)


IV push                           90 mg/mL(2,14) over 3–5 minutes(2,14)


Intermittent infusion             Over 15–60 minutes(14)


Continuous infusion               In D5W, D10W, NS, D5NS, D5½NS, or 1/6 molar sodium lactate injection(2)


Other routes of                   225 mg/mL reconstituted with SW for IM injection(2,14)
administration

Comments                          Rare adverse events: Infants and children with meningitis who were treated with cefu-
                                  roxime had an increased incidence of hearing loss(13,17) and an increased time to sterilization
                                  of their cerebrospinal fluid.(17) The AAP does not recommend cefuroxime for meningitis.(5)
                                  Although nephrotoxicity has been reported following concomitant administration of ami-
                                  noglycoside antibiotics,(2) the literature is conflicted on this adverse effect.(18)
                                  Several cephalosporins have been implicated in triggering seizures, particularly in patients
                                  with renal impairment when the dosage was not reduced.(2) If seizures associated with
                                  drug therapy occur, the drug should be discontinued. Anticonvulsant therapy can be given
                                  if clinically indicated.
                                  Cephalosporins may inhibit vitamin K-dependent clotting factors.(19) Patients with renal
                                  or hepatic impairment or poor nutritional status have an increased risk for this adverse
                                  effect.(2) (See Appendix C for specific information.)
                                  Monitor: CBC, prothrombin time (especially if on warfarin); signs and symptoms of Clos-
                                  tridium difficile-associated diarrhea. Although rare, cefuroxime may produce alterations
                                  in kidney function(2); hence, evaluation of renal function is recommended, especially in
                                  seriously ill patients receiving large doses.
                                  Drug interactions: Cephalosporins should be given with caution to patients receiving
                                  concurrent treatment with potent diuretics (e.g., furosemide) as these may negatively
                                  affect renal function.(2)
                                  Laboratory interference: A false-positive urinary glucose results when cupric sulfate
                                  solution-based tests (Clinitest, Benedict’s solution, Fehling’s solution) are used.(2) It is
                                  recommended that glucose tests based on enzymatic glucose oxidase reactions be used.(2)
                                  Positive direct Coombs tests have been reported during treatment with cefuroxime.(2)


