Comments                Significant adverse effects: Clofarabine is known to cause significant myelo-
                        suppression.(2,3) Febrile and nonfebrile neutropenia was seen in pediatric patients.
                        Myelosuppression is reversible and was seen to be dose-dependent. Patients should have
                        routine CBC's performed and treatment should be stopped if myelosuppression does occur.
                        Cerebral, gastrointestinal, and pulmonary hemorrhage have been observed in patients
                        receiving clofarabine. These serious and potentially fatal hemorrhages appeared to be
                        caused by thrombocytopenia. It is recommended to monitor platelets and coagulation
                        parameters.
                        Possible risk of severe infection has been observed with the use of clofarabine. These
                        infections have ranged in origin from fungal, viral and bacterial. If an infection should
                        occur, discontinue therapy immediately.
                        Other significant adverse effects include hyperuricemia, SIRS, hepatic sinusoidal obstructive
                        syndrome (SOS, hepatotoxicity, renal toxicity, enterocolitis, skin reaction and embryo-
                        fetal toxicity).
                        Clofarabine is associated with a moderate (30% to 90%) risk of emesis.(10,11) Antiemetic
                        therapy should be given in order to avert any acute or delayed nausea and vomiting. The
                        recommended therapy to prevent nausea and vomiting is a 5-HT3 antagonist along with
                        dexamethasone prior to chemotherapy administration.
                        Monitoring: The patient should have a CBC with differential daily during treatment and
                        then 1–2 times weekly as deemed necessary. Liver and kidney function should be moni-
                        tored while on therapy. Signs and symptoms should be monitored for infection, SIRS,
                        and cardiac function.(2)
