                        2-hour intervals during the procedure
                            Minor procedures postoperatively: 90 mcg/kg q 2 hr for 48 hours followed
                            by q 2–6 hr until healing
                            Major procedures postoperatively: 90 mcg/kg q 2 hr for 5 days after surgery
                            followed by q 4 hr intervals until healing. Alternatively, continuous infusion with
                            Factor VII concentration monitoring (target concentration 10 international units/
                            mL) to determine maintenance infusion rate has been used.(3) Others report infus-
                            ing 50 mcg/kg/hr for 5 days then 25 mcg/kg/hr for 5 days.(4)
                        Treatment: 90 mcg/kg (35–120 mcg/kg) q 2 hr until hemostasis occurs or treatment
                        is felt to be ineffective. The dosing interval may be adjusted according to bleeding
                        severity. Also, continuous infusion has been used.(5) For severe bleeds, continue dos-
                        ing q 3–6 hr to maintain hemostatic plug.(1)
                        Doses of 250–300 mcg/kg have been used successfully in joint or muscle spontane-
                        ous bleeds with a median number of 3 doses required.(6)
                    Congenital Factor VII deficiency (prophylaxis for invasive procedures): 15–30
                    mcg/kg q 4–6 hr until hemostasis.(1,7) Doses as low as 10 mcg/kg have been effective.(1)
                    Acquired hemophilia treatment: 70–90 mcg/kg q 2–3 hr until hemostasis occurs(1)
                    Glanzmann’s thromboasthenia bleeding episodes: 90 mcg/kg IV q 2–6 hr until
