Dosage (cont.)              Bleeding during extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO): Seven pediat-
                            ric ECMO patients received rFVIIa for intractable hemorrhage. Two developed occlusive
                            thrombosis of the oxygenator. Statistical significance was not achieved for the study
                            with respect to reduction of chest tube output or decreased need for transfusion.(12)
                            Both bleeding and transfusion needs were decreased in 11 pediatric ECMO patients
                            receiving rFVIIa compared to historical controls. There were two thrombotic events.
                            Usual dose was 90 mcg/kg.(13)
                            Miscellaneous bleeding: In 137 patients who received recombinant factor VIIa for
                            severe or life-threatening bleeding, laboratory parameters significantly improved, and
                            the need for blood products was reduced in patients over 1 year of age. Indications
                            include nontrauma and cardiac surgery, trauma, intracranial hemorrhage, and oncol-
                            ogy. Median total dose for infants and neonates was 300 mcg/kg, and for children
                            120 mcg/kg. There was one thrombotic event in an infant.(14)
                        Liver failure coagulopathy
                            Bleeding management: Twenty-two children with liver disease received doses rang-
                            ing from 36–118 mcg/kg to treat a variety of bleeding episodes.(15,16) A 3-month-old,
                            a 9-year-old, and an 11-year-old received 67 mcg/kg × 3, 130 mcg/kg × 2, and 200
                            mcg/kg to control bleeding from various sources.(17) Following a right hepatectomy,
