Brand names         Vaprisol


Medication error    None reported by ISMP or USP(1-3)
potential

Contraindications   Contraindications: Hypersensitivity to any component, allergy to corn or corn products
and warnings        (due to dextrose component), hypovolemic hyponatremia, anuria, and concomitant admin-
                    istration of medications that are potent CYP3A4 inhibitors.(4,6,7) (See Drug Interactions in
                    the Comments section.)
                    Warnings: Conivaptan is not indicated for treatment of congestive heart failure and
                    should only be used for treatment of hyponatremia in patients with heart failure after
                    consideration of other treatment options and when the benefit of raising serum sodium
                    outweighs the risk of adverse effects.(4,7)
                    Avoid rapid correction of serum sodium (>12 mEq/L in 24 hours) due to the risk of osmotic
                    demyelination syndrome. Discontinue therapy if the patient develops a rapid rise in serum
                    sodium. If hyponatremia persists, conivaptan may be resumed at a lower dose if there is
                    no evidence of neurologic sequelae from rapid rise in sodium.(4)


Infusion-related    Conivaptan has been associated with infusion site reactions, potentially due to propyl-
cautions            ene glycol, which was an additive in the previously marketed formulations available in
                    ampules.(4,6) The premixed conivaptan in D5W solutions do not contain propylene glycol.(4)
                    Administer via large veins and rotate infusion site every 24 hours to minimize risk of
                    irritation.(4) (See Appendix E for information regarding infiltration.)


Dosage              Conivaptan has not been extensively studied in pediatric patients. Currently there is no
                    established dosage range for pediatric patients. The following case reports describe the
                    use of conivaptan in single pediatric patients. Use this information cautiously:
                        One report describes conivaptan use for aggressive hydration to prevent tumor lysis
                        syndrome in a 13-year-old with lymphoma and syndrome of inappropriate antidi-
                        uretic hormone (SIADH) as follows(8): 10 mg IV infused over 30 minutes, followed by
                        10 mg/day infused over 24 hours titrated up to 30 mg/day infused over 24 hours. This
                        treatment regimen was successful and the patient experienced no adverse effects.(8)
                        A case report describes a 19-month-old 13.6-kg child with SIADH refractory to 3%
                        NaCl and fluid restriction who received conivaptan 2 mg (0.15 mg/kg) IV × 2 doses
                        12 hours apart. The serum sodium corrected from 124 to 135 mEq/L in about 24 hours;
                        the patient did not experience adverse effects.(10)
                        A case report describes a 4-month-old 8.5-kg baby with hypervolemic hyponatremia
                        due to congestive heart failure (severe left ventricular dysfunction). This baby received
                        a sodium chloride bolus initially (2 mEq/kg), then began conivaptan as follows:
                        0.3 mg/kg IV over 30 minutes followed by 0.3 mg/kg/day as a continuous infusion
                        × 24 hours, then 0.4 mg/kg/day × 24 hours, then a final bolus of 0.2 mg/kg over
                        30 minutes. The serum sodium increased from 119 to 124 mEq/L in the first 8 hours
                        and ultimately increased to approximately 126 mEq/L over the 48-hour duration of
                        infusion. No adverse effects occurred.(11)
                    The following doses are recommended in adults(4,6):
                        Loading dose: 20 mg IV infused over 30 minutes
                        Maintenance dose: 20 mg/day via continuous infusion over 24 hours. After first
                        day, if serum sodium is not increasing as much as desired, dose may be titrated to
                        40 mg/day. Total duration should not exceed 4 days.
                        Alternatively, one adult randomized, placebo-controlled trial reported that intermittent
                        30-minute infusions of 20 mg once or twice daily were effective in increasing serum
                        sodium compared to placebo.(9)




