IV push                 Dilute with NS for direct injection/IV push.(3)


Intermittent infusion   Most infusions occur over 30–60 minutes. If the dosage is >1800 mg/m2, the infusion
                        should last 4–6 hours.


Continuous infusion     Although cyclophosphamide may be administered continuously, (8,10) its use is not
                        established.


Other routes of         Cyclophosphamide has been administered IM,(10) but use is not established. IM administra-
administration          tion may be less effective as this route bypasses the liver. May also be given intraperito-
                        neally or intrapleurally.(10) Reconstitute with NS if administering via IM, IP, or intrapleural
                        route.(3)


Comments                Significant adverse effects: Cyclophosphamide is associated with hemorrhagic cystitis.(3)
                        Patients should be encouraged to drink excess fluids and void frequently starting 24 hours
                        before therapy and continuing 24 hours after therapy.(3) Mesna therapy should be used
                        with high dosages of cyclophosphamide (i.e., dosages >1 g/m2/day).(3) The urine should
                        be examined for the presence of RBCs, an indicator of hemorrhagic cystitis.
                        Cyclophosphamide is associated with long-term gonadal function damage and infertility
                        in pediatric patients.(3)
                        Cyclophosphamide is associated with a high (>90%) risk of emesis in IV dosages >1500
                        mg/m2 and a moderate (30% to 90%) risk of emesis with IV dosages ≤1500 mg/m2.(11)
                        Patients should receive antiemetic therapy to prevent acute and delayed nausea and
                        vomiting. The recommended therapy is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist in combination with
                        dexamethasone on each day chemotherapy is administered.(11-13) These agents may
                        be continued for up to 4 days after chemotherapy administration for the prevention of
                        delayed nausea and vomiting. Breakthrough medications should also be offered, such as
                        a phenothiazine (e.g., prochlorperazine), a butyrophenone (e.g., droperidol), a substituted
                        benzamide (e.g., metoclopramide), or a benzodiazepine (e.g., lorazepam).(13)
                        Rare adverse effects: Cardiac toxicity has been reported at dosages ranging from
                        2.4–26 g/m2.(3)
                        Monitoring: Urine output, electrolytes, BUN, SCr, CBC with differential and platelets,
                        urine specific gravity, and urinalysis.(3)
                        Drug interactions: Cyclophosphamide is a major substrate for CYP2B6 and 3A4 and a
                        minor substrate for CYP2A6, 2C9, and 2C19; it is associated with numerous drug interac-
                        tions. Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before combining any
                        drug with cyclophosphamide.(3)
                        Concomitant allopurinol use may enhance toxicity, particularly bone marrow suppression.(3)




