Contraindications      Contraindications: Hypersensitivity to cysteine or any component of the formulation.(1)
and warnings           Should not be used in patients with hepatic coma or metabolic disorders that impair
                       nitrogen metabolism.(1)
                       Warnings: Metabolic acidosis, primarily in neonates, has been reported and relates to
                       the “hydrochloride” component of the cysteine product.(2,3) Each 1 mmoL of cysteine
                       (175 mg) delivers 1 mEq of chloride and 1 mEq of hydrogen ion. In order to account for
                       the extra hydrochloride ions and prevent acidosis in patients receiving PN, add 1 mEq
                       acetate electrolyte salt for each mmoL (175 mg) of cysteine to the PN solution.
                       Contains aluminum, which may reach toxic serum concentrations with prolonged adminis-
                       tration especially if kidney function is impaired.(1) Premature neonates are at increased risk
                       for toxicity due to kidney immaturity. They also require large amounts of calcium and phos-
                       phate, which may also contain aluminum. (See the Additives section for more information.)


Infusion-related       Peripheral infusion of amino acids may result in local reactions (e.g., warm sensation,
cautions               erythema) and phlebitis or thrombosis at the infusion site.(1) The pH of cysteine HCl is
                       about 1–2.5.(1)


Dosage                 Cysteine HCl is added to PN solutions formulated with pediatric amino acids in order to
                       normalize plasma amino acid patterns in infants(2,4) and has been added to PN solutions
                       formulated with standard amino acid solutions to acidify the solution and enhance calcium
                       and phosphorous solubility.(5)
                       Normal dosing is 40 mg of cysteine per 1 g of amino acid, with doses ranging from
                       30–40 mg/g of pediatric amino acid(1,4,6) or 0.4-1 mmoL/kg (~48–121 mg/kg) per day.(2,3)
                       The American Society for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition (ASPEN) has recommended
                       alternate dosing during PN product shortages as follows(7): Restrict supplementation in
                       PN to neonates ≤1 kg and neonates >1 kg at risk for cysteine deficiency (postsurgical
                       neonates or neonates with sepsis). When cysteine is not added to PN solutions, calcium
                       and phosphorus solubility should be re-evaluated to ensure that a precipitate will not
                       develop due to increased solution pH.(7) Calcium–phosphorus solubility data exist for PN
                       solutions without cysteine.(8)


Dosage adjustment      Decrease the overall amino acid dose in patients with renal dysfunction and hepatic insuf-
in organ dysfunction   ficiency.(1) In these cases, dosing cysteine/g pediatric amino acids in mg is appropriate as
                       the dose of cysteine is based on the grams of amino acids provided and not on the patient’s
                       weight. Contains aluminum, which may reach toxic levels with prolonged parenteral
                       administration if kidney function is impaired.(1)


Maximum dosage         Should be based on mg/g pediatric amino acids. 30 mg/g in those prescribed 3 g/kg and 40
                       mg/g in those prescribed 2.5 g/kg results in a dose of 90 or 100 mg/kg, respectively.(1,4,6)
                       Doses of 121 mg/kg have been used.(2,3)


Additives              Cysteine hydrochloride contains 5.7 mEq Cl/g of cysteine HCl. Contains aluminum as a
                       contaminant.(1) Patients with impaired renal function, including neonates, who receive
                       >4–5 mcg/kg/day may accumulate aluminum at levels that produce central nervous
                       system and bone toxicity.(9)


Suitable diluents      PN solutions


