                                                                                                                 247
Cytarabine
Comments (cont.)                     Cytarabine at doses >200 mg/m2 is associated with a moderate (30% to 90%) risk of
                                     emesis.(15,16) Patients should receive antiemetic therapy to prevent acute and delayed nau-
                                     sea and vomiting. The recommended therapy is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist in combination
                                     with dexamethasone on every day chemotherapy is administered.(15,16) These agents may
                                     be continued for up to 4 days after chemotherapy administration for the prevention of
                                     delayed nausea and vomiting. Breakthrough medications should also be offered, such as
                                     a phenothiazine (e.g., prochlorperazine), a butyrophenone (e.g., droperidol), a substituted
                                     benzamide (e.g., metoclopramide), or a benzodiazepine (e.g., lorazepam).
                                     Cardiomyopathies are possible at extremely high doses of cytarabine.
                                     Cytarabine syndrome is characterized by fever, myalgia, bone pain, possible chest pain,
                                     maculopapular rash, conjunctivitis, and malaise.(17) It typically occurs within the first
                                     6–12 hours of therapy and can be prevented with the use of corticosteroids.
                                     Monitoring: LFTs, CBC with differential and platelets, BUN, serum creatinine, signs of
                                     neurotoxicity (cerebellar toxicity and encephalopathy with high dose), fevers, uric acid.
                                     With high-dose therapy, monitor for evidence of conjunctivitis, corneal toxicity, and capil-
                                     lary leak syndrome with pericardial and pleural effusions.(3)
                                     Drug interactions: Cytarabine may decrease the effects of antigout medications. It may
                                     increase bone marrow depression and cause the development of cardiomyopathy when
                                     coadministered with cyclophosphamide. Live vaccines should not be given with cytarabine
                                     as the combination may put the patient at risk for viral replication, increased side effects,
                                     and a decreased antibody response of the host.


