Comments                             Growth retardation: Has occurred in patients with low ferritin concentrations who
                                     receive large doses of deferoxamine (>60 mg/kg).(36) To minimize this, the dose should
                                     not exceed 25–30 mg/kg.(36) Following reduction in the deferoxamine dose, growth veloc-
                                     ity may partially return to pretreatment rate. Pediatric patients should be monitored for
                                     body weight and growth every 3 months.(2)
                                     Hearing impairment: Has been associated with chronic chelation treatment with def-
                                     eroxamine.(4,6,12,14,20,21,23,26) Doses that exceed a 0.025 “therapeutic index” (ratio of mean
                                     daily dose in mg/kg divided by serum ferritin) increase the risk for sensorineural hear-
                                     ing loss.(14) Cessation of chelation therapy and/or dosage reduction frequently leads to
                                     improvement.(4,12,21,26) In those with symptoms of hearing loss, the drug should be stopped
                                     for 4 weeks. When the audiogram is stable or improved, therapy should be restarted at
                                     10–25 mg/kg/dose. In those with mild toxicity, a dosage reduction to 30 or 40 mg/kg/
                                     dose should result in a reversal of the abnormal results within 4 weeks. Moderate abnor-
                                     malities require a reduction of deferoxamine to 25 mg/kg/dose with careful monitoring.
                                     Serial audiograms should be performed every 6 months in those without problems and
                                     more frequently in young patients with normal serum ferritin values and in those with
                                     auditory dysfunction.(12)
                                     Infection: Deferoxamine has been associated with an increased risk of infection. This is
                                     particularly true for Yersinia enterocolitica and Yersinia pseudotuberculosis.(2) Mucormy-
                                     cosis has also been reported, particularly in patients receiving dialysis.(2,16) Patients should
                                     be monitored for fever, abdominal complaints, and pharyngitis, and the drug should be
                                     discontinued until any suspected or documented infection resolves.(2)
                                     Nephrotoxicity: Adequate hydration decreases the incidence of this adverse effect.(35)
                                     Neurotoxicity: Has been reported.(4,12-14,20,21,23,26) Large doses may exacerbate seizures
                                     in patients with aluminum-related encephalopathy.(2)
                                     Pulmonary syndrome: Acute respiratory distress syndrome has been associated with
                                     the length of deferoxamine therapy(22,27-31) and total daily doses >6 g.(32) Another study
                                     suggests that the pulmonary abnormalities are related to inadequate chelation.(33) Inter-
                                     mittent infusions for <24 hours have been recommended.(29,24)
                                     Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                                     combining any drug with deferoxamine.
                                     Other: Patients with chronic iron overload usually become vitamin C deficient. Adults
                                     with chronic iron overload have developed cardiac impairment following concomitant
                                     treatment with deferoxamine and large doses of vitamin C.(2) The abnormalities reversed
                                     when vitamin C was discontinued.(2) Recommended daily allowance of vitamin C doses
                                     should not be exceeded.


