Brand names         Decadron, generic

Medication error    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
potential           USP reports that dexamethasone has been confused with betamethasone, doxazosin,
                    and methadone, and patient harm did not occur. Dexamethasone has been confused with
                    dexmedetomidine, and patient harm occurred. Decadron has been confused with Depacon
                    and Dexedrine, and patient harm did not occur.(1)

Contraindications   Contraindications: Patients with systemic fungal infections except in the management
and warnings        of drug reactions to amphotericin B.(64)
                    Patients with hypersensitivity to any component in the product.(63)
                    Live virus (live attenuated influenza; measles, mumps, and rubella [MMR]; oral polio;
                    varicella; rotavirus; smallpox; zoster) vaccinations should not be given during treatment
                    with immunosuppressive doses of glucocorticoids.(64) (See Drug Interactions in the Com-
                    ments section.)
                    Warnings: Anaphylactoid reactions have been reported and those with a prior history of
                    such reactions require appropriate precautions.(64)
                    Patients on chronic steroid therapy may require increased doses during stressful
                    situations.(64)
                    Supraphysiologic doses of corticosteroids may result in suppressed pituitary-adrenal func-
                    tion, so therapy of more than a few days should be decreased gradually.(3,64)
                    Immunosuppression and an increased risk for infection are possible during steroid
                    therapy.(64)

Infusion-related    In adults, pain, tingling, or burning sensations were reported with IV push administra-
cautions            tion of doses ≥16 mg; diluting and administering doses over 10–15 minutes avoided this
                    adverse effect.(70)

Dosage              Prevention or treatment of bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD)/chronic lung
                    disease (CLD) in very low birth weight (VLBW) infants
                        AAP recommendations: High-dose dexamethasone regimens (0.5 mg/kg/day)
                        reduce BPD/CLD but are associated with short- and long-term adverse outcomes
                        including neurodevelopmental impairment and are not recommended. In patients >1
                        week of age, low-dose regimens (<0.2 mg/kg/day) may facilitate extubation with
                        fewer adverse outcomes, but there is insufficient evidence to make a recommenda-
                        tion.(4) (See Other in the Comments section.)
                        DART (Dexamethasone: A Randomized Trial) trial regimen (all doses divided
                        q 12 hr): 0.15 mg/kg/day for 3 days followed by 0.1 mg/kg/day for 3 days, 0.05 mg/
                        kg/day for 2 days, and 0.02 mg/kg/day for 2 days (cumulative dose 0.89 mg/kg).(5)
                        Lower dose regimens: Initial dose 0.05 mg/kg/day then tapered over 9 days to
                        provide a cumulative dose of 0.24 mg/kg(6) or 0.05 mg/kg/day for 10 days followed
                        by alternate-day doses for 6 days (cumulative 0.65 mg/kg).(7) (See Other in the
                        Comments section.)
                    Prevention of postextubation stridor or laryngeal edema in high-risk patients:
                    (Dexamethasone given prior to extubation was not beneficial during uncomplicated airway
                    management.)(8,9)
                        Preterm infants: 0.25 mg/kg q 8 hr for 3 doses(10)
                        Infants and children: 0.5 mg/kg (max 8 mg) at 4 hours before extubation, at the time
                        of extubation, then at 6 and 12 hours after extubation successfully prevented laryngeal
                        edema or stridor in one study.(11) 0.5 mg/kg (max 8 mg) q 6 hr × 6 doses starting
                        24 hours before extubation was found to be superior to the same dose × 3 doses
                        starting only 6 hours before extubation in a resource-limited country.(65) 0.5 mg/kg
                        (max 10 mg) q 6 hr for 6 doses beginning 6–12 hours before anticipated extubation
                        reduced postextubation airway obstruction.(12) Other studies using similar dosing
                        regimens found no benefit.(8,13,14)




