Dosage (cont.)   Nausea and vomiting prevention
                     Prevention of chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting (in addition to
                     5-hydroxytryptamine 3 antagonists),(15,16) Pediatric Oncology Group of Ontario
                     (POGO) Guidelines, 2012(61)
                         Highly emetogenic chemotherapy: 6 mg/m2/dose IV q 6 hr. If given with
                         aprepitant, decrease dexamethasone dose by half.
                         Moderately emetogenic chemotherapy
                             ≤0.6 m2: 2 mg/dose IV q 12 hr
                             >0.6 m2: 4 mg/dose IV q 12 hr
                             If given with aprepitant, decrease dexamethasone dose by half.
                     Other recommendations vary. 10 mg/m2 q 12 hr on chemotherapy days(17); 8 mg/
                     m2 prior to chemotherapy followed by either 4 mg/m2 q 6 hr for 2 doses or 8 mg/
                     m2 q 4 hr for 2 doses(18); 10 mg/m2/day (max 10 mg/day).(19) In adults, for highly
                     or moderately emetogenic chemotherapy, dexamethasone is recommended on days
                     1–3 or 1–4; for low emetogenic chemotherapy, only 1 dose prior to chemotherapy is
                     recommended.(15)
                     Prevention of radiation-induced nausea and vomiting (in addition to
                     5-hydroxytryptamine 3 antagonists)(15): 5-day course of dexamethasone during
                     fractions 1–5
                     Prevention of postoperative nausea and vomiting: In children undergoing sur-
                     gery for strabismus, 0.25 mg/kg was as effective as 0.5 mg/kg and 1 mg/kg given
                     during induction.(20) In another study in 135 children undergoing strabismus surgery,
                     one intraoperative dose of 1 mg/kg dexamethasone (max 25 mg) was as effective
                     as ondansetron.(21) For tonsillectomy, single preoperative doses of 0.15 mg/kg (max
                     8 mg),(22) 0.5 mg/kg (max 8 mg),(23,34) and 1 mg/kg (max 50 mg)(25) have been
                     effective.(26)
                 Acute laryngotracheitis (croup): For those who are unable to take PO, 0.6 mg/kg
                 (max 16 mg) IM or IV as a single dose is recommended.(27-33) In children with moderate-
                 to-severe croup, 0.15 mg/kg (max 3 mg; n = 20) was as effective as 0.6 mg/kg (max
                 12 mg; n = 21).(31)
                 Meningitis, bacterial: 0.15 mg/kg q 6 hr for 2–4 days of antibiotic therapy was effec-
                 tive in reducing hearing loss in those with Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) disease.
                 However, the outcome in patients with pneumococcal meningitis treated with dexa-
                 methasone has been either worse or no different than controls.(34,35) With the use of
                 Hib and pneumococcal conjugate vaccines, both Hib and pneumococcal meningitis have
                 decreased.(36,37) In a randomized, placebo-controlled trial of 80 neonates with meningitis,
                 0.15 mg/kg q 6 hr × 48 hours starting with first dose of antimicrobial agent was found
                 to reduce mortality (40% versus 13%, p = 0.005). Organisms isolated in these babies
                 included Klebsiella, Coagulase-negative Staphylococcus, Pseudomonas, Staphylococcus
                 aureus, and Group B Streptococcus.(66) A Cochrane review published in 2015 analyzing
                 >4000 patients (>2500 pediatric) found that corticosteroids reduced hearing loss in
                 children, and, despite the declining prevalence of Hib and pneumococcal causes, recom-
                 mended dexamethasone 0.6 mg/kg/day for 4 days in patients in high-income countries
                 with bacterial meningitis.(38)
                 Asthma: Methylprednisolone sodium succinate is the recommended parenteral glu-
                 cocorticoid for asthma.(39) However, a randomized controlled trial found a single dexa-
                 methasone dose (0.6 mg/kg IM; max 15 mg) as effective as a 5-day course of oral
                 prednisolone for patients with moderate exacerbations discharged home from the ED, (40)
                 and findings from a meta-analysis support single or 2-dose IM or oral dexamethasone
                 regimens over 5-day prednisone or prednisolone regimens for patients in the emergency
                 department setting.(62)
                 Cerebral edema, elevated intracranial pressure due to brain tumor: A loading dose
                 of 1–2 mg/kg(33,41) followed by 1–1.5 mg/kg/day (max 16 mg/day) divided q 4–6 hr.(41)
                 Not recommended for treatment of elevated intracranial pressure in pediatric patients
                 with traumatic brain injury.(42)




