Dosage (cont.)          Septic arthritis: 0.15 mg/kg/dose IV q 6 hr for 4 days reduced the duration of IV anti-
                        biotics, local inflammatory signs, and acute phase reactants in a randomized, placebo-
                        controlled trial in 49 children 6 months to 18 years old. No adverse effects were noted.(43)
                        Contrast reactions (emergency premedication) (in patients with known allergy
                        to methylprednisolone, aspirin, or NSAID, especially in patients with asthma):
                        American College of Radiology Guidelines recommend (adults) 7.5 mg IV q 4 hr until
                        contrast study done (plus IV diphenhydramine 50 mg 1 hour before contrast injection).
                        Guidelines do not give pediatric age- or weight-based dosing recommendations.(60)
                        Intractable epilepsy (West syndrome or electrical status epilepticus in slow-
                        wave sleep): Pulse therapy with 20 mg/m2 IV daily for 3 days with 4 weeks between
                        each cycle appeared as effective as adrenocorticotropic hormone therapy in a small
                        retrospective study. Positive responses were observed from 1 week up to 10 weeks
                        after initiation.(44)

Dosage adjustment       Those with cirrhosis of the liver or who are hypothyroid may have an exaggerated
in organ dysfunction    response.(64) Dosing adjustment is not necessary for renal dysfunction.(45)

Maximum dosage          Not established(64)

Additives               Some products contain sodium sulfite, sodium metabisulfite, and/or benzyl alcohol. Pre-
                        servative-free products are available.(46,47,64) (See Appendix C for more specific information
                        about potential adverse effects and/or benzyl alcohol toxicity in neonates.)

Suitable diluents       D5W, NS(64,48)

Maximum                 Undiluted (10 mg/mL)(64)
concentration

Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions, compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(48)

IV push                 Over 1 to several minutes(48)
                        Doses ≥16 mg in adults have been diluted in 50 mL normal saline and infused over
                        10–15 minutes in order to avoid potential pain, tingling, and burning sensations associ-
                        ated with doses administered as IV push.(70)

Intermittent infusion   The dose can be infused intermittently.(64,70)

Continuous infusion     Not indicated

Other routes of         Formulations of 4 or 10 mg/mL may be used IM or IV.(28,29,40,64) 4 mg/mL can be given as
administration          intra-articular, intrasynovial, intralesional, or as a soft tissue injection.(64,63)

Comments                Other: Although early dexamethasone (initiated during first week of life) facilitated
                        extubation in VLBW infants, the Vermont Oxford Network Steroid Study Group concluded
                        that neither CLD nor mortality is favorably affected.(50) Cochrane and systematic reviews
                        report benefits including reductions in CLD and extubation failure with early dexametha-
                        sone(51,52,67) and reductions in mortality, CLD, and extubation failure with late dexametha-
                        sone (initiated after first week of life).(53,55,68) However, Cochrane and systematic reviews
                        raise concerns about serious adverse effects.(51-55,67) Dexamethasone is associated with
                        a worsened neurological outcome including an increased risk of cerebral palsy,(51,56,67)
                        poor weight gain,(50,51,57,67) GI bleeding,(51,67) intestinal perforation,(51,57,67) hyperglycemia,
                        hypertension, and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.(51,67) Although selected populations may
                        derive benefit from dexamethasone, clear recommendations cannot be made. The use of
                        dexamethasone should be reserved for infants >1 week of age who cannot be weaned
                        from mechanical ventilation, and the dose and duration should be minimized.(4,53)




