IV push                 Not recommended by manufacturer.(3) A study to determine safety of rapid bolus doses
                        found that 50% of patients had hemodynamic compromise when given doses >0.5 mcg/
                        kg over 5 seconds.(75) Other authors reported safe and effective use of 0.5–1 mcg/kg over
                        20 seconds for termination of reentrant SVT.(72)


Intermittent infusion   4 mcg/mL.(3) Bolus (loading) doses are typically administered over 5–10 minutes.(3,28)


Continuous infusion     Manufacturer recommends 4 mcg/mL.(3) 8, 12, and 20 mcg/mL in NS are stable in polyvi-
                        nyl chloride bags for 48 hours.(85) Manufacturer labeling limits recommended duration to
                        24 hours.(3) Longer durations have been reported (75 hours,(29) 378 hours,(44) 26 days,(74)
                        46 days,(40) >2 months,(30) and >3 months).(69,70)


Other routes of         Not indicated. However, various other routes have been reported.
administration
                        Caudal: Dexmedetomidine (1 or 2 mcg/kg) has been combined with bupivacaine and
                        given via caudal block in patients from 0.5–6 years of age in Cairo, Egypt.(45,46)
                        Regional anesthesia: 1 mcg/kg with bupivacaine was administered as greater palantine
                        nerve block for cleft lip/palate repair.(47)
                        Intranasal: 1 mcg/kg (age ≥1 month),(48,49,76-80) 2 mcg/kg (age ≥1 month),(79,81) 2.5 mcg/
                        kg (age 3–43 months),(83) and 3 mcg/kg (age 1–37 months)(82) have all been reported.
                        Following nasal administration of 1 mcg/kg in children, the median onset was 25 minutes
                        and median duration was 85 minutes.(50) Most studies used undiluted drug (100 mcg/
                        mL).(48,49,77-79,82,83)
                        Sub-Q: Seven patients, 6 months to 4 years old, received infusions for 4–7 days for drug
                        withdrawal.(51)
                        IM: 1–4 mcg/kg (using 4-mcg/mL injection); patients were premedicated with topical
                        lidocaine patches 20 minutes before injection.(52)
                        Buccal: 3–4 mcg/kg (using undiluted injection) administered 60 minutes before surgery
                        in patients 1–7 years old.(53)


Comments                Dexmedetomidine appears to have less effectiveness in infants <1 year old.(28,34)
                        Significant adverse effects: Bradycardia (high 40s to low 50s) was reported in a
                        5-week-old girl with trisomy 21 who had a large atrioventricular septal defect and was also
                        receiving digoxin.(54) Up to 30% of patients undergoing sedation for a CT scan experienced
                        bradycardia during the bolus (2 mcg/kg) or infusion (1 mcg/kg/hr).(6) In a second study
                        examining sedation for MRI, while less than 4% of patients experienced bradycardia (bolus
                        of 2–3 mcg/kg and infusion of 1–2 mcg/kg/hr), the heart rates at times fell more than
                        20% below the lower limit of normal.(7) No interventions were necessary for the brady-
                        cardia episodes.(6,7) An 11-year-old girl who received 100 mcg (2.4 mcg/kg) intranasally
                        developed bradycardia (heart rate 38 beats per minute) and syncope 2 hours after the
                        dose was given. This event occurred after completion of the procedure and the patient
                        had met discharge criteria.(84)
                        Withdrawal is possible after prolonged infusions.(70) Acute withdrawal occurred in a 2-year-
                        old who had received an infusion (0.3–0.8 mcg/kg/hr) for 7 days during and after cardiac
                        surgery. Withdrawal symptoms resolved with reinstitution of the infusion and did not
                        recur with gradual tapering (by 0.1 mcg/kg/hr q 8 hr).(55) Another report of withdrawal,
                        including seizures, occurred in an 8-week-old after a 3.5-day infusion (0.5–2 mcg/kg/
                        hr). Symptoms improved after starting clonazepam and reinstituting the infusion; the
                        infusion was tapered off over 9 hours.(42) A patient who had received an infusion for over
                        2 months (up to 1.4 mcg/kg/hr) was successfully tapered off by decreasing the infusion
                        by 0.06 mcg/kg/hr each day for 2 weeks.(30) In a review of cardiac ICU patients who had
                        received prolonged infusions (median duration 6 days), withdrawal symptoms occurred
                        in 30% of patients. The median time to wean off the infusion was 43 hours.(74)




