                                                                                                                 
                                                                               Dexmedetomidine HCl
Comments (cont.)                   Twelve children with supraventricular tachycardia who underwent cardiac electrophysiol-
                                   ogy study and ablation under anesthesia were given 1 mcg/kg over 10 minutes followed
                                   by a continuous infusion of 0.7 mcg/kg/hr for 10 minutes. During the infusion, sinus and
                                   atrioventricular nodal function were depressed, heart rate decreased, and arterial blood
                                   pressure increased.(56) A subsequent study in 51 postoperative cardiac surgery patients
                                   found no significant differences in electrocardiographic measurements taken before and
                                   during infusion, other than an expected trend toward decreased heart rate.(31) A case
                                   report described loss of pacemaker capture during infusion.(57)
                                   Transient hypertension may occur due to the acute increase in peripheral vasoconstric-
                                   tion during higher (>2 mcg/kg) bolus doses(41,58) or during higher (>1 mcg/kg/hr) dose
                                   infusions.(41,58,59) Hypertension occurred more frequently in infants who required more
                                   than 1 loading dose to achieve effective initial sedation scores for procedures.(41) A rec-
                                   ommendation for limiting hypertension in the postoperative cardiac population is to limit
                                   bolus doses to 0.5 mcg/kg.(58)
                                   A severe rash was noted within 4 hours of beginning a continuous infusion in a critically
                                   ill, intubated adult. The infusion was discontinued and by 48 hours the rash had disap-
                                   peared. According to the Naranjo adverse drug reaction probability scale, the relationship
                                   with dexmedetomidine was probable.(60)
                                   Pharmacokinetics: Molecular weight is 236.7.(3) The terminal half-life in 24 children
                                   from 2–12 years of age was 1.8 hours.(61) In 10 postsurgical, critically ill children from
                                   0.4–7.9 years of age, the terminal half-life was 2.65 ± 0.88 hours (range: 1.59–4.27
                                   hours).(62) A study evaluating population pharmacokinetics in children from 4 days to
                                   14 years found that clearance in neonates was about one third that of adults and reached
                                   87% of adult values at 1 year of age.(63) In a pooled analysis, clearance in postoperative
                                   cardiac surgical patients was 27% lower than all other patients.(64) A phase II/III phar-
                                   macokinetic study found that neonates 28–44 WGA had larger volume of distribution,
                                   increased unbound drug, decreased clearance, and increased AUC compared to children
                                   or adults.(68) The terminal half-life in adults is approximately 2 hours.(3)
                                   Impact on EEG: A bolus of 2 mcg/kg with an infusion of 1 mcg/kg/hr in patients with
                                   intractable epilepsy induced an EEG pattern resembling stage II sleep.(65) Another report
                                   indicates that dexmedetomidine had less effect on EEG than midazolam.(8)



