Brand names         Valium, Zetran, generic

Medication error    High-alert medication associated with an increased risk of causing significant patient
potential           harm if used in error.
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Confusion has been reported with ALPRAZolam,
                    clonazePAM, DiaBeta, dilTIAZem, Ditropan, doxepin, LORazepam, midazolam, oxazepam,
                    and temazepam.(2) Valium with Vicodin have also been confused.(2) Errors with dilTIAZem
                    have resulted in patient harm.(2)

Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Concomitant use of benzodiazepines and opioids may result in
and warnings        profound sedation, respiratory depression, coma, and death. Concomitant use should be
                    reserved for those in whom alternative treatment options are inadequate.(3)
                    Contraindications: Should not be used in patients with a known hypersensitivity to
                    diazepam or other benzodiazepines.(3) Contraindicated in those with acute narrow angle
                    glaucoma and in open angle glaucoma unless patient is receiving appropriate therapy.(3)
                    Warnings: Administer cautiously to very ill patients or those with limited pulmonary
                    reserve due to possible apnea and/or cardiac arrest.(3) Do not administer to patients in
                    shock, coma, or in acute alcoholic intoxication with depression of vital signs.(3) When
                    used with a narcotic analgesic, the dosage of the narcotic should be reduced by at least
                    one third and given incrementally.(3) May precipitate tonic status epilepticus in patients
                    receiving diazepam for absence status or absence variant status.(3) Withdrawal symptoms
                    (e.g., convulsions, tremor, abdominal and muscle cramps, vomiting, and sweats) may
                    occur following abrupt discontinuation. The more severe symptoms are usually seen
                    in patients who received excessive doses over an extended period of time.(3) (See the
                    Infusion-Related Cautions section.)

Infusion-related    Rapid administration may cause respiratory depression and/or hypotension. (See the
cautions            IV Push section.) Resuscitative equipment, including ones necessary to support respira-
                    tion, should be readily available.(3) Thrombophlebitis and infiltration-associated tissue
                    necrosis may develop.(3-5) To reduce the possibility of venous thrombosis, phlebitis, local
                    irritation, and (rarely) vascular impairment, inject slowly (at least 1 minute for each
                    5 mg)(3) into a large vein.(3)

Dosage              Respiratory depression may occur; hence, artificial ventilation equipment must be available.
                    Flumazenil should be readily available for complete or partial reversal of benzodiazepine
                    toxicity. (See the Comments section, Other Routes of Administration section, and Fluma-
                    zenil monograph.)
                    Procedural sedation: Midazolam is the preferred benzodiazepine because of its rapid
                    onset and short duration of action.(6-8) (See the Midazolam monograph.) If diazepam is
                    used, give 0.05–0.1 mg/kg slowly and titrated q 15–30 min(7,8) The lowest effective dose
                    should be used. Adolescents may be given 5 mg followed by 2.5 mg as needed.
                    Seizures (acute, nonstatus or status epilepticus): Lorazepam is the preferred IV ben-
                    zodiazepine for use in infants and children, and midazolam is preferred in neonates.(38-41)
                    (See the Lorazepam and Midazolam monographs.)
                        Neonates: Not recommended due to benzyl alcohol content and prolonged sedation
                        due to an inability to metabolize.(3) (See the Additives section and the Comments
                        section.) If a benzodiazepine is needed, midazolam would be preferred.
                        Infants (>30 days) and children (<5 years): 0.1–0.4 mg/kg repeat q 5–10 min
                        for 2–3 doses up to a total of 5–10 mg.(11,13-18) (See the Maximum Dosage section.)
                        May repeat in 2–4 hours as needed. Although continuous infusion diazepam has been
                        used in refractory epilepsy, midazolam is preferred for continuous infusion. (See the
                        Continuous Infusion section and the Midazolam monograph.)
                        Children (>5 years) and adolescents: 1 mg repeat q 5–10 min for 2–3 doses
                        up to a total 10 mg.(11,13-18) (See the Maximum Dosage section.) May repeat in 2–4
                        hours as needed. Although continuous infusion diazepam has been used, midazolam
                        is preferred for continuous infusion. (See the Continuous Infusion section and the
                        Midazolam monograph.)




