Dosage (cont.)          Tetanus (muscle spasms): The manufacturer recommends IV or IM administration of
                        1–2 mg/dose q 3–4 hr if >30 days and <5 years of age and 5–10 mg/dose q 3–4 hr if
                        ≥5 years of age.(3) A series of 19 neonates received 20–40 mg/kg/day (0.83–1.67 mg/
                        kg/hr) as continuous infusion accompanied by intermittent bolus doses of 5–10 mg.(19)
                        Three neonates (7–10 days) were given 48–50 mg/kg/day divided into 2-hour intervals.(20)
                        Three children (6–12 years) received 7.5–15 mg/kg/day divided into 2-hour intervals.(20)
                        (See Pharmacodynamics in the Comments section.)

Dosage adjustment       No adjustment required in renal dysfunction(3,21); however, prolonged administration to
in organ dysfunction    patients with renal dysfunction may result in propylene glycol toxicity.(22) (See Appendix
                        C for specific information about benzyl alcohol and propylene glycol potential for toxicity.)
                        Midazolam should be used in a situtaiton when propylene accumulation is a concern. Use
                        cautiously in hepatic dysfunction.(3)

Maximum dosage          0.3 mg/kg,(15,18) not to exceed a total dose of 5 mg in children <5 years or 10 mg in
                        children ≥5 years of age.(3) 2.7 mg/kg was given without adverse effects to a neonate(24)
                        and 1.7 mg/kg/hr was given via continuous infusion.(25) (See the Additives section and
                        the Comments section.)

Additives               1 mL contains 1.5% benzyl alcohol, 40% propylene glycol, 10% ethyl alcohol, and 5%
                        sodium benzoate and benzoic acid.(27) (See Appendix C for specific information about
                        benzyl alcohol and propylene glycol potential for toxicity.)

Suitable diluents       Administration of undiluted product is preferred due to concerns over precipitation in D5W,
                        LR, or NS.(27) (See the Preparation and Delivery section.)

Maximum                 5 mg/mL for IV push(16,22)
concentration

Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(27)
                        Preparation: Addition of the diluent to the diazepam injection results in a cloudy solu-
                        tion that clears.(27)
                        Stability: Store commercial product at controlled room temperature.(27) In tropical cli-
                        mates, discoloration due to oxidative hydrolytic degradation may occur. Over 4 weeks,
                        there was no loss of drug under simulated summer conditions (26°C to 38°C) in paramedic
                        vehicles.(27) No significant degradation was noted over 60(42) and 120 days, even at high
                        temperatures (>40°C).(43)
                        Delivery system issues: No significant loss of diazepam was noted following storage
                        in syringes composed of polypropylene and polyethylene.(27) Loss of potency appears to
                        be a function of drug concentration and time. Significant loss due to diazepam sorption
                        (24% loss of potency in 30 minutes and 80% to 90% loss in 24 hours) has been noted
                        with PVC bags. Diazepam 50 mg/L in D5W and NS was delivered over 7 hours through
                        four types of 0.2-m membrane filters. Diazepam loss of 7% to 17% was noted in the first
                        60 minutes; subsequent diazepam levels returned to the original concentration when the
                        binding sites became saturated.
                        Photosensitivity: Protect commercial product from light.(27)

IV push                 Over ≥3 minutes.(3,27) Rate should not exceed 2 mg/min in infants and children(28) and
                        5 mg/min in adults.(3) Apnea may occur if given too rapidly.(18)

Intermittent infusion   No information




