DiazePAM
Continuous infusion                Midazolam is the benzodiazepine of choice for continuous infusion as it does not contain
                                   benzyl alcohol or propylene glycol. (See the Midazolam monograph.)

Other routes of                    Midazolam is the benzodiazepine of choice for IM administration. (See the Midazolam
administration                     monograph.) Undiluted (5 mg/mL) diazepam (osmolality = 7775 mOsm/kg) can be given
                                   deep into a muscle, but it may cause tissue damage(3-5) and is erratically absorbed.

Comments                           Rare adverse effects
                                        Propylene glycol toxicity frequently occurs in patients with renal dysfunction, who
                                        receive prolonged infusions. Patients have hyperosmolality, increased anion gap
                                        and osmolar gap metabolic acidosis (due to lactic acidosis), acute kidney injury, and
                                        sepsis-like syndrome.(22,30,31)
                                        Abnormal movements of limbs (e.g., myoclonus or seizures) have been described in
                                        premature and full-term neonates given benzodiazepines.(32-34) The movements began
                                        a few minutes after a bolus injection and continued for several hours.(32,34)
                                        Withdrawal symptoms (e.g., convulsions, tremor, abdominal and muscle cramps,
                                        vomiting, and sweats) may occur following abrupt discontinuation. The more severe
                                        symptoms are usually seen in patients who received excessive doses over an extended
                                        period of time.(3) Exacerbation of seizures may occur in patients given flumazenil.
                                        (See antidote section below.)
                                   Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                                   combining any drug with diazepam. Diazepam is a substrate of CYP1A2, 2B6, 2C9, 2C19
                                   (major), and 3A4 (major).(9) It is a weak inhibitor of CYP2C19 and CYP3A4.(9)
                                   Pharmacodynamics: Use in neonates is controversial since benzodiazepines may
                                   decrease blood pressure and cerebral blood flow velocity.(35) Neonates may also experience
                                   prolonged CNS depression because of an inability to biotransform diazepam to an inactive
                                   metabolite.(3) Some recommend the initial dose be reduced by 50% in infants <2 months
                                   of age(36); regardless, diazepam should be used cautiously in neonates.
                                   Antidote: Flumazenil, a specific benzodiazepine-receptor antagonist, is indicated for com-
                                   plete or partial reversal of benzodiazepine toxicity.(37) (See the Flumazenil monograph.)
                                   0.01 mg/kg (maximum 0.2 mg/dose); if no effect in 1–2 minutes, repeat dose q 2 min until
                                   patient responds or a total cumulative dose of 0.05 mg/kg or 1 mg is given. Although rare
                                   (1% to 2%), patients given flumazenil may experience exacerbation of seizure activity,
                                   which can range from simple twitching and jerking, to single or multiple seizure events,
                                   to status epilepticus.(35,37) Seizures responded to reintroduction of a benzodiazepine or
                                   use of an anticonvulsant.(36,37) Because of the presence of flumazenil, larger than normal
                                   doses of benzodiazepines may be required.



