Brand names            Lanoxin, Lanoxin Pediatric Injection, generics


Medication error       High-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm if
potential              used in error.(1)
                       Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Confusion has been reported with Digoxin and
                       levothyroxine and Digibind.(2) Confusion has also been noted between Lanoxin and lasix,
                       levaquin, levoxyl, levsin, lomotil, lotensin, and levenox.


Contraindications      Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to digoxin or other digitalis preparations or
and warnings           in patients with ventricular fibrillation.(3)
                       Warnings: Caution is advised in patients with the following: acute myocarditis or amyloid
                       cardiomyopathy, sinus node disease and atrioventricular (AV) block, chronic constric-
                       tive pericarditis, electrical cardioversion (low voltage), electrolyte imbalance, hypo-or
                       hyperthyroidism, hypoxia, renal disease, severe bradycardia, severe heart failure, severe
                       pulmonary disease, sick sinus syndrome, ventricular tachycardia, ventricular premature
                       contractions, and Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome.(3,5)


Infusion-related       Inadvertent over administration may occur if a tuberculin syringe is used to measure very
cautions               small doses.(4,5) Do not aspirate fluid or blood into the syringe containing digoxin because
                       administration of residual drug left in the syringe hub may cause an overdose.(4,5) (See
                       the Preparation and Delivery section.)


Dosage                 Dosage is based on lean body weight and normal renal function.(3) Premature and immature
                       newborns display considerable variability in their tolerance to digoxin; hence, the dosage
                       of the drug must be individualized according to their degree of maturity. In general,
                       divided daily dosing is recommended for infants and children.(3) Administer one half of
                       total digitalizing dose (TDD) initially, one fourth 8–12 hours after the first dose, and the
                       remaining one fourth 8–12 hours after the second dose.(6-9) Assess clinical response before
                       administering each dose.(3) The initial maintenance dose is generally 20% to 30% and
                       25% to 35% of the total IV digitalizing dose in premature neonates and infants/children,
                       respectively.

                        Age                  TDD (mcg/kg)        Maintenance Dose (mcg/kg/day)
                        Premature            15–30  (3,5-9)
                                                                 5–10 divided q 12 hr(6,8,10-14)
                        neonates
                        Full-term            10–30(3,6,8,9)      8–10 divided q 12 hr(3,8,15)
                        neonates
                        Infants <2 yr        30–50(7,8,13)       10–12 divided q 12 hr(3,8,15
                        Children 2–10 yr     20–35(3,6,8)        8–10 divided q 12 hr(8,15)
                        Children >10 yr      8–12(3)             2–3 divided q 12 hr(3,16) or given once daily(17)

                       Because large variability exists in patient response to initial and maintenance doses,
                       individualize therapy based on serum digoxin concentration and clinical response.


Dosage adjustment      If CrCl is 30–50 mL/min/1.73 m2, give 75% of the dose; if 10–29 mL/min/1.73 m2, give
in organ dysfunction   50% of the normal dose or give a normal dose q 36 hr; and if CrCl is <10 mL/min/1.73
                       m2, give 25% of a normal dose or give a normal dose q 48 hr.(18) If on intermittent hemo-
                       dialysis or peritoneal dialysis, give 25% of the normal dose at recommended intervals or
                       give every 48 hours; if on continuos renal replacement therapy, give 75% of the normal
                       dose at recommended intervals.(18) Dosing should be optimized by measuring serum digoxin
                       concentrations using an assay that is sensitive and specific for digoxin.




