                                                                                                  Digoxin
Maximum dosage          TDD should not exceed 1 mg.(19) Maintenance dose generally approximates 30% of TDD
                        and rarely exceed 10 mcg/kg/day or 0.25 mg/day.(3-15)


Additives               Pediatric (100 mcg/mL) and adult (250 mcg/mL) formulations contains propylene ­glycol
                        40% and alcohol 10%.(3,20) (See Appendix C for specific information about propylene
                        glycol potential for toxicity.)


Suitable diluents       D5W, NS, ½NS, D5½NS, or LR(20)


Maximum                 100 mcg/mL for pediatric patients and 250 mcg/mL for adults(3)
concentration

Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(20)
                        Preparation: For dose volumes <0.1 mL, prepare a 1:10 dilution to improve measure-
                        ment accuracy. Dilute 0.1 mL of digoxin (100 mcg/mL) with 0.9 mL of saline to make a
                        concentration of 10 mcg/mL. Use the two-syringe technique to make the dilution: draw
                        up the drug in one syringe and the diluent in another. Inject drug into diluent syringe and
                        mix well. This technique avoids “syringe dead space overdose,” which can occur when
                        additional fluid is drawn into a drug-containing syringe. Failure to use the two-syringe
                        technique causes drug in the dead space to be drawn up also, and it can result in a sig-
                        nificantly larger dose than intended (5 mcg versus 8–12 mcg for digoxin).(4,5,11) Dilute at
                        least fourfold (25 mcg/mL).(3,20) Use of less than a fourfold volume of diluent may result
                        in precipitation.(3)
                        Stability: Use digoxin immediately after diluting.(3) Stable in D5W and NS for 48 hours.(20)
                        Store at controlled room temperature (25°C).
                        Photosensitivity: Protect from light exposure until ready to use.(3,20)


IV push                 Undiluted (100 mcg/mL) product should be given over at least 5 minutes.(3) Rapid IV
                        infusion causes systemic and coronary arteriolar vasoconstriction.(3)


Intermittent infusion   ≤25 mcg/mL(3,20) and infuse over >5 minutes.(3) (See the Comments section.)


Continuous infusion     No information available


Other routes of         IM administration is not recommended because of local pain, irritation, and tissue dam-
administration          age.(20) If it must be given IM, it should be given by deep injection followed by massage
                        of the area.(3)


Comments                Significant adverse effects: May cause virtually every arrhythmia including sinus
                        bradycardia, AV block, sinoatrial (SA) block, atrial or nodal ectopic beats, ventricular
                        arrhythmias, bigeminy, trigeminy, atrial tachycardia with an AV block.(3)
                        Monitoring: Serum electrolytes and renal function should be monitored. Hypercalcemia
                        may increase the risk of toxicity.(3) Toxicity may also occur with hypokalemia and hypomag-
                        nesemia despite serum digoxin concentrations within the therapeutic range.(3) Assessment
                        of heart rate and ECG may assist in the diagnosis of digoxin-associated toxicity.
                        Serum digoxin concentrations will be falsely elevated if drawn during the predistribution
                        phase (i.e., within 6 hours of digoxin dose).(8) Serum digoxin concentrations should be
                        obtained at least 6–8 hours after the last dose, regardless of the route of administra-
                        tion or formulation used.(3) “Therapeutic” serum concentrations generally range between
                        0.8–2 ng/mL. Although toxicity is usually accompanied by values >2 ng/mL, serum
                        concentration must be used in conjunction with clinical symptoms and ECG to confirm
                        diagnosis of digoxin toxicity.


                                                                                                               293
Digoxin
Comments (cont.)                     Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                                     combining any drug with digoxin. The metabolism of digoxin is not dependent on the
                                     cytochrome P-450 system, and it is not known to induce or inhibit the cytochrome P-450
                                     system.(3) Digoxin is a substrate of P-glycoprotein and should be used with caution when
                                     administered concomitantly with drugs known to inhibit glycoprotein (e.g., quinidine,
                                     verapamil, amiodarone, ketoconazole, and itraconazole).(22) Macrolides (erythromycin and
                                     clarithromycin) can increase digoxin bioavailability via inhibition of intestinal flora, which
                                     metabolizes digoxin in the gut while simultaneously inhibiting P-glycoprotein.
                                     Laboratory interference: Depending on the assay, serum digoxin concentrations may be
                                     falsely elevated because of endogenous digoxin-like immunoreactive substances in patients
                                     with renal or hepatic dysfunction, neonates, infants, and children.(23-28) Spironolactone
                                     may interfere with the digoxin radioimmunoassay. Digoxin immune FAB has been shown
                                     to interfere with many radioimmunoassays of digoxin.(30,31) This interference results in a
                                     significant overestimation of digoxin serum concentrations. To determine the degree of
                                     interference, review the manufacturer specification for the assay used at your institution.
                                     Other: Patients experiencing serious cardiac effects due to digitalis toxicity, may receive
                                     digoxin-immune Fab.(3) (See the Digoxin Immune Fab monograph.)
                                     Digoxin has been successfully used via transplacental administration to treat fetal supra-
                                     ventricular tachyarrhythmias.(32)


