Brand names         D.H.E. 45, and generics

Medication error    None reported
potential

Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Coadministration of ergot alkaloids with potent CYP3A4 inhibitors
and warnings        (e.g., protease inhibitors, macrolide antibiotics, and azole antifungals) may elevate the
                    serum concentrations of dihydroergotamine, which has resulted in vasospasm that causes
                    serious and/or life-threatening peripheral and cerebral ischemia.(1) Cases of amputation
                    have been reported. (See Drug Interactions in the Comments section.)
                    Contraindications: Should not be used in patients with a known hypersensitivity to dihy-
                    droergotamine. Should not be used with peripheral and central vasoconstrictors because
                    the combination may result in additive or synergistic elevation of blood pressure.(1) Do
                    not use within 24 hours of sumatriptan, zolmitriptan, other serotonin agonists, or ergot-
                    like agents; do not use during or within 2 weeks of discontinuing monoamine oxidase
                    inhibitors.(1)
                    Do not use in patients with (1) uncontrolled hypertension; (2) ischemic heart disease,
                    angina pectoris, history of myocardial infarction, silent ischemia, or coronary artery vaso-
                    spasm including Prinzmetal angina; (3) hemiplegic or basilar migraine; (4) peripheral
                    vascular disease, sepsis, severe hepatic, or renal dysfunction; and (5) following vascular
                    surgery.(1)
                    Warnings: Rare reports of increased blood pressure in patients without a history of hyper-
                    tension.(1) Although rare, there have been reports of pleural and retroperitoneal fibrosis
                    and cardiac valvular fibrosis following prolonged daily use of injectable dihydroergotamine
                    mesylate.(1) Patients who developed cardiac valvular fibrosis also received drugs associ-
                    ated with cardiac valvular fibrosis.(1)

Infusion-related    None reported
cautions

Dosage              Postmenarche female patients should undergo pregnancy screening prior to first dose of
                    dihydroergotamine.(2,3)
                    IV dihydroergotamine, given in conjunction with an antiemetic, is an appropriate treat-
                    ment of severe migraine; however, it should be reserved for patients who do not respond
                    to other drug therapy, including 5-HT1 selective receptor agonists.(4)
                    Safety and efficacy of dihydroergotamine has not been established in children.(1)
                    Intractable migraine >72 hours (status migraine)
                        In large doses, an initial test dose (50% of the appropriate dose for age/weight) was
                        used; if tolerated the remaining dose was administered 30 minutes later.(4)
                        Children 6–9 years of age or <25 kg: 0.1 mg/dose q 6 hr (small dose) up to 0.5
                        mg/dose q 8 hr (large dose); improvement usually seen within 5 doses; continue until
                        headache-free to a maximum of 16 doses (small dose) or 20 doses (large dose); if
                        no improvement noted after 5 doses, discontinue dihydroergotamine.(2,4)
                        Children 9–12 years of age: 0.15 mg/dose q 6 hr (small dose); improvement usu-
                        ally seen within 5 doses; continue until headache-free to a maximum of 16 doses;
                        if no improvement noted after 5 doses, discontinue dihydroergotamine.(2) One study
                        treated children in doses ranging from 0.1–0.5 mg/dose (increasing by 0.05 mg incre-
                        ments as tolerated) repetitively averaging 5 doses.(3)
                        Children >9 years of age or >25 kg and adolescents: 1 mg/dose q 8 hr
                        (large dose); improvement usually seen within 5 doses; continue until headache-
                        free to a maximum 20 doses; if no improvement noted after 5 doses, discontinue
                        dihydroergotamine.(2,4)
                        Adolescents ≤16 years of age: 0.2 mg/dose q 6 hr (small dose); improvement
                        usually seen within 5 doses; continue until headache-free to a maximum of 16 doses;
                        if no improvement noted after 5 doses, discontinue dihydroergotamine.(2)




