administration

Comments                          Monitoring: Blood pressure, heart rate, and electrocardiogram should be monitored
                                  throughout therapy. When possible, monitor cardiac output, central venous pressure, and
                                  pulmonary capillary wedge pressure. Consider urine output monitoring. Consider serum
                                  potassium monitoring due to the potential risk of hypokalemia.(4,25,26)
                                  Pharmacokinetic considerations: Pediatric pharmacokinetic studies have shown large
                                  variability in dobutamine clearance; changes in clearance do not appear to be associated
                                  with age, weight, underlying disease state, or addition of dopamine infusion.(8,20,21)
                                  Pharmacodynamic considerations: Long-term infusion (i.e., 3–4 days) has produced
                                  tolerance to hemodynamic effects and necessitated dosage increases in adults.(31)
                                  Other: Studies suggest dobutamine is less effective than dopamine in increasing blood
                                  pressure in premature neonates.(16,25,26,34) Dobutamine produced a greater increase in right
                                  and left ventricular output.(16,34) In fluid refractory neonatal septic shock, a combination
                                  of dobutamine and dopamine may be necessary.(35)


