  ($teddy_84_id$dopamine-hcl$teddy_84_id$, $teddy_84_name$DOPamine HCl$teddy_84_name$, $teddy_84_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_84_source$, 1185730, $teddy_84$DOPamine HCl
Brand names         Generic

Medication error    ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error.(1)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
                    ISMP and USP report that DOPamine has been confused with DOBUTamine.(2,3) USP also
                    reports that patient harm resulted when DOPamine was confused with Dobutrex.(3)

Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Extravasation may cause local ischemia and tissue necrosis.(4,47-50)
and warnings        Aliquots of phentolamine mesylate (0.1–0.2 mg/kg(5) up to 10 mg in 10–15 mL NS) should
                    be injected using a fine hypodermic needle into the ischemic area.(4,5,47-50) This should be
                    done as soon as possible and within the first 12 hours of noting the extravasation.(4,47-50)
                    (See the Infusion-Related Cautions section and Appendix E for additional information
                    regarding extravasation treatment.)
                    Contraindications: Do not use in patients with pheochromocytoma or in patients with
                    uncorrected tachyarrhythmias or ventricular fibrillation.(4,47-50)
                    Warnings: Patients who have received a monoamine oxidase inhibitor prior to use of
                    dopamine (within 2–3 weeks) will require lower dopamine doses.(4,47-50)

Infusion-related    Central access is preferred; however, peripheral IV access is acceptable during
cautions            resuscitation.(7)
                    A case report suggests that terbutaline may be an effective alternative for the treatment
                    of extravasation of dopamine when phentolamine is unavailable. A 65-year-old patient
                    experienced resolution of symptoms with local sub-Q injection of terbutaline (1 mg in
                    10 mL NS). Terbutaline concentration based on extent of extravasation, for smaller
                    areas of extravasation a 1:1 dilution may be preferred.(8) (See U.S. Boxed Warning in the
                    ­Contraindications and Warnings section and Appendix E for additional information regard-
                     ing extravasation treatment.)
                    Two case reports of distal extremity gangrene have been related to dopamine infusion,
                    and in one neonate the catheter had good blood return and no signs of infiltration or
                    extravasation were present.(9,10) Digital ischemia with sudden onset of extremity cyanosis
                    (without evidence of infiltration) was observed in a 2-week-old neonate after infusing
                    dopamine via a peripheral line into the left saphenous vein and right hand. The authors
                    suggested that dopamine be infused through a central line.(11)
                    Infusion through an umbilical artery catheter is not recommended.(12,48) Caution is advised
                    if infusion through umbilical vessels becomes necessary; vasospastic events have been
                    reported when administered through umbilical vessels.(4)

Dosage              Correct hypovolemia prior to dopamine administration.(4,47-50)
                    Shock, hypotension: 2–20 mcg/kg/min(4,6,7,14-18)
                    1–10 mcg/kg/min(4,19-25) increased by 1–10 mcg/kg/min q 10–40 min until desired hemo-
                    dynamic response is achieved, up to 20 mcg/kg/min(4,14,16,19,21,24)
                    In critically ill patients, may initiate at 5 mcg/kg/min and increase by 5–10 mcg/kg/min, up
                    to 20–50 mcg/kg/min.(4,47-50) (See the Maximum Dosage section.) Sudden discontinuation
                    may result in hypotension; consider gradually decreasing the dose.(4,47-50)
                    Other: Doses ≤2 mcg/kg/min stimulate dopamine receptors and cause increased
                    renal blood flow, glomerular filtration, and urine output.(4,47-50) Low-dose dopamine
                    (<5 mcg/kg/min) demonstrated increased urine output for patients as small as those in
                    the extremely low birth weight category (≤1000 g).(51) Despite increased urine output,
                    low-dose dopamine (<5 mcg/kg/min) does not appear to prevent or reduce the incidence
                    of acute oliguric renal failure in critically ill patients and is no longer recommended.(27)
                    Hypotension may occur at low infusion rates (0.5–2 mcg/kg/min).(4,47-50) ­Furthermore,
                    low doses of dopamine may suppress respiratory drive, increase myocardial oxygen
                    demand, worsen splanchnic oxygenation, impair GI function, and impair endocrine and
                    immunologic systems.(28)




