DOPamine HCl
Comments                             Significant adverse effects: A 16-year-old patient with pulmonary vascular obstruc-
                                     tive disease developed suprasystemic pressure in the right ventricle during dopamine
                                     infusion. It was suggested that dopamine may be contraindicated in patients with this
                                     underlying disease.(39) In a blinded, crossover trial in 19 children (2–54 months of age)
                                     requiring inotropic support after cardiac surgery, dopamine in doses >7 mcg/kg/min
                                     caused pulmonary vasoconstriction. The authors concluded that dopamine should not
                                     be used in infants with increased pulmonary vascular resistance secondary to pulmonary
                                     hypertension.(40)
                                     An evaluation of 594 children undergoing cardiovascular surgery found that a younger
                                     age, longer cardiopulmonary bypass time, and infusion of dopamine or milrinone were
                                     associated with the development of junctional ectopic tachycardia. The authors suggest
                                     that dopamine be discontinued in those who develop this condition.(41)
                                     Monitoring: Blood pressure, urine output, and cardiac output, when possible; monitoring
                                     should occur continuously throughout therapy.(4,47-50)
                                     Drug interactions: Monoamine oxidase inhibitor usage within 2–3 weeks prior to dopa-
                                     mine necessitates starting at an initial infusion rate of no more than one tenth the usual
                                     dose.(4,47-50)
                                     Pharmacokinetic considerations: Studies using a first-order kinetic model were unable
                                     to relate clearance to age.(31,42) Using a nonlinear model, clearance varied according to
                                     concentration, and increased weight (but not age) was related to a decreased clearance.(43)
                                     Still another study reported that clearance is almost 2 times greater in those <2 years
                                     old.(4,44)
                                     Other: A prospective study in 20 preterm infants with arterial hypotension refractory to
                                     volume therapy noted that dobutamine or dopamine (10 mcg/kg/min) increased mean
                                     arterial pressure and decreased superior mesenteric artery resistance and proposed that
                                     the risk for developing necrotizing enterocolitis during infusion would be reduced.(45)
                                     Use may reversibly inhibit secretion of thyroid-stimulating hormone, prolactin, and growth
                                     hormone.(46)



