Brand names            Doribax


Medication error       ISMP reports that Doribax has been confused with Zovirax.(1)
potential

Contraindications      Contraindications: Patients with known allergies to doripenem or to other carbapenems
and warnings           or anaphylaxis to beta-lactams(2)
                       Warnings
                           Allergic reactions: Serious hypersensitivity and occasionally fatal anaphylactic reac-
                           tions have been reported.(2) These are more likely in patients who are sensitive to
                           multiple allergens or other beta-lactams.(2)
                           Superinfection: Doripenem use may cause superinfection and/or Clostridium difficile-
                           associated diarrhea and may range in severity from mild diarrhea to fatal colitis. Anti-
                           biotic use that is not for the treatment of C. difficile may need to be discontinued.(2)


Infusion-related       Phlebitis may occur.(2)
cautions

Dosage                 Doripenem has not been well studied in children. The following are pediatric
                       doses currently being used in clinical trials:
                       Neonates and infants
                           5 mg/kg in infants <8 weeks old and 8 mg/kg in infants ≥8 weeks, but <12 weeks
                           old were used in a published single dose pharmacokinetics study.(3)
                           30 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr was used in infants ≤12 weeks old and 90 mg/kg/day
                           divided q 8 hr in infants 12 weeks to 1 year old in a CSF study; results of the trial
                           are unavailable.(4)
                       Infants and children (unpublished clinical trials)
                           3 months to 18 years old: 60 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr, up to 1500 mg/day(5-7)
                       Children (expert opinion): 60 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr, up to 1500 mg/day(17)
                       Cystic fibrosis: 75–90 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr administered over 4 hours has been
                       reported.(18)
                       Adults: 1500 mg/day divided q 8 hr(2)


Dosage adjustment      If CrCl >50 mL/min, no dosage adjustment is necessary.
in organ dysfunction
                       If CrCl is 30–50 mL/min, give half the normal dose q 8 hr. If CrCl is >10 but <30 mL/min,
                       give half the normal dose q 12 hr.(2) Hemodialysis may remove approximately 50% from
                       plasma.(8)


Maximum dosage         1 g/dose in adults.(9,10) Up to 1.6 g/dose was reported in a patient with cystic fibrosis.(18)


Additives              None


Suitable diluents      NS, D5W(2,11)


Maximum                4.5 mg/mL(2,11)
concentration




