Brand names            Dopram, generic


Medication error       None reported
potential

Contraindications      Contraindications: Hypersensitivity to doxapram or any component. Seizure disorders;
and warnings           possible pulmonary embolism; mechanical disorders of ventilation (e.g., mechanical
                       obstruction, muscle paresis (including neuromuscular blockade), flail chest, pneumothorax,
                       acute bronchial asthma, pulmonary fibrosis or other conditions resulting in restriction of
                       chest wall, muscles of respiration or alveolar expansion); evidence of head injury, cerebral
                       vascular accident, or cerebral edema; and significant cardiovascular disorders including
                       those associated with hyperthyroidism.(1)
                       Warnings: Avoid use in conjunction with mechanical ventilation.
                       Doxapram contains benzyl alcohol. Large amounts of benzyl alcohol may cause a poten-
                       tially fatal toxicity (“gasping syndrome”) in neonates. (See Appendix C.)


Infusion-related       Monitor blood pressure closely for evidence of hypertension.(1-3)
cautions
                       Extravasation should be avoided because of the potential for thrombophlebitis or local
                       skin irritation.(1) (See Appendix E for information on extravasation.)


Dosage                 Typically considered a third-line agent that should be reserved for neonates unresponsive
                       to theophylline or caffeine. (See the Additives and the Comments sections.)
                       Neonatal apnea: Loading dose of 2.5–3 mg/kg(4,5) followed by continuous infusion of
                       1–1.5 mg/kg/hr.(2,4-7) Patients who do not receive a loading dose should be started on
                       2 mg/kg/hr. Alternatively, a continuous infusion of 0.5 mg/kg/hr with increases of 0.5 mg/
                       kg/hr up to a maximum of 2.5 mg/kg/hr for a total length of infusion of 48 hours.(6) Then
                       decrease to lowest dose that controls apnea.(6) In one study, 0.2 mg/kg/hr was as effec-
                       tive as 1–2.5 mg/kg/hr when used with a methylxanthine.(4)
                       Neonatal extubation: A review concluded that the routine use of doxapram to facili-
                       tate extubation in premature neonates was not warranted.(9) A small study in premature
                       neonates began with an infusion of 0.5–1 mg/kg/hr and titrated to ≤2.5 mg/kg/hr for
                       48 hours.(7) In another larger study, 27 preterm infants who received an infusion of
                       3.5 mg/kg over 20 minutes followed by 1 mg/kg/hr for 48 hours after extubation had no
                       greater likelihood of remaining extubated than the control group.(8)
                       Adults and adolescents >12 yr with postanesthesia respiratory depression, drug-
                       induced respiratory depression, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: 0.5–1
                       mg/kg q 5 min up to a maximum dose of 2 mg/kg.(1) Alternatively, 5 mg/min until a sat-
                       isfactory response is obtained and maintained at an infusion of 1–3 mg/min up to a total
                       dose of 4 mg/kg (maximum 300 mg).(1) The regimen may be repeated if relapse occurs.


Dosage adjustment      Use cautiously in significantly impaired renal or hepatic function.(1)
in organ dysfunction

Maximum dosage         2.5 mg/kg/hr for 48 hours in neonates.(2,6,7,10) 3 g/day in adults.(1)


Additives              Contains benzyl alcohol 0.9% as a preservative.(1) Doses used in neonates may exceed
                       those noted to cause toxicity. (See Appendix C for specific information about benzyl
                       alcohol toxicity.)


Suitable diluents      D5W, D10W, NS(1)


Maximum                2 mg/mL(1,11)
concentration




