  ($teddy_87_id$doxorubicin$teddy_87_id$, $teddy_87_name$DOXOrubicin$teddy_87_name$, $teddy_87_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_87_source$, 1223585, $teddy_87$DOXOrubicin
Brand names         Adriamycin, generic


Medication error    ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error.(1)
                    Look alike, sound-alike drug names. DOXOrubicin may be confused with DAUNOrubicin,
                    DAUNOrubicin liposomal, doxapram, doxazosin, DOXOrubicin liposomal, epiRUBicin, IDA-
                    rubicin, and valrubicin.(2)
                    DOXOrubicin conventional formulation may be confused with the liposomal formulation
                    (Doxil). They are the same concentration.


Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Doxorubicin should be administered under the supervision of an
and warnings        experienced physician and in facilities with laboratory and supportive resources adequate
                    to monitor drug tolerance and manage drug toxicity. It can cause serious tissue necrosis
                    if given IM or sub-Q. May cause severe myelosuppression at therapeutic doses, leading to
                    infection or hemorrhage. Dosages should be reduced in patients with hepatic impairment.(3)
                    Pediatric patients are at increased risk of anthracycline-induced cardiotoxicity, includ-
                    ing impaired left ventricular ejection fraction reduction and/or heart failure that is life-
                    threatening. Cardiotoxicity is dose-dependent and may occur months or years after therapy
                    is completed. The incidence increases rapidly after a total cumulative dose 300–500 mg/
                    m2. Clinicians should monitor for total cumulative dose and patients should be monitored
                    long-term. Left ventricular ejection fraction with either an echocardiogram or MUGA scan
                    should be assessed before, during and after therapy.(3)
                    Secondary AML or myelodysplastic syndrome, have been reported in patients treated
                    with doxorubicin.(3)
                    Contraindications: Doxorubicin is contraindicated in patients with hypersensitivity
                    to doxorubicin or any components of the formulation or other anthracyclines; recent
                    myocardial infarction, severe myocardial insufficiency, severe arrhythmia; previous high
                    cumulative doses of any anthracycline; severe persistent drug-induced myelosuppres-
                    sion or baseline neutrophil count <1500/mm3; severe hepatic impairment or bilirubin
                    >5 mg/dL.(3)


Infusion-related    Doxorubicin can cause an extravasation reaction, which can lead to severe tissue necrosis,
cautions            cellulitis, thrombophlebitis, or painful induration.(4) If extravasation occurs, stop infusion
                    and disconnect, but leave cannula in place; gently aspirate extravasated solution; remove
                    cannula and elevate extremity. Administer antidote (dexrazoxane or DSMO); apply cold
                    compresses for 20 minutes 4 times daily for 1–2 days.(4)


Dosage              Consult individual protocols for complete dosing information.
                    Doxorubicin is indicated for metastatic solid tumors, leukemia, or lymphomas. Off
                    label use includes acute lymphoblastic leukemia, Ewing sarcoma, osteosarcoma, and
                    rhabdomyosarcoma.
                    General dosing for malignancy, infants, children, and adolescents(3)
                        Single agent therapy: 60–75 mg/m2 q 21 days
                        Combination therapy: 40–75 mg/m2 q 21–28 days
                    Selected pediatric regimens
                        Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (combination therapy)(5)
                                Induction: 30 mg/m2 on days 0 and 1 of a 4-week cycle
                                CNS therapy for high-risk patients: 30 mg/m2 on day 1 of a 3-week
                                cycle
                                Intensification for high-risk patients: 30 mg/m2 on day 1 of a 3-week
                                cycle




