                                                                                 DOXOrubicin
Dosage (cont.)          Ewing sarcoma
                            VIDE regimen: 20 mg/m2 over 4 hours on days 1–3 q 21 days for 6 cycles (in com-
                            bination with vincristine, ifosfamide, etoposide)(6)
                            VAIA regimen: 30 mg/m2 on days 1 and 2 q 21 days for 14 cycles (doxorubicin
                            alternates with dactinomycin; combination with vincristine and ifosfamide).(7)
                            VAC/IE regimen: 75 mg/m2 on day 1 every 21 days for 5 cycles in combination with
                            vincristine and cyclophosphamide (after 5 cycles, replace doxorubicin with dactino-
                            mycin); alternating cycles with ifosfamide and etoposide for a total of 17 cycles.(18)
                        Hodgkin lymphoma
                            BEACOPP regimen: 35 mg/m2 on day 0 of 21 day cycle (in combination with bleo-
                            mycin, etoposide, cyclophosphamide, vincristine, procarbazine, and prednisone)(8)
                        Osteosarcoma: 25 mg/m2 (bolus) on days 1–3 q 21 days (in combination with cisplatin)(9)
                        High-dose methotrexate/cisplatin/doxorubicin/ifosfamide regimen
                            Preoperative: 75 mg/m2 continuous infusion over 24 hours on day 3 of weeks 1 and 7
                            Postoperative: 90 mg/m2 continuous infusion over 24 hours on weeks 13, 22, and
                            31 (alternating methotrexate, cisplatin, and ifosfamide)(19)
                        Rhabdomyosarcoma
                            VAC/IE regimen: 37.5 mg/m2 over 18 hours each on days 1 and 2, every 6 weeks
                            in combination with vincristine and cyclophosphamide, alternating cycles with ifos-
                            famide and etoposide(20)


Dosage adjustment       Doxorubicin should be discontinued if the patient experiences any signs or symptoms of
in organ dysfunction    cardiomyopathy.
                        Renal impairment: No dosage adjustment necessary secondary to limited renal
                        excretion.(3)
                        Hepatic impairment: If the patient has a bilirubin concentration of 1.2–3 mg/dL, the
                        doxorubicin dose should be decreased by 50%. If the bilirubin concentration is 3.1–5
                        mg/dL, the dose should then be decreased by 75%. For bilirubin concentration >5 mg/
                        dL, doxorubicin should be discontinued or withheld.(2)


Maximum dosage          Cumulative doses 300–500 mg/m2 or greater are associated with increased risk for
