DOXOrubicin
Continuous infusion                Doxorubicin has been administered as a continuous infusion. Compared to bolus intermit-
                                   tent administration, this route is thought to be associated with cardioprotective properties
                                   in adults; however, this has not been proven in pediatric studies.(10-12)


Other routes of                    Do not administer IM or sub-Q.
administration

Comments                           Significant adverse effects: Doxorubicin is a known cardiotoxic anthracycline, which
                                   causes myocardial damage such as acute left ventricular failure.(13,14) Clinical studies
                                   have shown cumulative doses of 300–500 mg/m2 administered every 3 weeks to induce
                                   cardiomyopathy.(3) Coadministration with other cardiotoxic chemotherapy medications can
                                   have a synergistic increase risk for cardiomyopathy. Dexrazoxane can be used to reduce
                                   the severity of cardiomyopathy that is possible with doxorubicin.
                                   Doxorubicin has been found to cause severe myelosuppression. Neutropenia is the pre-
                                   dominant, reversible form of hematologic toxicity, which is dose dependent. Patients should
                                   have their baseline blood counts obtained and assessed at regular intervals.(2)
                                   Depending on the dose, doxorubicin is associated with a moderate (30% to 90%) to high
                                   (>90%) risk of emesis.(15-17) Patients should receive antiemetic therapy to prevent acute
                                   and delayed nausea and vomiting. The recommended therapy is a 5-HT3 receptor antago-
                                   nist in combination with dexamethasone on every day chemotherapy is administered.(15-17)
                                   These agents may be continued for up to 4 days after chemotherapy administration for
                                   the prevention of delayed nausea and vomiting. Breakthrough medications should also
                                   be offered, such as a phenothiazine (e.g., prochlorperazine), a butyrophenone (e.g.,
                                   droperidol), a substituted benzamide (e.g., metoclopramide), or a benzodiazepine (e.g.,
                                   lorazepam).(17)
                                   Monitoring: Cardiac function at baseline and throughout therapy and follow-up, LVEF,
                                   ECG, MUGA or ECHO, CBC with differential and platelets, complete liver function tests,
                                   blood uric acid, potassium, calcium, phosphate, and creatinine levels at baseline and
                                   throughout therapy. Watch infusion site for signs of extravasation.(3)
                                   Drug interactions: Doxorubicin is a substrate for CYP3A4, CYP2D6, and pgp. Concomi-
                                   tant use with inhibitors or inducers of these isoenzymes may cause a drug interaction.(3)
                                   Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before combining any drug
                                   with doxorubicin.


