Brand names         Edetate calcium disodium (calcium disodium versenate) has also been referred to as
                    calcium EDTA (see the Contraindications and Warnings section).

Medication error    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
potential           ISMP and USP report that edetate calcium disodium (calcium EDTA) has been confused
                    with edetate disodium (disodium EDTA); patient death resulted.(1,2)

Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Mortality from lead encephalopathy in pediatric patients has been
and warnings        high. Patients with lead encephalopathy and cerebral edema may experience a lethal
                    increase in intracranial pressure following IV infusion. IM administration is the preferred
                    route in these patients. When giving intravenously, avoid rapid infusion. Do not exceed
                    the recommended daily dose.(3)
                    Other warnings: In January 2008, the FDA issued an advisory regarding patient deaths,
                    including the death of at least one child (see the Comments section), that have occurred
                    when edetate disodium was mistakenly given instead of edetate calcium disodium.(4,5)
                    Edetate disodium (disodium EDTA) should never be used to treat lead poisoning because
                    it chelates calcium and induces tetany and potential fatal hypocalcemia.(6-8) Edetate diso-
                    dium is no longer available in the United States.

Infusion-related    Rapid infusion may result in thrombophlebitis or precipitation of encephalopathy.(3)
cautions            Diluted solutions (≤5 mg/mL) infused over at least 4 hours may decrease the risk of
                    thrombophlebitis.(9)

Dosage              Treatment of lead poisoning (dosing is based on blood lead level [BLL] and clinical
                    presentation). Continuation of therapy should also be based on BLL. Patients who require
                    >5 days of therapy should have a 2–4 day drug-free period between courses.(8-10)
                    Measure BLL 7–21 days after therapy to determine if retreatment is necessary.(8,9)
                    Combine with dimercaprol (British anti-Lewisite or BAL); second course may be required
                    after 2–4 days; give third course if BLL ≥50 mcg/dL within 48 hours (wait 5–7 days before
                    third course).(8-10)
                    Neonates: Two case reports describe treatment with edetate calcium disodium in new-
                    borns. A term gestation, 3-kg baby received a continuous confusion of 1000 mg/m2/day
                    beginning on 4th day of life (at the time of the second BAL injection) and continued for
                    3 days.(11) A term gestation, 3.7-kg newborn underwent exchange transfusion 12 hours
                    after birth followed by BAL and continuous infusion edetate calcium disodium 50 mg/kg/
                    day initiated at 36 hours of life and continued for 5 days.(12)
                    Acute lead encephalopathy: 1500 mg/m2/day for 5 days.(8,10) IM administration is the
                    preferred route in patients with lead encephalopathy and cerebral edema.(3) (See the
                    Contraindications and Warnings section.)
                    Symptomatic and/or BLL ≥70 mcg/dL (symptomatic or asymptomatic): In con-
                    junction with dimercaprol therapy, 1500 mg/m2/day(8) or 50 mg/kg/day(9) for 5 days via
                    an infusion over 8–24 hours.(3,8-10) One reference recommends 1000 mg/m2/day if BLL
                    ≥70 mcg/dL and patient is asymptomatic.(10)
                    Combination therapy with BAL until BLL is ≤50 mcg/dL.(10) Second and third courses may
                    be given depending on BLL and severity of symptoms.(3,8-10)
                    BLL of 45–69 mcg/dL (asymptomatic): 1000 mg/m2/day(8,10) or 25 mg/kg/day(9) for
                    5 days via an infusion over 8–24 hours(3,8-10) or in divided doses q 8–24 hr (IV/IM)(10); a
                    second course may be required if BLL rebounds.(8)
                    BLL 25–44 mcg/dL: Chelation therapy is not routinely given.(8,9)
                    Provocative chelation test: No longer recommended by the AAP.(9) After patient empties
                    bladder, infuse 500 mg/m2 in D5W over 1 hour(8,10,13) and collect urine for the following
                    8 hours. Dose may also be given IM. Urinary excretion ≥200 mcg of lead or a ratio of uri-
                    nary lead (mcg) to edetate calcium disodium dose (mg) of ≥0.6 (children <36 months) or
                    ≥0.7 (children >36 months) identifies patients who will respond to chelation therapy.(8,10,14)
                    A 5-hour lead mobilization test has also been validated.(15) With IM therapy, infusing IV
                    fluids after administration ensures a greater diuresis.(16)




