Dosage adjustment       Reduce dose with preexisting renal disease.(3) Stop therapy if anuria or severe oliguria
in organ dysfunction    develops.(3) Avoid in patients with inadequate urine flow.(8,10)
                        Dosing suggestions for adults with lead nephropathy(3)
                            SCr 2–3 mg/dL: 500 mg/m2/dose q 24 hr for 5 doses
                            SCr 3–4 mg/dL: 500 mg/m2/dose q 48 hr for 3 doses
                            SCr >4 mg/dL: 500 mg/m2/dose once weekly
                            These regimens may be repeated monthly if needed.


Maximum dosage          75 mg/kg/day (reserved for severely symptomatic patients) not to exceed 1 g/24 hr(17)
                        Five times the recommended dose was infused over 24 hours in a 16-month-old without
                        adverse effects.(3)


Additives               Contains 5.3 mEq sodium/g of edetate calcium disodium.(18)


Suitable diluents       NS, D5W(8,18)


Maximum                 0.5% or 5 mg/mL(8-10)
concentration

Preparation and         Supplied from the manufacturer as a solution of 200 mg/mL; must be diluted prior to
delivery                administration.(3,18)


IV push                 Not recommended(3,9,18)


Intermittent infusion   Dilute to a concentration of <0.5%(8) or 2–4 mg/mL.(18) Although infusions of 15–60 min-
                        utes have been used,(8,10) 4 hours or greater is recommended(9) and usually given over
                        8–12 hours; however, continuous infusion is preferred.(8,9)


Continuous infusion     Preferred method of administration in hospitalized patients.(8,10) Dilute to a concentration
                        of <0.5%(8) or 2–4 mg/mL.(18)


Other routes of         IM administration is extremely painful; should be given with procaine or lidocaine (final
administration          anesthetic concentration = 0.5%) at intervals of 8–12 hours.(3,10,18)


Comments                Significant adverse effects: Renal toxicity is dose-related and reversible, and it rarely
                        occurs at daily doses <1500 mg/m2 if the patient is adequately hydrated.(8,10) Edetate
                        calcium disodium is renally excreted; adequate hydration and urine output must be main-
                        tained to reduce nephrotoxicity.(3,8-10) Beginning 1–2 days after 5 days of edetate calcium
                        disodium and BAL therapy, about 16% of children developed increased SCr lasting for
                        ≤22 days and oliguria lasting for 2–4 days.(19)
                        Hypercalcemia has been reported following treatment with edetate calcium disodium.(3)
                        Laboratory interference: A falsely elevated lead concentration may occur if blood is
                        drawn during a continuous infusion of edetate calcium disodium; therefore, the infusion
                        should be stopped at least 1 hour before a test is performed.(20)
                        Other: Animal studies found a redistribution of lead from bone to target organs (e.g.,
                        brain and kidneys) following edetate calcium disodium administration.(21)
                        Neonatal lead poisoning has been treated with edetate calcium disodium in combination
                        with BAL, with and without exchange transfusion within the first week of life.(11,12)
                        A 5-year-old child died from cardiac arrest due to hypocalcemia during his third chela-
                        tion treatment when edetate disodium was given instead of edetate calcium disodium.(5)


