                    nium is used to determine the adequacy of chronic dosing of another anticholinesterase
                    medication in a patient with myasthenia gravis.
                    1–2 mg given 1 hour after administration of the oral drug being used to chronically treat
                    myasthenia gravis.(2) If the patient’s muscle strength or forced vital capacity improves
                    following edrophonium, the dose of the chronic therapy should be increased.(2,5,6) If cho-
                    linergic symptoms appear, the dose of chronic therapy should be decreased.(2)
                    Because it requires several days for results to occur following changes in chronic oral
                    dosage, the patient should be tested with edrophonium every 1–3 days after dosage
                    adjustment of the chronic medication.(4)
