                 prophylaxis and treatment. Mean dose required in infants <2 months of age for prophylaxis
                 was 1.87 mg/kg q 12 hr and for treatment was 2.37 mg/kg q 12 hr. Mean dose required
                 for patients >2 months was 1.06 mg/kg q 12 hr for prophylaxis and 1.82 mg/kg q 12 hr
                 for treatment.(20) In 192 patients in a pediatric ICU with VTE, younger patients (age 2
                 mo–1 yr) required 1.37 mg/kg q 12 hr to reach target anti-Xa range (0.5–1 units/mL);
                 patients on inotropes also required higher doses. Authors suggested starting with 1.3
                 mg/kg q 12 hr in both of these groups.(35)
                 Once-daily dosing has been studied. For treatment of venous thromboembolism, patients
                 received standard doses for 10–14 days (q 12 hr regimen), followed by either a twice-daily
                 or a once-daily regimen. Patients in the once-daily regimen group received the following:
                 if <1 year old, 1.5 mg/kg q 24 hr; if >1 year old, 1 mg/kg q 24 hr. Goal peak anti-Xa was
                 0.8 unit/mL at 2–4 hours after dose.(21) Another (pilot) study reported a similar regimen
                 in patients >3 months old with deep vein thrombosis (DVT). Patients received twice-daily
                 dosing during the initial treatment period (7–14 days) but then could receive 1.5 mg/kg
                 q 24 hr or 1 mg/kg q 12 hr. No differences in safety or efficacy were found between the
                 two regimens.(22)
                 Anti-Xa concentrations should be drawn 4–6 hours after a sub-Q dose. The following
                 recommendations are made for dosage adjustment(24):

