Dosage adjustment      If CrCl is 10–29 mL/min/1.73 m2, give 70% of usual dose; if CrCl <10 mL/min/1.73 m2,
in organ dysfunction   give 50% of usual dose q 24 hr. For patients receiving intermittent hemodialysis or perito-
                       neal dialysis, give 50% of usual dose. For patients receiving continuous renal replacement
                       therapy, give 70% of usual dose and monitor anti-Xa concentrations.(25) In adults with
                       CrCl <30 mL/min who require a prophylaxis regimen, decrease dose to 30 mg sub-Q once
                       daily. For adults with CrCl <30 mL/min requiring treatment of acute ST-segment elevation
                       myocardial infarction and are <75 years old, in conjunction with aspirin give an IV bolus
                       of 30 mg plus 1 mg/kg sub-Q followed by 1 mg/kg sub-Q once daily. For all other adults
                       with CrCl <30 mL/min requiring a treatment regimen, give 1 mg/kg sub-Q once daily.(4)
                       Use caution in patients with hepatic failure.(4,15,26)
                       Standard doses may produce variable anti-Xa results and require adjustments more fre-
                       quently in patients with cardiac conditions.(26)


Maximum dosage         One group reported that 3.5 mg/kg q 12 hr was required to produce therapeutic anti-
                       Xa concentrations in a preterm neonate.(16) In pediatric patients with varied diagnoses
                       (n=14 patients) undergoing treatment for VTE, doses as high as 2.7 mg/kg q 12 hr were
                       required.(36) In a pediatric neurosurgical population, up to 3.5 mg/kg/day (dose clarification,
                       personal communication with Dr. Jea, March 21, 2016) was utilized for VTE treatment;
                       however, 18% (n = 3 out of 17) of patients in the treatment group had major or minor
                       bleeding (all with bleeding had coagulopathies or concomitant antiplatelet therapies).(37)


