Comments                          Enoxaparin diluted to 20 mg/mL with preservative-free SW in 1-mL tuberculin syringes
                                  did not lose significant anticoagulant activity for 4 weeks.(29)
                                  Pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics/IV administration: Seven infants and
                                  children in a pediatric ICU (age 2 months to 3 years) received enoxaparin IV every
                                  8 or 12 hours. The therapeutic dose for those <1 year was 2.4 ± 0.58 mg/kg/dose and
                                  for those ≥1 year it was 1.11 ± 0.13 mg/kg/dose. Most patients had subtherapeutic
                                  anti-Xa concentrations by 6–8 hours after a dose, suggesting the need for more fre-
                                  quent (i.e., q 8 hr) dosing.(28) A subsequent matched case-control study of 45 patients
                                  in neonatal and pediatric ICUs found that infusing the dose IV over 30 minutes achieved
                                  target anti-Xa concentrations at 4 hours (after the start of the infusion) similarly to sub-Q
                                  administration, suggesting that a 30-minute infusion may obviate the need for more
                                  frequent administration if using the IV route.(38)
                                  One 29-WGA preterm infant (normal cranial ultrasound) with a suspected radial artery
                                  thrombus and discolored fingers was given 1 mg/kg IV q 8 hr for 7 days. Perfusion improved
                                  and only the tip of one finger was ultimately affected. There was no bleeding associated
                                  and no future thromboses were noted.(30)
                                  Adverse effects: Enoxaparin has been associated with the development of heparin-
                                  induced thrombocytopenia in a pediatric patient.(31)
                                  Monitoring: CBC including platelet count during treatment. Anti-Xa concentrations should
                                  be measured 4–6 hours after a dose.(9) One hospital reported that after changing assays,
                                  the mean dose required to achieve anti-Xa concentrations in the treatment range signifi-
                                  cantly decreased in patients <2 years old (from 2.2 to 1.8 mg/kg in patients <3 months
                                  old, and from 2.1 to 1.4 mg/kg in patients 3 months to 2 years old, p <.05).(39)
                                  A retrospective review in patients 0–18 years found that enoxaparin titrated to anti-Xa
                                  concentrations of 0.5–1 unit/mL was not protective against recurrent VTE, especially in
                                  patients with one or two coexisting clinical risk factors (most commonly presence of a
                                  central venous catheter and/or malignancy).(40) In patients who have recurrent VTE on
                                  long-term LMWH (and are believed to be compliant), the CHEST Guideline and Expert
                                  Panel suggest increasing the dose of LMWH by about one quarter to one third (Grade 2C:
                                  weak/conditional recommendation based on low or very low quality evidence).(41)


