                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
                    ISMP and USP report that EPINEPHrine has been confused with ePHEDrine.(2,3) The USP
                    also reports that EPINEPHrine has been confused with Neo-Synephrine, norepinephrine,
                    and phenylephrine. Adrenalin has been confused with ePHEDrine.(3)
                    EPINEPHrine is available in two concentrations for IV use (1:1000 or 1 mg/mL dilu-
                    tion(4,22,35,36,38,39) and 1:10,000 or 0.1 mg/mL dilution(37)). Choose products carefully. (See
                    the Dosage section.) Of note, EPINEPHrine autoinjector concentrations vary (0.5 mg/mL(5)
                    and 1 mg/mL(5,6,34)).


Contraindications   Contraindications: No absolute contraindications exist in a life-threatening situa-
and warnings        tion.(5,6,34,36-38) Otherwise, contraindications include presence of cardiac dilatation or coro-
                    nary insufficiency, organic brain damage,(4) patients receiving halogenated hydrocarbon
                    or cyclopropane anesthetics,(4,37-39) and narrow angle glaucoma.(4,37,38)
                    Warnings: Use with caution, if at all, in patients with cardiovascular disease,(4,5,39) hyper-
                    tension, diabetes, and hyperthyroidism.(4-6,34-39)
                    Overdose may result in cerebrovascular hemorrhage.(4-6,34,36,39)


Infusion-related    Bradycardia, tachycardia, dysrhythmias, myocardial ischemia, syncope, weakness, renal
cautions            failure, and hypertension have occurred.(7-10)
                    IO infusion may be used while obtaining central IV access.(10,13,14)
                    Extravasation may cause local ischemia and tissue necrosis.(11-13,22,35) Therefore, infusion
                    should be via a secure peripheral catheter or, preferably, a central route.(10,14) To prevent
                    necrosis, aliquots of phentolamine mesylate (0.1–0.2 mg/kg(13) up to 10 mg in 10–15 mL
                    NS) should be injected intradermally into the ischemic area(13,22,35) as soon as possible,
                    preferably within the first 12 hours of noting extravasation.(22,35) Terbutaline was an effective
                    alternative to phentolamine in several case reports of accidental epinephrine autoinjector
                    delivery.(15) See Appendix E for additional information regarding extravasation treatment.
                    Gangrene has been reported after infusion via an ankle vein(22,35) and injection into the
                    buttock.(5,6,34,35,38) Do not inject into hands, digits, or feet.(5,6,34-36,38)
                    Per the manufacturer, some injectable dosage forms are not for IV use; IM or sub-Q
                    only.(5,6,34)


