Dosage (cont.)         Shock/postresuscitation stabilization: 0.05–0.1 mcg/kg/min increased by 0.1 mcg/
                       kg/min to desired response. Usual dose range 0.1–1 mcg/kg/min IV or IO,(10,14,43) up to
                       5 mcg/kg/min in children,(13) and 2.6 mcg/kg/min in neonates.(45) In adults, epinephrine
                       is titrated on mean arterial pressure goals q 10–15 min by 0.05–0.2 mcg/kg/min to a
                       usual dose of 0.05–2 mcg/kg/min. Do not abruptly discontinue; wean dose to off (e.g.,
                       every 30 minutes.(22,35)
                       Toxins/overdose (e.g., beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, tricyclic anti-
                       depressants): 0.01 mg/kg (0.1 mL/kg of 1:10,000 dilution) up to 1 mg given IV or IO.
                       If no response, may give higher doses up to 0.1 mg/kg (0.1 mL/kg of 1:1000 dilution) IV
                       or IO.(13,18) Alternately, may infuse 0.1–1 mcg/kg/min; consider higher doses in the case
                       of refractory hypotension.(18)
                       Anaphylaxis
                           Cardiac arrest due to anaphylaxis: Manage with standard arrest treatment (see
                           Asystole/Bradycardia in the Dosage section).(10,13,18,40)
                           For all other anaphylaxis: Give IM epinephrine in the anterolateral aspect of the
                           middle third of the thigh.(40) Sub-Q administration is no longer recommended as higher
                           concentrations are more rapidly achieved with IM administration.(13,19,40) IV dosing
                           may be used.(40)
                               Healthcare setting/individualized dosing: 0.01 mg/kg (0.01 mL/kg of the
                               1:1000 dilution, max of 0.3–0.5 mg/dose) given IM(9,12,35,36,42); may be repeated
                               every 5–20 minutes(13,18,35,41) for up to 3 doses if symptoms continue or recur.(19)
                               If IV access is established, IV epinephrine may be used; in adults, 0.05–0.1 mg (5%
                               to 10% of the cardiac arrest dose.)(40) Caution is advised as reports of accidental
                               overdose (and resultant adverse cardiovascular events) are significantly higher
                               with IV administration compared to IM administration.(44) Continuous infusion may
                               also be given if a patient is not responding to injections or if repeated doses are
                               required(10,13,18,40); for children, 0.1–1.5 mcg/kg/min has been reported,(19) and
                               5–15 mcg/min for adults.(40)
                               Some anaphylactic reactions (e.g., latex allergy) require large doses.(13)
                               If anaphylaxis was caused by an agent that was injected, epinephrine can be
                               injected into the same site to minimize absorption.(19)
                               Community setting (autoinjector availability): Repeat doses every 5–15
                               minutes may be needed if symptoms continue or recur.(41)
