Other routes of         In pediatric emergencies, vascular (e.g., IV or IO) administration is preferred. ET admin-
administration          istration is an alternative if vascular access is not obtained.(10,13,16,18) In anaphylaxis,
                        the IM route is preferred, while sub-Q is an alternative. Sub-Q route has been used in
                        asthma.(10,18) Epinephrine may be given via intracardiac administration.(4) Intraocular indi-
                        cations exist.(35,36,38) Epinephrine may be combined with regional and local anesthesia.(39)


Comments                Rare adverse events: Death due to pulmonary edema has occurred.(4,22,35)
                        Monitoring: Blood pressure, heart rate, central venous pressure, and ECG monitoring
                        should occur continuously throughout therapy; intra-arterial monitoring is preferred.(10,35)
                        Monitor electrolytes.(35)
                        Drug interactions: Effects may be increased with other sympathomimetics (i.e., iso-
                        proterenol),(35-39) tricyclic antidepressants, certain antihistamines (e.g., diphenhydramine,
                        chlorpheniramine), levothyroxine,(4-6,22,34-36) and monoamine oxidase inhibitors.(4-6,22,34-39)
                        Patients previously on beta-adrenergic blocking agents may be less responsive to
                        epinephrine and may require more aggressive therapy.(19) Hypokalemia risk may be
                        increased by other potassium-depleting drugs.(22,35) Monitor for arrhythmias with digoxin
                        or diuretics. Beta-adrenergic and alpha-adrenergic antagonists may diminish the effects
                        of epinephrine.(4-6,22,34-36,39)
                        Other: High-dose epinephrine IV (0.1–0.2 mg/kg) is no longer recommended in resus-
                        citation(13) and may be harmful,(16) particularly in asphyxia.(10,28-32) High-dose epinephrine
                        may be considered in special circumstances such as beta-blocker overdose.(10,13,29)




