Brand names            Nexium, generic


Medication error       Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
potential
                       USP reports that esomeprazole has been confused with lansoprazole; no patient harm
                       resulted. USP also reports that Nexium has been confused with Nexavar (a high-alert
                       chemotherapy agent), Fexium and Norvasc; no patient harm resulted.(1)


Contraindications      Esomeprazole is contraindicated in patients with a hypersensitivity to esomeprazole,
and warnings           substituted benzimidazoles, or any of the constituents in the formulation.(2)
                       Proton-pump inhibitor (PPI) use has been associated with an increased risk for Clostridium
                       difficile-associated diarrhea. Patients receiving PPI therapy who develop diarrhea that
                       does not improve should be evaluated and a diagnosis of C. diff should be considered.(3)
                       Studies suggest that PPI therapy increases the risk of osteoporosis-related fractures of the
                       wrist, hip, or spine, especially in patients receiving high-doses or therapy over a year or
                       longer.(2) It is recommended to use the lowest dose and the shortest duration of therapy
                       appropriate for the patient’s condition.
                       Hypomagnesemia has been reported in patients receiving PPIs for at least 3 months of
                       therapy. Patients who are expected to be on longer-term treatment, or those receiving
                       concomitant medications that may cause hypomagnesemia should be monitored closely.(2)
                       Use of PPIs with high-dose methotrexate may elevate and prolong serum concentrations
                       of methotrexate and/or its metabolite leading to toxicities. Temporary withdrawal of the
                       PPI should be considered.(2,4)


Infusion-related       None
cautions

Dosage                 In adults, oral and IV dosage have been found to be similar in gastric acid suppression.(2)
                       Therapy should be changed to oral route as soon as appropriate.
                       Infants 1 month to <1 year
                           Gastroesophageal reflux: 0.5 mg/kg/day(2)
                       Children 1–17 years: The safety and efficacy of doses >1 mg/kg/day and/or treatment
                       duration >8 weeks have not been determined.(5-8)
                           Gastroesophageal reflux with erosive esophagitis(2)
                           <55 kg: 10 mg every day
                           ≥55 kg: 20 mg every day
                       Adults, gastroesophageal reflux with erosive esophagitis: 20–40 mg every day for
                       4–8 weeks; treatment may be continued for additional 4–8 weeks if incomplete healing.(2)


Dosage adjustment      No dosage adjustment is required in renal dysfunction or in mild-to-moderate hepatic
in organ dysfunction   impairment. Do not exceed 20 mg/day in patients with severe hepatic disease (Child
                       Pugh Class C).(2)


Maximum dosage         The manufacturer states 40 mg/day in adults; single doses of 80 mg have not been
                       associated with adverse effects.(2)


Additives              Edetate disodium(2)


Suitable diluents      D5W, LR, NS(2,9)




