                                                                                                           Ethacrynic Acid
Comments                             Ethacrynic acid may be used to manage edema in patients with impaired renal function.(1)
                                     Alternating doses of ethacrynic acid with furosemide may not overcome diuretic resistance.(5)
                                     Adverse effects: Local pain and irritation at the infusion site have occurred.(1)
                                     The duration of treatment is directly related to the development of sensorineural hearing
                                     loss in neonates with persistent pulmonary hypertension.(10) Investigators point out that
                                     this may be transient; however, they suggest that infusion over at least 5–10 minutes
                                     may be protective.(10) In a retrospective review analyzing the correlation between ototoxic
                                     drugs and the development of hearing loss in children who underwent surgical palliation
                                     for hypoplastic left heart syndrome, two of the four patients who received ethacrynic acid
                                     developed permanent hearing loss. The cumulative dose in these patients was (mean ±
                                     SD) 3.9 ± 3.3 mg/kg.(11)
                                     In adults, irreversible deafness(12) and upper GI bleeding(13) have occurred following infusion.
                                     Electrolyte abnormalities, including hypokalemia, occur.
                                     Monitoring: Serum potassium and magnesium concentrations.(1)


