Contraindications      Contraindications: Should not be used in a person with known hypersensitivity to
and warnings           etomidate.(3)
                       Warnings: Should only be given by persons trained in the administration of general
                       anesthetics and management of their complications.(3)
                       May cause suppression of endogenous cortisol and aldosterone and is not intended for
                       prolonged infusion.(3) (See Significant Adverse Effects in the Comments section.)


Infusion-related       Avoid administration into small vessels.(4) Transient pain at the injection site may be man-
cautions               aged by preadministration with lidocaine (0.5 mg/kg).(5) Involuntary muscle movements
                       have been reported (30% of patients).(4)


Dosage                 Dosing adjustment for obesity: Based on pharmacokinetic parameters, lean body
                       weight (LBW) has been recommended as the best weight on which to calculate doses.(31)
                       Alternatively, an open-label study of adults with BMI >40 kg/m² compared to a control
                       group of nonobese adults, found the total etomidate dose (in mg) required to achieve
                       desired level of anesthesia (defined as bispectral index of <60) to be the same in the two
                       groups (approximately 21 ± 6 mg). The weight-based dosing recommendations were
                       0.3 mg/kg based on total body (TBW) weight in nonobese adults and 0.3 mg/kg based
                       on ideal body weight (IBW) in obese adults.(32)
                       Etomidate is not recommended for children <10 years of age because of lack of sufficient
                       safety and efficacy data.(4) Current evidence suggests etomidate should be avoided in
                       children with known or suspected septic shock.(6,7) (See Significant Adverse Effects in the
                       Comments section.)
                       Sedation: 0.3 mg/kg given over 30–60 seconds(4,8-10); usual dosing range is between
                       0.2–0.6 mg/kg,(4,5,11-15,33) with maximum single doses reported as 10 mg(13) or 20 mg.(16)
                       Hypnosis generally occurs within 60 seconds of administration and typically lasts 3–5
                       minutes (after doses of 0.3 mg/kg).(4)
                       The manufacturer recommends against administering etomidate for prolonged periods as
                       a continuous infusion due to the potential for adrenal suppression(4,17) and propylene glycol
                       toxicity.(18) In severely stressed patients, consider the need for cortisol replacement.(4)
                       Severe hypercortisolemia: 0.03–0.1 mg/kg/hr(19-23)


Dosage adjustment      No dosage adjustment is necessary in renal dysfunction.(24) No dosing adjustment is
in organ dysfunction   recommended for patients with cirrhosis; however, the duration of action may be pro-
                       longed.(4) In adults with obstructive jaundice, a lower dose was required (0.15 mg/kg
                       versus 0.2 mg/kg) to reach an equivalent level of sedation, and the dosing requirement
                       was negatively correlated with total bilirubin values.(25)
                       Neonates and infants with congenital heart disease may experience prolonged duration
                       of action.(34)


Maximum dosage         1.1 mg/kg as a single dose(14)


Additives              Contains propylene glycol 35%.(3,4,26) (See Appendix C for specific information about pro-
