Suitable diluents                  Administered undiluted


Maximum                            2 mg/mL (commercially available)(4)
concentration

Preparation and                    Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                           before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                                   other drugs and solutions, compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                                   and extended stability.(35)
                                   Stability: Do not administer unless solution is clear.(4)


IV push                            Over 30–60 seconds(4)


Intermittent infusion              No information


Continuous infusion                Not recommended for patients with severe stress or septic shock. Prolonged infusion
                                   increases risk of adverse effects.(4,18) (See Significant Adverse Effects in the Comments
                                   section.)


Other routes of                    None
administration

Comments                           Significant adverse effects: Etomidate inhibits 11-B-hydroxylase, an enzyme necessary
                                   for the production of cortisol.(19) Reduced plasma cortisol levels have been reported with
                                   doses of 0.3 mg/kg; the reduction persists for 6–8 hours and appears to be unresponsive
                                   to ACTH administration.(3) This suppression does not appear to be dose related and has
                                   been noted after a single dose.(27) An increase in adrenal insufficiency and mortality was
                                   reported among critically ill and multitrauma patients receiving etomidate infusions.(17,28)
                                   Pediatric Advanced Life Support guidelines recommend against routine use of etomidate
                                   in patients with septic shock.(6) Until the risk for developing adrenal insufficiency has been
                                   assessed in a large, randomized controlled trial, alternative agents (e.g., midazolam,
                                   pentobarbital, or ketamine) should be considered for sedation in this patient population.
                                   Rare adverse effects: Transient skeletal muscle movements occur in 30% to 50% of
                                   patients.(4) Although the movements are usually bilateral and predominately myoclonic,(29,30)
                                   tonic, ocular, and averting movements have been reported. Movements appear to be
                                   more frequent in patients who also manifest venous pain on injection.(4) Administration
                                   of a benzodiazepine or fentanyl may decrease movements.(4) One report noted successful
                                   termination with dantrolene.(29)
                                   Pharmacokinetic considerations: Molecular weight 244.29(3)


