  ($teddy_103_id$etoposide$teddy_103_id$, $teddy_103_name$Etoposide$teddy_103_name$, $teddy_103_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_103_source$, 1422740, $teddy_103$Etoposide
Brand names         Toposar, VePesid, VP-16, generic


Medication error    ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error.(1)
                    Etoposide may be confused with etoposide phosphate. Etoposide phosphate is a prodrug
                    of etoposide. Each 100 mg of etoposide is equivalent to 114 mg of etoposide phosphate.


Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Etoposide should be administered under the supervision of a quali-
and warnings        fied physician. Severe myelosuppression with resulting infection or bleeding may occur.(2)
                    Contraindications: Etoposide is contraindicated in patients who have a hypersensitivity
                    to etoposide or any of the components of the formulation.(2)
                    Other warnings: May cause myelosuppression, hypotension, severe emesis (see Sig-
                    nificant Adverse Effects in the Comments section) and anaphylaxis.(2)


Infusion-related    Hypotension has been reported with rapid administration of etoposide.(2) Stop or slow the
cautions            rate of the infusion, and administer IV fluids if necessary. Infusing etoposide at a rate slower
                    than 100 mg/m2/hr (or 3.3 mg/kg/hr) has been shown to minimize hypotensive effects.
                    Anaphylactic reactions have been seen with etoposide administration, manifesting as
                    bronchospasm, tachycardia, dyspnea, and hypotension. Higher rates of anaphylaxis have
                    been seen in children that receive higher than recommended concentrations of etopo-
                    side.(2) Extravasation has occurred but has rarely been associated with necrosis.(2) (See
                    Appendix E for additional information regarding extravasation treatment.)


Dosage              Consult individual protocols for complete dosing information.
                    Etoposide is used as a component of combination therapy or as single agent therapy in
                    multiple pediatric and hematologic neoplasms.
                    A general dosage regimen has included 60–150 mg/m2/day IV for 2–5 days q 3–6 wk.(4,3)
                    Specific regimens include the following:
                    Acute myeloid leukemia
                        Remission induction: Combination therapy(5)
                            BSA <0.6 m2: 3.3 mg/kg once daily on days 1–5
                            BSA ≥0.6 m2: 100 mg/m2 once daily on days 1–5
                        Intensification(5)
                            BSA <0.6 m2: 3.3 mg/kg once daily on days 1–5
                            BSA ≥0.6 m2: 150 mg/m2 once daily on days 1–5
                        Consolidation: 100 mg/m2 once daily for 4–5 days (combination therapy)(6)
                    CNS tumor: 150 mg/m2 on days 1–3 or days 2–3 of treatment course(7)
                    Neuroblastoma: 100 mg/m2 over 1 hour on days 1–5 of cycle or 75 mg/m2 over 1 hour
                    on days 1–4 of weeks 4–9 in combination with other chemotherapeutic agents(8)
                    HSCT conditioning regimen: 30 or 60 mg/kg as a single dose in combination chemo-
                    therapy 3 or 4 days prior to transplant(9,10)
                    Sarcoma (combination with ifosfamide and carboplatin): 100 mg/m2/dose once
                    daily for 5 doses, days 0–4 q 3 wk(11)
                    Sarcoma (combination with vincristine, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide, ifos-
                    famide): 150 mg/m2/dose once daily for 3 doses, days 1–3(12)
                    Wilms tumor (combination therapy with ifosfamide and carboplatin): 100 mg/
                    m2/dose once daily for 3–5 doses; repeat cycle q 21 days(13)




