                                                                                          Etoposide
Dosage adjustment       GFR 10–50 mL/min/1.73 m2: Administer 75% of normal dose.(14)
in organ dysfunction
                        GFR <10 mL/min/1.73 m2: Administer 50% of normal dose.(14)
                        Hemodialysis: Administer 50% of normal dose after dialysis.(14)
                        Continuous renal replacement therapy (CRRT): Administer 75% of normal dose.(14)
                        Use caution in patients with hepatic dysfunction. One source recommends that if the serum
                        bilirubin is 1.5–3 mg/dL or the aspartate aminotransferase (AST) is >3 times ULN, the
                        dosage should be reduced by 50%.(15)


Maximum dosage          Not established


Additives               Each mL contains 30 mg benzyl alcohol.(2,16) (See Appendix C for more specific information
                        about potential adverse effects and/or benzyl alcohol toxicity in neonates.)
                        Contains polysorbate 80. In premature infants, life-threatening liver and renal failure,
                        pulmonary compromise, thrombocytopenia and ascites have been associated with some
                        products containing polysorbate 80.(2)


Suitable diluents       D5W or NS(2)


Maximum                 0.2–0.4 mg/mL.(2,16) Precipitation has developed with concentrations >0.4 mg/mL.(16)
concentration
                        For high doses, may use 0.6 mg/mL (8-hour stability), but an in-line filter during infusion
                        is recommended.(16)


Preparation and         Must be diluted prior to use with D5W or NS to a final concentration of 0.2–0.4 mg/mL.
delivery                Precipitation may occur at concentrations >0.4 mg/mL. The surfactant component may
                        alter drop size. Administration with infusion devices that do not operate via drop size is
                        recommended.(16)
                        Administration devices made up of acrylic or acrylonitrile, butadiene, and styrene (ABS)
                        should be avoided, as they have cracked and leaked when used with undiluted etoposide.(2)


IV push                 Not recommended.(2,16) Rapid administration has been associated with the development
                        of hypotension. (See the Infusion-Related Cautions section.)


Intermittent infusion   0.2–0.4 mg/mL may be infused over at least 30–60 minutes.(2,16) If volume is a concern,
                        it may be infused over a longer time period, up to 210 minutes.(2) If infusion reaction
                        occurs, may prolong infusion to 2–4 hours, but rate should not exceed 100 mg/m2/hr
                        (3.3 mg/kg/hr).


Continuous infusion     Not recommended but prolonged infusions have been used. To minimize hypotensive
                        reactions, rate should not exceed 100 mg/m2/hr (or 3.3 mg/kg/hr) at final concentration
                        of 0.2–0.4 mg/mL. High-dose etoposide administered as a continuous infusion has been
                        associated with more nonhematological toxicities compared to intermittent infusions.(17)


Other routes of         IT, IP, and intrapleural administration are not recommended.
administration




