Flumazenil
Other routes of                     Undiluted flumazenil has been given rectally to seven children.(24,25) Doses ranged from
administration                      0.02–0.033 mg/kg and time to regain consciousness ranged from 3–15 minutes. No rese-
                                    dation was reported. It has also been given intranasally, in conjunction with naloxone,
                                    and to reverse over-sedation in a child undergoing dental restorations.(26)


Comments                            Rare adverse effects
                                         Seizures: Although rare (1% to 2%), seizure activity may occur in patients (1) depen-
                                         dent on benzodiazepines for seizure control; (2) with severe hepatic impairment, who
                                         are physically dependent on benzodiazepines; and (3) who have ingested large doses
                                         of other drugs (mixed-drug overdose).(2) Activity has ranged from simple twitching and
                                         jerking, to single or multiple seizure events, to status epilepticus.(4) Seizures responded
                                         to reintroduction of a benzodiazepine or use of an anticonvulsant.(2,4) Because of the
                                         presence of flumazenil, larger than normal doses of benzodiazepines may be required.
                                         Resedation: Due to the short duration of flumazenil, compared to some benzodiaz-
                                         epines, resedation may occur. This is especially true in the pediatric population. The
                                         use of flumazenil to reverse the effects of benzodiazepines used for conscious sedation
                                         was evaluated in one uncontrolled trial involving 107 pediatric patients (1–17 years).(8)
                                         Although seven patients (1–5 years) experienced resedation between 19 and 50 ­minutes
                                         (mean 25 minutes) after the start of flumazenil administration, none return to the
                                         baseline level of sedation.(8) The company reported that 7 of 60 patients who were
                                         fully alert 10 minutes after beginning flumazenil experienced resedation.(2) Patients
                                         experiencing resedation may require repeat bolus doses or continuous infusion.
                                         Paradoxical reaction to midazolam: A 4-year-old developed behavioral symptoms
                                         (i.e., frail, kick, scream, writhe) and vital sign changes (i.e., increased heart and
                                         respirator rates) after receiving a normal dose of midazolam for a minimal sedational
                                         procedure.(28) Behaviors and vital signs returned to normal immediately following a
                                         0.005 mg/kg dose of flumazenil.
                                         Elevation in ICP: Has been noted to increase ICP and decrease cerebral perfusion
                                         pressure in those who already have an elevated ICP; hence, should be used cautiously
                                         in those with head trauma.(4)
                                    Monitoring: Monitor patient for return of sedation or respiratory depression for 2 hours
                                    after administration of last dose of flumazenil.(2,5,7) (See Rare Adverse Effects in the Com-
                                    ments section.)
                                    Other: Symptoms of tricyclic antidepressant overdosage include motor abnormalities
                                    (e.g., twitching, rigidity, focal seizures), arrhythmias (e.g., wide QRS complexes, ven-
                                    tricular arrhythmias, heart block), anticholinergic effects (e.g., mydriasis, dry mucosa,
                                    hypoperistalsis), or cardiovascular collapse.(2)
                                    Flumazenil has been reported to reverse zopiclone overdose in 17 of 20 adult patients.(27)



