Brand names         Cerebyx, generic


Medication error    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Cerebyx has been confused with Avelox, Celebrex,
potential           cetirizine, Celexa, and Precedex.(1) Confusion with phenytoin has resulted in patient harm.(1)
                    Confusion between the mg/mL concentration of fosphenytoin (50 mg PE/mL) and total
                    drug content per vial (either 100 mg PE per 2-mL vial or 500 mg PE per 10-mL vial) has
                    resulted in overdose and death.(5) To avoid confusion, the total drug content per container
                    should be used (instead of the concentration in mg per mL) and pediatric hospitals should
                    stock only the 2-mL vial. No order for fosphenytoin should be accepted if “PE” is omitted.(4)
                    (PE = phenytoin sodium equivalent.)


Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: The rate of IV administration should not exceed 3 mg PE/kg/min
and warnings        or 150 mg PE/min due to the risk of severe hypotension and cardiac arrhythmias (see
                    the Comments section). Careful cardiac monitoring is needed during and after adminis-
                    tration. Reduction in rate of administration or discontinuation of dosing may be needed.
                    Although the risk of cardiovascular toxicity increases when infusion rates exceed those
                    recommended, these events have also been reported at or below the recommended
                    ­infusion rate.(5)
                    Contraindications: Patients with a known hypersensitivity to fosphenytoin or its ingre-
                    dients or to other hydantoins (e.g., phenytoin) should not receive fosphenytoin.(5) Par-
                    enteral phenytoin affects ventricular automaticity and is contraindicated in patients with
                    sinus bradycardia, sinoatrial block, second- and third-degree atrioventricular block, and
                    Adams-Stokes syndrome.(5) Coadministration with delavirdine is contraindicated due to
                    potential for loss of virologic response and possible resistance to delavirdine or to the
                    class of non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors.(5)
                    Warnings: Fosphenytoin should always be prescribed and dispensed as PEs. Do
                    not make any adjustment in the recommended doses, concentration in solutions, or rate
                    of administration when substituting fosphenytoin for phenytoin sodium or vice versa.(5)
                    Abrupt discontinuation of fosphenytoin may increase seizure frequency and/or precipi-
                    tate status epilepticus.(5) If an allergic or hypersensitivity reaction or if a life-threatening
                    adverse effect occurs, rapid substitution of alternative anticonvulsant may be necessary.(5)
                    If phenytoin is discontinued due to development of a rash, an anticonvulsant belong-
                    ing to the hydantoin family and one structurally dissimilar should be used.(5-8) (See the
                    Comments section.)
                    The FDA is investigating the possibility of an increased risk of serious skin reactions (e.g.,
                    Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis) in patients given phenytoin
                    who have the human leukocyte antigen allele HLA-B*1502.(5) This allele occurs almost
                    exclusively in individuals with ancestry across broad areas of Asia, including Han Chinese,
                    Filipinos, Malaysians, South Asian Indians, and Thais.(5) Until the FDA evaluation is final-
                    ized, fosphenytoin should be avoided as an alternative for carbamazepine in patients who
                    test positive for HLA-B*1502.(5) (See the Comments section.)


Infusion-related    Administration at rates >3 mg PE/kg/min (150 mg PE/min) or doses >15 mg PE/kg may
cautions            lead to hypotension, bradycardia, and/or arrhythmias.(5) Vomiting, transient pruritus (groin
                    and neck areas), tinnitus, nystagmus, somnolence, and ataxia have also occurred when
                    faster rates or larger doses have been used.(5,9) Paresthesia and pruritus usually occur
                    within several minutes of beginning the infusion and generally resolved within 10 minutes
                    after completion of delivery. “Purple glove syndrome” may occur following peripheral
                    administration. Edema, discoloration, and pain, not associated with extravasation, may
                    occur distal to the injection site and may not develop for several days after injection.




