                                                                                  Fosphenytoin
Dosage                 Each 75 mg of fosphenytoin sodium is metabolically converted to 50 mg of phenytoin
                       after administration. Doses of fosphenytoin are expressed in PEs; therefore, fosphenytoin
                       and phenytoin products can be converted directly (1 mg PE = 1 mg phenytoin).(5) For
                       example, if a patient is receiving 100 mg PE of fosphenytoin, that is equal to 100 mg of
                       phenytoin sodium.
                       Acute seizure or generalized convulsive status epilepticus(31-33)
                           Loading doses (assumes no previous fosphenytoin/phenytoin)
                           Although larger doses may be required in obese adult and adolescent patients,(10) a
                           recent study reported no need to adjust the loading does of fosphenytoin in pediatric
                           patients.(30) (See the Comments section.)
                               Neonates: Few reports have been published in newborns; hence, phenobarbital or a
                               benzodiazepine may be preferred.) 15–24 mg PE/kg at a rate not to exceed 1–3 mg
                               PE/kg/min have resulted in total phenytoin concentrations of 10.5–34.5 mg/L
                               (mean 21.6 mg/L) 8–12 hours after an IV dose.(11,12)
                               Infants and children: 15–20 mg PE/kg.(5,13,14) at a rate not to exceed 3 mg PE/
                               kg/min or 150 mg PE/min(5)
                           Maintenance doses: 4–8 mg PE/kg/day given as 2–3 doses.(5,13,14) Final mainte-
                           nance dose should be individualized using total or free (if indicated) serum phenytoin
                           concentrations. Some reports have noted a failure to obtain a “therapeutic” serum
                           concentration despite adequate doses.(13,15,16)
                       Post-traumatic epilepsy (see status epilepticus for dosing): Although prophylactic
                       phenytoin for the prevention of epilepsy following head trauma is controversial, some
                       have reported that it reduces early post-traumatic epilepsy.(17-19) Recently, several studies
                       have recommended the use of levetiracetam over fosphenytoin/phenytoin.(34-36) (See the
                       Levetiracetam monograph.) Fosphenytoin should not be used for the prevention of late
                       epilepsy.(17) (See the Comments section.)


Dosage adjustment      Dosage adjustment may be required in patients with hepatic or renal dysfunction. Due
in organ dysfunction   to an increased fraction of unbound phenytoin in neonates, patients with renal or hepatic
                       disease, or in those with hypoalbuminemia, the interpretation of total phenytoin serum
                       concentrations should be made with caution. Free phenytoin serum concentrations should
                       be measured in patients with renal dysfunction, or in those who are hypoalbuminemic
                       because of decreases in protein binding, increases in volume of distribution, and altered
                       clearance.(5,21) (See the Comments section.) Formaldehyde is produced on conversion to
                       phenytoin. No accumulation has been noted in adults and children during short-term use;
                       however, caution is warranted in those with renal impairment.(5)


Maximum dosage         Typically patients require <20 mg PE/kg loading dose (up to 1000 mg PE) and <8 mg
                       PE/kg/day from chronic dosing. One study in infants suggests that larger fosphenytoin
                       maintenance doses (10 mg PE/kg/day) and more frequent dosing may be required.(16,21)
                       Patients receiving medications known to increase the clearance of phenytoin may require
                       larger doses.


Additives              There are approximately 0.0037 mmoL of phosphate in 1 mg PE of fosphenytoin.(5)
                       Consider the amount of phosphate delivered in those who require phosphate restriction
                       since hyperphosphatemia has been attributed to fosphenytoin in a 17-year-old patient
                       with renal failure.(22)


