Brand names            Lasix


Medication error       Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
potential
                       ISMP reports that Lasix has been confused with Luvox.(1)
                       USP reports that Lasix has been confused with Lovenox and Lanoxin. Furosemide has been
                       confused with famotidine, finasteride, fluconazole, fluoxetine, fosinopril, loperamide, and
                       torsemide. No patient harm resulted.(2)


Contraindications      Contraindications: Patients with anuria or hypersensitivity to furosemide.(3)
and warnings
                       Warnings: Loop diuretics used in excessive amounts may lead to profound diuresis with
                       fluid and electrolyte losses.(3)
                       Patients with sulfonamide allergy may be hypersensitive to furosemide.(3)


Infusion-related       Transient and permanent ototoxicity has been associated with administration rates >4 mg/
cautions               min or >0.5 mg/kg/min.(3-5) (See the Comments section.)


Dosage                 Diuresis
                           Intermittent dosing
                               Neonates, preterm (<36 weeks gestational age): 1 mg/kg(3,6-8) q 24 hr.(9)
                               Single doses up to 1.5 mg/kg(10) and 2 mg/kg(11) have been studied. 1 mg/kg
                               q 12 hr × 4 doses has also been studied(12,13); in babies with respiratory distress
                               syndrome receiving furosemide prophylactically, the q 12 hr regimen was found to
                               be no better than PRN dosing.(13) After doses of indomethacin for closure of patent
                               ductus arteriosus, 1 mg/kg has shown conflicting results in placebo-controlled
                               trials; a single dose regimen showed decreased renal adverse effects,(8) while a
                               3-dose regimen following each q 12 hr dose of indomethacin produced an increase
                               in acute renal failure.(14)
                               Neonates, term: 0.5–2 mg/kg q 12–24 hr.(9,10,15-17) One study including neonates
                               used 1–2 mg/kg q 4 hr following cardiac surgery.(18)
                               Infants and children: 0.5–2 mg/kg(3,17,19-21) q 4,(18,22) 6,(15,23,24) or 12 hr.(15) Dose
                               may be increased by 0.25–1 mg/kg to achieve desired response.(19,22) In hemody-
                               namically stable patients after cardiac surgery, doses of 0.25–1 mg/kg were given
                               and titrated based on urine output (UOP) and central venous pressure measure-
                               ments.(25) For medical emergencies (e.g., hyperkalemia), the typical maximum
                               pediatric dose for patients not chronically taking loop diuretics is 20 mg,(21) and
                               the recommended adult dose is 40–80 mg.(26)
                           Continuous infusion dosing: 0.1 mg/kg (minimum 1 mg)(22) or 1–2 mg/kg(27) bolus
                           followed by continuous infusion of 0.05–0.4 mg/kg/hr.(18,22-25,27,28) A 24-hour study
                           in infants found that continuous infusion resulted in a similar UOP at a lower dose
                           than intermittent bolus doses (2.5 ± 0.3 mg/kg [n = 11] versus 6.8 ± 1.2 mg/kg
                           [n = 15]).(18)
                           Alternating furosemide with ethacrynic acid may not overcome diuretic resistance.(29)
                       Nephrotic syndrome: 0.5–2 mg/kg with or after albumin 25% infusion.(30-37) A loading
                       dose of 1 mg/kg followed by 0.3 mg/kg/hr for 24 hours has also been used.(30)


Dosage adjustment      Larger doses may be required in patients with renal dysfunction.(38)
in organ dysfunction
                       During extracorporeal membrane oxygenation, 63% to 87% of the dose was adsorbed to
