                                                                                      Furosemide
Maximum dosage          The manufacturer suggests that doses should not exceed 1 mg/kg in premature neonates
                        and 6 mg/kg in infants or children.(3) Maximum IV doses reported in neonates, infants
                        and children have not exceeded 2 mg/kg.(11,18) One study reported continuous infusion
                        dosing up to 0.8 mg/kg/hr, but only three patients in that study received doses >0.4 mg/
                        kg/hr.(57) In adults with acute renal failure, initial dose regimens up to 3 g/day have been
                        used(38); however, these doses have been associated with ototoxicity.(40)


Additives               Contains 0.162 mEq sodium/10 mg furosemide(41)


Suitable diluents       D5W, NS, LR(41)


Maximum                 10 mg/mL (undiluted)(3,41)
concentration

Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions, compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(41)
                        Stability: The final pH of diluted furosemide must be slightly alkaline or in the neutral
                        pH range or precipitation of furosemide may occur.(41)
                        Photosensitivity: Light exposure may cause discoloration. Discolored products should
                        not be used.(41)


IV push                 Over 1–2 minutes(6,16,18,19,22); 20–40 mg over 1–2 minutes may be given in adults.(3,41)
                        Infusion at a rate not to exceed 0.5 mg/kg/min may provide protection against ototoxicity
                        in infants and children.(42) (See the Comments section.)


Intermittent infusion   The ISMP and Vermont Oxford Network recommend standard concentrations of 2 or 10
                        mg/mL for neonates.(43) Infusion at a rate not exceeding 0.5 mg/kg/min may provide
                        protection against ototoxicity in infants.(42) Infusion rates not exceeding 4 mg/min are
                        recommended in adults.(3)


Continuous infusion     ≤10 mg/mL.(3) The ISMP and Vermont Oxford Network recommend standard concentra-
                        tions of 2 and 10 mg/mL for neonates.(43) May be diluted in D5W, LR, or NS for continuous
                        infusion.(41)


Other routes of         IM(3)
administration

Comments                Adverse events: Electrolyte abnormalities including hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia
                        can occur.(3)
                        The duration of treatment is directly related to the development of sensorineural hearing
                        loss in neonates with persistent pulmonary hypertension. Investigators point out that
                        this may be transient; however, they suggest that infusion at a rate not exceeding 0.5
                        mg/kg/min may be protective.(42) Bolus administration as opposed to continuous infusion
                        was associated with a higher incidence of permanent hearing loss in children following
                        congenital heart surgery.(44)
                        Premature neonates with respiratory distress syndrome may have an increased risk of
                        persistent patent ductus arteriosus secondary to furosemide stimulation of renal produc-
                        tion of prostaglandin E2.(45)
                        Up to 65% of premature infants develop nephrocalcinosis following prolonged use.(5,46)
                        About 50% of these resolve within 5–6 months of drug discontinuation(47); however,
                        obstructive nephrolithiasis can occur.(48) In patients requiring long-term furosemide, renal
                        ultrasonography is warranted a few months after initiation of therapy.(49)



                                                                                                               431
Furosemide
Comments (cont.)                     In a retrospective review, furosemide was associated with an increased incidence of
                                     nephrotoxicity in patients receiving vancomycin.(50)
                                     A case report described hyperthermia and dermal blisters in an 18-year-old female. These
                                     recurred upon rechallenge with furosemide.(51)
                                     Monitoring: Serum potassium, magnesium, sodium, chloride and calcium concentrations(3)
                                     Drug interactions: Aminoglycosides potentiate the ototoxicity of loop diuretics.(3,52-55)
                                     Pharmacokinetic considerations: Furosemide is 91% to 99% protein bound. Use cau-
                                     tiously in jaundiced neonates at risk for kernicterus. In vitro studies have shown bilirubin
                                     displacement in pooled cord blood samples from critically ill neonates receiving furose-
                                     mide.(56) Molecular weight 330.75.(3)


