potential

Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Granulocytopenia, anemia, and thrombocytopenia are possible
and warnings        with ganciclovir therapy.(2)
                    Contraindications: Patients with hypersensitivity to ganciclovir or valganciclovir(44)
                    Other warnings: Do not administer if absolute neutrophil count <500 cells/μL or platelet
                    count is <25,000 cells/μL. Hemoglobin is <8 g/dL.(44) Ganciclovir has been shown to impair
                    fertility in animal studies. Because of the possible teratogenic potential, contraception
                    should be used for an appropriate time frame during and after therapy.(2)


Infusion-related    After initial reconstitution, the solution has a pH of 11. Despite further dilution, phlebitis
cautions            and/or pain may occur at infusion site, and it should be administered only into veins with
                    adequate blood flow to permit rapid dilution and distribution.(2)


Dosage              Adequate hydration is recommended during therapy.(2)
                    Cytomegalovirus (CMV) prophylaxis in high-risk hosts: Product information and
                    authoritative sources recommend 10 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr for 1–2 weeks, then 5
                    mg/kg/day given once daily or 6 mg/kg/day given 5 days/week for 100–120 days.(2,5)
                    Variations in regimens have been reported including starting prophylaxis 1 week prior to
                    transplantation,(3) pretransplant dosing, then delaying post-transplant prophylaxis until
                    day 21 or until neutrophil count recovery,(3) only administering prophylaxis if CMV positive
                    at day 35(4) short-term prophylaxis of only 2 weeks,(6) 2 weeks of prophylaxis with pre-
                    emptive monitoring for CMV thereafter,(8) long-term prophylaxis up to 200 days(41) and
                    prophylaxis for only 4 weeks combined with CMV immunoglobulin therapy.(9)
                    CMV retinitis: 10 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr for 14–21 days, then 5 mg/kg/day given
                    7 days/week or 6 mg/kg/day given 5 days/week(2,5)
                    CMV infection in allograft recipients: 7.5–10 mg/kg/day divided q 8–12 hr for 5–47
                    days(12-14) Some have based continuation of therapy past 2 weeks on continued CMV
                    positivity.(15,42,43)
                    Congenital (symptomatic) CMV infections: 5–15 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr in neo-
                    nates and infants. The higher doses have been used in CNS infections.(5,16,18-22,25)
                    CMV infection in immunocompetent host: One child (30 months old) received 10
                    mg/kg/day in 2 divided doses for 21 days due to a deteriorating case of primary CMV.(27)
                    Human herpes virus-6 in bone marrow transplant (BMT) patients (use not
                    established)
                        Prophylaxis: 10 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr for 1 week prior to transplantation fol-
                        lowed by 5 mg/kg/day for 120 days after transplant(28)
                        Treatment: 10 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr for 2 weeks followed by 5 mg/kg given
                        daily or 3 times/week for 4 weeks.(28) One 7-year-old child received ganciclovir up to
                        24 mg/kg/day divided into 3 doses for treatment of HHV-6 encephalitis.(29) (See the
                        Maximum Dosage section.)




