Gentamicin Sulfate
Comments                             Rare adverse effects: Aminoglycosides accumulate in renal cortical tissue and may
                                     damage proximal tubule cells leading to oliguric renal failure. Risk of nephrotoxicity may
                                     be influenced by type of aminoglycoside, dose, duration (cumulative dose), frequency of
                                     therapy and elevated serum concentration.(3,4) It is also increased by advancing age, pre-
                                     existing renal or hepatic dysfunction, decrease renal perfusion, hypoalbuminemia, and
                                     dehydration.(3,4) Concurrent administration of nephrotoxic drugs may increase the risk of
                                     toxicity and should be avoided.(3)
                                     Cochlear and/or vestibular ototoxicity has been associated with aminoglycoside antibi-
                                     otics.(5) Total AUC may be a better indicator of ototoxic risk than either peak or trough
                                     serum concentration.(74,75) Use cautiously with other drugs known to cause ototoxicity.
                                     Aminoglycosides may potentiate the curare-like effects on the neuromuscular junction and
                                     should be used with caution in patients with neuromuscular disorders because they may
                                     aggravate muscle weakness.(3,6) Aminoglycosides may enhance the respiratory depressant
                                     effect of neuromuscular-blocking agents and may prolong blockade.(3,7) Neuromuscular
                                     blockade may be reversed by administration of calcium salts.(3)
                                     Monitoring: Because large variability exists in patient response to therapy, individualize
                                     dosage based on serum concentrations, clinical response, and renal function. Recom-
                                     mended peak and trough serum gentamicin concentrations are 4–12 mg/L and <2 mg/L,
                                     respectively.(3)
                                     Although serum concentration monitoring has become routine practice in many institu-
                                     tions, not all patients may require monitoring.(78,79)
                                     Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources before combining any drug with
                                     gentamicin.
                                     Laboratory interference: Serum concentrations may be falsely elevated when blood
                                     samples are collected through central venous Silastic catheters.(80)


