Brand names            Voraxaze


Medication error       None reported
potential

Contraindications      Hypersensitivity to glucarpidase or any component of the formulation.(1) Anaphylaxis has
and warnings           occurred in <1% of patients.(1)
                       Methotrexate (MTX) concentrations may be unreliable when samples are collected within
                       48 hours of glucarpidase administration and measured using an immunoassay.(1) (See
                       the Comments section.)
                       Continue glucarpidase with leucovorin until the MTX concentration has been maintained
                       below the leucovorin treatment threshold for a minimum of 3 days.(1)


Infusion-related       Flush line before and after administration of a dose. Do not administer leucovorin 2 hours
cautions               before or after glucarpidase.(1) (See the Dosage section.)


Dosage                 Glucarpidase is not indicated for patients with “normal” clearance of MTX (plasma MTX
                       concentrations within 2 standard deviations of the mean MTX excretion curve specific for
                       the dose administered) or in those with normal or mildly impaired renal function due to
                       potential risk of subtherapeutic exposure to MTX.(1)
                       Continue hydration and urinary alkalinization as indicated.(1)
                       MTX toxicity (serum concentration >1 μmol/L) due to renal impairment (FDA-
                       approved in ages ≥1 month): A single dose of 50 units/kg IV given over 5 minutes.(1,3-12)
                       A retrospective review of 20 pediatric patients used mean dose of 51.6 units/kg (range of
                       13–65.6 units/kg).(4) Others have used 2 doses of 50 units/kg given 24 hours apart, or 50
                       units/kg q 4 hr for 3 doses in patients with a persistent MTX concentration >1 μmol/L.(13,14)
                       The manufacturer notes that multiple doses did not achieve a rapid and sustained clini-
                       cally important reduction in MTX concentration.(1) A recent study found no significant
                       relationship between glucarpidase dosage (units/kg) and percent decrease in MTX serum
                       concentrations measured by immuno- or chromatographic assays. When measured by
                       chromatographic assay, patients receiving <50 units/kg had a median percent reduction in
                       MTX concentration of 99.4% (range, 98–100) compared to a median percent reduction of
                       99.4% (range, 77.2–100) in those given ≥50 units/kg. There was also no difference in
                       time to recovery of SCr based on dose glucarpide.(16)
                       Accidental intrathecal (IT) MTX overdose: IT glucarpidase should be used in con-
                       junction with immediate lumbar drainage.(14) 1000(15) or 2000(14) units, diluted in 12 mL of
                       preservative-free NS and immediately given over 5 minutes, rapidly decreased cerebral
                       spinal fluid MTX concentrations by >98%.(14,15)
                       Concurrent leucovorin therapy (see the Leucovorin Calcium monograph): Leucovo-
                       rin dosing should be given in tandem with hydration and urinary alkalization. Leucovorin
                       is a substrate for glucarpidase and may compete with MTX for binding sites.(1) It should
                       not be given 2 hours before or after a glucarpidase dose. Once 2 hours has passed,
                       administer the same leucovorin dose as was given prior to glucarpidase administration
                       for 48 hours. (See the Leucovorin Calcium monograph). After 48 hours of leucovorin,
                       adjust dosing based on the measured MTX concentration. Continue to adjust leucovorin
                       dose based on preglucarpidase MTX concentrations and continue to administer for the
                       first 48 hours. After that time dosing should be based on MTX concentration. (See the
                       Leucovorin Calcium monograph). Continue for at least 3 days after the MTX concentration
                       is below the leucovorin treatment threshold.(1,2)

Dosage adjustment      No adjustment needed in renal failure.(1) No data are available regarding dosage adjust-
in organ dysfunction   ment in those with hepatic impairment.(1)

Maximum dosage         Not reported




