Additives               Each vial contains 1000 units of glucarpidase, lactose monohydrate (10 mg), Tris-HCl
                        (0.6 mg), and zinc acetate dihydrate (0.002 mg).(1) The product does not contain preser-
                        vatives and should be administered immediately after reconstitution.(1)

Suitable diluents       None reported

Maximum                 1000 unit/mL(1)
concentration

Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(17)
                        Preparation: For IV administration reconstitute 1000-unit vial with 1 mL of NS for injec-
                        tion. Roll and tilt to mix vial,(1) do not shake. Inspect vial and discard if the solution is not
                        clear, colorless, and free of particulate matter.(1) For IT administration (i.e., via lumbar
                        route, ventriculostomy, Ommaya reservoir, or lumbar and ventriculostomy) reconstitute
                        2000 units with 12 mL preservative-free NS.(14)
                        Stability: Do not freeze, but store intact vials at 2°C to 8°C (36°F to 46°F). Stable for
                        4 hours if refrigerated but should be used immediately as the product does not contain
                        a preservative.(1)
                        Access: Distributed though a speciality pharmacy (ASD Healthcare) and is available
                        24 hours a day, 365 days out of the year by calling 1-855-786-7292 or via https://www.
                        btgplc.com/products/specialty-pharmaceuticals/voraxaze/ (accessed June 5, 2016).

IV push                 Not recommended(1)

Intermittent infusion   Administer as a 5-minute bolus. Flush IV line before and after bolus.(1)

Continuous infusion     Not recommended

Other routes of         Following accidental administration of intrathecal MTX, glucarpidase has been given by the
administration          lumbar route, ventriculostomy, Ommaya reservoir, or lumbar and ventriculostomy.(14,15)
                        Doses (1000 units) have been administered through ventricular or lumbar catheter over
                        5 minutes.(15)

Comments                After glucarpidase therapy and upon resolution of renal dysfunction, high-dose MTX therapy
                        has been successfully used provided renal function and serum MTX concentrations are
                        monitored closely.(4)
                        Monitoring: The patient should be monitored for signs or symptoms of MTX toxicity. Serum
                        MTX concentration (see the Laboratory Test section below) should be assessed; however,
                        discontinuation of leucovorin should not be determined based on a single concentration.
                        SCr, liver function test including bilirubin, and CBC with differential should be assessed.
                        Laboratory test: An overestimation of MTX concentration is due to an inactive metabolite
                        (DAMPA, or 4-deoxy-4-amino-N10-methylpteroic acid), which interferes with the immu-
                        noassays assay; hence, a chromatographic assay must be used. MTX concentrations may
                        be unreliable when samples are collected with 48 hours of glucarpidase administration.(1)
                        Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                        combining any drug with glucarpidase.




