Dosage (cont.)   Neonates: Loading dose of 50 units/kg of preservative-free heparin followed by 20–35
                 units/kg/hr, titrated to an initial activated clotting time (ACT) of 180–250 seconds (1.5–2.5
                 × baseline) and to maintain plasma heparin concentrations of 0.3–0.5 unit/mL.(14) (Note
                 that anti-Xa levels have replaced plasma heparin concentration monitoring; the therapeutic
                 range for neonates and children has not been established by outcomes-based studies; and
                 variability exists among different kits that measure anti-Xa levels.)(8)
                 Arterial thrombosis resulting in limb ischemia: In infants <2 years, a loading dose of
                 75 units/kg followed by 25 units/kg/hr titrated to aPTT 80–100 seconds has been recom-
                 mended.(15) The loading dose may be withheld in patients with increased risk of bleeding.
                 Cardiac catheterization: Current evidence-based guidelines recommend 100 units/kg
                 initial bolus; for prolonged cases, additional boluses are suggested.(8,16,17) One study found
                 50 unit/kg bolus to be equally as effective as 100 units/kg followed by 20 units/kg/hr.(16)
                 Catheter patency
                     Arterial line: 0.5–5 units/mL at rates of 0.5–2 mL/hr.(18,19,52) Current evidence-based
                     guidelines recommend 0.5 unit/mL at 1 mL/hr.(8)
                     Peripheral IV catheter: (All data from neonatal studies.) 0.5–1 unit/mL (final con-
                     centration) added to infusate(20,21) or PN.(22,23) Intermittent flushes of 1 mL heparin 10
                     unit/mL before and after IV antibiotic doses prolonged catheter patency compared to
                     saline placebo flushes (72 versus 58 hours, p <.001).(53)
                     Peripheral IV lock irrigation: Usually 1 mL of 10 units/mL instilled q 8 hr.(24-26)
                     However, concentrations (10–100 units/mL), volumes (2–5 mL), and dosing intervals
                     vary widely depending on institutional protocols.(24) Some studies suggest there is no
                     clinical difference between 2 units/mL, 10 units/mL, and saline.(27,28)
                     Peripherally inserted central (venous) catheter: 0.5 unit/kg/hr continuously.(8) In
                     preterm neonates, heparin at 0.5 units/kg/hr (in D5W or D10W, n = 100) compared
                     to placebo (D5W or D10W, n = 101) resulted in fewer occlusions (6% versus 31%,
                     respectively) and more completed therapies (63% versus 42%, respectively). No dif-
                     ference in thrombosis (20% versus 21%) or infection (10% versus 6%) was noted.(29)
                     In another study in preterm neonates, heparin 0.5 unit/kg/hr as part of PN solution
                     resulted in a longer catheter survival and fewer occlusions without an increase in
                     adverse events compared to babies receiving PN without heparin.(30) A randomized,
                     controlled trial found that the addition of heparin 0.5 unit/mL to PN solution reduced
                     the incidence of culture-positive, catheter-related sepsis in neonates.(31)
                     Nontunneled central venous catheter: In infants after cardiac surgery, prophy-
                     lactic heparin 10 units/kg/hr was no more effective than placebo (D5W) in reducing
                     catheter-related thrombi.(32)
                     Tunneled central venous catheter (flush): Current evidence-based guidelines
                     recommend saline or heparin flushes (no specific concentration is recommended).(8) In
                     children with hematologic or oncologic disease who were up to 17 years of age, a 3 mL,
                     200 unit/mL flush twice a week resulted in fewer complications than an NS flush once a
                     week in those with a positive-pressure-valve cap device.(33) Investigators could not rule
                     out the possibility that more frequent flushing in the heparin group was also important.
                     Umbilical artery catheter: Current evidence-based guidelines recommend 0.25–1
                     unit/mL infused continuously.(8,34-37) (See the Comments section.)
                 Continuous arteriovenous hemofiltration: Loading dose of 100 units/kg followed by
                 5–7 units/kg/hr was successfully used in 4 neonates.(38)
                 Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO): Bolus 50–100 unit/kg at time of can-
                 nulation. When activated clotting time (ACT) is ≤300 seconds, typically begin infusion at
                 7.5–20 units/kg/hr (lower range in adults, higher for pediatric and neonatal patients, unless
                 excessive bleeding). Therapeutic anticoagulation (ACT 180–220) typically achieved with infu-
                 sion rates 20–50 units/kg/hr. Some centers have adopted a minimum dose of 10–20 units/
                 kg/hr and maximum 40–50 units/kg/hr regardless of ACT result due to possibility that ACT
                 may under- or overestimate heparin effect in children.(54) Dosages higher than normal may
                 be required due to the extracorporeal circuit or due to patient-specific factors.(39) If blood
                 is used to prime the circuit, 1 unit heparin per mL of blood may be added to the prime.(39)
                 Up to one half of the heparin administered may be adsorbed or inactivated by the circuit.(40)



