Heparin Sodium
Dosage (cont.)          Promotion of fat emulsion clearance: 1 unit/mL of infusate as continuous infusion.(41,42)
                        Thrombosis prevention in kidney transplantation: 50 units/kg q 8 hr (route not speci-
                        fied) for 7 days postoperatively (with aspirin 5 mg/kg 3 times a week starting postoperative
                        day 3 for 3 months) was studied for prevention of kidney allograft thrombosis compared
                        to matched controls (thrombosis rate: 0% versus 7.9%, p = 0.19).(55)

Dosage adjustment       Dosage should be titrated to the appropriate aPTT or ACT.(4,5,43) Patients with renal dysfunction
in organ dysfunction    may be at greater risk for hemorrhage. If CrCl <10 mL/min/1.73 m2, give 50% of usual dose.(43)


Maximum dosage          Individualize dosage is based on aPTT,(4,8) or in some neonatal cases, ACT.(14)
                        Loading dose not to exceed 100 units/kg.(8) ≤50 units/kg/hr have been used in neonates
                        with major vessel thrombosis.(44)

Additives               Although some products are preservative- and antioxidant-free, many heparin products
                        contain benzyl alcohol or parabens.(45) (See Appendix C for specific information about
                        adverse effects and potential toxicity from benzyl alcohol in neonates.)

Suitable diluents       D5LR, D5R, D5NS, D5½NS, D5W, NS, ½NS, PN, R (partial list only). Consult detailed refer-
                        ence as some variability exists with different heparin concentrations.(45)

Maximum                 Undiluted solutions may be used.(5) In order to infuse the correct dose safely, the product
concentration           concentration must be considered; using a less concentrated product for lower doses results
                        in a dosage that is more accurate.

Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions, compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(45)
                        Preparation: The addition of heparin to infusates requires adequate mixing to ensure that pooling
                        of heparin does not occur.(5,45) One manufacturer recommends inverting the container ≥6 times.(5)

IV push                 Not indicated(5)


Intermittent infusion   Over 10 minutes(5)


Continuous infusion     The ISMP and Vermont Oxford Network recommend a standard concentration of 0.5 unit/
                        mL (in 0.45% NaCl) for maintaining line patency in neonates.(46)

Other routes of         Deep sub-Q. Not for IM use.(5)
administration

Comments                Pharmacokinetic considerations: Protamine sulfate reverses the effects of heparin. (See
                        the Protamine Sulfate monograph.)
                        Rare adverse effects: Heparin induced thrombocytopenia is reported to occur in 0% to 2.3%
                        of pediatric patients. In these patients, heparin exposure ranged from low doses (line flushes)
                        up to supratherapeutic doses used during cardiopulmonary bypass and hemodialysis.(8)
                        Other: One group of investigators concluded that the addition of heparin to fluids infused to
                        neonates peripherally did not affect catheter life significantly.(21) A prospective, randomized
                        controlled trial of heparin 0.7 mL of 10 unit/mL solution versus saline flush for peripheral
                        IV patency in neonates (administered a minimum of every 8 hours) found no difference
                        between lock solutions.(47) Similarly, a systematic review of 10 studies that met review
                        criteria could not support the use of heparin in neonates receiving peripheral infusions.(48)
                        Frequent IV line flushes may result in pharmacologic doses being delivered to very low
                        birth weight infants.
                        FDA alert: On October 1, 2009, the FDA alerted healthcare professionals that the USP
                        adopted new manufacturing controls for heparin. On October 8, 2009, the reference standard
                        for heparin products changed to the World Health Organization’s International Standards
                        (IS) unit dose definition. This is about 10% less potent than the previous USP unit. Thus,




458
                        the potency of heparin products has decreased.(49)

