Brand names            Generics


Medication error       None reported
potential

Contraindications      Warnings: HCl has sclerosing potential if used incorrectly.
and warnings

Infusion-related       Infusion of 0.1 N HCl through a peripheral line results in pain at the injection site and may
cautions               cause thrombophlebitis.(1) Infusion via a large vein or central venous line is preferred.(2,3)
                       Because of the sclerosing potential, the rate of infusion should not exceed 0.2 mmol/kg/hr.(2)


Dosage                 Central venous catheter occlusion: Using a 3-mL or larger syringe (see the Comments
                       section), instill a volume of 0.1 N HCl equal to the catheter volume (0.2–2 mL) and allow it
                       to dwell for at least 20 minutes.(4-8) Withdraw and discard HCl, and then flush the catheter
                       with an appropriate solution. If unsuccessful, the procedure can be repeated.(4-6)
                       Severe metabolic alkalosis (not first-line treatment): HCl is indicated for hypochlore-
                       mic metabolic alkalosis if NaCl or KCl are contraindicated or if correction is required quickly,
                       such as with an arterial pH >7.55, during hepatic encephalopathy, cardiac arrhythmia,
                       or digitalis cardiotoxicity.(2)
                       0.1–0.2 mmol/kg/hr until alkalosis is corrected.(9) A 16-day-old infant with refractory
                       metabolic alkalosis received 0.5 mmol/kg/hr for 5.5 hours, then 0.75 mmol/kg/hr for 4.5
                       hours, and 1 mmol/kg/hr for 4 hours.(10) 0.1 N HCl has been added to an amino acid and
                       lipid solution and infused via peripheral vein in infants with alkalosis.(1)
                       The amount of HCl to correct the alkalosis can be calculated using the following(11):
                       Chloride deficit
                           mEq HCl = 0.2 L/kg × weight (kg) × [103 – observed serum Cl (mEq/L)]
                       Bicarbonate excess(2,3)
                           mEq (mmol) HCl = 0.5 L/kg × weight (kg) × [observed serum HCO3 (mEq/L) – 24]
                       Base-excess
                           mEq HCl = 0.3 L/kg × weight (kg) × measured base excess (mEq/L)
                       The chloride-deficit method usually results in a lower dose than the other two. The dose
                       calculated using the chloride deficit should be infused over ≥12 hours, and the dose cal-
                       culated using the bicarbonate excess or base-excess method over ≥24 hours.(11) Arterial
                       blood gas and electrolytes should be measured every 4 hours during the infusion and
                       therapy adjusted PRN.(3)

Dosage adjustment      No information available
in organ dysfunction

Maximum dosage         For treatment of life-threatening alkalosis, 0.2 mmol/kg/hr has been recommended.(2)
                       Rates up to 25 mEq/hr in adults have been reported.(3) Monitor arterial pH closely during
                       therapy and discontinue HCl when alkalosis has been corrected.

Additives              None reported. 0.1 N HCl provides 0.1 mEq/mL of hydrogen and chloride ion and has a
                       pH of 1–1.5.

Suitable diluents      0.1 N HCl is ready for catheter instillation.




