                    Neonatal uses (preservative-free)
                        Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD)/chronic lung disease (CLD) preven-
                        tion: Although controversial and associated with little or no benefit (in systematic
                        reviews) on mortality or chronic lung disease,(32) the AAP states that low dose
                        hydrocortisone (1 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr × 9 days followed by 0.5 mg/kg/
                        day divided q 12 hr × 3 days) may increase rates of survival without BPD/CLD,
                        especially for infants born in a setting of prenatal inflammation.(7-10)
                        Hypoglycemia: 5 mg/kg/day divided q 8–12 hr or 1–2 mg/kg/dose q 6 hr(6,11)
                        Hypotension: Many regimens have been studied with varying results. One study
                        in <32 WGA babies with birth weight <1500 g and age <7 days with hypotension
                        refractory to fluids and dopamine reported statistically significant reduction in the
                        need for vasopressors when hydrocortisone was added at 1 mg/kg/dose q 8 hr × 5
                        days.(12) The range of dosing reported in other studies is generally 2–6 mg/kg/day
                        divided q 6, 8, 12, or 24 hr depending on response, or as tapering regimens.(12-15,35)
