Contraindications      Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to ibandronate or to any of its excipients.(1)
and warnings           Uncorrected hypocalcemia.(1)
                       Warnings: May cause a transient decrease in serum calcium values(1)
                       Do not administer to patients with severe renal impairment.(1)
                       Atypical bone fractures have been reported in patients receiving bisphosphonates, espe-
                       cially in those receiving long-term (3–5 years) therapy.(1)
                       Osteonecrosis of the jaw has been reported in adult patients receiving bisphosphonates.(1)
                       Risk factors include diagnosis of cancer, concomitant therapies (e.g., chemotherapy, radio-
                       therapy, corticosteroids) and comorbid disorders such as anemia, coagulopathy, infection,
                       and preexisting dental disease.(1) Invasive dental procedures should be avoided in patients
                       with additional risk factors while receiving ibandronate.(1)


Infusion-related       Local reactions at the injection site, such as redness or swelling, were observed infre-
cautions               quently. In most cases, the reaction was mild to moderate in severity.(1)


Dosage                 Osteogenesis imperfecta (OI): Ibandronate 2 mg IV q 3 mo was compared to calcitriol
                       PO 0.25 mcg daily for 2 years in 30 children with OI. All children also received calcium
                       500 mg and vitamin D 200 units daily. Children receiving ibandronate had significantly
                       fewer new fractures and demonstrated significant increases in bone mineral density at
                       the lumbar spine, femoral neck, trochanter, and total hip compared to calcitriol alone.(2)
                       Idiopathic hyperphosphatasia (IH): 5 mg ibandronate IV was administered monthly
                       over 2 hours to an 11-year-old female with IH until alkaline phosphatase was <300 units/L.
                       Thereafter, infusions were administered to keep alkaline phosphatase <300 units/L and
                       were continued until skeletal maturity was reached. Deformity was prevented and hearing
                       improved. Bone mineral density improved significantly.(3)

Dosage adjustment      Do not administer in severe renal impairment (SCr >2.3 mg/dL or CrCl <30 mL/min).(1)
