  ($teddy_129_id$ibuprofen-lysine$teddy_129_id$, $teddy_129_name$Ibuprofen Lysine$teddy_129_name$, $teddy_129_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_129_source$, 1782149, $teddy_129$Ibuprofen Lysine
Brand names            NeoProfen, Caldolor


Medication error       None reported
potential

Contraindications      Contraindications: Hypersensitivity to ibuprofen or any component of the formulation.
and warnings           Also contraindicated in those with a history of asthma, or urticaria/allergic reaction to
                       aspirin, or other nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs.)(1,39) Neonates with (1)
                       active bleeds (especially intracranial or GI), thrombocytopenia, or coagulation defects;
                       (2) proven or suspected infections that are untreated; (3) significantly impaired renal
                       function; (4) existing or suspected necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC); and (5) congenital
                       heart disease in whom patency of the ductus arteriosus is necessary for satisfactory pul-
                       monary or systemic blood flow (e.g., pulmonary atresia, severe tetralogy of Fallot, severe
                       coarctation of the aorta).(1,39)
                       Warnings: Increasing BUN, creatinine, decreased glomerular filtration rate, and a small
                       reduction in urine output (UOP) may be seen in neonates.(1,39) These effects are generally
                       transient and resolve upon discontinuation of ibuprofen.(1) Ibuprofen may cause acute
                       kidney injury especially in neonates.(1) It should be withheld in any patient with significant
                       decreases in UOP until output returns to normal. It has been known to displace bilirubin
                       and should be used cautiously in patients with hyperbilirubinemia.(1) May cause exacerba-
                       tion of asthma of aspirin-sensitive asthma.(1,39)


Infusion-related       Avoid extravasation.(1) Administration via umbilical arterial line has not be evaluated, but
cautions               caution should be used when given via an umbilical catheter into a vein near the supe-
                       rior mesenteric artery as NSAIDs will reduce blood flow to the intestines and may cause
                       bowel ischemia.


Dosage                 Analgesia and fever: Caldolor is approved for use in closure of a patent ductus arteriosus
                       (PDA), but Neoprofen is not.(1,39) If 6 months to 12 years of age, infuse 10 mg/kg (up to
                       400 mg/dose) over 10 minutes q 4–6 hr as needed. Maximum of 40 mg/kg or 2400 mg/
                       day.(39) If 12–17 years of age, give 400 mg q 4–6 hr as needed up to 2400 mg/day.(39) IV
                       ibuprofen has been evaluated in adults for management of postoperative pain and was
                       found to be effective and safe at a dose of 800 mg IV q 6 hr.(10)
                       PDA (closure of the ductus arteriosus): Neoprofen is approved for use in closure of
                       a PDA, but Caldolor is not.(1,39) The prophylactic use of ibuprofen is not currently indi-
                       cated nor recommended.(2,3) IV ibuprofen is indicated in premature infants (500–1500 g)
                       ≤32 weeks gestational age.(1) Dose should be based on birth weight and the patient
                       should be well hydrated prior to ibuprofen use. Although limited data are available, orally
                       administered ibuprofen has been reported to be as effective as IV ibuprofen in closure
                       of a PDA.(34,36,37,43,44) Neurological, neurosensory, and cognitive outcomes were similar.(44)
                       The initial dose of 10 mg/kg should be infused over 15 minutes. The first dose should
                       be followed by 2 doses of 5 mg/kg at 24 and 48 hours.(1,4-9) Alternatively, a large dose of
                       ibuprofen (20 mg/kg followed by 2 doses of 10 mg/kg/dose administered at 24-hour inter-
                       vals) has been associated with a higher rate of PDA closure without an increase in adverse
                       effects.(40,41) Sustained pharmacological closure of the PDA was significantly better in very
                       low birth neonates given continuous infusion compared to standard intermittent dosing
                       (10/5/5 mg/kg). Continuous infusion doses were 0.416 mg/kg/hr over 24 hours, followed
                       by 0.208 mg/kg/hr over the next 48 hours. The continuous infusion group had fewer symp-
                       toms of NEC and other neonatal morbidity and mortality rates were similar.(42) Regardless
                       of which regimen is used, UOP should be assessed prior to each dose. If <0.6 mL/kg/hr or
                       anuria occurs, no dose should be given until renal function normalizes.(1) A second course
                       of treatment, alternative pharmacologic therapy, or surgery may be needed if the ductus
                       arteriosus fails to close or reopens following the initial course of therapy.


Dosage adjustment      Should be withheld if oliguria (<0.6 mL/kg/hr) or anuria occur.(1) Avoid use in severe
in organ dysfunction   hepatic impairment.




