Other routes of         None reported(1)
administration

Comments                Anaphylaxis: Ibuprofen has been associated with anaphylactic reactions in patients with
                        and without known hypersensitivity to ibuprofen.(39) Because cross-reactivity between
                        aspirin and other NSAIDs has been reported, patients with aspirin-sensitive asthma should
                        not receive ibuprofen.(39)
                        Bleeding: Ibuprofen can inhibit platelet aggregation and adhesion, which may lead to
                        prolonged bleeding time or bleeding. It also appears to have little effect on platelet plug
                        formation compared to indomethacin.(29) A 1350-g, 31-week gestational age newborn
                        developed GI bleeding after standard dose therapy for closure of a PDA.(28)
                        Hepatic dysfunction: In adults, NSAID therapy has led to liver disease that may be
                        associated with an increase in ALT and AST that may be 3 times above the upper limits
                        of normal.(13,14) If signs or symptoms develop, ibuprofen should be discontinued.
                        Intraventricular hemorrhage (IVH): Is a known complication of prematurity. Because
                        ibuprofen may cause a transient reduction in cerebral blood flow(15,16) and is neuroprotec-
                        tive in animals,(9) some hypothesized that it might reduce the incidence or severity of
                        IVH. In a large prospective study of 415 infants, 8% given ibuprofen developed severe
                        IVH compared to 9% receiving placebo.(3) In a second study of 155 infants, grade 2 to
                        4 IVH developed in 16% of the ibuprofen-treated infants and 13% of the infants in the
                        placebo group.(17)




                                                                                                               479
Ibuprofen Lysine
Comments (cont.)                    Kernicterus: Ibuprofen lysine is about 99% bound to albumin(1) and was found to be
                                    94% to 95% protein bound in preterm newborns.(18) Ibuprofen should be used cautiously
                                    in infants with elevated total bilirubin concentrations since it may displace bilirubin from
                                    high-affinity albumin-binding sites.(1,19) One report noted possible ibuprofen-induced ker-
                                    nicterus in a preterm infant with hyperbilirubinemia.(20)
                                    NEC: Although NSAIDs have the ability to reduce mesenteric blood flow,(21-24) one study
                                    reported that ibuprofen did not significantly reduce mesenteric blood flow velocity in
                                    infants.(25) The ability of NSAIDs to affect mesenteric blood flow raised concerns about
                                    its association with GI bleeding, isolated bowel perforation, and NEC in infants. Although
                                    an isolated case has been reported,(26) the incidence of NEC is similar between those
                                    given ibuprofen and indomethacin.(4,7,9,27) The incidence of NEC was significantly greater
                                    in those with oliguria (21%) compared to those without (5%) oliguria.(9) A recent case
                                    report identified ibuprofen as the causative agent in a premature newborn developing GI
                                    bleeding as a complication.(28)
                                    Pulmonary dysfunction: Three cases of pulmonary hypertension have been reported.(30,31)
                                    A meta-analysis identified an association with ibuprofen and about a 30% higher risk for
                                    chronic lung disease compared to indomethacin.(32) Another study reported no difference
                                    between ibuprofen and placebo in the development of bronchopulmonary dysplasia.(33)
                                    The need for corticosteroids for chronic lung disease may be less with oral ibuprofen.(34)
                                    Renal dysfunction: A dose dependent reduction in urinary PGE2 formation is markedly
                                    reduced in patients given NSAID,(38) which decreases renal blood flow. Although it appears
                                    that oral ibuprofen may have more of an effect on renal function compared to IV ibupro-
                                    fen,(36,37) preterm infants treated with IV ibuprofen experience lower SCr values, higher
                                    UOP, and less undesirable decreased organ blood flow and vasoconstrictive adverse effects
                                    than those given indomethacin.(4,7,9,27,35) In a separate study, oliguria was commonly seen
                                    with both agents within the first 3 days of treatment but resolved sooner in the ibuprofen
                                    group first.(9) Patients at greatest risk of this reaction are those with impaired renal func-
                                    tion, dehydration, hypovolemia, heart failure, liver dysfunction, those taking diuretics and
                                    ACE inhibitors or ARBs. Discontinuation of NSAID therapy is usually followed by recovery
                                    to the pretreatment state.(39)
                                    Skin reactions: NSAIDs can cause exfoliative dermatitis, Stevens-Johnson Syndrome,
                                    and toxic epidermal necrolysis, which can be fatal. These serious events may occur without
                                    warning. Discontinue at first sign of rash.(39)
                                    Monitoring: When used in patients with preexisting asthma (without known aspirin
                                    sensitivity) monitor patients for changes in the signs and symptoms of asthma. Renal
                                    function (SCr and BUN, UOP), CBC and platelets, and liver function. Patients receiving
                                    NSAID may develop hyperkalemia; hence, serum potassium should be monitored closely.
                                    Patient should also be monitored for hypertension.(39)
                                    Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                                    combining any drug with ibuprofen. Ibuprofen is a minor substrate of CYP2C9 and 2C19,
                                    and it is a major inhibitor of CYP2C9.(1) It is also highly protein bound to albumin.


