Brand names         Carimune NF, Flebogamma, GAMMAGARD LIQUID, GAMMAGARD S/D, Gamunex, Octagam,
                    Privigen, Gammaked


Medication error    None reported
potential

Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Acute renal dysfunction, acute renal failure, and death in predis-
and warnings        posed patients have been associated with IGIV administration.(2-8,48) Thrombosis can occur
                    in patients receiving immune globulin. Patients at risk for thrombosis should receive the
                    minimum dose and rate practical.(2-8,48) (See the Comments section.)
                    Contraindications: Previous severe hypersensitivity reaction to immune globulin.(2-8,48)
                    Those with selective immunoglobulin A (IgA) deficiency and antibodies to IgA are at risk
                    for developing severe hypersensitivity and anaphylactic reactions.(2-8,48) Octagam is con-
                    traindicated in patients with a history of acute hypersensitive reaction to corn.(7)
                    Other warnings: Aseptic meningitis, hemolysis, transfusion-related acute lung injury,
                    osmotic nephrosis, and thrombotic events have been reported.(2-8,48)


Infusion-related    Patients should not be volume depleted prior to infusion.(2-8,48)
cautions
                    Inflammation reactions including facial flushing, headache, chest tightness, chills, fever,
                    dizziness, nausea, vomiting, diaphoresis, and hypotension may occur.(2-5) Infusion reac-
                    tions may be attenuated by decreasing the infusion rate or stopping the infusion until the
                    reaction subsides; then restarting at a slower rate usually decreases the severity.(2-5,8)
                    One large study found that infusion reactions occurred at a rate of 0.7% in children <10
                    years, which is not significantly different from the adult rate of 0.8%.(10)
                    Infusion reactions are more common when administered for the first time, when chang-
                    ing brands of IVIG, and when it has been a long interval since the last infusion. Use a
                    slower rate of infusion in these patients. The infusion rate may be increased as the patient
                    tolerates the change.(4,8)


Dosage              It has been suggested that change in IgG level best correlates with doses calculated
                    using IBW.(49)
                    Primary immunodeficiency: 200–800 mg/kg/day q 3–4 wk; adjust dose based on
                    patient response.(2-5,7,8,11,12,48)
                    Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP): 0.4–2 g/kg/day for 1–5 days(2,5,6,13-16,48)
                    Kawasaki disease: 2 g/kg in a single dose.(16-23) Patients who have persistent fever or
                    who have recrudescent fever after treatment may be retreated.(19,23,24)
                    Chronic lymphocytic leukemia: 400 mg/kg q 3–4 wk(5)
                    Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy: Loading dose of 2 g/kg in
                    divided doses given over 2–4 days with maintenance doses of 1 g/kg q 3 wk(6)
                    Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation with severe hypogammaglobulinemia
                    (IgG <400 mg/dL): 400 mg/kg monthly in pediatric patients and 500 mg/kg weekly
                    in adolescents to maintain IgG ≥400 mg/dL.(25) Because the half-life is shorter in severe
                    hypogammaglobulinemia, patients may need larger than usual doses to maintain the
                    desired IgG concentrations.(25)
                    Guillain-Barré syndrome: 1 g/kg/day for 2 days or 400 mg/kg/day for 3–6 days(16,26,27)




