                       Assess UOP after first and second dose to determine dosing interval. If UOP is >1 mL/kg/hr,
                       dose q 12 hr; if 0.6–1 mL/kg/hr, dose q 24 hr; if <0.6 mL/kg/hr, no dose should be given
                       until renal function normalizes. In a study of ventilated neonates with respiratory distress
                       syndrome, conventional doses were given every 12–18 hours.(12) Reopening of the ductus
                       arteriosus, after a successful course of indomethacin, may require a second course of
                       1–3 doses using the above recommendations. If unresponsive, surgical correction may
                       be needed.(1) Administration via continuous infusion at doses of 0.004–0.01 mg/kg/hr
                       over 36 hours has been reported to reduce alterations in renal, cerebral, and mesenteric
                       blood flow compared to bolus administration.(8,13,14)
                       Prevention of IVH: 0.1 mg/kg q 24 hr for 3 doses beginning at 6–12 hr of age.(15-17) Lim-
                       ited information is available. Nineteen trials involving 2872 very low birth weight (1202 of
                       which extremely low birth weight infants) infants were evaluated. The incidence of severe
                       IVH was reduced by prophylactic indomethacin; however, meta-analyses did not reveal
                       any evidence that prophylactic indomethacin reduced mortality or severe neurodevelop-
                       mental disability at 18–36 months old.(4) A multivariate analysis compared prophylactic
                       indomethacin given at <6 hours of life (n = 730) to administration after >6 hours of life
                       (n = 168). There were no differences in all-grade or severe IVH between groups.(45) In a
                       follow-up study, the authors evaluated the influence of time of administration of prophy-
                       lactic indomethacin (<6 hours of age) and found that time was not associated with less
                       IVH or death.(46)

Dosage adjustment      Should be withheld if oliguria (<0.6 mL/kg/hr) or anuria is present.(1)
in organ dysfunction

Maximum dosage         0.25–0.4 mg/kg/dose(9,19) or cumulative 0.9–1 mg/kg in 3 days.(20,21)

Additives              None reported

Suitable diluents      Use preservative-free SW or NS for reconstitution. Dilution following reconstitution is not
                       recommended.(22)

Maximum                1 mg/mL(1,22)
concentration

Preparation and        Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery               before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                       other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                       and extended stability.(22)
                       Stability: Reconstitute with 1 or 2 mL of preservative-free diluent (SW or NS) just prior to
                       administration. Unused portions should be immediately discarded.(1,22) However, 1 mg/mL
                       in NS is chemically stable for 16 days at room temperature.(22) Indomethacin 0.5 mg/mL
                       prepared with SW in the original vial had little to no drug loss with refrigeration after
                       14 days and only 9% loss at room temperature in 10 days; when prepared under the
                       same manner and repackaged into 1-mL polypropylene syringes, there was little to no
                       loss reported at room temperature or refrigeration at 14 days.(22)




