Preparation and                    Compatibility: See Appendix D or appropriate resources(22) for PN compatibility information.
delivery (cont.)                   Photosensitivity: Protect from light.(22)

IV push                            Although indomethacin has been given over 5–10 seconds,(23,24) rapid administration causes
                                   a significant decrease in mesenteric artery and cerebral blood flow that may contribute
                                   to the development of NEC or cerebral ischemia.(25-27)

Intermittent infusion              Over 20–30 minutes.(1,22) (See the Infusion Related Cautions section)

Continuous infusion                Although continuous infusion is not routinely used,(1) it has been used (0.004 mg/kg/hr)
                                   for ductal closure.(8)

Other routes of                    IM administration not recommended(1)
administration

Comments                           Cardiovascular: A study reported a decline in coronary perfusion without major cardiac
                                   output changes following indomethacin administration in patients with hemodynamically
                                   significant PDAs. The significance of this is still unknown, but authors suggest cardiac
                                   hemodynamics be carefully monitored.(28)
                                   IVH: Indomethacin may cause a transient reduction in cerebral blood flow.(1) Two small
                                   studies demonstrated that slower administration appears to be safer with diminished effect
                                   on cerebral blood flow; a 30-minute infusion time(29) and a 120-minute infusion time were
                                   both found to be protective.(30) In a large multicenter study, the incidence of IVH in neo-
                                   nates was not significantly different in neonates given indomethacin and placebo.(17) Others
                                   have reported that indomethacin may decrease the incidence and severity of IVH.(16,31,45,46)
                                   Necrotizing enterocolitis (NEC): Bleeding, with a focus on GI or NEC, has been reported
                                   with IV indomethacin regimens that consisted of 6 doses.(20,32-34) Although major GI bleeding
                                   was no more common in neonates receiving indomethacin than in those given placebo,
                                   minor GI bleeding (i.e., chemical detection of blood in the stool) was more commonly
                                   noted in those receiving indomethacin.(1)
                                   Pulmonary dysfunction: Indomethacin appears to have less risk of chronic lung disease
                                   as compared to ibuprofen.(35)
                                   Hepatic dysfunction: Nonsteroidal therapy in adults has caused liver disease.(1,36,37) If
                                   signs or symptoms develop, therapy should be discontinued.
                                   Other effects: Hypoglycemia that developed during indomethacin therapy persisted for
                                   up to 72 hours.(38-40) Neurological effects (dizziness, headache, and somnolence) may be
                                   attributed to the variable absorption into the CNS and rapid concentrations found in the
                                   cerebral spinal fluid.(41)
                                   Monitoring: Renal function (SCr and BUN), UOP, CBC and platelets, serum transaminases,
                                   electrolytes (especially K and Na), and glucose.(1)
                                   Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                                   combining any drug with indomethacin. Digoxin is dependent on renal function for elimina-
                                   tion; hence, the dose may need to be reduced when used with indomethacin. Additional
                                   monitoring with ECGs or serum digoxin concentrations may be needed to detect digoxin
                                   toxicity.(1,34,42) Concurrent aminoglycoside and indomethacin (standard course was given
                                   every 8 hours) produced elevation in peak and trough aminoglycoside serum concentra-
                                   tions.(1,43) Concomitant therapy with indomethacin and furosemide may dampen natriuresis
                                   resulting in greater urinary output, greater sodium and chloride excretion, and higher
                                   GFR compared to indomethacin alone; this suggests that furosemide may offer some
                                   renal protection.(1) Indomethacin is a minor substrate of CYP2C9 and 2C19, and it is a
                                   strong inhibitor of CYP 2C9. It is also bound to albumin and could displace bilirubin from
                                   albumin-binding sites.(44)


