Contraindications   U.S. boxed warnings
and warnings
                        Risk of serious infections: Patients receiving infliximab are at risk for developing
                        serious infections. Those with active infections should not begin treatment. Those who
                        develop infection while on treatment should be monitored closely.(3)
                        Patients should be evaluated for latent tuberculosis infection with a tuberculin skin
                        test. If the skin test is positive, treatment of tuberculosis should be started prior to
                        beginning treatment with infliximab.(3) (See the Comments section.)
                        Hepatosplenic T-cell lymphomas: These have been reported in adolescents and
                        young adults receiving infliximab. These cases occurred in patients with Crohn disease
                        or ulcerative colitis who were receiving concomitant azathioprine or 6-mercaptopurine.(3)
                        Malignancies: Lymphomas and other cancers in children and adolescents treated
                        with TNF blockers have been reported.(4)
                    Contraindications: Patients who have had a previous severe hypersensitivity reaction
                    to infliximab should not receive any additional doses.(3)
                    Patients with severe heart failure should not receive infliximab doses >5 mg/kg.(3)
                    Other warnings: Anaphylactic type reactions have been reported.(3) Appropriate medical
                    care should be available in the event this occurs.
                    Hepatotoxicity has been reported, sometimes requiring transplantation. Infliximab should
                    be discontinued in patients with jaundice and/or marked liver enzyme elevations.(3)


Infusion-related    Flushing, chest pain, and shortness of breath have occurred during infusion in up to
cautions            38.6% of pediatric patients.(5-8) Premedication with acetaminophen, antihistamines, and/
                    or corticosteroids may be required. Infusion-related reactions should be managed by
                    administration of acetaminophen, antihistamines, corticosteroids, or epinephrine, or by
                    slowing the rate of infusion.(3,7) Stop infusion if symptoms do not improve or if epinephrine
                    is required. Infusion reactions can occur with the first or any subsequent dose(5) but are
                    most likely to occur after the second or third infusion.(5) A history of an infusion reaction
                    is not predictive of future infusion-related reactions.
                    A delayed infusion reaction may be experienced 3–5 days after treatment. The physician
                    should be notified if this occurs. Longer intervals between treatments (more than a year)
                    increase the risk of delayed infusion reactions.(3)


Dosage              Premedication with antihistamines, acetaminophen and/or corticosteroids may be used
                    to prevent or manage infusion-related reactions.
                    Crohn disease: 5 mg/kg IV initially at 0, 2 and 6 weeks, followed by maintenance treat-
                    ment of 5 mg/kg IV q 8 wk.(9-12) Dose may be increased up to 10 mg/kg if response is
                    incomplete.(13) Patients who do not respond to treatment by week 14 are not likely to
                    respond with further treatment. Dosing should be discontinued.
                    Ulcerative colitis: 5 mg/kg IV as induction therapy at 0, 2, and 6 weeks with maintenance
                    treatment of 5 mg/kg IV q 8 wk.(14-16) Doses may be increased up to 10 mg/kg if response
                    is incomplete.(13)




496
                                                                                                              
                                                                                        InFLIXimab
Dosage (cont.)          Refractory juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA): 3–4 mg/kg initially or at weeks 0, 2,
                        and 6 weeks followed by up to 10 mg/kg q 4–8 wk.(17,18) One study in 122 children who
                        were receiving methotrexate found that infusion reactions were greater in the group
                        receiving 3 mg/kg than those receiving 6 mg/kg.(19)
                        JIA uveitis: 5–10 mg/kg at weeks 0, 2, and 4 followed by infusions q 6–8 wk with a more
                        rapid infusion rate based on tolerance (stable vital signs).(20) In a case series of pediatric
                        uveitis patients with or without associated JIA, all patients responded to infliximab at
                        5–10 mg/kg at 2–8 week intervals.(21) (See the Comments section.)
                        Refractory Kawasaki syndrome: Limited data suggest that a single infusion of 5–10
                        mg/kg may be beneficial in patients who have not responded to 2 doses of IV immuno-
                        globulin and high-dose daily aspirin therapy.(22)


Dosage adjustment       Information not available
in organ dysfunction

Maximum dosage          Doses up to 20 mg/kg have been used in adults,(3) and one case series reported successful
                        treatment of a 15-year-old patient receiving a dose of 18 mg/kg.(21)


Additives               Sucrose, polysorbate 80(3)


Suitable diluents       SW to reconstitute, NS to dilute(3)


Maximum                 4 mg/mL(3)
concentration

Preparation and         Preparation: Reconstitute a 100-mg vial by adding 10 mL SW via a 21-gauge or small
delivery                needle with the stream directed to the glass wall of the vial. Gently rotate the vial to dis-
                        solve. Do not shake. The reconstituted vial should be allowed to sit for 5 minutes prior
                        to dilution.(3)
                        After the total dose (which may include multiple vials) has been reconstituted, withdraw an
                        equivalent dose volume of NS from a 250-mL bag or bottle. Slowly add the total volume
                        of reconstituted infliximab to the 250-mL bag or bottle. Gently mix.(3)
                        Stability: The vial contains no preservatives and once reconstituted the infusion must
                        begin within 3 hours.(3)
                        Delivery: Administer the infusion using an in-line, low-protein-binding filter with a pore
                        diameter ≤1.2 microns.(3) The infliximab infusion should be administered over a period
                        not less than 2 hours.


IV push                 Not indicated(3)


Intermittent infusion   The infusion rate was increased after 15 minutes from 15 mL/hr to 30 mL/hr for 15 minutes
                        to 60 mL/hr for 30 minutes to 90 mL/hr to complete the infusion(9) or was increased q 15
                        min from 10 mL/hr to 20 mL/hr to 40 mL/hr to 80 mL/hr to a maximum of 125 mL/hr
                        based on the stability of vital signs.(5) The infusion should be administered over ≥2 hours.(3)


Continuous infusion     Not indicated


Other routes of         Not indicated
administration




                                                                                                                 497
InFLIXimab
Comments                            JIA, juvenile rheumatoid arthritis, and juvenile chronic arthritis may be used interchangeably.
                                    A negative skin test for tuberculosis does not rule out the possibility of disease since
                                    patients with immune diseases may exhibit cutaneous anergy.(23)
                                    Infusion-related reactions are believed to be caused by the production of antibodies to
                                    infliximab. The concomitant administration of immunomodulating medications (or use
                                    within the preceding 4 months) is believed to be protective.(3)
                                    Infliximab was a more effective therapy for JIA uveitis than etanercept.(24,25)
                                    Drug interactions: Patients receiving anakinra and abatacept should not receive inflix-
                                    imab due to a possible increased risk for infection.(3)
                                    Live-virus vaccines (measles, mumps, rubella; varicella; rotavirus) should be avoided
                                    during treatment.(3) It is recommended that all pediatric patients should be brought up
                                    to date on vaccinations prior to beginning therapy with infliximab.
                                    Infliximab can cause reactivation of hepatitis B virus (HBV). Patients should be tested
                                    for HBV prior to therapy, and if reactivation occurs, infliximab should be discontinued.(3)


