  ($teddy_134_id$insulin-regular$teddy_134_id$, $teddy_134_name$Insulin (Regular)$teddy_134_name$, $teddy_134_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_134_source$, 1882440, $teddy_134$Insulin (Regular)
Brand names         HumuLIN R, NovoLIN R, HumuLIN R (concentrated U-500)


Medication error    ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error.(1)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
                    ISMP reports that HumuLIN R has been confused with HumaLOG and NovoLIN and that
                    HumuLIN R U-100 has been confused with HumuLIN R U-500.(2)
                    USP reports that HumuLIN R has been confused with HumuLIN 70/30, NovoLIN 70/30,
                    NovoLIN R, and NovoLOG. Patient harm resulted when HumuLIN R was confused with
                    HumaLOG.(3)
                    USP reports that NovoLIN R has been confused with HumuLIN R. Patient harm resulted
                    when NovoLIN R was confused with NovoLIN 70/30, NovoLIN GE NPH, and NovoLOG.(3)


Contraindications   FDA alert: On March 19, 2009, the FDA reminded healthcare providers and patients that
and warnings        insulin pens must not be shared due to the potential of transmission of viruses (including
                    HIV and hepatitis C) and other blood borne pathogens.
                    Contraindications: Hypersensitivity to regular insulin or any of its components;
                    hypoglycemia.(4,5)
                    Warnings: Hypoglycemia is the most common adverse effect.(4,5) Serum glucose concen-
                    trations must be monitored closely to detect hypoglycemia early.
                    Accurate choice of product is critical since many different types of insulin preparations are
                    available. Availability and use of concentrated insulin should be restricted.


Infusion-related    Regular insulin products can be infused intravenously.(4-6)
cautions

Dosage              Hyperglycemic crises in diabetes (diabetic ketoacidosis [DKA] and hyperosmolar
                    hyperglycemic state [HHS]): Biochemical evidence of DKA includes HCO3 <15 mmol/L,
                    pH <7.25 and an increased blood glucose (BG). Baseline electrolytes must be obtained
                    and rehydration with NS should be done prior to beginning insulin as BG concentrations
                    will begin to decrease with rehydration. Institutional protocols for fluid and electrolyte
                    replacement, monitoring and use of insulin should be followed.(7)
                    Initially, 0.05–0.1 unit/kg/hr.(7-13) A loading dose is no longer recommended.(7) BG should
                    be measured hourly to ensure that the decrease in BG is 50–90 mg/dL/hr.(7) Too rapid
                    a decrease in BG may cause cerebral edema and may require the addition of a glucose
                    infusion even before glucose is <300 mg/dL. If the biochemical parameters of DKA do
                    not improve, the insulin infusion may be increased. Sub-Q dosing can be started when
                    the HCO3 ≥18 mmol/L, pH >7.3 and BG <200 mg/dL.(7)
                    Hyperkalemia (after or with NaHCO3 and calcium gluconate): 0.05–0.1 unit/kg/hr
                    infused with 400 mg dextrose/kg or 1 unit insulin/4 g dextrose.(12,14-16) A systematic review
                    included two unblinded studies in preterm neonates.(17) One of these reported that 20
                    infants who received 1 unit insulin/10–15 g dextrose had shorter duration of hyperkalemia
                    and fewer episodes of intraventricular hemorrhage than those (n = 20) receiving sodium
                    polystyrene sulfonate. However, authors of the review concluded that no firm practice
                    recommendations could be made for preterm neonates.
                    Hyperglycemia: The consensus target BG range for most critically ill patients is 140–180
                    mg/dL.(42) The best method to attain this BG and avoid hypoglycemia is unknown; insti-
                    tutional protocols including frequent monitoring and safeguards against hypoglycemia
                    are encouraged.(18)




500
                                                                                                              
                                                                           Insulin (Regular)
Dosage (cont.)             Critically ill children: Initial infusion rates of 0.01–0.2 unit/kg/hr(19-26) titrated to
                           achieve euglycemia have demonstrated (in retrospective and randomized controlled
                           trials) reductions in morbidity (reduced infections and inflammatory markers in critically
                           ill patients, and enhanced insulin sensitivity with reduced resting energy expenditure
                           in severely burned children).(20,21,26) However, the largest trial to date (randomized,
                           prospective, multicenter trial in 1369 patients), showed that tight glycemic control
                           (target BG 72–126 mg/dL; initial insulin dose 0.05–0.1 unit/kg/hr) had no significant
                           effect on clinical outcomes.(43) Rates of hypoglycemia were as high as 30% in trials
                           aiming for tight glycemic control.(21,25,26) Some protocols have successfully reduced
                           hypoglycemia rates.(19)
                           Glucose intolerance in premature low birth weight (LBW) infants: A 2008
                           multicenter trial compared early insulin treatment in 195 LBW infants (0.05 unit/kg/
                           hr with D20W to maintain euglycemia) within 24 hours of age to conventional therapy
                           (sliding scale and infusion starting at 0.05 unit/kg/hr) in 194 infants (with dextrose
                           titrated to avoid hyperglycemia [BG >180 mg/dL] and hypoglycemia [BG <47 mg/
                           dL]). The trial was ended early because of increased mortality at 28 days of life in the
                           early group (11.9% versus 5.7%; odds ratio 0.45, confidence interval 0.21–0.96).(27)
                           A 2012 randomized controlled trial of 88 premature infants who received insulin to
                           achieve tight glycemic control (0.05 unit/kg/hr titrated to achieve BG 70–108 mg/
                           dL compared to standard glycemic control) found no improvements in mortality
                           or increased hypoglycemia (<47 mg/dL; 58% in tight control group), but found a
                           decrease in linear growth. Authors concluded that insulin may not be safe or effective
                           for neonatal glucose intolerance.(28) Earlier studies evaluated 0.03–0.08 unit/kg/hr
                           with the dose titrated to desired BG concentration.(29-34) (See the following sections:
                           Maximum Dosage, Preparation and Delivery, and Comments.)

Dosage adjustment      If CrCl 10–50 mL/min (adult recommendation), give 75% of the normal dose.(35)
in organ dysfunction
                       If CrCl <10 mL/min (adult recommendation), give 50% of normal dose.(35)
                       Monitor frequently in patients with renal or hepatic impairment.(4,5)

Maximum dosage         Up to 4.2 units/kg/hr were required occasionally in hyperglycemic very low birth weight
                       neonates (<1000 g) during first 2 weeks of life.(33,34) It has been speculated that the
                       increased requirement in these infants was due to insulin resistance, inappropriate insulin
                       secretion, or decreased sensitivity of the liver to insulin effects.(32,37)
                       In DKA, some endocrinologists do not use more than 3 units/hr in children.(8)

Additives              100 units contain 7–15 mcg Zinc.(4,5) Novolin R contains glycerol and metacresol.(4) Humulin
                       R contains glycerin and metacresol.(5)

Suitable diluents      Humulin R: NS(5,6)
                       Novolin R: NS, D5W, or D10W with 40 mmol/L potassium chloride(4)

Maximum                1 unit/mL(4,5)
concentration
                       100 units/mL (commercially available) for sub-Q dosing(4,5)

Preparation and        Insulin binds to glass bottles, plastic IV bags, syringes, and tubing and results in decreased
delivery               delivery of insulin. IV tubing may be primed with an insulin solution to saturate insulin-
                       binding sites and prevent further absorption.(6,31,38,39) Alternatively, human serum albumin
                       occupies binding sites on glass(40) and plastic and has been added to insulin-containing
                       solutions(7,29,32) to increase insulin delivery.



IV push                A loading dose has been used in DKA and HHS(8) but is no longer recommended.(7)




                                                                                                               501
Insulin (Regular)
Intermittent                        Not indicated
infusion

Continuous infusion                 Novolin R: 0.05–0.1 unit/mL in polypropylene infusion bags(4)
                                    Humulin R: 0.1–1 unit/mL in polyvinyl chloride bags(5)

Other routes of                     Sub-Q; IM administration has been reported(8) but is not listed as a recommended route
administration                      by manufacturers.(4,5) In DKA and HHS, these routes of administration may require hourly
                                    dosing of insulin. IM and sub-Q dosing should not be used in patients with hypovolemic
                                    shock, hyperosmolar hyperglycemia, or hyperkalemia.
                                    A 5-year-old with DKA and dehydration received insulin via IO infusion.(41)

Comments                            Blood glucose measurements: 120 mg/dL = 6.7 mmol/L
                                    In LBW infants, a 12- to 14-hour delay in response was noted when IV tubing was not
                                    primed with insulin.(31) Whether this was due to lack of priming or insulin resistance was
                                    not evaluated.
                                    Monitoring: It is essential that BG concentrations be measured regularly during insulin
                                    therapy to avoid hypoglycemia as well as large shifts in BG. Bedside measurements should
                                    be periodically compared to plasma samples performed in the laboratory to ensure their
                                    accuracy.
                                         DKA(7): Electrolytes, magnesium, phosphorous, and blood gas must be measured
                                         every 2–4 hours or more frequently if clinically indicated. BUN, SCr, and hematocrit
                                         should be measured every 6–8 hours. Intake and output should be measured hourly
                                         along with neurological assessments and vital signs.
                                         Hyperkalemia: Serum potassium concentrations and BG. Treatment should be dis-
                                         continued when potassium concentrations are normalized.



