Brand names            Intron A for Injection


Medication error       Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
potential
                       Interferon alfa-2b may be confused with interferon alfa-2a, interferon alfa-n3, pegylated
                       interferon alfa-2b, peginterferon beta-1a.
                       USP reports that interferon alfa-2a has been confused with interferon alfa-2b.(1)


Contraindications      U.S. boxed warning: Alpha interferons cause or aggravate fatal or life-threatening
and warnings           neuropsychiatric, autoimmune, ischemic, and infectious disorders. Patients should be
                       monitored closely, and periodic clinical and laboratory valuations should be performed.
                       Treatment should be stopped in patients with persistently severe or worsening signs or
                       symptoms of these conditions. In many cases, but not all, these disorders resolve after
                       therapy is discontinued.(2)
                       Use with ribavirin: Ribavirin is mutagenic and genotoxic and is potentially carcinogenic.
                       It may cause birth defects and/or fetal death; therefore, pregnancy should be avoided in
                       female patients and female partners of male patients during ribavirin therapy. Ribavirin
                       causes hemolytic anemia.(1)
                       Contraindications: Hypersensitivity to interferon alfa-2b or product components, auto-
                       immune hepatitis, and decompensated liver disease.(2)
                       Warnings: Should be used with caution in patients with a history of pulmonary disease,
                       diabetes mellitus with a history of diabetic ketoacidosis, and in patients with coagulation
                       disorders or severe myelosuppression.(2)


Infusion-related       The ready-made solution for injection and the multidose pens for injection are not indi-
cautions               cated for IV use.(2) For IV administration, use the preservative-free reconstituted solution.
                       Injection site reactions occur in 5% of pediatric patients with chronic hepatitis B.(2)


Dosage                 Chronic hepatitis B with compensated liver disease: 3 million units/m2 sub-Q
                       3 times/week for 1 week, then increase to 6 million units/m2 sub-Q 3 times/week for
                       16–24 weeks.(2) Maximum dose 10 million units/week.(2,3)
                       Chronic hepatitis C: Children ≥3 years and adolescents: 3–5 million units/m2 sub-Q
                       3 times/week(3,4)
                       Melanoma: In two small studies, children from 1.5–17 years old received induction
                       therapy with 20 million units/m2 IV 5 times/week for 4 weeks. This was followed by
                       10 million units/m2 sub-Q 3 times/week for 48 weeks.(5,6)
                       Hemangiomas or Kasabach-Merritt syndrome: 3 million units/m2/day sub-Q for 6–12
                       months.(7-10)


Dosage adjustment      Recommendations for dosage decreases are according to hematologic indices, liver, and
in organ dysfunction   renal function. In addition, recommended dosage changes vary according to disease being
                       treated. (See the Comments section.)
                       Interferon alfa-2b may be used in combination with ribavirin for the treatment of hepatitis
                       C and hepatitis B. This combination should not be used in patients with CrCl <50 mL/min.
                       Hepatic toxicity: Discontinue for grade 3 (severe) hepatic injury or hepatic decompen-
                       sation (Child-Pugh class B and C). Discontinue if AST >5 times upper limit of normal.
                       Hematologic toxicity(2)

