Suitable diluents       NS


Maximum                 ≥10 million units/100 mL for IV infusion(2)
concentration
                        Concentrations for sub-Q and IM use range from 6–50 million units/mL depending on the
                        product and indication.(2)


Preparation and         The lyophilized powder is reconstituted with the provided diluent (SW).(2) After the dilu-
delivery                ent is added, swirl the vial gently to dissolve the powder, do not shake. The reconstituted
                        powder is suitable for IM or sub-Q administration. For IV infusion, the reconstituted dose
                        should be withdrawn and added to 100 mL of NS. The final concentration for IV infusion
                        should be ≥10 million units/100 mL. This contains no preservative and should be discarded
                        after the first dose is withdrawn.
                        Multidose vials (IM, sub-Q) and pens (sub-Q) are available in a variety of concentrations
                        that are ready to use.(2)
                        Stability: Store powder and solution for injection under refrigeration at 2°C to 8°C; do
                        not freeze.
                        Powder for injection: Following reconstitution may be stored in refrigerator up to
                        24 hours.
                        Prefilled pens: After first use, discard unused portion after 4 weeks.


IV push                 Not indicated


Intermittent infusion   Over 20 minutes(2) Administer in the evening to enhance tolerability.


Continuous infusion     Not indicated


Other routes of         IM, sub-Q, intralesional(2)
administration

Comments                Adverse effects: A flu-like syndrome of fever, fatigue, malaise, myalgia, chills, headache,
                        arthralgia, and rigors occurs frequently.(2) Severity of this increases with higher dos-
                        ages.(2,11,12) Fevers peak within 6 hours and may persist for up to 12 hours. Pretreatment
                        with antipyretics (e.g., APAP or NSAIDs) may be beneficial. Dosing at night may decrease
                        the flu-like adverse effects.(2)
                        Children may exhibit significant changes in body weight, linear growth, BMI, and body
                        composition.(13)
                        Ischemic retinopathy and uveitis have been reported in children treated for hepatitis C
                        with or without ribavirin.(14)




                                                                                                              505
Interferon Alfa-2b
Comments (cont.)                    Interferon alfa-2b used in combination with ribavirin for the treatment of hepatitis C and
                                    hepatitis B resulted in hemolytic anemia in 10% of patients.
                                    Monitoring: CBC with differential and platelet count pretreatment and routinely during
                                    therapy, blood glucose, liver function tests, renal function tests, thyroid-stimulating hor-
                                    mone levels, triglyceride levels, chest x-ray, and neuropsychiatric monitoring.(2)
                                    Patients who develop moderate depression should be monitored closely during treatment
                                    and for 6 months after completion of treatment. If severe depression, or worsening psy-
                                    chiatric symptoms occur, discontinue treatment and the patient should have immediate
                                    psychiatric care.(2)
                                    Drug interactions: Weak inhibitor of CYP1A2. Interferon decreases theophylline clear-
                                    ance.(2,26) Theophylline serum concentrations should be monitored during treatment.
                                    Interferon alfa may enhance the adverse/toxic effect of clozapine. Specifically, the risk of
                                    agranulocytosis may be increased.(2)


