Brand names         Camptosar, generic


Medication error    ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error.(1)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
                    USP reports that irinotecan has been confused with topotecan; no patient harm resulted.(2)
                    Irinotecan HCl and irinotecan liposomal are not interchangeable; verify product and dose
                    prior to preparation and administration, as doses are not equivalent.


Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Irinotecan must be administered under the supervision of physician
and warnings        experienced with chemotherapeutic agents. Irinotecan can induce both late and early forms
                    of diarrhea and severe myelosuppression can occur.(3) (See Significant Adverse Effects in
                    the Comments section.)
                    Contraindications: Irinotecan is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity
                    to the drug or any of its components.(3)
                    Other warnings: May cause severe diarrhea, neutropenia, hypersensitivity, colitis/ileus,
                    renal impairment, interstitial pulmonary disease symptoms, severe emesis, and risk of
                    thromboembolism.(3,4) (See Significant Adverse Effects in the Comments section.)


Infusion-related    Irinotecan is an irritant. A higher incidence of cholinergic side effects has been reported
cautions            with shorter infusion times.(5) Extravasation should be avoided. If extravasation occurs,
                    flush site with sterile water and apply ice.(3)


Dosage              Consult institutional protocols for complete dosing information.
                    Irinotecan is used as a component of combination therapy or as a single agent to treat
                    multiple pediatric tumors, including neuroblastoma, hepatocellular tumors, Wilms tumor,
                    Ewing sarcoma, osteosarcoma, rhabdomyosarcoma, and CNS tumors, including medul-
                    loblastoma, ependymomas, brain stem gliomas, and astrocytomas.(4)
                    Ewing sarcoma, progressive or recurrent: 10–20 mg/m2/dose daily on days 1–5 and
                    days 8–12 q 3 wk, combined with temozolomide(5,6)
                    Neuroblastoma, refractory: 50 mg/m2/dose daily on days 1–5, repeated q 21 days(7)
                        With temozolomide: 10 mg/m2/dose daily on days 1–5 and days 8–12 q 3 wk. May
                        repeat up to 6 cycles.(8)
                    Refractory solid tumors (low-dose, protracted schedule): 15–20 mg/m2/day for
                    5 days for 2 consecutive weeks repeated q 21 days(3,9,10)
                        With temozolomide: 10 mg/m2/dose once daily for days 1–5, repeated days 8–12
                        q 3 wk. May repeat up to 6 cycles.(8)
                        With temozolomide and vincristine: 15 mg/m2/dose once daily on days 1–5 and
                        days 8–12 of a 28-day treatment cycle. May repeat if tolerated.(11)
                    Refractory solid tumors or CNS tumors: 50 mg/m2/day for 5 days repeated q 21
                    days(4,12-14,12)
                        Heavily pretreated: 125 mg/m2/dose once weekly for 4 weeks, repeated q 6 wk(4,15)
                        Less heavily pretreated: 160 mg/m2/dose once weekly for 4 weeks, repeated q 6 wk(4,15)




