adjustment          (i.e., serum bilirubin >2 mg/dL) or renal dysfunction.(3)
in organ
dysfunction         Some studies suggest that patients with moderate hepatic dysfunction (i.e., serum bilirubin
                    of 1–2 mg/dL) and a prior history of abdominal or pelvic radiation may be at increased risk
                    of developing neutropenia.(4) Some clinicians reduce dose in this scenario. In patients with
                    serum bilirubin >2 mg/dL, use is not recommended.(3)
                    A 6-year old with neuroblastoma who developed renal failure and required hemodialysis,
                    was treated with 13 cycles of irinotecan 20 mg/m2/day and oral temozolomide for 5 days
                    without any dose adjustments, transfusions, febrile neutropenia or diarrhea.(18)


Maximum             Not established. In clinical trials, single dosages up to 345–750 mg/m2 have been admin-
dosage              istered to adult cancer patients.(3) No antidotes for overdoses are known. Patients should
                    receive supportive care to prevent neutropenia and diarrhea.


