  ($teddy_137_id$iron-dextran$teddy_137_id$, $teddy_137_name$Iron Dextran$teddy_137_name$, $teddy_137_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_137_source$, 1930818, $teddy_137$Iron Dextran
Brand names         DexFerrum, INFeD


Medication error    Iron dextran products are available in two formulations: a high-molecular-weight (DexFer-
potential           rum) and a low-molecular-weight (INFeD) formulation. Because these are not clinically
                    interchangeable, care should be taken to verify the product and dose. This is especially
                    true when substituting one form of iron for another as significant dosing errors can occur.
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. DexFerrum has been confused with Desferal; how-
                    ever, no patient harm resulted.(1)


Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Parenteral iron is associated with anaphylactic-type reactions, which
and warnings        have caused death. (See the Comments section.)(2) Use particular caution in patients with
                    multiple drug allergies as these individuals have an increased risk for anaphylaxis.(2) Should
                    also be used with caution in those who have asthma.(2) During infusion, patients should
                    be monitored for signs and symptoms of anaphylactic reaction.(2) Administer only when
                    resuscitation equipment and personnel trained to detect and treat anaphylactic reactions
                    are readily available.(2) (See the Comments section.)


Infusion-related    During infusion, patients should be monitored for signs and symptoms of anaphylactic
cautions            reaction (e.g., hypotension, bronchospasm, pharyngeal/angioedema, urticaria, pruritus).(2-6)
                    Premedication does not appear to decrease the incidence of acute reactions. (See the
                    Comments section.) A test dose should be given to all patients prior to administration.
                    (See Test Dose in the Dosage section.) Test dose should be given over 5 minutes, and
                    the rate of administration should not exceed 1 mg/min.(2) Pain and phlebitis may occur in
                    association with IV administration.(2) (See the Other Routes of Administration section for
                    information on IM administration.)


Dosage              The two available formulations (dextran, sucrose) differ in chemical characteristic and
                    are not interchangeable. One study reported a higher rate of hypersensitivity reactions
                    with the larger molecular weight dextran (Dexferrum) than the lower molecular weight
                    (InFeD) product.(4-6) A hypersensitivity reaction to one parenteral iron product does not
                    predict a similar response to a different product. Dosage adjustment must be made when
                    interchanging these formulations. Iron dextran contains 50 mg of elemental iron per
                    mL. Parenteral iron therapy should be reserved for patients with iron deficiency who are
                    unable to take iron orally.
                    Resuscitation equipment and personnel trained to detect and treat anaphylactic reactions
                    should be available.(2)
                    Test dose: A one-time test dose should be administered prior to initiating therapy.
                    The test dose should be administered slowly over ≤50 mg/min (not exceed 1 mg/min).
                    If there are no signs or symptoms of allergic reaction after 1 hour, the remainder of the
