Dosage adjustment       Use extreme caution in patients with significant liver dysfunction.(2) Considering the dis-
in organ dysfunction    tribution characteristics of iron, there should be no need to adjust dose in patients with
                        renal dysfunction. However, guidelines for the use of parenteral iron in children with renal
                        disease recommend an adjustment in the dose of the test regimen.(7) (see the Dosage
                        section: iron deficiency in peritoneal dialysis patients with chronic renal failure.) Should
                        not be used in the early stages of infection associated with the kidney.(2)


Maximum dosage          In most cases, daily doses should not exceed the following weight-based doses.(2) If
                        <5 kg, give 25 mg (0.5 mL); if ≥5 kg but ≤10 kg, give 50 mg (1 mL); and if >10 kg,
                        give 100 mg (2 mL).


Additives               None reported


Suitable diluents       The manufacturer does not recommend dilution because the product was originally
                        developed for IM use. However, dilution with NS is preferred for IV administration.(8,11,13)
                        Although dextrose solutions may be used, this may increase the incidence of phlebitis.(13)


Maximum                 50 mg/mL(2,12)
concentration

Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(13)
                        Preparation: Iron dextran is a dark brown, slightly viscous liquid that is a complex of
                        ferric hydroxide and dextran in NS providing 50 mg of elemental iron per milliliter.
                        Delivery system issues: The dextran formulation (5 mcg/mL) adsorbs to filters com-
                        posed of cellulose nitrate and acetate resulting in 93% loss in the first mL that crosses
                        the filter. Over time the filter become saturated.(13)
                        Compatibility: Although not recommended by the manufacturer, iron dextran has been
                        administered in PN formulations. Results of studies evaluating iron dextran stability and
                        compatibility in PN solutions are inconsistent.(25,26) To ensure compatibility, the PN solution
                        should contain at least 2% amino acids (10 mg iron dextran/L was visually compatible in
                        neonatal PN solutions containing at least 2% amino acids).(27) (For additional PN compat-
                        ibility information, see Appendix D.) Addition to a 3-in-1 PN solution is not recommended.(2)


IV push                 Test dose should be infused over at least 30 seconds (INFeD) or 5 minutes (Dexferrum).
                        May be administered undiluted (50 mg/mL) at a rate <1 mg/min.(2,13) (See the Infusion-
                        Related Cautions section.)


Intermittent infusion   Dilute in NS and infuse over 4–6 hours.(2,13)


Continuous infusion     The iron deficit can be replaced over several days by adding iron to the PN solution in daily
                        amounts not exceeding the maximum daily recommendations.(20-22,28) (See the Maximum
                        Dosage section.)


Other routes of         Although the IV route is preferred, iron dextran has been administered IM using Z-track
administration          technique into upper quadrant of buttock.(2,13) IM administration may be associated with
                        pain, staining of skin, tissue necrosis or abscess, and sarcoma development at the injec-
                        tion site.(29) Staining of the skin can be minimized by using a separate needle to withdraw
                        the drug from the container and by displacing the skin laterally prior to injection.(13)




