Comments                            Significant adverse drug reactions: Patients with multiple drug allergies may be at
                                    increased risk of anaphylactic-type reactions.(2) Although a test dose should be admin-
                                    istered prior to the first therapeutic dose,(2) deaths have occurred following a test dose,
                                    as well as during administration of therapeutic doses after a test dose was tolerated.(2)
                                    In fact, successful use of a test doses, or prior therapeutic doses does not ensure safety
                                    because most patients who develop severe reactions have received prior doses without
                                    incident.(19) Delayed reactions may appear within 24–48 hours and may include arthralgias,
                                    myalgias, headache, fever, chills, nausea and vomiting, and lymphadenopathy.(2) These
                                    symptoms should resolve within 3–4 days.
                                    Monitoring: The patient should be monitored for signs and symptoms of hypersensitivity
                                    or anaphylaxis during and for 1 hour after an infusion. IV site should be monitored for
                                    evidence of phlebitis.
                                    Serum ferritin should be monitored to prevent potential iron overload.(2,12,19) Serum fer-
                                    ritin peaks about 7–9 days after IV iron administration and slowly returns to baseline in
                                    about 3 weeks.(2)
                                    Hemoglobin should also be monitored. A 2007 update to the KDOQI guidelines for the
                                    treatment of anemia in chronic kidney disease addressed the target hemoglobin range. The
                                    recommendations specified a target hemoglobin concentration of 1–12 g/dL for pediatric
                                    dialysis and nondialysis patients with chronic kidney disease receiving erythropoietin-
                                    stimulating agents.(30)
                                    Laboratory test: Large doses may cause a brown discoloration to serum and urine. Iron
                                    may cause a false decrease in serum calcium.
                                    Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                                    combining any drug with iron. Concomitant use with angiotensin-converting enzyme
                                    inhibitors may increase the risk of adverse reaction to iron.(2)


