Comments                           Significant adverse effects: Risk factors for the development of arrhythmias include
                                   severe asthma, acidosis, hypoxemia, and concomitant use of methylxanthines or
                                   corticosteroids. Doses sufficient to increase the heart rate to more than 130 BPM may
                                   increase the likelihood of inducing ventricular arrhythmias; consider reducing the rate or
                                   discontinuing the infusion if heart rate exceeds 110 BPM.(3) Cardiac dysrhythmias(4) and
                                   abnormal ECG and enzymatic findings suggestive of myocardial dysfunction have been
                                   reported in pediatric patients.(5,6) Although rare in pediatric patients, myocardial ischemia
                                   may occur.(3,5-8) Two adolescents with severe asthma who were treated with isoproterenol
                                   had myocardial necrosis at autopsy.(7,8)
                                   Monitoring: Systemic blood pressure, heart rate, urine output, ECG, as well as central
                                   venous pressure and blood gases should be closely monitored. Daily measurement of
                                   cardiac enzymes including CPK-MB is recommended when used in refractory asthma.(3)
                                   Pharmacokinetic considerations: Ten postoperative cardiac pediatric patients received
                                   doses of 0.029 ± 0.002 mcg/kg/min while nine with reactive airway disease received 0.5
                                   ± 0.2 mcg/kg/min. Clearance was lower in the postoperative cardiac patients compared
                                   to those with reactive airway disease.(11)
                                   Pharmacodynamic considerations: Tolerance may occur following prolonged use.(3)
                                   Drug interactions: Avoid concurrent use with epinephrine due to the risk of serious
                                   arrhythmias.(3)
                                   Other: May be safer than isosorbide dinitrate or nitroglycerin for use during tilt table
                                   testing for syncope.(19,20)


