Brand names         Ketalar, generic

Medication error    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Confusion has been reported between Ketalar and
potential           ketorolac.(1)
                    High-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm should
                    an error occur.(36)

Contraindications   Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to ketamine or any component of the formula-
and warnings        tion. Should not be used when a significant increase in blood pressure would be harmful.(2)
                    Warnings: Does not impair laryngeal reflexes or independent airway maintenance; how-
                    ever, transient laryngospasm, apnea, and respiratory arrest have occurred.(3-5)
                    Use with caution in patients at risk for increasing intracranial pressure.(2,3) (See the Comments
                    section.)

Infusion-related    Rapid administration (<60 seconds) may result in increased pressor response and/or
cautions            increased apnea and respiratory depression.(2) May cause local pain at the injection site.(2)

Dosage              Infants ≤3 months have a higher incidence of airway complications, leading some to
                    recommend that ketamine be avoided in this age group.(3) Oxygen saturation should be
                    monitored, and resuscitation and intubation equipment should be readily available for
                    respiratory support.(2) Pretreatment with atropine or other drying agent prevents vagal-
                    mediated bradycardia and decreases secretions.(2,6,7,9)
                    Anesthesia: 1–3 mg/kg IV over 60 seconds or 5–13 mg/kg IM.(2,10) Followed by one-half
                    to full induction dose repeated PRN(2) or 1–5 mg/kg/hr.(2,11)
                    Endotracheal intubation (adjunct therapy): 1–2 mg/kg IV over 60 seconds(6)
                    Procedural sedation/analgesia: Usually IV doses of 1–2 mg/kg (0.25–5 mg/kg) and
                    IM doses of 4–5 mg/kg (0.5–13 mg/kg) are used for nonsurgical procedures.(3,6,12,13,45) In
                    32 children undergoing muscle biopsy, 2 mg/kg IV repeated in 10 minutes if needed or 10
                    mg/kg IM was used.(14) In the ED, 3–4 mg/kg IM has been used.(9,15-17) For emergencies,
                    the AAP recommends 1–2 mg/kg IM or 0.5–1 mg/kg IV.(6) One group reported using an
                    IV loading dose of 1 mg/kg followed by 50–75 mcg/kg/min for the procedure’s duration.(4)
                    Seven pediatric oncology patients with propofol associated emergence agitation were given
                    concurrent ketamine. Prior to the addition of ketamine, all of the patients had experienced
                    emergence agitation during a previous anesthetic session. Patients received a mean load-
                    ing dose of 1.3 mg/kg (range 0.35–6.56 mg/kg) followed by a mean continuous infusion of
                    37 mcg/kg/min (range 10–132 mcg/kg/min) for a total of 123 anesthetic sessions. There were
                    no recurrences of emergence agitation in any of the patients after the addition of ketamine.(18)
                    Other uses
                        Sedation/analgesia in the ICU: 0.5–2 mg/kg followed by a continuous infusion of
                        5–20 mcg/kg/min. After cardiac surgery, 10 children were given 16.6–33.3 mcg/kg/
                        min (1 or 2 mg/kg/hr) for 24 hours.(19) Others report doses of 0.5–1 mg/kg followed
                        by 10 mcg/kg/min for 48–72 hours.(20)
                        Severe bronchospasm/refractory status asthmaticus: The use of ketamine is
                        controversial. Several cases have noted the efficacy of IV ketamine in non-ventilated
                        and ventilated pediatric patients in the ED or ICU who were unresponsive to standard
                        therapy.(21,23,39,40,41,42) A retrospective review identified 17 mechanically ventilated chil-
                        dren who improved with bolus doses of 2 mg/kg followed by a continuous infusion
                        of 20–60 mcg/kg/min for 12–96 hours.(22) Ten patients enrolled in the ED received
                        ketamine at a loading dose of 1 mg/kg IV followed by a continuous infusion of
                        0.75 mg/kg/hr for 1 hour. Addition of ketamine to standard therapy was associated
                        with improved indices of acute asthma severity.(43) Conversely, another study involved
                        68 patients, with 33 randomized to ketamine and 35 randomized to placebo. Admin-
                        istration of ketamine (0.2 mg/kg followed by an infusion of 0.5 mg/kg per hour for
                        2 hours) provided no incremental benefit to standard therapy in this cohort of children
                        with a moderately severe asthma exacerbation.(44)
                        Opioid withdrawal: A 2-year-old (9.5-kg) who failed traditional fentanyl weaning
                        strategies was started on ketamine at 17.5 mcg/kg/min. The fentanyl was success-
                        fully weaned off and then the ketamine was weaned.(24)




