Ketamine HCl
Comments (cont.)                         Increased intracranial pressure (ICP): A systemic review of 179 citations that
                                         included 127 adult and 87 pediatric patients assessed the effect of ketamine on ICP.
                                         The authors concluded that there exists Oxford level 2b, GRADE C evidence in adults
                                         and level 4, GRADE C in pediatrics to support that ketamine does not increase ICP in
                                         nontraumatic neurological illness when patients are sedated and ventilated.(38)
                                         Pulmonary edema: A previously healthy 8-year-old female developed severe neu-
                                         rogenic pulmonary edema following an IM dose of 6.25 mg/kg for dressing changes
                                         of first-degree burns.(35)
                                         Vomiting: Ketamine-associated vomiting is not related to the initial dose, but it may
                                         be increased in those receiving large cumulative doses.(33) In one study, the incidence
                                         of vomiting in 128 children receiving ketamine alone was 12.6% and in 127 children
                                         receiving concomitant ondansetron was 4.7%.(13) Ketamine used with midazolam
                                         resulted in fewer respiratory adverse events but more vomiting than midazolam and
                                         fentanyl.(34) Similarly, combining ketamine with propofol resulted in fewer episodes of
                                         hypotension and bradycardia but more agitation than propofol alone.(12)
                                    Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources before combining any drug with ket-
                                    amine. Substrate for CYP 450 isoenzymes 2B6, 2C9, and 3A4.


