Ketorolac Tromethamine
Comments                            Allergic reaction: Hypersensitivity reactions, ranging from bronchospasm to anaphy-
                                    lactic shock. Anaphylaxis may occur in patients with and without known hypersensitivity.
                                    Because cross-reactivity between aspirin and other NSAIDs has been reported patients
                                    with aspirin-sensitive asthma should not receive ketorolac.(2)
                                    Bleeding: Ketorolac can inhibit platelet aggregation and adhesion, which may lead to
                                    prolonged bleeding time or bleeding. Bleeding can occur at any time during therapy and
                                    may present without warning. Ketorolac was considered a contributing factor to hematuria
                                    in a 16-year-old and to incision-site bleeding in an 11-year-old child.(21) A retrospective
                                    review of 57 postsurgical neonates (0–3 months) concluded that infants <21 days and
                                    <37 weeks calculated gestational age are at increased risk for bleeding and that ketorolac
                                    should be avoided in this population.(32) Although post-tonsillectomy patients receiving
                                    1 mg/kg of ketorolac had fewer emetic episodes than those given 0.1 mg/kg/dose of
                                    morphine, they had more major bleeding.(8) More measures to control bleeding were also
                                    noted post-tonsillectomy in children receiving ketorolac (1 mg/kg) compared to those
                                    receiving 35 mg acetaminophen/kg rectally.(7) Conversely, a retrospective review(40) and
                                    prospectively randomized trial(41) in infants and children given standard doses of ketorolac
                                    did not experience an increase risk of bleeding complications or postoperative bleeding
                                    that required surgical exploration.
                                    Hepatic dysfunction: In adults, NSAID therapy has led to liver disease that may be
                                    associated with an increase in ALT and AST that may be 3 times above the upper limits
                                    of normal. If signs or symptoms develop, ketorolac should be discontinued.(2)
                                    Renal dysfunction: Acute renal failure associated with ketorolac administration has
                                    been reported in infants and children.(33-35) One study reported development of irreversible
                                    renal failure in a 17-year-old in sickle cell crisis, despite adequate hydration and recom-
                                    mends following administration guidelines for renal comprised patients and monitoring
                                    renal function closely.(35)
                                    Pharmacokinetics: A study in 36 children and another in 17 infants found that an
                                    IV dose of 0.5 mg/kg resulted in similar concentrations over a 6-hour period as that
                                    reported for adults.(5,36) The pharmacokinetic parameters were not different among ages
                                    1–3 years, 4–7 years, 8–11 years, and 12–16 years.(5) Another study in 14 infants aged
                                    2–6 months reported similar clearance of single doses of 0.5 mg/kg and 1 mg/kg as
                                    compared to patients aged 6–18 months.(19) The IV and IM bioavailability and efficacy
                                    are comparable.(37) One group found a difference in the stereo-specific pharmacokinetics
                                    of ketorolac in infants 2–6 months. This population had more rapid clearance of the (S-)
                                    isomer, which is associated with analgesic affect.(43)
                                    Opioid-sparing: Ketorolac has been used concomitantly with morphine and meperidine
                                    and is opioid-sparing effect. A study evaluating 19 infants <6 months of age postcardio-
                                    thoracic surgery found that ketorolac was safe, but that it did not decrease the require-
                                    ment for standard analgesia.(38) However, another study in children 1–5 years of age with
                                    postinguinal hernia repair noted that a preoperative dose of 1 mg/kg (along with 20 mg/
                                    kg of IV acetaminophen) was safe, effective, and reduced the need for rescue analgesics
                                    (i.e., fentanyl).(39)
                                    Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                                    combining any drug with ketorolac.



