  ($teddy_146_id$levetiracetam$teddy_146_id$, $teddy_146_name$LevETIRAcetam$teddy_146_name$, $teddy_146_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_146_source$, 2068438, $teddy_146$LevETIRAcetam
Brand names         Keppra, generic

Medication error    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Levetiracetam has been confusion with lamotrigine,
potential           levoFLOXacin, and levOCARNitine. Confusion with levoFLOXacin has resulted in patient
                    harm. Confusion with Kaletra and Keflex has also been reported.(1)

Contraindications   Contraindications: Hypersensitivity to levetiracetam or any component of the formulation.(2)
and warnings        Anaphylaxis, within seconds of administration, has been reported in a neonate given 10 mg/
                    kg. The erythematous rash and urticaria began on the scalp/face and progress to the entire
                    body within 3 minutes. The neonate experienced hypotension and severe metabolic acidosis
                    that required CPR and administration of IM epinephrine. Heart rate, blood pressure, and blood
                    gases returned to normal within 60 minutes, and the generalized rash faded within 18 hours.(24)
                    Warnings: Severe dermatologic reactions, including toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-
                    Johnson syndrome, have been reported in children and adults.(2) Onset generally occurs within ~2
                    weeks of initiation but may be delayed more than 4 months. Levetiracetam should be discontinued
                    if signs of a hypersensitivity reaction or unspecified rash occur. (See the Comments section.)
                    Anticonvulsants should not be discontinued abruptly because of the possibility of increas-
                    ing seizure frequency but should be gradually withdrawn when possible.(2)
                    Neuropsychiatric adverse events including somnolence, fatigue, coordination difficulties,
                    behavioral abnormalities (e.g., aggression, agitation, anxiety, anger, hostility, irritabil-
                    ity, depression) and psychotic symptoms (e.g., psychosis, hallucinations) may occur.(2,3)
                    Behavioral abnormalities may occur more frequently in children and may required dosage
                    reductions.(2) The incidence of suicidal behavior/ideation is greater than placebo (0.43%
                    versus 0.24% risk) in patients receiving anticonvulsants, including levetiracetam, as either
                    monotherapy or as adjuvant therapy for the treatment of epilepsy, psychiatric disorders,
                    and other conditions.(2) The symptoms were observed as early as 1 week after initiation
                    of antiepileptic drugs and continued through duration of trials (most trials ≤24 weeks).
                    Risk did not vary significantly by age (age range: 5–100 years). Patients should be moni-
                    tored for emergence of suicidal thoughts or behavior, thoughts of self-harm, any unusual
                    changes in behavior or mood, or the emergence or worsening of depressive symptoms.

Infusion-related    None reported
cautions

Dosage              Although levetiracetam is labeled as replacement therapy for patients who have been on oral
                    therapy but cannot temporarily tolerate oral medications,(2) it is frequently being used in refrac-
                    tory status epilepticus(25,26) and in adult patients as prophylaxis for post-traumatic epilepsy.(27,39,40)
                    Seizures (no previous levetiracetam)
                        Neonatal seizures: IV levetiracetam has been given to premature and term
                        neonates.(3,4,6-13,28-31) There are no recommended loading doses, but most studies note
                        20–35 mg/kg with a range of 10–60 mg/kg. Maintenance dose have generally been
                        25 mg/kg/day divided TID or BID but have ranged from 10–80 mg/kg/day. Doses
                        should be slowly titrated by increasing 10 mg/kg every 3–5 days.
                        Infants and children(3-7,14-16,20-23,32-38)
                             Loading dose: 20 mg/kg (10–60 mg/kg) over 15 minutes up to 500 mg
                             Maintenance dose: 25–50 mg/kg/day divided q 8–12 hr up to 3000 mg/day(3,4,6,14-16,32-38)
                        Adolescents and adults
                             Loading dose: 50 mg/kg/dose (maximum dose: 500 mg)
                             Maintenance dose: 30–55 mg/kg/day divided BID up to 3000 mg/day. Initial
                             use of fixed dose of 500 mg BID is suggested with titration of dose every 2 weeks
                             as needed. Efficacy of doses other than 3000 mg/day has not been established.
                    Prophylaxis of post-traumatic epilepsy: Historically, phenytoin has been used for this
                    purpose; however, practice is shifting to the use of levetiracetam.(39,40)
                    Replacement therapy (prior levetiracetam): The initial total daily IV dosage should equal
                    the total daily dosage and frequency of oral levetiracetam.(2) The patient may be switched to oral
                    levetiracetam at the same daily dosage and frequency as that administered intravenously.(2)




