                                                                            LevETIRAcetam
Dosage adjustment       If CrCl is <50 mL/min/1.73 m2, give 50% of the normal dose(18); in adults with moderate
in organ dysfunction    renal dysfunction (30–50 mL/min/1.73 m2), give 250–750 mg q 12 hr and 250–500 mg
                        q 12 hr if CrCl <30 mg/mL/1.73 m2.(2) Patients on hemodialysis, continuous ambulatory
                        peritoneal dialysis, or continuous renal replacement therapy should receive 50% of normal
                        dose every 24 hours(18); a supplemental dose after hemodialysis is recommended.(18) Phar-
                        macokinetics parameters were unchanged in mild (Child-Pugh A) to moderate (Child-Pugh
                        B) hepatic impairment.(2) Total body clearance was half that of normal subjects compared
                        to those with severe hepatic impairment (Child-Pugh C).(2) No dose adjustment is needed
                        for patients with hepatic impairment.(2) (See Other in the Comments section.)

Maximum dosage          Loading dose of 70 mg/kg.(7) The manufacturer recommends 60 mg/kg/day,(2) but doses
                        of 120 mg/kg/day have been given(4) up to 3000 mg/day.(2)

Additives               None reported

Suitable diluents       NS, LR, or D5W as a 1:1 dilution(2)

Maximum                 1500 mg (3 × 5 mL vials) should be diluted in 100 mL of compatible diluents.(2) Others
concentration           have safely used a 1:1 dilution of drug from vial with D5W or NS.(19)

Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(41)
                        Preparation: Vial must be diluted prior to use. Do not dilute premixed 100-mL bag.
                        Stability: Intact vials should be store at 25°C. Reconstituted and prefilled products are
                        stable for at least 24 hours at room temperature (15°C to 30°C).(2) Product with particu-
                        late matter or discoloration should not be used.(2) Any unused portion of a vial should be
                        discarded as the product does not contain preservative.(2)

IV push                 Not recommended. Has been given over 3–5 minutes in adults.(42)


Intermittent infusion   2–5 mg/kg/min with dose generally given over 15 minutes.(2) Doses as large as 60 mg/
                        kg have been given safely over 5–6 minutes.(19)

Continuous infusion     One paper noted a dose of 5 mg/kg/min.(20)

Other routes of         Can be safely given by IM administration.(43)
administration

Comments                Anticonvulsant hypersensitivity syndrome/DRESS syndrome: DRESS (drug reac-
                        tion with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms) is an acute, life-threatening, idiosyncratic
                        reaction that includes a classic triad of fever, rash, and lymphadenopathy and may involve
                        multisystem organ failure (e.g., liver, kidney, heart, lungs). Historically, DRESS has been
                        noted in patients receiving anticonvulsants containing an aromatic structure (i.e., phenytoin,
                        phenobarbital, carbamazepine, primidone, and lamotrigine). Although levetiracetam is struc-
                        turally unrelated, as it is a nonaromatic drug, cases of DRESS have been reported follow-
                        ing oral therapy.(44,45) Median time to develop dermatologic symptoms (SJS, TEN) is 14–17
                        days following initiation, but cases have occur with 4 months.(2) Immediate discontinuation
                        of levetiracetam is essential for good outcome. Large doses of steroids may be indicated.
                        Monitoring: Virtually no laboratory tests are altered.(2) Although abnormalities have been
                        noted in hematologic parameters (e.g., decreases in RBC counts, hemoglobin, hematocrit, WBC
                        counts and neutrophils) and serum transaminases (increased), the values remained within
                        the normal range.(21,22) Benefit of therapeutic drug monitoring has not been established(2);
                        however, concentrations of 6–20 mg/L were noted in adults given doses of 1000–3000 mg/day.
                        Drug interactions: Levetiracetam is not a substrate for any CYP-isozyme, is not highly
                        protein bound, and does not interfere with the metabolism of other anticonvulsants.(23)
                        No clinically significant pharmacokinetically based drug interactions have been reported.(2)


