Preparation and                    Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                           before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                                   other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                                   and extended stability.(2)
                                   Preparation: Mix with suitable diluents to concentrations of 0.5–8 mg/mL.(2)
                                   Stability: May be stored at room temperature for up to 24 hours in PVC bags.(2)
                                   Compatibility: May be added to PN solutions and infused over 24 hours or during a PN
                                   cycling schedule.(10,13,15-18) One study documented stability of carnitine via PN and TNA
                                   with Y-site administration of 20% fat emulsion at all study points (IV bag, distal to filter,
                                   after mixing with fat emulsion) for up to 24 hours at room temperature and up to 30 days
                                   when stored at 4°C to 5°C(23); however, another study evaluated stability with carnitine
                                   in TNA and observed creaming.(24)


IV push                            200 mg/mL over 2–3 minutes(2)


Intermittent infusion              May give by intermittent infusion(2,9)


Continuous infusion                May be given by continuous infusion(2)


Other routes of                    No information is available to support administration by other routes.
administration

Comments                           Significant adverse effects: L-carnitine (oral and IV) can cause seizures in patients
                                   with and without a preexisting seizure disorder.(2) Children and infants who received
                                   large doses and experienced seizures had other risk factors, including microcephaly, and
                                   metabolic acidosis.(11)
                                   Higher metabolic rate, greater nitrogen excretion, and lower weight gain has been reported
                                   following 48 mg/kg/day given via PN in 12 preterm neonates.(22)
                                   Other: IV carnitine has been beneficial in childhood cardiomyopathy.(25,28)
                                   Life-threatening lactic acidosis associated with reverse transcriptase inhibitors was
                                   ­corrected in an HIV-positive, 10-year-old patient with 12–50 mg/kg/day L-carnitine.(26)
                                   Neuroprotective therapy with L-carnitine (150 mg TID), pyridoxine (35 mg daily), and
                                   N-acetylcysteine (50 mg BID) was given to a 35-week gestation preterm infant with acute
                                   lymphoblastic leukemia who developed vincristine-associated neuropathy after the first
                                   phase of induction therapy (weight of 2.3 kg during induction therapy). Neuroprotective
                                   therapy was initiated at 46 days of age; neurologic recovery began at 57 days of age,
                                   and the infant had complete neurologic recovery by 5 months of age.(27)


