                                                                                 Levothyroxine Sodium
IV push                             40–100 mcg/mL in NS(3)


Intermittent infusion               No information is available to support administration by this method. Should not be mixed
                                    with IV infusion solutions.(3,9)


Continuous infusion                 No information is available to support administration by this method. Should not be mixed
                                    with IV infusion solutions.(3,9)


Other routes of                     Has been administered by IM injection; however, current manufacturer labeling does not
administration                      include this route.(9)


Comments                            Rare adverse effects: Pseudotumor cerebri has been reported to follow levothyroxine
                                    therapy in both adults and infants and children.(10)
                                    Premature infants <26 weeks gestational age who received 5 mcg/kg/day were reported
                                    to be at increased risk of refractory hypotension.(8)
                                    Drug interactions: Because levothyroxine is associated with numerous drug interac-
                                    tions, consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before combining any
                                    drug with levothyroxine.
                                    Other: The aim of therapy is to ensure normal growth and development.(7)
                                    Early, high-dose levothyroxine therapy has been shown to nearly eliminate the negative
                                    impact that congenital hypothyroidism has on IQ and CNS development.(7,11,12)
                                    Decreased oral bioavailability and need for IV therapy was reported in a cholestatic pre-
                                    term infant receiving TPN.(14)


