Brand names         Xylocaine, generic


Medication error    High-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm should
potential           an error occur.(1)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Confusion with bupivacaine and linezolid has been
                    reported.(2)


Contraindications   Contraindications: In patients with a known history of hypersensitivity to local anesthet-
and warnings        ics of the amide type.(3) Premixed lidocaine plus dextrose may also be contraindicated in
                    patients with known allergy to corn or corn products.(29) Lidocaine should not be used in
                    patients with Stokes-Adams syndrome, Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome, or with severe
                    degrees of sinoatrial, atrioventricular, or intraventricular block.(3)
                    Warnings: Emergency resuscitative equipment and drugs should be immediately avail-
                    able to manage adverse reactions involving cardiovascular, respiratory, or CNS systems. (3)


Infusion-related    Bronchospasm has been reported.(3) (See Rare Adverse Effects in the Comments section.)
cautions            Local thrombophlebitis may occur with prolonged infusions.(3) Lidocaine hydrochloride
                    products containing preservatives should not be given intravenously.(4)


Dosage              Anesthesia, local injectable: 4.5 mg/kg (maximum 300 mg). Dose varies based on
                    procedure, vascularity of tissue, degree and duration of anesthesia required, and physical
                    condition of patient.
                    Arrhythmias: Use for pulseless ventricular tachycardia of fibrillation that is unrespon-
                    sive to defibrillation, CPR, and epinephrine.(5) Data suggest that lidocaine is less effective
                    than amiodarone but may be used if amiodarone is not available.(31) Lidocaine solutions
                    that contain epinephrine must not be used to treat arrhythmias. (See the Other Routes
                    of Administration section for information related to ET and IO administration in those
                    without IV access.)
                        Initial: 0.5–1 mg/kg (maximum single dose 100 mg) IV/IO q 5–10 min until desired
                        effect or maximum total dose of 3 mg/kg is given.(5,6,31) Decrease loading dose by
                        50% in patients with reduced hepatic function or decreased hepatic blood flow (e.g.,
                        severe congestive heart failure or postcardiac surgery).(5)
                        Maintenance: 20–50 mcg/kg/min(5,6) If more than 15 minutes has lapsed between
                        the initial dose and start of the infusion, give 0.5–1 mg/kg.(31) Smaller doses may be
                        required in neonates with shock or patients with reduced hepatic function, decreased
                        hepatic blood flow, and severe congestive heart failure.
                    Epilepsy (refractory status): Initial dose of 1–3 mg/kg over 2 minutes.(8-17) If seizures
                    do not stop in 2 minutes, give an additional dose of 0.5 mg/kg. A continuous infusion
                    of 2–6 mg/kg/hr effectively controlled severe intractable seizures in neonates,(10,11,16)
                    infants,(12,13,17) and children.(14,17) A dose of 1.5–3.5 mg/kg/hr has been used in adults.(15)
                    Several investigators have evaluated dosing requirements in neonates.(10,11,16,32-34) One
                    study evaluated dosing in 20 neonates who were given a loading dose of 2 mg/kg over
                    10 minutes that was followed by followed by a continuous infusion of 6 mg/kg/hr for 12
                    hours, 4 mg/kg/hr for 12 hours, and finally 2 mg/kg/hr for 12 hours. No cardiac arrhyth-
                    mias were observed; however, some lidocaine serum concentrations were above 9 mg/L.
                    Using a simulated pharmacokinetic model, the infusion duration of lidocaine at 6 mg/kg/
                    hr was decreased from 12 to 6 hours. This dosing strategy was retested and evaluated in
                    neonates. Again, no cardiac arrhythmias were observed, lidocaine was effective in 78%
                    of the treatments, and maximal lidocaine plasma concentrations were <9 mg/L. Preterm
                    infants showed relatively high lidocaine plasma concentrations.
                    Another group retrospectively evaluated lidocaine for neonatal seizures in 319 full-term
                    and 94 preterm infants.(33) All patients received a loading dose of 2 mg/kg that was gen-
                    erally followed by 6–7 mg/kg/hr for 4 hours, 3–3.5 mg/kg/hr for 12 hours, or 1.5–1.75
                    mg/kg/hr for 12 hours. Response was lower in preterm infants (55.3%) versus full-term
                    infants (76.1%).




