Comments                          Rare adverse effects: Transient mydriasis has been reported in a neonate treated with
                                  lidocaine for seizures.(26) Excessive serum concentrations may also produce CNS symptoms
                                  (e.g., drowsiness, nausea, vomiting, disorientation, seizures, and muscle twitching).(3)
                                  Transient bronchospasm was noted in a 17-month-old with mild intermittent asthma.(27)
                                  Monitoring: Because large variability exists in patient response to initial and maintenance
                                  doses, individualize dosage based on serum concentration and clinical response. Reference
                                  range: 1.5–5 mg/L for antiarrhythmic effect.(3) The reference range for the management
                                  of status epilepticus has not been established. Potentially toxic at serum concentrations
                                  above 6 mg/L and toxic above 9 mg/L.
                                  Monitor for bradycardia and hypotension. Widening of the QRS interval by >0.02 sec-
                                  onds prolongation of the PR interval, significant ventricular slowing, or the appearance
                                  or aggravation of arrhythmias suggests toxicity.(3)
                                  Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                                  combining any drug with lidocaine. Should be used with caution in patients with digitalis
                                  toxicity accompanied by atrioventricular block.(3) Beta-blocking agents and cimetidine
                                  may reduce hepatic blood flow and decrease lidocaine clearance, which increases serum
                                  lidocaine concentrations to cause an increased incidence of adverse reactions.
                                  Hypothermia: One group evaluated the use of lidocaine in asphyxiated newborns under
                                  hypothermia.(34) A total of 22 patients given controlled hypothermia (rectal temperature
                                  33.5°C) were compared to 26 normothermic historical controls. The author reported a
                                  24% decrease in lidocaine clearance in those given hypothermia. Although no patient
                                  experienced cardiac effects, the authors used computer simulation to project a new dosing
                                  strategy. The only difference in dosing was a 0.5-hour reduction in continuous infusion
                                  after the loading dose. Although a multicenter study is being done to evaluate the phar-
                                  macokinetic and pharmacodynamic effects of hypothermia in newborns,(35) at this time
                                  there is no evidence in patients to support the need for this change in dosing.

