Brand names         Zyvox


Medication error    The ISMP reports confusion of Zyvox with Vioxx and Zovirax.(2)
potential

Contraindications   Contraindications: In those with a known hypersensitivity reaction to linezolid or its
and warnings        components(3)
                    Linezolid should not be used within 2 weeks of or in combination with a monoamine oxi-
                    dase inhibitor.(3)
                    Serotonin syndrome could occur in patients receiving concurrent serotonergic agents.(3)
                    Linezolid should not be administered to patients currently taking SSRIs, tricyclic antide-
                    pressants, buspirone, meperidine, or serotonin (5-HT1) receptor agonists unless no other
                    therapy is available.(3) (See Rare Adverse Effects in the Comments section.)
                    Unless patients are monitored for potential increases in blood pressure, linezolid should
                    not be given to those with uncontrolled hypertension, thyrotoxicosis, pheochromocytoma,
                    and/or patients with medicines that are direct or indirect acting sympathomimetics, vaso-
                    pressive agents or dopaminergic agents.(3)
                    Warnings: Transient myelosuppression has been reported, especially when therapy
                    lasts >2 weeks. CBC should be monitored more closely if (1) there is a known history of
                    myelosuppression; (2) other bone marrow suppressing drugs are given; or (3) used in
                    those with chronic infection who have received previous concomitant antibiotics.(3)
                    Linezolid use may cause superinfection and/or Clostridium difficile-associated diarrhea
                    (CDAD), which has been reported and may range in severity from mild diarrhea to fatal
                    colitis.(3) If CDAD is suspected or confirmed, appropriate fluid and electrolyte manage-
                    ment, protein supplementation, antibiotic treatment of C. difficile, and surgical evaluation
                    should be instituted as clinically indicated.(3)


Infusion-related    None reported
cautions

Dosage              Neonates
                        ≤7 days: 20 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr,(5,6) some sources recommend 30 mg/kg/day
                        divided q 8 hr if weight >2 kg(4) or >34 WGA(3,41)
                        >7 days: 30 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr.(3-6,41) Every 12-hour dosing may be used in
                        neonates <1000 g until 2 weeks of life.(4)
                    Infants and children (<12 yr): 30 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr usually up to 600 mg/
                    dose(3-5,9-11,41)
                    Adolescents (≥12 yr): 600 mg q 12 hr(3,4,12,41)
                    The manufacturer does not recommend the use of linezolid for empiric treatment of
                    pediatric CNS infections.(3) Limited data, as adult and pediatric case reports, suggest
                    that linezolid may be used to treat gram-positive CNS infections that failed to respond
                    to other treatment.(13-16)
                    Bacterial endocarditis (treatment): The American Heart Association guidelines state
                    linezolid may be effective for treatment of E. faecium endocarditis of native or prosthetic
                    valve that is resistant to penicillin, aminoglycosides, and vancomycin based on reports in
                    adults(17) 45 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr was reported in an infant with E. faecium endocar-
                    ditis.(18) Linezolid use has been reported for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus
                    endocarditis in a child(42) and an extremely low birth weight neonate.(19)
                    Central line infections: 2 mg/mL plus 100 units of heparin as an 8-hour catheter lock
                    used in combination with 30 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr of systemic linezolid has been
                    reported.(20) Another method instilled 3 mL of a linezolid solution (2 g/L) into the lumen
                    of the catheter for 16 hours without the use of heparin.(21)




