concentration

Preparation and         Stability: Do not freeze. The solution is a yellow color that can intensify with no affect
delivery                on potency.(3,23)
                        Compatibility: See Appendix D for PN compatibility information.(24)

IV push                 Not recommended(3)

Intermittent infusion   2 mg/mL over 30–120 minutes(3,23)

Continuous infusion     Use of 1200 mg/day or 30 mg/kg/day by continuous infusion has been reported.(25,26)

Other routes of         None
administration

Comments                Rare adverse events: Lactic acidosis has been reported in some patients that experi-
                        ence repeat nausea and vomiting.(3,27) Death has been reported; however, most patients’
                        serum lactate levels return to normal after discontinuation of linezolid.(28,29) Nonspecific
                        clinical symptoms make it difficult to detect; therefore, serum bicarbonate or lactate levels
                        should be monitored.(28,29)
                        Peripheral and optic neuropathy has occurred with treatment >28 days; however, it can
                        manifest within days to months. Linezolid should be discontinued if neuropathy is attrib-
                        uted to linezolid. Recovery is variable.(28) Auditory nerve neuropathy(30) and peripheral and
                        optic neuropathy has been reported in children.(31)
                        Serotonin syndrome has been reported in patients given concurrent serotonergic
                        agents.(3,28,32,33) A 4-year-old burn victim who received linezolid and fluoxetine became
                        agitated and developed myoclonic movement in limbs.(34) Symptoms resolved following
                        discontinuation of linezolid.
                        Brownish tooth and tongue discoloration has been reported in pediatric patients receiving
                        IV linezolid.(35,36) The discoloration is reversible with discontinuation of linezolid and with
                        manual removal by a dentist.(35,26)
                        Convulsions have been reported in pediatric patients receiving linezolid.(10) One report
                        described a patient with known epilepsy who experienced an increased frequency and wors-
                        ened control of seizures while on IV linezolid therapy that occurred again with rechallenge.(37)
                        In a 2-year-old boy receiving linezolid for treatment of infective endocarditis, progressive
                        fatigue and anemia developed 2 weeks post-linezolid initiation. Four days after discontinu-
                        ation of linezolid hemoglobin, hematocrit, and reticulocytes increased.(38)
                        Monitoring: Serum bicarbonate, serum transaminases, CBC and platelet, blood pressure,
                        signs of acidosis, and signs or symptoms of serotonin syndrome (e.g., hyperreflexia,
                        clonus, hyperthermia, etc.). Although transient thrombocytopenia may occur,(3,39) one
                        study found no significant difference in the platelet count for linezolid (n = 215) versus
                        vancomycin (n = 101) treated pediatric patients except those treated for ≥2 weeks.(40)
                        Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before



