Dosage adjustment       No adjustment in renal dysfunction due to lorazepam itself.(4,26) Patients receiving large
in organ dysfunction    doses for a prolonged time my accumulate one of the additives (i.e., propylene glycol)
                        and experience toxicity.(27,28) (See the Comments section and Appendix C.) Not metabo-
                        lized by cytochrome oxidation, thus liver disease should not affect metabolic clearance.(4)
                        Use cautiously in patients with combined renal and hepatic dysfunction.(4) Larger doses
                        may be required in patients on extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO).(33)
                        (See Delivery System Issues in the Preparation and Delivery section.)


Maximum dosage          4 mg/dose.(11,23) Doses as large as 0.4 mg/kg have been safely administered.(5,30) A
                        14-year-old with refractory seizures who had been on chronic lorazepam was given 0.25
                        mg/kg/dose to a cumulative dose of 186 mg (4.3 mg/kg) over 24 hours.(31) Patients on
                        benzodiazepines prior to admission may require larger doses for control.


Additives               Contains benzyl alcohol 2% and 40% propylene glycol.(32) (See Appendix C for specific
                        information about benzyl alcohol and propylene glycol toxicity.)


Suitable diluents       D5W, NS, SW(4)


Maximum                 Although 2 mg/mL and 4 mg/mL(23,25) have been given, the manufacturer recommends
concentration           dilution immediately prior to administration by using an equal volume of compatible
                        diluent (≤1 mg/mL).(4,32)


Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(32)
                        Stability: Store in refrigerator.(4) When kept at room temperature or used in emergency
                        transport vehicles, lorazepam became unstable within weeks (e.g., 60 days)(50,51) A follow-
                        up study by the same group noted significant degradation over 120 days.(56) If discoloration
                        is noted do not use.
                        Delivery system issues: Larger doses may be required in patients on extracorporeal
                        membrane oxygenation since 50% of a dose may be extracted by the PVC tubing and the
                        membrane oxygenator during bypass.(33)
                        Compatibility: See Appendix D or other appropriate resources for PN compatibility
                        information.
                        Photosensitivity: Protect commercial product from light until use.(4)


IV push                 Not to exceed 2 mg/min(4,25) or 0.05 mg/kg over 2–5 minutes(18)


Intermittent infusion   Generally, not administered by this method. Is given intermittently for treatment of
                        busulfan-associated seizures.


Continuous infusion     0.2 mg/mL has been administered in adults(34-38); however, little information is available
                        in pediatric patients.(13,14) Midazolam is preferred when continuous infusion is needed due
                        to propylene glycol accumulation. (See the Comments section.)


Other routes of         Midazolam is the benzodiazepine of choice for IM and prehospital administration.(39) (See
administration          the Midazolam monograph.) Undiluted lorazepam can be given deep into a muscle, but it
                        may cause pain at the injection site, a burning sensation, or observed redness.(4)




