LORazepam
Comments                             Rare adverse effects
                                           Propylene glycol toxicity: Continuous infusion of medications containing propylene
                                           glycol has been known to cause hyperosmolality, increased anion gap and osmolar
                                           gap metabolic acidosis (due to lactic acidosis), acute kidney injury, and sepsis-like
                                           syndrome.(27,28,40-44) This most frequently occurs in patients with renal dysfunction, who
                                           were receiving prolonged infusions of drugs that contain propylene glycol.(27,28) For
                                           this reason, midazolam is preferred when a continuous infusion of a benzodiazepine
                                           is required. (See the Comments section and the Midazolam monograph.)
                                           Movement disorders: Brief tonic–clonic seizures have occurred in patients with
                                           atypical absence (petit mal) status epileptics.(4) Seizure activity and myoclonus have
                                           also been reported following administration, especially in very low birth weight neo-
                                           nates.(4,45,46) The movements began a few minutes after a bolus injection and contin-
                                           ued for several hours.(40,45,46) Paradoxical reactions (i.e., anxiety, excitation, hostility,
                                           aggression, rage, etc.) have been reported in 10% to 30% of those <8 years of age.(4)
                                     Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources before combining any drug with
                                     lorazepam.
                                     Pharmacokinetic considerations: Mean total clearance normalized to bodyweight was
                                     reduced by 80% in neonates (≥37 weeks gestational age) compared to normal adults.(4)
                                     Terminal half-life was prolonged threefold, and volume of distribution was decreased by
                                     40% in neonates with asphyxia neonatorum compared to normal adults.(4)
                                     Pharmacodynamic considerations: Use in neonates is controversial because benzodiaz-
                                     epines may decrease blood pressure and cerebral blood flow velocity.(47) Some recommend
                                     the initial dose should be reduced by 50% in infants <2 months of age(13); regardless,
                                     lorazepam should be used cautiously in neonates.
                                     Antidote: Flumazenil, a specific benzodiazepine-receptor antagonist, is indicated for
                                     complete or partial reversal of benzodiazepine toxicity.(48,49) (See the Flumazenil mono-
                                     graph.) 0.01 mg/kg (maximum 0.2 mg/dose); if no effect in 1–2 minutes repeat dose
                                     q 2 min until patient responds or a total cumulative dose of 0.05 mg/kg or 1 mg is given.
                                     Rebound or withdrawal symptoms may occur following abrupt discontinuation or large
                                     decreases in dose.(4) Use caution when reducing dose or withdrawing therapy; decrease
                                     slowly and monitor for withdrawal symptoms.


