Contraindications      U.S. boxed warning: Antithymocyte globulin can cause anaphylaxis; clinicians should
and warnings           be prepared for this risk and monitor the patient during infusion(1)
                       Contraindications: Patients who have a severe systemic reaction including a generalized
                       rash, tachycardia, dyspnea, hypotension, or anaphylaxis to a previous Atgam infusion or
                       any other equine gamma globulin product.(1)


Infusion-related       The manufacturer recommends that patients be tested with an epicutaneous skin prick of
cautions               undiluted product; if no reaction within 10 minutes then an intradermal injection of 0.02
                       mL of 1:1000 dilution and a contralateral saline control should be placed. The appearance
                       of a wheal 3 mm or more larger than the saline site suggests sensitivity. In the event of a
                       positive test, alternative therapies should be considered and the risk benefit ratio weighed.
                       A negative test dose does not preclude the possibility of an anaphylactic reaction.(1)
                       Chills, fever, itching, and erythema are common.(1) Many centers have developed pre-
                       medication protocols to minimize these reactions.


