Preparation and                    Preparation: Add Atgam to an inverted container of the sterile diluent to avoid contact
delivery                           with the air; swirl or rotate to mix. Do not shake. The diluted infusate should be at room
                                   temperature prior to infusion.(1)
                                   Stability: Store refrigerated.(1)
                                   Compatibility: Addition to dextrose injection is not recommended because low-salt con-
                                   centrations can cause precipitation.(1)


IV push                            Not indicated(1)


Intermittent infusion              Over ≥4 hours into a high-flow vein.(1) A 0.2–1 micron in-line filter should be used.(1)
                                   Resuscitative equipment should be at the bedside during infusion.(1)


Continuous infusion                Not indicated


Other routes of                    Not indicated
administration

Comments                           Significant adverse effects: One study reported phlebitis occurred in 46% of children
                                   given the drug through a peripheral vein.(6) Another study, including six pediatric patients
                                   from 7–16 years of age, reported that infusion of Atgam (15 mg/kg) plus 1000 units
                                   heparin and 20 mg Solu-Cortef per 250–500 mL of ½NS over 4–6 hours was associated
                                   with a peripheral IV site complication rate of 3.2%.(25) Hence, this study concluded that
                                   Atgam could be safely administered via a peripheral vein.
                                   Thrombocytopenia,(6) neutropenia,(26) leukopenia,(1) and serum sickness(1,26) have occurred.
                                   This product is made using both human and equine blood components; thus, there is a
                                   risk of the transmission of infectious agents, including viruses and the prion that causes
                                   Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease.(1)
                                   Monitoring: Monitoring for bacterial and viral infections during therapy is important.(12)
                                   Antimicrobial prophylaxis may be included as a part of the treatment regimen.(3,12) Anti-
                                   biotics can be discontinued if fever persists <48 hours and cultures are negative.(27)
                                   Other: A desensitization protocol has been proposed for children with aplastic anemia in
                                   whom no other treatment is available.(29)


