Contraindications      U.S. boxed warning: Only physicians experienced in immunosuppressive therapy for
and warnings           renal transplant patients should use lymphocyte immune globulin–antithymocyte globulin
                       (rabbit).(1)
                       Serious immune-mediated reactions such as anaphylaxis may occur.(1)
                       Contraindications: Patients who have a history of allergy or anaphylaxis to rabbit proteins
                       or product excipients. Patients who have active infections that contraindicate additional
                       immunosuppression.(1)


Infusion-related       Fever, chills, dyspnea, hypotension or hypertension, rash, nausea/vomiting may occur dur-
cautions               ing the infusion. Premedication with corticosteroids, acetaminophen, and an antihistamine
                       or decreasing the infusion rate may lessen the incidence and duration of the reaction.(1)
                       Infusion site reactions include pain, swelling, and erythema.(1)


Dosage                 Transplant
                           Renal: 1.5 mg/kg intra-operatively followed by 1.5 mg/kg/day for 4–14 days(2) or
                           1.5–2 mg/kg/day for 5–15 days during induction therapy.(3,4) Others reported admin-
                           istering 0.5–2.5 mg/kg/day for up to 14 doses.(5) More recently, 5 mg/kg was given
                           q 24 hr for 2 doses followed by 2.5 mg/kg/day for 6–10 doses.(6) 1.5 mg/kg for 7–14
                           days is recommended for treatment of acute renal graft rejection.(1)
                           Liver and/or intestinal: 5 mg/kg divided in 2 doses and infused just prior to and
                           soon after transplantation(7-9)
                           Bone marrow: 2–2.5 mg/kg/day for 3–5 days before transplantation (cumulative
                           dose up to 10 mg/kg)(11,12)
                           Cardiac: 1–2.5 mg/kg/day for 1–7 days, or every other day for a total of 5 doses,
                           has been used as induction therapy(13,14)
                       Aplastic anemia: 2.5–3.5 mg/kg/day for 5 days either as initial treatment or following
                       relapse.(15-17) A study found horse lymphocyte immune globulin to be superior to rabbit
                       lymphocyte immune globulin at 3.5 mg/kg/day for 5 days for severe aplastic anemia.(19)


