  ($teddy_154_id$magnesium-sulfate$teddy_154_id$, $teddy_154_name$Magnesium Sulfate$teddy_154_name$, $teddy_154_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_154_source$, 2184514, $teddy_154$Magnesium Sulfate
Brand names         Generics, concentrations of 50%, 8%, 4%, 2%, and 1% are available.


Medication error    ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error.(1)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
                    USP reports that magnesium sulfate has been confused with morphine sulfate. No harm
                    resulted.(2)


Contraindications   Contraindicated in patients with heart block or with myocardial damage.(3)
and warnings

Infusion-related    Magnesium is available in several different salt forms, and magnesium sulfate is available
cautions            in multiple concentrations. Choose products carefully.


Dosage              1 g magnesium sulfate = 8.12 mEq magnesium = 98.6 mg elemental magnesium =
                    4.06 mmol magnesium(4)
                    Hypomagnesemia
                        Neonates: 25–50 mg magnesium sulfate/kg q 8–12 hr for 2 or 3 doses.(4-6) (See the
                        Comments section.)
                        Infants and children: 25–50 mg magnesium sulfate/kg (maximum 2 g/dose) q 6 hr
                        for 2 or 3 doses.(4) One 9-year-old, 19-kg child receiving a 21-day course of tobramycin
                        developed tetany, hypomagnesemia (serum magnesium 1 mg/dL), and hypocalcemia
                        and was treated with 950 mg magnesium sulfate and 2 g calcium gluconate and had
                        symptom improvement within hours.(7)
                    PALS (torsades de pointes, resuscitation with hypomagnesemia): 25–50 mg
                    magnesium sulfate/kg up to a maximum of 2 g. A more rapid infusion rate (bolus over
                    several minutes) may be needed in pulseless torsades de pointes.(8,9)
                    Asthma: 25–75 mg magnesium sulfate/kg (NHLBI Guidelines) or 40 mg magnesium
                    sulfate/kg (British Thoracic Society Guidelines) up to a maximum of 2 g infused over
                    15–30 minutes for patients refractory to standard initial asthma therapy.(8,11-18,29) A high-
                    dose regimen targeting serum magnesium concentrations consistent with smooth muscle
                    relaxation in 18 patients aged 2–18 years old demonstrated clinical feasibility and safety.
                    The regimen was as follows: loading dose over 30–45 minutes (weight <30 kg, 75 mg/kg;
                    weight >30 kg, 50 mg/kg; no max dose; however, doses were based on ideal body weight
                    if BMI >30 kg/m²) followed by a continuous infusion of 40 mg/kg/hr × 4 hr.(30)
                    Epilepsy: Daily magnesium sulfate 250 mg/kg for 3 weeks as an adjunct to adrenocor-
                    ticotropic hormone was studied in 19 patients with infantile spasms.(19)
                    Following cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB): 25–50 mg magnesium sulfate/kg up to
                    a maximum up 2 g.(20,21) In one study, the development of arrhythmia was compared in
                    those given 30 mg magnesium sulfate/kg (n = 13) or saline control (n = 15) after CPB.(20)
                    If serum magnesium concentration decreased to <1.6 mg/dL at any time during the ICU
                    stay, 10 mg/kg was given. Junctional ectopic tachycardia occurred in 27% of the saline
                    control group (n = 15) compared to none of the treatment group.
                    Perinatal/birth asphyxia in term newborns with hypoxic ischemic encephalopa-
                    thy: 250 mg magnesium sulfate/kg over 1 hour q 24 hr for 3 doses(22)
                    Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn (PPHN): 200 mg magnesium
                    sulfate/kg over 30 minutes as an initial dose(23-25) followed by an infusion of 20–50,(23)
                    20–100,(25) or 50–150(24) mg magnesium sulfate/kg/hr. Seven of nine infants who received
                    200 mg magnesium sulfate/kg followed by 20–50 mg/kg/hr for up to 8 hours survived.(23)
                    Predicted mortality in these patients was 100%. These high-dose magnesium sulfate regi-
                    mens are studied primarily in developing countries where advanced treatments for PPHN
                    such as inhaled nitric oxide, extracorporeal membrane oxygenation, or high-frequency
                    oscillatory ventilation are not available.(24,25)




