Brand names            Osmitrol, generic


Medication error       Osmitrol may be confused with esmolol.
potential

Contraindications      Contraindications: Patients with a known hypersensitivity to mannitol or its ingredients
and warnings           should not receive the medication. Should not be used in well-established anuria due to
                       severe renal disease, severe pulmonary congestion or frank pulmonary edema, active
                       intracranial bleeding except during craniotomy, severe dehydration, and progressive renal
                       dysfunction. If progressive heart failure or pulmonary congestion develops after starting
                       mannitol the drug should be discontinued.(1)
                       Warnings: Excessive fluid and electrolyte losses can occur; serum electrolytes, especially
                       sodium and potassium, should be monitored.(1)


Infusion-related       The solution should be at body temperature prior to infusion. Rapid administration can
cautions               cause hypotension, hyperosmolality, and elevations in intracranial pressure (ICP).(2) Osmo-
                       lality of 25% mannitol is 1375 mOsmol/L; hence, significant tissue damage may occur
                       following extravasation.(1) For this reason, make sure the IV catheter is securely placed
                       and functional before infusion. (See Appendix E for treatment information.)


Dosage                 Acute renal failure (oliguria)
                           Assessment of renal function: The manufacturer recommends that patients with
                           marked oliguria or suspected renal insufficiency be given a test dose of mannitol before
                           beginning therapy. 0.2 g/kg to a maximum of 12.5 g over 3–5 minutes to produce a
                           urine output of ≥1 mL/kg/hr or 30–50 mL/hr in adults for 1–3 hours.(1,3) This may be
                           repeated after 2 hours if the response is inadequate.
                           Prevention of renal failure: Mannitol is not recommended for the prevention of
                           acute renal failure.(6)
                           Treatment: 0.25–2 g/kg/dose over 2–6 hours.(1,4,5) Should not be repeated if oliguria
                           persists.(1)
                       Cerebral edema/elevated ICP: Generally used short-term for diagnostic procedures or
                       for prolonged therapy for elevation ICP.(7) For use before a procedure to prevent spikes in
                       ICP, give 0.25–1 g/kg over 5–15 minutes.(2,3,8-12) The AAP recommends 0.25–0.5 g/kg over
                       15 minutes for treatment an acute increase in ICP.(2) There is little information regarding
                       the repeated and regular use of mannitol over several days.(7) There is also a lack of evi-
                       dence to support or refute the use of mannitol in severe traumatic brain injury.(13) (See
                       the Comments section.)
                       Nephrotic syndrome: Three children (ages 4, 7, and 9 years) with nephrotic syndrome
                       and diuretic-resistant edema responded to furosemide (2 mg/kg) and 5 mL/kg/day of
                       20% mannitol infused over 1 hour for 5–7 days.(9)


Dosage adjustment      Mannitol should not be used in patients with severe renal disease and well-established
in organ dysfunction   anuria.(1)


Maximum dosage         Although 2.5 g/kg has been used in adults,(8) there may be no therapeutic advantage to
                       giving doses larger than 1 g/kg.(8)


Additives              None reported


Suitable diluents      D5W, NS, D5NS, LR(1)


Maximum                25% solution(1,14)
