                                                                                                                
                                                                                                                       Mannitol
Preparation and                   Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                          before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                                  other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                                  and extended stability.(14)
                                  Stability: Mannitol ≥15% solution may crystallize at lower temperatures.(14) To avoid
                                  crystal formation, store the solutions in a warming chamber. If crystals are present,
                                  warm the vial using a dry heat cabinet, or warm water bath and vigorously shake the
                                  vial. Explosions of glass ampuls has occurred when mannitol crystals were resolubilized
                                  using a microwave.(14)
                                  Supersaturated mannitol solutions in PVC bags may cause a heavy white flocculant pre-
                                  cipitate to develop within minutes.(14) This may also occur when mannitol comes in contact
                                  with other plastics or rough glass surfaces. Although heating the bag may temporarily
                                  dissolve the crystals, they may quickly reappear.(1)
                                  Delivery system issues: Solutions containing ≥20% mannitol should be infused through
                                  a 0.5 micron in-line filter.(1,14)
                                  Compatibility: See Appendix D or other appropriate resources for PN compatibility
                                  information.


IV push                           3–5 minutes in oliguria(1)


Intermittent infusion             Over 15–60 minutes for cerebral edema, elevated ICP, or preoperative in neurosurgery(2,3,8-12)


Continuous infusion               Sixty patients from age 1–73 years were given a continuous infusion over 6–100 hours
                                  (total dose 2–20 g/kg).(8) Large doses of mannitol may accumulate in the brain to cause
                                  a rebound increase in ICP. For this reason intermittent infusion is preferred.


Other routes of                   None reported
administration

Comments                          Adverse effects: Excessive diuresis may cause dehydration and significant changes in
                                  potassium and sodium. Mannitol may also increases intravascular volume and worsen
                                  hyponatremia.(1)
                                  Mannitol increases the intravascular volume and may exacerbate coronary heart failure
                                  and pulmonary edema; hence, furosemide should be used for increased ICP in patients
                                  with preexisting cardiac disease.(15)
                                  A 16-year-old and a 28-year-old with increased ICP and normal renal function developed revers-
                                  ible acute renal failure after receiving 2 and 4 days of doses ranging from 50–900 g/day.(16)
                                  Monitoring: Serum electrolytes should be monitored as the resultant diuresis can lead to
                                  significant sodium and potassium losses. Serum creatinine and BUN should also be moni-
                                  tored.(1) Serum osmolality should be maintained between 300 and 320 mOsm/kg when
                                  mannitol is used for elevated ICP. Maintaining serum osmolality below 300 mOsm/kg may
                                  minimize the risk of renal failure.(1)
                                  Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                                  combining any drug with mannitol. Use with caution is patients receiving other medica-
                                  tions known to be nephrotoxic.


