Brand names            Demerol, generic


Medication error       High-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm if
potential              used in error.(1)


Contraindications      Contraindications: Those with hypersensitivity to meperidine and those on a monoamine
and warnings           oxidase (MAO) inhibitor or who have recently received a MAO inhibitor.(2)
                       Warnings: Use with caution in those receiving other CNS depressants. Meperidine may
                       elevate cerebrospinal fluid pressure and, if possible, an alternative agent should be used
                       in patients with head injury.(2)


Infusion-related       For IV infusion, the patient should be lying down.(2) Rapid injection may cause severe
cautions               respiratory depression, apnea, hypotension, peripheral circulatory collapse, and cardiac
                       arrest. For this use, the patient should be in a facility with appropriate supportive mea-
                       sures and a narcotic antagonist must be available.
                       Anaphylaxis has been reported.(3)


Dosage                 Because of the potential for adverse effects, meperidine has largely been replaced by other
                       analgesics.(4-6) Neonates have decreased elimination placing them at risk for accumulation
                       of drug and increased adverse effects (e.g., respiratory depression). (See Pharmacokinet-
                       ics in the Comments section.) If used, dose should be titrated to desired analgesic effect.
                       DPT cocktail: Demerol, Phenergan, Thorazine (DPT) cocktail has been used to sedate
                       children,(7-10) safer and more effective agents are available,(7-11) and this combination is
                       no longer recommended.(10,13)
                       Analgesia: 0.5–2 mg/kg given IM, IV or sub-Q q 3–4 hr as needed.(2,12,14,15) Some recom-
                       mend dosing in children that is based on weight.(28) In children <50, kg give 0.8–1 mg/
                       kg q 2–3 hr and if >50, kg give 50–75 mg q 2–3 hr.
                       In severe sickle cell pain crisis give a loading dose of 0.3–1 mg/kg followed by con-
                       tinuous infusion, beginning at 0.3 mg/kg/hr and titrating to desired response up to
                       0.7 mg/kg/hr.(16) (See the Comments section.)
                       Preoperative sedation: 0.25–1.5 mg/kg (usually IM or sub-Q) and ≤100 mg given
                       30–90 minutes prior to procedure.(11,17,19) 2 mg/kg and ≤100 mg have been used IV.(19)


Dosage adjustment      Those with renal and hepatic disease are at risk for accumulation of meperidine and
in organ dysfunction   normeperidine (an active metabolite).(21) (See the Comments section.) If CrCl is 10–50,
                       mL/min/1.73 m2, give 75% of the usual dose, and if CrCl is <10 mL/min/1.73 m2, give
                       50% of the usual dose.(20) Avoid repeated or large doses in patients with renal impair-
                       ment.(2) The dose or frequency of dosing should be decreased in hepatic disease.(2)


Maximum dosage         2 mg/kg(15,16,18,19) and ≤100 mg/dose.(2,19) A continuous infusion of 1.5 mg/kg/hr has been
                       used in children with sickle-cell disease.(16) (See the Comments section.)


Additives              Products may contain sodium metabisulfite and/or phenol and m-cresol as preservatives.(22)
                       (See Appendix C for specific information about adverse effects.)


Suitable diluents      D-LR, D-R, D-S, D5W, D10W, LR, NS, or ½NS(2,22)


Maximum                10 mg/mL for IV injection. 1 mg/mL for IV infusion.(2)
