                                                                                                    Meperidine HCl
Preparation and                    Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                           before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                                   other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                                   and extended stability.(22)
                                   Storage: 20°C to 25°C(22)
                                   Compatibility: Meperidine is incompatible with heparin(22,23) and barbiturates.(2,22) To
                                   avoid precipitation with heparin, flush heparinized IV catheters with NS before and after
                                   meperidine injection.(22,23)
                                   See Appendix D and other appropriate resources for PN compatibility information.


IV push                            Not indicated. Rapid injection increases risk for adverse reactions (e.g., respiratory depres-
                                   sion, apnea, hypotension, cardiac arrest).(2) One group reported that five of 154 children
                                   undergoing endoscopy required supplemental oxygen for a short time after receiving
                                   2 mg/kg infused over 1–2 minutes.(19)


Intermittent infusion              Over >5 minutes(2)


Continuous infusion                May be given by continuous infusion.(2,26)


Other routes of                    Usually given IV or IM. Sub-Q can be used occasionally.(2,11,17) Local tissue irritation and
administration                     induration are more common with sub-Q administration.(2)


Comments                           Adverse effects
                                        Neurological: The meperidine metabolite, normeperidine, may cause a variety of CNS
                                        effects including excitability, twitches, tremors, or seizures.(24,25) When pain manage-
                                        ment was inadequate with morphine, a 32-month-old febrile and hyponatremic boy with
                                        90% body surface area (BSA) burns was changed to a meperidine infusion.(26) One hour
                                        after the dose had been gradually increased to 25 mg/kg/hr, he began having inter-
                                        mittent seizures and became comatose. The meperidine was stopped, phenobarbital
                                        was started, and the seizures stopped after 6 hours. Although there were confounding
                                        factors, it was felt that the seizures were due to the large dose of meperidine.
                                        Dyskinesias: A 6-week-old infant received 2 IV doses of 1 mg/kg during rigid bron-
                                        choscopy and developed acute orofacial dyskinesias (e.g., tongue thrusting, pursing
                                        or puckering, facial grimacing) that were unresponsive to 0.1 mg/kg of naloxone.(27)
                                        This lasted about 36 hours; he had no residual effects.
                                   Monitoring: Pain relief, blood pressure, oxygen saturation, and mental status.(15)
                                   Pharmacokinetics: The pharmacokinetics of meperidine is highly variable in neonates
                                   and infants. Half-life ranged from 3.3–59.4 hours in 21 infants.(12) Clearance increased
                                   with increasing BSA; however, half-life was not different among term infants <1 week
                                   of age, term infants >3 weeks of age (26–150 days of age), and preterm neonates
                                   (3.6–65 days of age).
                                   Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                                   combining any drug with meperidine.


