Brand names            Merrem


Medication error       None reported
potential

Contraindications      Contraindications: In patients with a hypersensitivity to meropenem or to other car-
and warnings           bapenems or in patients who have had anaphylactic reactions to beta-lactams.(2)
                       Warnings
                           Allergic reactions: Serious hypersensitivity and occasionally fatal anaphylactic reac-
                           tions have been reported.(2) These are more likely in patients who are sensitive to
                           multiple allergens or other beta-lactams.(2)
                           Seizures: Neurotoxicity of the carbapenem antibiotics has been reported.(2) Seizures
                           occur most commonly in patients with renal impairment and/or underlying neurologic
                           disorders.(2) (See Rare Adverse Effects in the Comments section.)
                           Superinfection: Meropenem use may cause superinfection and/or Clostridium difficile-
                           associated diarrhea, which has been reported and may range in severity from mild
                           diarrhea to fatal colitis.(2) Antibiotic use that is not for the treatment of C. difficile may
                           need to be discontinued.(2)


Infusion-related       If a decision is made to give meropenem to a patient with known penicillin, cephalosporin
cautions               or beta-lactam hypersensitivity, the patient should be closely observed for allergenicity.(2)
                       May cause inflammation or phlebitis at the injection site.(2)


Dosage                 Neonates
                           Based on weeks gestational age (WGA) or weight and PNA for non-CNS
                           infections(2,5,8,26):

                                               ≤2 kg OR <32 WGA                    >2 kg OR ≥32 WGA
                           <14 Days Old        40 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr        60 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr
                           ≥14 Days Old        60 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr         90 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr

                           Based on postnatal age (PNA)
                                ≤7 days: 40 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr(4)
                                >7 days: 30–40 mg/kg/day divided q 8–12 hr(3,4)
                       A 15-day-old premature neonate (33 WGA; 1.59 kg) was reported to receive 120 mg/kg/day
                       divided q 8 hr for 8 weeks as treatment for a brain abscess.(6)
                       Infants and children
                           Complicated skin and skin structure infection: 30 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr up
                           to 1.5 g/day(2)
                           Non-CNS infection: 30–60 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr up to 6 g/day(2,8)
