Comments                           Rare adverse effects: Neurotoxicity of the carbapenem antibiotics has been reported.(22,23)
                                   Seizures appear to be related to an underlying CNS disorder or impaired renal function.(2,23)
                                   If seizures occur, the need for meropenem should be considered and the dosage should
                                   be assessed.(2)
                                   Thrombocytopenia has been reported in patients with renal dysfunction who are receiving
                                   meropenem.(2)
                                   An increase in alkaline phosphatase occurred in cystic fibrosis patients.(24)
                                   Drug interactions: Carbapenems may reduce serum concentrations of valproic acid, which
                                   can result in a loss of seizure control.(2,25) Serum valproic acid concentrations should be
                                   monitored frequently after beginning or increasing the dose of a carbapenem. Alternative
                                   antibiotics should be considered in patients with seizures well controlled with valproic acid.(2)
                                   Other: Although there is a theoretical allergic cross-reactivity of carbapenems with peni-
                                   cillins and cephalosporins studies have found this to be very rare.(30,31)


