Brand names         Folex, generic

Medication error    ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error.(1)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
                    USP reports that methotrexate (MTX) has been confused with methylPREDNISolone
                    sodium succinate, metolazone, metroNIDAZOLE, mercaptopurine, and mitoxantrone;
                    no patient harm resulted. USP also reports that MTX has been confused with minoxidil;
                    patient harm resulted.(2)
                    ISMP reports that Folex has been confused with Foltx.(3)

Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Administer under the supervision of a physician with experience
and warnings        in antimetabolite therapy. Because of the toxic and potentially life-threatening toxicity
                    associated with MTX, it should only be used in life-threatening neoplastic disease or in
                    patients with severe, recalcitrant psoriasis or rheumatoid arthritis unresponsive to other
                    therapies.(4)
                    MTX may cause serious neoplastic disease (malignant lymphoma) and increased risk of
                    death. It has also been associated with tumor lysis syndrome and serious immune sup-
                    pression, bone marrow suppression, and liver, lung, and kidney toxicities. May cause fetal
                    death or congenital abnormalities when given to pregnant women. MTX also associated with
                    life-threatening diarrhea/stomatitis, dermatologic reactions, and opportunistic infections.(4)
                    Use caution when administering MTX concurrently with other hepatotoxic and nephrotoxic
                    agents. Concurrent use with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs); may cause
                    serious adverse events (bone marrow suppression, aplastic anemia, and GI toxicity).(4)
                    Use with radiotherapy may increase risk of soft tissue necrosis and osteonecrosis.(4)
                    MTX elimination is decreased in patients with renal insufficiency,(5) ascites, or pleural effu-
                    sions; use cautiously with increased monitoring for toxicity in these patients.(4)
                    High-dose regimens for osteosarcoma require considerable caution. (See the Infusion-
                    Related Cautions section.) Other high-dose therapies should be considered investigational.(4)
                    MTX formulations containing preservatives must be not be used intrathecally or as high-
                    dose therapy.(4)
                    Use caution when administering high-dose MTX to patients receiving proton pump inhibitors
                    (PPIs). Use of PPIs with MTX may lead to elevated serum levels of MTX and an increased
                    risk of MTX toxicity.(4)
                    Contraindications: Hypersensitivity to MTX or any component of the formulation, severe
                    renal or hepatic impairment, preexisting profound bone marrow suppression, alcoholic
                    liver disease, AIDS, and preexisting blood dyscrasias.(4)
                    Other warnings: Neurotoxicity leading to seizures has been reported in pediatric acute
                    lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) patients.(4)

Infusion-related    Aggressive hydration should accompany administration. (See Significant Adverse Effects
cautions            in the Comments section.)
                    Two patients experienced anaphylactoid reactions to high-dose MTX and BCG injections
                    for osteosarcoma.(5)

Dosage              Consult protocol for complete information regarding MTX and leucovorin dosages, hydra-
                    tion, and alkalinization.
                    MTX is used for a variety of neoplastic disease states.
                    Doses >500 mg/m2 require leucovorin rescue.




