Brand names            Aldomet, generic


Medication error       Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Aldomet has been confused with Aldactone, and
potential              methyldopa has been confused with levadopa, but no patient harm resulted.


Contraindications      Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to methyldopa or to any component of this
and warnings           product, including sulfites and therapy with monoamine oxidase inhibitors.(2) Active hepatic
                       disease (hepatitis, cirrhosis), liver disorders previously associated with methyldopa,
                       hypersensitivity to methyldopa or any of its components, including sulfites.(2)
                       Warnings: Methyldopa therapy has been associated with a positive Coombs test,
                       hemolytic anemia, and liver disorders; these may be fatal if not identified and managed
                       appropriately.(2) (See the Comments section.)


Infusion-related       The injectable product contains sodium bisulfite and anaphylaxis may occur.(2) (See the
cautions               Additives section.) A paradoxical pressor response has been reported if infused too rapidly.(4)


Dosage                 No longer used as a first- or second-line agent in the treatment of acute hypertension.(3,9)
                       A decrease in blood pressure generally occurs within 4–6 hours and lasts 10–16 hours.(2)
                       Hypertensive crisis
                           Initial dose: 2–4 mg/kg/dose; if no effect within 4–6 hours, double the dose.(4)
                           Maintenance dose: 5–50 mg/kg/day (usually 20–40 mg/kg/day) divided q 6 hr(3,5)
                       In hypertensive emergencies, blood pressure should be frequently monitored to ensure
                       that it does not decrease too quickly.(6) One group recommended that the blood pressure
                       decrease by one third of the desired total blood pressure decrease within 6 hours, a fur-
                       ther one-third decrease within the next 24–36 hours, with the final one-third decrease
                       achieved over the next 48–72 hours.(7)


Dosage adjustment      Active metabolite may accumulate in uremia. Adjust dosage in patients with renal dys-
in organ dysfunction   function.(8) If CrCl is 10–50 mL/min, administer normal dose q 8–12 hr; if CrCl is <10
                       mL/min, give normal dose q 12–24 hr.(8)


Maximum dosage         65 mg/kg/day,(2) not to exceed 3 g/day.(2) The maximum adult dose recommended by the
                       manufacturer is 1 g q 6 hr.(2)


Additives              Each mL contains 3.2 mg sodium bisulfite and 1.5 mg methylparaben and 0.2 mg propyl-
                       paraben.(2) (See Appendix C for more specific information about potential adverse effects
                       of sulfites and parabens.)


Suitable diluents      D5W(2,10)


Maximum                10 mg/mL(2,10)
concentration

Preparation and        Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery               before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                       other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                       and extended stability.(10)
                       Stability: Store intact vials at controlled room temperature and protect from freezing.(10)
                       Excursions permitted to 15°C to 30°C (59°C to 86°F).(2) Oxidation of the catechol ring
                       causes inactivation and dark-colored solution.(10) The rate of oxidation increases with
                       increasing oxygen supply, increasing pH, and decreasing drug concentration.(10)
                       Compatibility: For PN compatibility information, please see Appendix D and other appro-
                       priate resources.




