  ($teddy_160_id$methylprednisolone-sodium-succinate$teddy_160_id$, $teddy_160_name$MethylPREDNISolone Sodium Succinate$teddy_160_name$, $teddy_160_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_160_source$, 2264309, $teddy_160$MethylPREDNISolone Sodium Succinate
Brand names         A-METHAPRED, Solu-Medrol, generic


Medication error    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
potential
                    ISMP reports that Solu-Medrol has been confused with Depo-Medrol and Solu-Cortef.(1)
                    USP reports that Solu-Medrol has been confused with salmeterol and Solu-Cortef and no
                    patient harm occurred. Solu-Medrol has been confused with Depo-Medrol and patient
                    harm resulted.(2)
                    USP reports that methylPREDNISolone sodium succinate has been confused with hydrocorti-
                    sone sodium succinate, methotrexate, metoclopramide, metroNIDAZOLE, and prednisolone
                    sodium phosphate and no patient harm occurred. MethylPREDNISolone sodium succinate
                    has been confused with methylPREDNISolone acetate and patient harm resulted.(2)


Contraindications   Contraindications: Neonates (products with benzyl alcohol), patients with systemic
and warnings        fungal infections, and patients with hypersensitivity to any component in the product.
                    Intramuscular preparations are contraindicated for idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura.
                    Intrathecal administration is contraindicated.(3)
                    Live virus (live attenuated influenza; measles, mumps, and rubella [MMR]; oral polio;
                    varicella; rotavirus, smallpox; zoster) vaccinations should not be given during treatment
                    with immunosuppressive doses of glucocorticoids.(4) (See Drug Interactions in the Com-
                    ments section.)
                    Warnings: Anaphylactoid reactions have been reported, and those with a prior history
                    of such reactions require appropriate precautions.(3,5-8)
                    Patients on chronic steroid therapy may require increased doses during stress.(3)
                    Supraphysiologic doses of corticosteroids may result in suppressed pituitary-adrenal func-
                    tion, so therapy of more than a few days should be decreased gradually.(3,9,10)
                    Immunosuppression and an increased risk for infection are possible during steroid
                    therapy.(3)


Infusion-related    Large doses should be infused slowly (over at least 30 minutes or 1 hour). Cardiac
cautions            arrhythmias and cardiac arrest has been reported within minutes or hours of infusion in
                    adults receiving ≥500 mg over <10 minutes.(11-13) Do not administer methylprednisolone
                    acetate intravenously.(40)


Dosage              Doses are based on severity of disease and patient response. The lowest dose that results
                    in the desired effect should be used.(3)
                    Dosing adjustment for obesity: In adult otherwise healthy obese volunteers, volume
                    of distribution at steady state (Vss) was closely associated with ideal body weight, and
                    clearance was reduced by 40% compared to nonobese adults. Suggested dosing altera-
                    tions included dosing based on ideal body weight, with consideration for lengthening
                    dosing intervals.(14)
                    Anti-inflammatory/immunosuppressive therapy: 0.11–1.6 mg/kg/day (or 3.2–48
                    mg/m²/day) divided q 6–8 hr(3)
                        “Pulse” or high-dose anti-inflammatory therapy: 10–30 mg/kg/day either
                        daily for 3–6 days or every other day for 3–6 doses(15-20) or 600 mg/m2/day for
                        3 days.(21) 30 mg/kg as a single intraoperative dose has been used in neonatal and
                        pediatric cardiac surgery to reduce the postsurgical inflammatory response.(60,61)
                    Asthma exacerbations (ED or hospital treatment regimens): Continue therapy until
                    peak expiratory flow is 70% of predicted or personal best.(22)
                        <12 years old: 1–2 mg/kg/day (max 60 mg/day) divided q 12–24 hr
                        ≥12 years old: 40–80 mg/day divided q 12–24 hr




