Dosage (cont.)         Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura: 30 mg/kg/dose daily for 3 days(23); 20 mg/
                       kg/dose daily for 3 days has been used in combination with IVIG 0.4 g/kg/dose daily on
                       the first 2 days of therapy.(62)
                       Pneumocystis carinii pneumonia
                           Infants and children: 4–8 mg/kg/day divided q 6–12 hr for 5–7 days followed by
                           oral prednisone.(24-26) CDC/NIH/IDSA/AAP guidelines recommend the following regimen
                           as an adjunct to antibacterials in children infected with or exposed to HIV: 1 mg/kg/
                           dose q 6 hr (days 1–7), 1 mg/kg/dose q 12 hr (days 8–9), 0.5 mg/kg/dose q 12 hr
                           (days 10–11), 1 mg/kg/dose every day (days 12–16).(27)
                           Adolescents and adults: CDC/NIH/IDSA guidelines recommend the following regimen
                           as an adjunct to antibacterials in patients infected with or exposed to HIV: 30 mg q
                           12 hr (days 1–5), 30 mg every day (days 6–10), and 15 mg every day (days 11–21).(28,29)
                       Spinal cord injury: A loading dose of 30 mg/kg over 15 minutes followed in 45 minutes
                       by a continuous infusion of 5.4 mg/kg/hr × 23 hours (if started within 3 hours of injury)
                       or × 48 hours (if started 3–8 hours after injury).(30,31) Although the National Acute Spinal
                       Cord Injury trial did not include any patients <13 years old,(31) some pediatric clinicians
                       have adopted this practice,(32,33) while others have abandoned the practice.(34,35) The adult
                       Consortium for Spinal Cord Medicine no longer recommends steroids for acute spinal cord
                       injury due to the lack of definitive evidence showing efficacy and the risk of significant
                       adverse events.(36)
                       Other uses
                           Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis: 10–30 mg/kg/day(37,38) (1 g in patients
                           >30 kg)(38) for 3–5 consecutive days, usually followed by an oral steroid taper for
                           4–6 weeks
                           Anaphylaxis: 1 mg/kg (max 50 mg in children, max 100 mg in adults)(63)
                           ARDS (regimen from pediatric pilot study in ARDS): 2 mg/kg over 15 minutes
                           followed by continuous infusion as follows: 1 mg/kg/day on days 1–7, 0.5 mg/kg/day
                           on days 8–10, 0.25 mg/kg/day on days 11–12, and 0.125 mg/kg/day on days 13–14.
                           If patient is extubated between days 1–7, regimen is advanced to day 8 dosing then
                           tapered per remainder of protocol. Infusion is stopped on day 14 or sooner if patient
                           is discharged from hospital.(64)
                           Chronic interstitial lung disease: 300 mg/m2/day for 3 days, repeated q 4–6 wk(39)
                           Contrast reactions(65)
                               Emergency premedication (adults): 40 mg IV q 4 hr until contrast study
                               Reaction rebound prevention: 1 mg/kg (max 40 mg)
                           Esophageal burns (grade IIb): From ingestion of corrosive material, 1 g/1.73 m²
                           daily for 3 days reduced esophageal stricture formation(66)
                           Lupus nephritis: 30 mg/kg every other day for 6 doses(40)
                           Periocular hemangiomas of infancy: 2 mg/kg/dose q 12 hr for 2 days, then transi-
                           tioned to an oral corticosteroid taper resulted in rapid hemangioma shrinkage and visual
                           improvement in 15 infants.(41) Oral prednisolone (n = 10) was as effective as pulse
                           high-dose IV methylprednisolone (n = 10) in problematic infantile hemangiomas.(42)
                           Pulmonary hemorrhage: 2–4 mg/kg/day(43)
                           Ventricular tachycardia/silent lymphocytic myocarditis: 30 mg/kg/day for
                           3 days(44)


Dosage adjustment      Those with cirrhosis of the liver or who are hypothyroid may have an exaggerated
in organ dysfunction   response.(3) Dosing adjustment is not necessary for renal dysfunction.(45)




