administration

Comments                Other/Kawasaki disease: An early study found 30 mg/kg methylprednisolone as an
                        initial treatment of Kawasaki disease (in combination with IV immunoglobulin [IVIG] and
                        aspirin) resulted in faster resolution of fever, improvement in inflammatory markers, and
                        shorter hospital stay.(47) A randomized, multicenter trial of 198 patients found no benefit
                        of adding methylprednisolone to IVIG as primary treatment in all patients diagnosed with
                        Kawasaki disease, but in patients that required retreatment with IVIG, coronary artery
                        outcomes were better in those who had received methylprednisolone.(48) A pilot study
                        found that 48 patients predicted to have refractory Kawasaki disease responded favorably
                        to the addition of 30 mg/kg methylprednisolone to the standard IVIG and aspirin regi-
                        men (86.4% remained afebrile for 36 hours after treatment versus 23.1% who received
                        standard therapy without methylprednisolone, p <.001).(49) A case report described a
                        child with Kawasaki disease who had a severe rash similar to Stevens-Johnson syndrome
                        (without mucosal involvement) who failed to respond to three IVIG treatments but was
                        felt to respond after three methylprednisolone doses of 30 mg/kg.(50)
                        Adverse effects: Intractable hiccups, palpitations, and anaphylaxis have been
                        reported.(6,8,51) Additionally, atrial fibrillation,(52) seizures, transient blindness,(53) and fatal
                        varicella(54) have occurred after methylprednisolone administration.
                        Children with a history of drug-induced cutaneous reactions are more likely to have an
                        adverse reaction to IV corticosteroids.(55)
                        Corticosteroid-induced bronchospasm with methylprednisolone in an adult with severe
                        asthma was reported. The author recommended considering corticosteroids as a contrib-
                        uting factor in asthmatics who do not improve on IV steroids (especially if aspirin allergy
                        is also present). Skin testing may help guide safe alternative corticosteroids.(56)
                        A pediatric study looking at effects of short-term (3 days) methylprednisolone 2 mg/
                        kg/day on bone metabolism in infants and children reported significant, but reversible,



                                                                                                                          
                        inhibition of bone formation markers.(57)



602
                        MethylPREDNISolone Sodium Succinate
Comments (cont.)                    A 14-year-old male with nephrotic syndrome being treated with oral prednisolone
                                    developed Stevens-Johnson syndrome. The boy was changed to IV methylprednisolone
                                    20 mg/kg/dose every other day. By day 2 of treatment, fever subsided and by the fifth
                                    day the bullae began to subside.(58)
                                    Drug interactions: Drugs that enhance hepatic clearance (e.g., phenytoin, phenobarbi-
                                    tal, carbamazepine, rifampin) may decrease blood levels and lessen physiologic activity
                                    of methylprednisolone.(3)
                                    Live virus vaccines may be given 1 month after ending a ≥2-week course of high-dose
                                    systemic corticosteroids (2 mg/kg/day of prednisone or its equivalent; or 20 mg/day if
                                    >10 kg.(59)


