  ($teddy_161_id$metoclopramide-hcl$teddy_161_id$, $teddy_161_name$Metoclopramide HCl$teddy_161_name$, $teddy_161_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_161_source$, 2291703, $teddy_161$Metoclopramide HCl
Brand names         Reglan, generic


Medication error    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Metoclopramide has been confused with methocar-
potential           bamol, methylPREDNISolone sodium succinate, metolazone, and metoprolol tartrate.
                    Confusion with metroNIDAZOLE resulted in patient harm. Reglan has been confused
                    with Regitine, Regonol, Relafen, Renagel, Requip, and Robaxin, but no patient harm
                    occurred.(1)


Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Because long-term therapy and total cumulative dose are associ-
and warnings        ated with an increased risk of tardive dyskinesia, metoclopramide should not be continued
                    beyond 12 weeks, unless benefits of therapy clearly outweigh the risk of tardive dyski-
                    nesia.(3) Metoclopramide therapy should be discontinued in patients who develop signs or
                    symptoms of tardive dyskinesia as there is no known treatment for this adverse effect.
                    (See the Comments section.) In some patients, symptoms of tardive dyskinesia may
                    lessen or resolve after metoclopramide is stopped.(3)

                    Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to metoclopramide or any of its compo-
                    nents. Patients with pheochromocytoma, epilepsy, or those on medications that cause
                    extrapyramidal symptoms. Dangerous to use in patients with a decrease in GI motility.(3)

                    Other warnings: Extrapyramidal symptoms, tardive dyskinesia, and neuroleptic malignant
                    syndrome may develop during metoclopramide use. Dystonias and other extrapyramidal
                    symptoms are more common in pediatric patients.(3) (See the Comments section.)


Infusion-related    Rapid administration may result in anxiety, restlessness, and drowsiness.(3)
cautions

Dosage              Should not be used as first-line treatment due to the risk of tardive dyskinesia. Some
                    countries restrict use to patients older than 1 year of age. Metoclopramide (0.05 mg/kg/
                    dose given q 8 hr) has been given to a small group of preterm infants (mean gestation
                    29 weeks) for approximately 3 weeks. No adverse effects were documented.(30) Pharma-
                    cokinetics performed in infants <6 months following administration of 0.15 mg/kg q 6 hr
                    noted prolonged clearance in those <1 month of age.(34)

                    Nausea and vomiting

                        Antineoplastic-induced nausea and vomiting: 3–5 mg/kg 30 minutes prior to che-
                        motherapy, with repeat doses q 2–3 hr for 8–12 hours(3-5) or 1 mg/kg IV pretreatment
                        followed by 0.0375/mg/kg/dose orally q 6 hr.(35) Pretreatment with diphenhydramine
                        may decrease the likelihood of extrapyramidal side effects seen with this dose.(3)
                        One study used 0.4 mg/kg/hr for 6 hours in a 2-year-old boy.(6) In adults, a bolus
                        of 0.6–3.5 mg/kg 30 minutes prior to cisplatin infusion followed by 0.5 mg/kg/hr
                        for 8 hours has been used.(7,8)

                        Persistent vomiting due to acute gastroenteritis: 0.3 mg/kg to a maximum of
                        10 mg.(12,13) Cochrane Database review noted that metoclopramide reduced the number
                        of vomiting episodes and subsequent need for hospital admission.(13)

                        Prophylaxis for postsurgical nausea and vomiting: 0.15–0.5 mg/kg up to 10 mg
                        as a single dose infused after anesthesia induction or on arrival in the recovery
                        room.(14-16) Repeat q 6–8 hr as needed. Studies comparing metoclopramide to ondan-
                        setron for postoperative emesis after strabismus surgery(17,18) and post-tonsillectomy/
                        adenotonsillectomy(19,20) have found ondansetron to be more effective.




