Dosage (cont.)          Prokinetic agent
                            Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD): Currently, North American Society
                            for Pediatric Gasroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition (NASPGHAN) and European
                            Society for Paediatric Gastroenterology, Hepatology and Nutrition (ESPGHAN) prac-
                            tice guidelines concluded that there is insufficient evidence to justify the routine use
                            of metoclopramide.(31,32) The American Academy of Pediatrics also recommends that
                            there is insufficient evidence to support the routine use of metoclopramide in infants
                            and children.(36) If used, a dose of 0.4–0.8 mg/kg/day divided q 6–8 hr is suggested.(9)

                        Small bowel intubation (single dose only)(3): Metoclopramide has been used for bed-
                        side transpyloric tube placement in pediatric ICU patients(28) and more recently to ease
                        discomfort while placing nasogastric tubes in adults.(29) Recommended doses are as follows:
                        <6 years give 0.1 mg/kg; if 6–14 years give 2.5–5 mg; and if >14 years give 10 mg.(3)


Dosage adjustment       The manufacturer recommends a 50% decrease in dose if CrCl <40 mL/min.(3) Another
in organ dysfunction    reference recommends dosing as follows(21): if CrCl is 30–50 mL/min give 75% of a nor-
                        mal dose; if it is 10–29 mL/min give 50% of a normal dose; and if CrCl is <10 mL/min
                        give 25% of a normal dose. Supplement above doses in patients on hemodialysis and
                        peritoneal dialysis (25% of a normal dose) and continuous renal replacement therapy
                        (75% of a normal dose).(21)


Maximum dosage          3 mg/kg up to 10 mg as a single dose.(3,5) In adults, 4 mg/kg as single dose(22) or 2 mg/kg
                        q 2–3 hr for a total of 10 mg/kg over 8.5 hours.(23)


Additives               Each mL contains 8.5 mg NaCl.(24)


Suitable diluents       NS is the preferred diluent due to the stability of the solution.(3) D5W, D5½NS, NS, LR, R.(3,24)


Maximum                 5 mg/mL(24)
concentration

Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(24)
                        Stability: Should be a clear, colorless solution that is stored at room temperature and
                        protected from freezing.(3)
                        Delivery system issues: To use a prepacked syringe in pediatric patients who need a
                        dose less than 10 mg, adjust the dose by expelling extra volume from the syringe into
                        sterile material prior to administration.(3) Refer to the manufacturer information for addi-
                        tional directions.
                        Compatibility: For PN compatibility information, please see Appendix D and other
                        appropriate resources.
                        Photosensitivity: Although the product should be protected from light during storage,
                        once diluted it can be stored under lighting for 24 hours.(3)


IV push                 5 mg/mL over 1–2 minutes in adults receiving a dose of 10 mg(3,24)


Intermittent infusion   Dilute doses >10 mg in 50 mL of a compatible fluid and infuse over ≥15 minutes.(3,24) NS
                        is the preferred diluent due to the stability of the solution.(3)


Continuous infusion     Dilute doses >10 mg in at least 50 mL of a compatible fluid.(3,24) NS is the preferred dilu-
                        ent due to the stability of the solution.(3)




