Metoclopramide HCl
Other routes of                   Solution of 5 mg/mL may be given IM.(3)
administration

Comments                          Significant adverse effects
                                       Extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS): Symptoms of metoclopramide-induced move-
                                       ment disorders may present acutely. These are generally noted in the first 24–48
                                       hours and include involuntary dystonia (continuous spasms and muscle contractions),
                                       akathisia (motor restlessness), parkinsonism (characteristic symptoms such as rigid-
                                       ity, bradykinesia, and tremor), and tardive dyskinesia (irregular, jerky movements).
                                       (See Tardive Dyskinesia below.) The manufacturer notes that the incidence of EPS is
                                       0.2% (1 in 500 patients), but children are also more likely to experience acute dys-
                                       tonic reactions (25%). These reactions occur more frequently with larger doses.(3) A
                                       bolus of 2.5 mg/kg followed by a continuous infusion of 0.4 mg/kg/hr resulted in a
                                       dystonic reaction (urinary retention) after 6 hours in a child receiving chemotherapy.(6)
                                       Commonly used medications for EPS are anticholinergic agents such as benztropine
                                       and diphenhydramine. Diphenhydramine is given IM in doses of 0.5–1 mg/kg.(23,25,26)
                                       (See the Diphenhydramine monograph.)
                                       Neuroleptic malignant syndrome: Although this is extremely rare, it can prove
                                       fatal. Presentation includes hyperthermia, muscular rigidity, altered consciousness, and
                                       autonomic instabilty (e.g., irregular pulse/blood pressure, tachycardia an arrhythmias.
                                       Discontinuation of metoclopramide should be accompanied by aggressive symptomatic
                                       treatment and administration of bromocriptine and dantrolene.(3)
                                       Proarrhythmic: Although rare, metoclopramide has been report to cause supraven-
                                       tricular tachycardia, bradycardia, and artioventricular block.(3) Drug-induced prolonged
                                       QT syndrome leading to torsades de pointes has been reported.(37)
                                       Other: Neonates are more susceptible to methemoglobinemia during metoclopramide
                                       use.(3,9) Gynecomastia related to metoclopramide use has also been reported in pedi-
                                       atric patients.(27)
                                  Tardive dyskinesia: Metoclopramide carries a U.S. boxed warning for this adverse
                                  effect.(3) This is generally associated with long-term therapy and total cumulative dose. For
                                  this reason, metoclopramide should not be continued beyond 3 months, unless benefits of
                                  therapy clearly outweigh the risk of tardive dyskinesia.(3) The disorder is characterized by
                                  repetitive, involuntary, purposeless, movements (e.g., grimacing tongue movements, lip
                                  smacking, puckering and pursing of the lips, excessive eye blinking, rapid movements of
                                  the limbs, torso, and fingers). Metoclopramide therapy should be discontinued in patients
                                  who develop signs or symptoms of tardive dyskinesia as there is no known treatment
                                  for this adverse effect. In some patients, symptoms of tardive dyskinesia may lessen or
                                  resolve after metoclopramide is stopped, but the movement disorder may be permanent.(3)
                                  An 8-year-old boy developed tardive dyskinesia when he received metoclopramide as part
                                  of therapy for gastroesophageal reflux and erosive esophagitis.(33)
                                  Drug interactions: Metoclopramide is associated with numerous drug interactions.(3)
                                  Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before combining any drug
                                  with metoclopramide.


