Comments                           Rare adverse events: Seizures may occur with large cumulative doses and prolonged
                                   use.(15) In cases involving adults the cumulative dose has been >40 g. No correlation has
                                   been noted between this adverse effect and serum concentration of metronidazole.(16)
                                   Neuropathy has been reported when cumulative doses exceed 30 g.(17) It is usually revers-
                                   ible when the drug is discontinued.(18) Continuation of therapy, even at a lower dose, may
                                   potentiate symptoms.(17)
                                   Metronidazole is a nitroimidazole and should be used cautiously in patients with evidence
                                   of or history of blood dyscrasia. Although a mild leukopenia has been observed, no per-
                                   sistent hematologic abnormalities have been seen in clinical studies.(3)
                                   Monitoring: Total and differential leukocyte counts before and after therapy.
                                   Drug interactions: Metronidazole inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase and other alcohol oxi-
                                   dizing enzymes to produce a disulfiram-type reaction (e.g., flushing, sweating, headache,
                                   and tachycardia) in patients who are concurrently given ethanol. Shown to potentiate
                                   the anticoagulant effect of warfarin and can result in an increase in prothrombin time.(3)
                                   Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before combining any drug
                                   with metronidazole.
                                   Laboratory interference: Metronidazole may interfere with some assays to decrease
                                   values for aspartate aminotransaminase, alanine transaminase, lactate dehydrogenase,
                                   triglycerides, or glucose hexokinase.(3)


