potential

Contraindications   Contraindications: Patients with known hypersensitivity to the drug, any of its compo-
and warnings        nents, or other echinocandins.(4)
                    Warnings: Hypersensitivity reactions (see the Infusion-Related Cautions section), hema-
                    tologic effects (acute intravascular hemolysis, hemoglobinuria, hemolytic anemia), abnor-
                    malities in liver function tests, hepatitis and hepatic failure, and renal dysfunction and acute
                    renal failure have been reported.(4) Postmarketing reports have described cases of Stevens-
                    Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis, and disseminated intravascular coagulation.(5)

Infusion-related    Phlebitis and thrombophlebitis have been reported and occur more frequently with admin-
cautions            istration via a peripheral line.(4)
                    Rapid infusions may result in histamine-mediated reactions.(4) Isolated cases of serious
                    hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis have also occurred.(4)

Dosage              Experience with micafungin in children, particularly neonates, is limited.(4,6)
                    Neonatal fungal infections
                        <1000 g: 10 mg/kg once daily
                        ≥1000 g: 7–10 mg/kg once daily; HIV-exposed or HIV-positive neonates may require
                        10–12 mg/kg/once daily.(7)
                    Pediatric and adolescent invasive infections
                        Aspergillosis
                            Children: 1.5–3 mg/kg/dose q 24 hr. May increase dose to maximum of 8.6 mg/
                            kg/day if no clinical response or persistent cultures.(8,9)
                            Adolescents: 1.5 mg/kg/day; increase if indicated to maximum daily dose of
                            150 mg/day.(8,9)
                        Candidemia
                            Children: 2 mg/kg/dose q 24 hr. May increase to 4–10 mg/kg/day if no clinical
                            response or persistent cultures.(10,11,12) Maximum dose 100 mg/day.
                        Children with HIV(7)
                            Infants <4 months: 5–7 mg/kg/day q 24 hr
                            Infants ≥4 months and <15 kg: 5–7 mg /kg/day q 24 hr
                            Children up to 8 years and ≤40 kg: 3–4 mg/kg/day q 24 hr
                            Children ≥9 years and adolescents: 2–3 mg/kg/day q 24 hr. Maximum dose
                            100 mg/day.
                    One study examined the benefit of high doses of micafungin given to young infants, most
                    <34 weeks gestational age. Six infants weighing <1000 g (mean 688 g) received 10 mg/
                    kg/day for 4–6 days. Seven infants weighing >1000 g (mean 2101 g) were treated with
                    7 mg/kg/day for 4–6 days. All patients achieved targeted AUC0-24 by day 4 of treatment;
                    few adverse events were attributed to the drug. One patient received 7 mg/kg/day for a
                    total duration of 33 days without adverse event.(10)
                    Prophylaxis in transplant: 1 mg/kg given once daily. Maximum dose 50 mg.(13,14)
                    3 mg/kg given every other day to children 10 years of age and under who were undergo-
                    ing hematopoietic stem-cell transplant resulted in trough serum concentrations of at least
                    0.3 mcg/mL. This regimen was judged to be a suitable alternative to other prophylactic
                    regimens in this population.(15)




