Brand names         Versed, generic


Medication error    High-alert medication (moderate sedation agent, IV) that has an increased risk of signifi-
potential           cant harm if used in error.(1)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Confusion has been reported with diazePAM and
                    LORazepam(2) and between Versed and Valium, VePesid, and Vistaril.(2)


Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Respiratory depression and arrest may occur, especially when used
and warnings        for sedation in noncritical care settings. If not recognized promptly and treated effec-
                    tively, death or hypoxic encephalopathy may occur.(3) Should only be used in settings that
                    can continuously monitor respiratory and cardiac function. For deeply sedated pediatric
                    patients, a dedicated individual, other than the practitioner performing the procedure,
                    should monitor the patient throughout the procedures. Oxygen, resuscitative drugs,
                    age- and size-appropriate equipment for bag/valve/mask ventilation and intubation, and
                    skilled personnel for the maintenance of a patent airway and support of ventilation must
                    be immediately availability.(3) Doses in pediatric patients should be calculated based on
                    mg/kg and the initial dose and all subsequent doses should always be titrated slowly.
                    Administer over at least 2 minutes and allow an additional 2 or more minutes to fully
                    evaluate the sedative effect. The use of the 1 mg/mL formulation or dilution of the 1 mg/
                    mL or 5 mg/mL formulation is recommended to facilitate slower injection.(3) Should not
                    be administered by rapid injection to neonates (less than 2 minutes) as severe hypoten-
                    sion and seizures have been reported, particularly when fentanyl is given concomitantly.(3)
                    Contraindications: In patients with a known hypersensitivity to midazolam (or any other
                    benzodiazepines), or any component of the formulation.(3) Although benzodiazepines are
                    contraindicated in patients with acute narrow-angle glaucoma, they may be used in patients
                    with open-angle glaucoma provided they are receiving appropriate therapy for glaucoma.(3)
                    Other warnings: Do not use in patients with shock or coma if vital signs are decreased,
                    or in acute alcohol intoxication associated with depression of vital signs.(3) Use cautiously in
                    patients with uncompensated acute illnesses (e.g., severe fluid or electrolyte disturbances).(3)


Infusion-related    Respiratory depression and arrest requiring mechanical ventilation may occur following
cautions            excessive dosing, rapid administration,(3) or use with fentanyl.(3,4) Rapid administration
                    or large doses may decrease blood pressure and cerebral blood flow velocity.(48) For this
                    reason, midazolam should be used cautiously in neonates.(3) The use of the 1-mg/mL
                    formulation or dilution of the 1- or 5-mg/mL formulation is recommended to facilitate
                    slower injection.(3) Avoid intra-arterial injection or extravasation.(3)


Dosage              Oxygen, resuscitative drugs, age- and size-appropriate equipment for bag/valve/mask
                    ventilation and intubation, and skilled personnel for the maintenance of a patent airway
                    and support of ventilation must be immediately availability.(3) Flumazenil should be readily
                    available for reversal.(55) (See Other in the Comments section and Flumazenil monograph.)
                    The dose should be calculated using ideal body weight (see Appendix B) in obese patients
                    as mean half-life is prolonged (6.5 versus 12 hours) in these patients. This alteration was
                    due to a 50% increase in volume of distribution when corrected for total body weight.
                    Clearance was not affected.(3)
                    Anesthesia induction: 0.15 mg/kg (maximum single dose of 5 mg)(55) followed by up
                    to 3 doses of 0.05 mg/kg at 2-minute intervals.(5) Time to induction was shorter with a
                    dose of 0.3 mg/kg.(6) Doses of 0.6 mg/kg did not reliably induce anesthesia in children
                    given atropine and meperidine.(7)
                    Procedural sedation: For deeply sedated pediatric patients, a dedicated individual, other
                    than the practitioner performing the procedure, should monitor the patient throughout
                    the procedures. Midazolam is the preferred benzodiazepine because of its rapid onset fol-
                    lowing IV administration (2–5 minutes) and short duration (30–60 minute).(3,8-10) It does
                    not possess analgesic activity and must be combined with other agents (i.e., fentanyl)
                    when analgesia is needed.(10)




