                                                                             Midazolam HCl
Dosage (cont.)         When administering IV give immediately before the procedure, and if given IM adminis-
                       ter 30–60 minutes before the procedure. 0.05–0.1 mg/kg up to 5 mg over 2–3 minutes.
                       Observe and repeat dose every 2–5 minutes to desired effect.(9,11-15,55) Single doses up to
                       2 mg(15,16) or 5 mg(11,13) and a total of 10 mg have been used.(16) Some recommend age
                       dependent dosing as follows: 0.05–0.1 mg/kg titrating up to 0.6 mg/kg or 6 mg (0.5–5
                       years) or 0.025–0.05 mg/kg titrating up to 0.4 mg/kg up to 10 mg (6–12 years).(8,9,16)
                       Sedation with mechanical ventilation: Because hypotension may occur following a
                       loading dose in neonates, some recommend that one not be given. If a loading dose is
                       not used some suggest that a continuous infusion be given at a faster rate for the first
                       several hours(17) and have recommended 0.03 mg/kg/hr for those younger than 32 weeks
                       gestational age and 0.06 mg/kg/hr for those older than 32 weeks.(19) In pediatric patients,
                       give 0.1–0.2 mg/kg over 2–5 minutes,(17-23) followed by 0.03–0.4 mg/kg/hr.(17-21) For rapid
                       sequence intubation, doses <2 mg/kg are ineffective. Allow sufﬁcient time (2–3 minutes)
                       for midazolam to take effect before administration of a muscle relaxant.(55) Paradoxical
                       agitation may occur, especially in younger children.
                       Seizures (acute, status epilepticus)(55-60): Other benzodiazepines (e.g., lorazepam)
                       are typically used for initial IV treatment of acute seizures including status epilepticus in
                       the hospitalized patient. Recent data suggest that IM midazolam may be preferred in the
                       prehospital setting.(61,62) (See the Other Routes of Administration section.) 0.1–0.2 mg/kg
                       up to 6 mg as a single dose. May repeat q 10–15 minutes as needed up to 3 doses.(22-35,55)
                       Investigators reported that adults who were given IM midazolam by paramedics using an
                       autoinjector, were more likely to have seizures terminate prior to arrival at the emergency
                       department than those given IV lorazepam.
                       Seizures (refractory generalize convulsive status epilepticus)(55-60): Tachyphylaxis
                       may require progressively larger dose within the first 24–48 hours. A loading dose of
                       0.02–0.6 mg/kg followed by a continuous infusion of 0.04–3 mg/kg/hr.(22,24-35) Titrate
                       by 0.06 mg/kg/hr q 15 min.(28) Mean maintenance infusion ranged from 0.14–3 mg/
                       kg/hr.(22,24-35) In a report of 306 pediatric patients, the dose was <0.4 mg/kg/hr in 81%
                       of patients.(31) Most patients regain full consciousness by an average of 4 hours after
                       discontinuation of midazolam.(35)


Dosage adjustment      Adjust dosage in patients with hepatic dysfunction.(3) Patients with chronic renal failure
in organ dysfunction   and/or congestive heart failure eliminate midazolam more slowly. No dosage adjustment
                       is required in renal dysfunction.(36) Patients receiving extracorporeal membrane oxygen-
                       ation (ECMO) may require larger doses due to drug absorption in the ECMO circuit.(38)
                       (See Drug Delivery Issues in the Preparation and Delivery section.)


Maximum dosage         An initial dose for sedation in adults may be as little as 1 mg but should not exceed 2.5
                       mg.(3) Total doses >5 mg are not usually necessary for conscious sedation in adults.(3)
                       For anesthesia induction, single doses of ≤0.6 mg/kg.(5,6) not to exceed 5 mg/dose have
                       been used.(11,13,55) The largest dose given by continuous infusion has been 3 mg/kg/hr.(26)


Additives              Preservative-free midazolam hydrochloride is available. Some products also contain 0.01%
                       disodium edetate, and benzyl alcohol 1%, with hydrochloric acid and, if necessary, sodium
                       hydroxide to adjust the pH.(37) (See Appendix C for specific information about benzyl
                       alcohol’s potential for toxicity.)


Suitable diluents      D5W, NS, D5NS.(37) Use preservative-free SW for dilution in neonates.


Maximum                <5 mg/mL for IV push(3,4) and 1 mg/mL to facilitate slow administration or dosage titra-
concentration          tion or IM injection(3)


Preparation and        Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery               before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                       other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                       and extended stability.(37)




