Preparation and         Preparation: To minimize excipient load, preservative-free preparations should be used.
delivery (cont.)        If this is not possible, use the 5 mg/mL product and dilute to 0.5 mg/mL with SW without
                        preservatives.(37)
                        Stability: Midazolam is a colorless to light yellow solution. It should be stored at room tem-
                        perature.(37) Frozen product is physically stable for 3 days after thawing at room temperature
                        thawing.(37) Midazolam was stable in polypropylene syringes stored at room temperature,
                        not exposed to direct light, for 10 days, 13 days, 36 days, and 4 weeks.(37) No degradation
                        of midazolam (5 mg/mL in glass cartridge within an autoinjector) was noted over 60 days
                        in the EMS environment, even when stored at high temperatures (>40°C).(63) A follow-up
                        study noted the same outcomes were noted at 120 days.(64)
                        Delivery system issues: Patients receiving extracorporeal membrane oxygenation
                        (ECMO) may require larger doses due to drug absorption in the ECMO circuit.(38) No sig-
                        nificant absorption was noted when administered by PVC, glass, and polyethylene-lined
                        laminated container.(37)
                        Compatibility: For PN compatibility, please see Appendix D and other available resources.
                        Photosensitivity: For long-term stability, protect from light.(37) Provided the solution is
                        prepared with compatible solutions it does not require protection for short-term storage
                        and administration.(37)


IV push                 1 or 5 mg/mL given over 20–30 seconds for anesthesia induction.(3,6) Should not be given
                        to neonates via rapid administration (<2 minutes) as severe hypotension may occur,
                        especially if fentanyl is given concurrently.(3) Seizures have also been reported in neonates
                        following rapid administration.(3)


Intermittent infusion   ≥2 minutes(3)


Continuous infusion     Dilute to a concentration of 0.5 mg/mL with NS or D5W.(3,41)


Other routes of         1 mg/mL.(3) IM doses of 0.15–0.5 mg/kg (maximum total dose 10 mg) have been admin-
administration          istered safely in children requiring sedation or in patients in status epilepticus.(39,40,55,62)
                        Although a 20% failure rate was noted when IM administration was given prehospitaliza-
                        tion,(40) the larger RAMPART study noted that the rates of hospital and ICU admission,
                        endotracheal intubation, and recurrent seizure are all favorable in children when IM mid-
                        azolam was compared with IV lorazepam in the prehospital administration in adults.(41,62)
                        Intranasal (IN) administration is rapidity gaining favor in prehospital and ED use.(59,60)
                        When given IN, administer 0.15–0.3 mg/kg using a mucosal atomizing device. Has been
                        given by buccal administration.


Comments                Rare adverse effects
                            Paradoxical reactions: Hyperactive, agitations, or aggressive behavior have been
                            reported with benzodiazepines, particularly in adolescent/pediatric or psychiatric
                            patients.(3)
                            Involuntary movements: Epileptiform movements may occur in preterm infants
                            <32-weeks-old.(42-45) Brief tonic–clonic seizures have occurred in patients with atypical
                            absence (petit mal) status epilepticus.(3) Seizure activity and myoclonus have also been
                            reported, especially in very low birth weight neonates.(3,42,44,46) The movements gener-
                            ally began a few minutes after a bolus injection and continue for several hours.(44,46)
                        Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                        combining any drug with midazolam. Midazolam is a substrate of CYP2B6 and 3A4 (major)
                        and is a weak inhibitor of CYP2C8, 2C9, and 3A4.
                        Pharmacokinetic considerations: Pharmacokinetic properties in those >1 year of age
                        are similar to adults.(3) The terminal half-life is significantly prolonged and clearance is
                        reduced in critically ill neonates.(3) Children <6 years of age may require larger doses
                        (mg/kg) than older pediatric patients and adults.(3)




