  ($teddy_164_id$milrinone-lactate$teddy_164_id$, $teddy_164_name$Milrinone Lactate$teddy_164_name$, $teddy_164_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_164_source$, 2360784, $teddy_164$Milrinone Lactate
Brand names         Primacor, generic

Medication error    High-alert medication (inotropic medication, IV) that has an increased risk of causing
potential           significant patient harm if it is used in error(1)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Confusion has been reported for Primacor with
                    Natrecor and Primaxin.(2)

Contraindications   Contraindications: Hypersensitivity to milrinone, any component, or inamrinone
and warnings        (amrinone)(3)
                    Warnings: Regardless of route or method of delivery, milrinone has not been shown
                    to be safe or effective when used longer than 48 hours in patients with heart failure.(3)
                    (See Other in the Comments section.) It should not be used in patients with severe obstruc-
                    tive aortic or pulmonic valvular disease(3) and may aggravate outflow tract obstruction
                    in those with hypertrophic subaortic stenosis.(3) Use caution if vigorous diuretic therapy
                    has been used or has been suspected of causing significant decreases in cardiac-filling
                    pressure.(3)

Infusion-related    Spontaneous bronchospasm has been reported.(3) IV site should be monitored for extrava-
cautions            sation, which should be avoided. (See Appendix E.)

Dosage              The dosage and side effect profile have not been established for pediatric patient patients.
                    Myocardial dysfunction/hemodynamic support (including normotensive septic
                    shock)
                        Loading dose: 50 mcg/kg administered over 10–60 minutes (average 15 minutes).(4)
                        Doses of 50–100 mcg/kg have been used.(4-11,24) The loading dose has been given
                        over 3 hours.(12-14)
                    A milrinone infusion without a loading dose was as effective inotropic strategy when com-
                    pared to continuous infusion with a loading dose. Omitting the loading dose may decrease
                    the risk of hypotension.(27)
                        Continuous infusion: 0.25–0.75 mcg/kg/min. Doses of 0.125–1.2 mcg/
                        kg/min(4-16,24,25) have been used and should be titrated to desired effect. One group
                        recommends that for every increase of 0.25 mcg/kg/min, a bolus of 25 mcg/kg be
                        given.(9) Another suggests that in the postoperative setting, if renal function is impaired,
                        that the initial dosages not exceed 0.2 mcg/kg/min.(11)
                    Outpatient use (awaiting transplant): Although dose was not reported, one retrospec-
                    tive study was conducted in 106 children (10.1 ± 6.4 years) with congestive heart disease
                    (47%) or dilated cardiomyopathy (n = 53%) awaiting transplant received milrinone. This
                    was the most frequently used inotropic therapy with 91% given this agent.(26)
                    Peripherial necrosis (prevention): Loading dose of 25 mcg/kg over 10 minutes fol-
                    lowed by doses up to 0.75 mcg/kg/min have been used to decreased the risk of peripheral
                    necrosis (i.e., toes) associated with meningococcal sepsis.(15)
                    Prevention of postoperative low cardiac output syndrome
                        Premature neonates: A study in 29 premature neonates (<29 weeks) found that a
                        loading dose of 0.75 mcg/kg over 3 hours, followed by 0.2 mcg/kg/min for 18 hours
                        effectively prevented low systemic blood flow.(14) Conversely, a randomized, placebo-
                        controlled study involving 90 premature infants (<30 weeks) found that the same
                        regimen did not prevent low systemic blood flow during the first 24 hours; hence,
                        milrinone should not be used for that purpose.(12)
                        Full-term neonates, infants, and children: A loading dose of 75 mcg/kg admin-
                        istered over 60 minutes followed by a continuous IV infusion of 0.75 mcg/kg/min for
                        35 hours was used in a randomized, placebo-controlled trial of 227 patients (age:
                        2 days to 6.9 years; median: 3 months).(17) These large doses of milrinone significantly
                        reduced the risk of low cardiac output syndrome by 64%.(17)




