                                                                        Milrinone Lactate
Dosage (cont.)             A prospective, open-label trial compared a small and larger dose milrinone.(10) The small
                           dose group (n = 11) was given 25 mcg/kg over 5 minutes followed by 0.25 mcg/kg/
                           min; 30 minutes later, a second 25 mcg/kg was given and the infusion was increased to
                           0.5 mcg/kg/min. The large dose group (n = 8) received a loading dose of 50 mcg/kg over
                           10 minutes followed by 0.5 mcg/kg/min; 30 minutes later, a second dose of 25 mcg/
                           kg was given and the rate was increased to 0.75 mcg/kg/min. A third loading dose of
                           25 mcg/kg was given to either group if needed. Using NONMEM analysis (two-
                           compartment model), the following were recommended: 104 mcg/kg loading dose
                           followed by an infusion of 0.49 mcg/kg/min in infants and 67 mcg/kg loading dose
                           followed by an infusion of 0.61 mcg/kg/min in children.
                       Pulmonary hypertension (PPHN)
                           A Cochrane Review assessed published and quasi randomized controlled trials that
                           compared milrinone with placebo, inhaled nitric oxide (iNO), or potential treatments
                           other than iNO in neonates with PPHN.(18) The authors concluded that the efficacy and
                           safety of milrinone in the treatment of PPHN is unknown.
                           Six infants with chronic heart disease and severe PPHN were studied. Within 72 hours
                           of beginning milrinone, there was a significant increase in early diastolic myocardial ve
                           locities in the right ventricle, accompanied by increasing systolic velocities in the right
                           ventricle and interventricular septum. Oxygenation index was significantly reduced,
                           blood pressure unchanged, and ductal shunt velocity minimally altered over the same
                           time period.(19) Successful use of 50 mcg/kg followed by 0.2 mcg/kg/min has also
                           been given to a term newborn.(20)

Dosage adjustment      Adjust dosage in renal dysfunction (see below) as impaired milrinone clearance may
in organ dysfunction   result in elevated milrinone concentrations, significant hypotension, and potentially
                       life-threateningrrhythmias(3,30)

                                        CrCl (mL/min/1.73 m2)            Dose (mcg/kg/min)
