  ($teddy_165_id$morphine-sulfate$teddy_165_id$, $teddy_165_name$Morphine Sulfate$teddy_165_name$, $teddy_165_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_165_source$, 2379494, $teddy_165$Morphine Sulfate
Brand names         Astramorph PF, DepoDur PF (extended-release liposome epidural injection), Duramorph
                    PF, Infumorph (preservative-free for microinfusion devices only), generics

Medication error    ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error.(1)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. ISMP reports that morphine has been confused with
                    HYDROmorphone.(2) USP reports that morphine sulfate has been confused with HYDRO-
                    morphone, magnesium sulfate, methadone, and OxyContin.(3) Patient harm resulted when
                    morphine sulfate was confused with meperidine.(3)

Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Because of the risk for severe adverse effects with the epidural or
and warnings        intrathecal (IT) routes of preservative-free morphine, patients must be observed in a fully
                    equipped and staffed environment for at least 24 hours after the initial dose.(4)
                    Naloxone (opioid antagonist) injection and resuscitative equipment should be immediately
                    available in case of life-threatening or intolerable side effects and whenever IT or epidural
                    preservative-free morphine therapy is being initiated.(4)
                    Contraindications: Those with known hypersensitivity to morphine should not receive
                    morphine sulfate.(4,5) Some manufacturers list convulsive states(6) (morphine stimulates the
                    spinal cord), paralytic ileus,(6) heart failure resulting from chronic lung disease,(9) cardiac
                    arrhythmias,(9) and brain tumor(9) as contraindications. Those with acute bronchial asthma
                    or upper airway obstruction should not receive morphine.(4,5)
                    Other warnings: Products containing preservatives (generics) are not for IT or epidural use.(9)
                    Some products are for use with specific patient controlled analgesia pumps or auto-injectors.
                    Morphine sulfate is available in a variety of concentrations ranging from 0.5–50 mg/mL.(7)
                    Choose products carefully.
                    Errors in programming patient-controlled analgesia pumps, including the substitution of
                    a more concentrated product than the pump was programmed to infuse, have resulted
                    in deaths.(8)
                    The respiratory depressant effects of morphine and its ability to increase intracranial
                    pressure may be exaggerated in patients with head injury.(6)
                    IT use has been associated with a higher incidence of respiratory depression than epidural use.(4)

Infusion-related    Confirm the choice of the correct morphine product and concentration on the vial prior to use.
cautions
                    Respiratory depression and arrest requiring mechanical ventilation may occur and is
                    reversible with naloxone.(9,10) This is more likely to occur with rapid administration.(6)
                    Rapid IV administration may result in chest wall rigidity.(4)

Dosage              Dosing is titrated to effect and the lowest effective dose should be used. Chronic opioid
                    use results in tolerance and an increased dose requirement. In these patients, a wean-
                    ing protocol should be implemented when morphine is discontinued to avoid withdrawal
                    symptoms.
                    Dosage adjustment in obesity: Dosing based on ideal or lean body weight has been
                    recommended.(42)
                    Neonates (preservative-free morphine): World Health Organization (WHO) Guidelines
                    recommend the following as starting doses in opioid-naive neonates.(43)
                        Intermittent (IV/sub-Q): 0.025–0.05 mg/kg q 6 hr
                        Continuous infusion (IV): 0.025–0.05 mg/kg once followed by 0.005–0.01 mg/kg/hr




