                 Additional regimens published, intermittent dosing: 0.05–0.2 mg/kg (usual max
                 doses: infants, 2 mg/dose; children <6 years, 4 mg/dose; children 7–12 years, 8 mg/dose;
                 adolescents, 15 mg/dose) q 2–4 hr PRN.(11,12,16,17,21-25) Continuous infusion of 0.005–0.03
                 mg/kg/hr that may be preceded by a bolus.(16,17,21,23,26) A systematic review of sedation
                 regimens to facilitate mechanical ventilation in the pediatric ICU reported bolus doses of
                 0.05–0.1 mg/kg with continuous infusions of 0.01–0.06 mg/kg/hr.(27) Daily sedative inter-
                 ruption was shown to be feasible in patients age 0–12 years and resulted in decreases in
                 sedative use, mechanical ventilation duration, and PICU length of stay with no increase
                 in complications.(44)
                 Sickle cell disease with pain crisis: 0.1–0.15 mg/kg q 2–4 hr usual starting dose
                 in opioid-naïve patients.(28) Bolus of 0.15 mg/kg followed by a continuous infusion of
                 0.03–0.15 mg/kg/hr achieved adequate analgesia in hospitalized children.(29) Of note,
                 a pharmacokinetic study found an eightfold variability in clearance in 18 children that
                 resulted in a larger dose requirement (without increased side effects) in some patients.(30)
                 This may be due to anemia-related changes in hepatic blood flow that accelerate renal
                 or hepatic clearance.
                 Severe pain: Larger doses may be needed in cancer, burn, or other patients with severe
                 pain. In eight children with terminal malignancy, continuous infusions ranging from
                 0.03–0.15 mg/kg/hr provided adequate or complete pain relief.(31) However, doses from
                 0.76–2.6 mg/kg/hr were needed in two of these.
