administration
                                     Only preservative-free products are given via the epidural or IT route.(4,5,38)

Comments                             Monitoring: Patients should be monitored for adequacy of analgesia/sedation and signs
                                     and symptoms that would indicate toxicity such as decreased respiratory rate, oxygen
                                     saturation, and alertness.
                                     Pharmacokinetics: A pharmacokinetic study in 17 preterm neonates (26–34 weeks
                                     gestational age) found a morphine clearance of 2.4 mL/kg/min, half-life of 8.75 hours,
                                     and volume of distribution of 1.82 L/kg.(39) Another study comparing preterm with term
                                     neonates found no statistically significant difference in half-life between the two groups
                                     (10.6 ± 2.7 hr versus 7.6 ± 2.6 hr, respectively), clearance (2.23 ± 0.95 mL/kg/min
                                     versus 2.03 ± 1.46 mL/kg/min), and volume of distribution (2.04 ± 1.01 L/kg versus
                                     2.07 ± 1.26 L/kg).(40)
                                     The age-related effects on morphine requirements and plasma concentrations of mor-
                                     phine and its metabolites were compared in postsurgical term infants from 0–4 weeks
                                     of age (n = 63), 4–26 weeks (n = 65), 26–52 weeks (n = 30), and 1–3 years (n = 39).
                                     All received a bolus of 0.1 mg/kg followed by a continuous infusion of 0.01 mg/kg/hr
                                     or 0.03 mg/kg/dose q 3 hr. At 24 hours after surgery, neonates had higher morphine,
                                     morphine-3-glucuronide, and morphine-6-glucuronide concentrations than the three older
                                     age groups indicating a capacity for glucuronidation and a lower renal clearance.(41)
                                     Drug interactions: Use morphine cautiously in patients receiving other CNS depressants
                                     as effects are additive.(9)
                                     Other: In a randomized, placebo controlled trial in preterm infants, morphine 0.03 mg/kg
                                     with atropine was found to be inferior to rapid sequence intubation (RSI) with glycopyr-
                                     rolate 0.005 mg/kg, thiopental 2–3 mg/kg (2 mg/kg if <2 kg), succinylcholine 2 mg/kg
                                     and remifentanil 0.001 mg/kg. The RSI regimen achieved better intubating conditions as
                                     well as shorter procedure duration with less impact on heart rate and blood pressure.(46)


