potential           nubain.(2) Naloxone has been confused with nalbuphine and nalmefene.(2)

Contraindications   Contraindications: Hypersensitivity to naloxone or any component of the formulation.(3)
and warnings        Warnings: An acute abstinence syndrome can be precipitated by abrupt and complete
                    reversal of narcotic effects.(3) Naloxone should be administered cautiously to persons who
                    are known or suspected to be physically dependent on opioids. This includes newborns of
                    mothers who are physically dependent on opioids. (See Other in the Comments section.)
                    Naloxone is not effective in (1) treating respiratory depression due to nonopioid drugs;
                    (2) the management of acute toxicity caused by levopropoxyphene; and (3) reversal of
                    respiratory depression by partial agonists (e.g., buprenorphine) or mixed agonist/antago-
                    nists (e.g., pentazocine). In the case of partial agonists or mixed agonist/antagonists,
                    reversal may be incomplete or may require larger doses due to the drugs prolonged dura-
                    tion, slow rate of binding, and slow dissociation from opioid receptor.(3)

Infusion-related    None reported
cautions

Dosage              When used for acute opiate overdose, other resuscitative measures (e.g., maintenance of
                    an adequate airway, artificial respiration, cardiac massage, vasopressor agents) should
                    be readily available.
                    Neonatal respiratory depression/asphyxia
                        Initial resuscitation: There is no role for naloxone in the resuscitation of newborns in
                        the delivery room who have respiratory depression that is unrelated to opioid use.(38,39)
                        Maternal exposure during labor and delivery: Although manufacturer information
                        suggest that naloxone be give to neonates whose mother received opioids associated
                        with delivery,(3) the American Academy of Pediatric does not recommend its use.(7) A
                        2013 Cochran Review concluded that there is no evidence to support the use of nalox-
                        one in infants whose mother received opiates during labor and delivery.(40) 0.01 mg/kg
                        (IV) administered into the umbilical vein and repeated q 2–3 min until clinical response;
                        repeat q 20–60 min for 1–2 hours as needed.(3,4) Additional doses may be necessary
                        at 1- to 2-hour intervals depending on the response of the neonate and the dosage
                        and duration of action of the opiate administered to the mother.
                        Maternal dependence/in utero exposure: Naloxone should be administered with
                        extreme caution to newborns of opioid dependent mothers as abrupt and/or complete
                        reversal may precipitate acute withdrawal (e.g., tremors [trembling], hyperactive
                        reflexes, irritability/excessive/high pitched crying, sleep problems, seizures, poor
                        feeding and suck).(35) Naloxone-induced seizures did not respond to traditional anti-
                        convulsants but did terminate following a dose of morphine.(36)
                    Opiate intoxication (full reversal): Dosage requirements and the need for repeat
                    doses is dependent on the amount and type of opioid agonist/antagonist being reversed.
                    Repeat doses may be necessary, because the duration of action of some narcotics may
                    exceed that of naloxone. Monitor for at least 24 hours because relapse may occur as
                    naloxone is metabolized.(3)
                        <5 years or ≤20 kg (includes premature and term neonates): 0.1 mg/kg q 2–3
                        min IV/IO/ET as needed until opiate effects are reversed.(3,5-10) Repeat q 20–60 min
                        for 1–2 hours if inadequate response or symptoms recur.(3) ET administration in not
                        recommended for use in neonates.(37)
                        5 years or >20 kg: 2 mg/dose IV/IO/ET(3,5,8,11-13) with subsequent doses of 0.1 mg/kg
                        q 2–3 min if no improvement occurs.(3,8,12,13) Repeat q 20–60 min if symptoms recur.(3)
                        When repeated doses are frequently required in infants, children, and adolescents,
                        some suggest the use of continuous infusion naloxone. (i.e., 2.5–160 mcg/kg/hr).(14,15)
                        After titrating clinical effectiveness, decrease by 25%.(14-16) Wean infusion in 50%
                        increments over 6–12 hours, depending on the half-life of the opiate.(16)




