Dosage (cont.)         Respiratory depression associated with postoperative/therapeutic opioid use:
                       Doses are smaller that those given for opiate intoxication.(5) American Academy of Pediat-
                       rics’ recommends does ranging from 0.001–0.015 mg/kg(37) while the 2010 PALS Guide-
                       lines recommend 0.001–0.005 mg/kg.(5) doses may be repeated as needed until clinical
                       response is seen and then at 1- to 2-hour intervals as necessary.(5,16)
                       Septic shock: Dose and dosage regimens have not been established. Although nalox-
                       one causes a sustained increase in the blood pressure, especially mean arterial pressure
                       in of those with septic shock,(17-19) and does not appear to improve survival.(20) To date,
                       there have not been any controlled trials in children. Three case reports have described
                       success with 0.01–0.05 mg/kg/dose intermittently in infants and children with septic shock
                       secondary to Neisseria meningitides, Escherichia coli, and group B Streptococcus.(14,21,22)
                       Intermittent doses have been followed by infusions of 0.13–0.45 mg/kg/hr.(14)
                       Pruritus associated with opioid therapy
                           Prevention: One double-blind prospective trial in 46 postoperative children
                           (6–17 years old) used a continuous infusion of 0.25 mcg/kg/hr and found a lower
                           incidence of adverse effects (nausea/vomiting, pruritus) without loss of pain control.(23)
                           A study of 59 postoperative adolescents (12–16 years old) used doses from 0.25–1.65
                           mcg/kg/hr and found the lowest effective dose was 1 mcg/kg/hr.(24) They also noted
                           that success/failure and opioid-associated adverse effect were unrelated to serum
                           concentration.(24) Another report in 18 sickle cell patients in crisis (8–18 years old)
                           used 0.25 mcg/kg/hr or 1 mcg/kg/hr, which was given as a separate infusion from
                           morphine.(25) They noted that 1 mcg/kg/hr IV resulted in less diphenhydrAMINE use
                           and less severe pruritus than the 0.25 mcg/kg/hr dose without loss of a pain control.(25)
                           A recent randomized control trial note that an admixture of morphine (1 mg) plus
                           naloxone (12 mcg) in NS did not decrease the incidence or severity of puritis and con-
                           cluded that separate administration of naloxone may be a more effective strategy.(43)
                           Treatment: A retrospective study noted the successful treatment of opioid-induced
                           pruritis in 30 patients (3–20 years old who were administered 2 mcg/kg/hr initially,
                           titrating by 0.5 mcg/kg/hr every few hours if antihistamine-resistant pruritis continued.
                           Doses ≥3 mcg/kg/hr increased the risk of loss of pain control.(26)
                       Tetrahydrozoline intoxication: A neonate who was accidentally administered tet-
                       rahydrozoline nasal drops PO developed apnea and bradycardia but responded to one
                       0.1 mg/kg dose of naloxone.(27)

Dosage adjustment      Although one reference notes that no adjustment is needed in renal dysfunction,(28) the
in organ dysfunction   manufacturer recommends that naloxone be use with caution when renal or hepatic dys-
                       function is present.(3)

Maximum dosage         Not established.(3) 0.4 mg/kg in neonates(29) or 2 mg/dose in those >5 years or weigh-
                       ing >20 kg.(3,5,10,13) If no response is seen after a 10-mg total dose, diagnosis of opioid
                       toxicity is questionable.
                       The following doses were given without evidence of toxicity: an 8-day-old received
                       0.45 mg/kg/hr for 2 hours(14); a total of 0.8 mg/kg was given in 27 hours to a 1-month-
                       old(17); continuous infusion of 0.04 mg/kg/hr (12-month-old) and 0.16 mg/kg/hr (3 day
                       old; 5 days)(15); a 4½-year-old received 11 doses of 0.2 mg (2.2 mg) and a 2½-year-old
                       was accidentally given 20 mg(3); a 13-year-old required a cumulative dose of 0.65 mg/kg
                       over 65.6 hours(30); and a 17-year-old received 0.8 mg/hr for 15.5 hours.(31)

Additives              Some products contain methylparabens or propylparabens.(3) (See Appendix C for specific
