Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions, compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(32)
                        Stability: Store at room temperature. Diluted solutions should be used within 24 hours.(3)
                        No loss of drug was noted when naloxone was stored for 4 weeks in paramedic vehicles
                        under simulated summer conditions (26°C to 38°C).(34) Naloxone (0.133 mg/mL in NS)
                        packaged in polypropylene syringes and stored for 24 hours at 4°C to 23°C was physi-
                        cally stable.(32)
                        Compatibility: Do not mix with alkaline solutions/medications, bisulfites, or sulfites.(32)
                        Photosensitivity: Protect from excessive light.(32)

IV push                 0.4 or 1 mg/mL undiluted over 30 seconds(32)

Intermittent infusion   Not given by this method

Continuous infusion     2 mg in 500 mL (4 mcg/mL) in D5W or NS for continuous infusion.(3,32) A solution of 8 mcg/mL
                        has been infused in an infant.(12)

Other routes of         IM, IO, and ET.(3,5) The American Academy of Pediatrics does not recommend IM or sub-Q
administration          administration due to erratic absorption(10); however, recent citations recommend IM use.(37)
                        ET use is not recommended in neonates.(37) When used, the ET dose may be 2–3 times
                        that for opiate intoxication/dependency and should be followed by a 5 mL NS flush and
                        five ventilations.(5) It has been given in adults by inhalation via nebulization in NS, but
                        evidence is lacking in the pediatric population. Naloxone use intranasally (IN) via a Mucosal
                        Atomization Device has been reported in adults in the prehospital setting, but it recom-
                        mended for use in any setting.(41) A 3-year-old (14-kg) who experienced laryngospasm
                        and excessive sedation following sufentanil was given 0.4 mg of IN naloxone using the
                        MAD.(42) When IN administered is used, 50% of the dose should be given in each nostril.

Comments                Rare adverse effects: Two adolescents developed acute pulmonary edema postopera-
                        tively after receiving 0.1 mg and 0.5 mg (200 IV and 300 mg IM) of naloxone, respec-
                        tively.(33) The pathogenesis of pulmonary edema is thought to be similar to neurogenic
                        pulmonary edema.(3) In this condition, centrally mediated massive catecholamine response
                        causes a dramatic shift of blood volume into the pulmonary vascular bed producing an
                        increased hydrostatic pressures.
                        Hypotension/hypertension, ventricular tachycardia/fibrillation, and cardiac arrest have
                        occurred in patients given naloxone for reversal of postoperative opioid depression.(3)
                        Naloxone should be used cautiously in patients with preexisting cardiac disease and in
                        those receiving medications known to cause cardiovascular effects.(3)
                        Monitoring: Signs/symptoms of acute opioid withdrawal may occur as naloxone is metabo-
                        lized; hence, patients should be continuously monitored for at least 2 hours after the last
                        dose is given.(37) Signs and symptoms of withdrawal in those who are physically dependent
                        on opioids may include, body aches, diarrhea, tachycardia, fever, runny nose, sneezing,
                        piloerection, sweating, yawning, nausea or vomiting, nervousness, restlessness or irritabil-
                        ity, shivering or trembling, abdominal cramps, weakness, and increased blood pressure.(3)
                        Neonates may also display seizures, excessive crying, and hyperactive reflexes.(3)
                        Opioid tolerance in critically ill children: One group(44) compared 14 pediatric ICU
                        patients receiving low-dose naloxone and opioid infusions (0.1 mcg/kg/hr) with 12 matched
                        controls receiving opioid infusions. Naloxone did not reduce the need for opioid during
                        the infusion period but tended to reduce opioid requirements in the postnaloxone period
                        without additional need for sedation. Another double-blinded, randomized, placebo-control
                        trial(45) was conducted in children age 1 day to 18 years requiring mechanical ventilation
                        and fentanyl infusions. There was no difference in the maximum cumulative daily fentanyl
                        dose between the naloxone (n = 37) and placebo (n = 35) groups. Total fentanyl dose
                        received throughout the study in the naloxone and placebo groups were not statistically dif-
                        ferent. The authors concluded that low-dose naloxone (0.25 mcg/kg/hr) does not decrease
                        fentanyl requirements in critically ill, mechanically ventilated children.




