  ($teddy_171_id$nesiritide$teddy_171_id$, $teddy_171_name$Nesiritide$teddy_171_name$, $teddy_171_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_171_source$, 2454455, $teddy_171$Nesiritide
Brand names            Natrecor

Medication error       Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
potential
                       USP reports that nesiritide has been confused with Nipride. USP reports that Natrecor has
                       been confused with Primacor. USP also reports that patient death resulted when Natrecor
                       was confused with Norcuron.(2)

Contraindications      Contraindications: Patients with hypersensitivity to the drug or any of its components;
and warnings           patients in cardiogenic shock or with systolic blood pressure <100 mm Hg.(3)
                       Warnings: The potential for allergic reactions, worsening of renal function and hypo-
                       tension exists. (See Significant Adverse Effects in the Comments section). Use caution
                       in patients with a previous hypersensitivity reaction to recombinant peptides. Avoid use
                       in patients with known or suspected low cardiac filling pressures or if cardiac output is
                       dependent on venous return.(3)

Infusion-related       Because hypotension is a dose-limiting adverse effect that may be prolonged, nesiritide
cautions               should not be titrated at frequent intervals as is done with other medications that have a
                       short half-life.(3) (See Significant Adverse Effects in the Comments section.)
                       Nesiritide is an E. coli-derived product; therefore, appropriate precautions should be taken
                       during infusions in case of allergic reactions.(3)

Dosage                 Per the manufacturer, safety and effectiveness in pediatric patients have not been estab-
                       lished.(3) The manufacturer only supports use in hospitalized adult patients with acute
                       decompensated congestive heart failure with dyspnea at rest.(3,16) Manufacturer recom-
                       mends against the use of nesiritide to enhance diuresis or improve renal function.(16)
                       Congestive heart failure/cardiomyopathy: An IV bolus of 1–2 mcg/kg (optional due
                       to concerns of hypotension) followed by a continuous infusion of 0.01 mcg/kg/min.(3-11,19,22)
                       Increases in continuous infusion rates by 0.005 mcg/kg/min are recommended at 3-hour
                       intervals to a maximum of 0.03 mcg/kg/min.(3-11,21)
                       Infusions of up to 0.09 mcg/kg/min have been reported in children on extracorporeal
                       membrane oxygenation (ECMO).(12) Higher dosing requirements in patients on ECMO may
                       be related to the drug’s incompatibility with heparin.
                       Pulmonary hypertension with cor pulmonale: A single case report describes a
                       4-month-old former 23 WGA baby with BPD and pulmonary hypertension who developed
                       cor pulmonale; improvement was noted during and continued after a 6-day treatment
                       with nesiritide given as a 1 mcg/kg bolus, 0.005 mcg/kg/min infusion, and repeat boluses
                       and dose increases with 0.005 mcg/kg/min q 3 hr to a max of 0.03 mcg/kg/min.(25)
                       Septic shock/ARDS: Variable results from case reports following infusions of 0.01–0.04
                       mcg/kg/min.(14,15)
                       Other: A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial of nesiritide, milrinone, or
                       placebo in patients undergoing primary Fontan surgery did not demonstrate improved
                       early clinical outcomes with nesiritide.(20)

Dosage adjustment      No dose adjustment is recommended for renal(3) or hepatic insufficiency.(4)
in organ dysfunction

Maximum dosage         The maximum recommended dose by the manufacturer is 0.03 mcg/kg/min.(3) Reports of
                       up to 0.09 mcg/kg/min in a pediatric ECMO patient treated for hypertension(12); however,
                       larger dosing requirements may be due to the drug’s incompatibility with heparin in the
                       ECMO circuit. An adult study included incremental doses up to 0.1 mcg/kg/min; however,
                       this dose was eliminated from the protocol after a case of prolonged symptomatic hypo-
                       tension, and the max dose was limited to 0.03 mcg/kg/min.(24)
                       Limited experience exists for use greater than 96 hours(3); however, it has been given for
                       up to 45 days in a patient awaiting heart transplantation.(7)




644
                                                                                                                 
                                                                                            Nesiritide
Additives               Does not contain an antimicrobial preservative.(3)

Suitable diluents       D5W, NS, D5½NS, D5¼NS. Use only preservative-free diluents.(3)

Maximum                 6 mcg/mL(3)
concentration

Preparation and         Preparation: Reconstitute nesiritide using 5 mL of suitable diluent from a 250-mL plastic
delivery                infusion container; rock gently until solution is clear and colorless; add drug back to the
                        250-mL bag to make a solution of 6 mcg/mL; invert several times to ensure adequate
                        mixing.(3)
                        Stability: Use solution within 24 hours of reconstitution.(3)
                        Compatibility: Although information on infusion compatibility is limited, known incompat-
                        ibilities include heparin, bumetanide, enalaprilat, ethacrynic acid, furosemide, hydralazine,
                        and insulin.(3)
                        Sodium metabisulfite is incompatible with nesiritide; thus, injectable drugs or solutions
                        containing sodium metabisulfite as a preservative should not be co-infused with nesiritide.(3)
                        Delivery: Prime IV tubing with 5 mL of drug solution prior to initial bolus dose or initiation
                        of the infusion. Due to its incompatibility with heparin, infusion through a heparin-coated
                        catheter is not recommended.(3)

IV push                 Loading doses of 1–2 mcg/kg should be administered over 60 seconds.(3) Bolus dosing
                        may not be appropriate in patients with systolic blood pressure <110 mm Hg or those
                        recently treated with afterload reducers (i.e., nitroglycerin).(3)

Intermittent infusion   Not recommended(3,16)

Continuous infusion     6 mcg/mL(3)

Other routes of         Not indicated
administration

Comments                Significant adverse effects
                            Worsened renal function: Concerns regarding increasing SCr in adults given the
                            recommended infusion rates prompted an FDA MedWatch to alert practitioners of its
                            potential effects on renal function.(16) It is unclear whether increases in SCr reflect
                            hemodynamic effects or renal effects.(4,16) A recent report indicated the successful
                            management of heart failure in a term infant with polycystic kidney disease without
                            worsening of renal function.(17) In hospitalized children with acute decompensated
                            heart failure, continuous infusions of dopamine or nesiritide were associated with
                            worsening renal failure.(18) Other pediatric studies show increased urine output and
                            decreased SCr; however, these may be confounded by other therapies.(22) Another
                            study reported using nesiritide to augment diuresis with favorable results.(23)
                            Increased mortality: Concerns for increased mortality rates in adults receiving
                            nesiritide have prompted further investigation into its impact on survival of patients
                            with congestive heart failure.(16)
                            Hypotension: The dose-limiting adverse effect of nesiritide. Some pediatric intensiv-
                            ists recommend that the bolus dose be eliminated and that therapy begin with a con-
                            tinuous infusion at the recommended dose rate.(7,12,21) Others continue to recommend
                            bolus dose.(6,9,10) When it occurs, hypotension may be prolonged (mean 2.2 hours);
                            therefore, observation for a prolonged period of time may be necessary.(3) Dose reduc-
                            tions, discontinuation of therapy, and/or supportive measures may be required.(3)
                            Premature ventricular contractions: Have been reported in children with conges-
                            tive heart failure.(6)




                                                                                                                645
Nesiritide
Comments (cont.)                     Monitoring: Blood pressure and SCr should be monitored closely.(3)
                                     Drug interactions: Hypotension risk is increased with concomitant use of afterload reduc-
                                     ers and medications affecting the renin-angiotensin system (i.e., angiotensin-converting
                                     enzyme inhibitors and/or angiotensin receptor blockers).(3)
                                     Other: An overdose of 36 mcg/kg was reported in a child with decompensated heart failure
                                     resulting in no hemodynamic change, renal function, and/or changes in inotropic and/or
                                     vasopressor support.(7) The patient later underwent successful heart transplantation and
                                     developed no untoward long-term effects.


