                    SandoSTATIN injection may be administered sub-Q or IV; Sandostatin LAR Depot should
                    not be given by these routes and is only given IM.(3,4)


Contraindications   Contraindications: Hypersensitivity to octreotide or any of its components.(3)
and warnings
                    Warnings: May inhibit gallbladder contraction and decrease bile secretion. Gallstones,
                    acute cholecystitis, ascending cholangitis, biliary obstruction, cholestatic hepatitis, and
                    pancreatitis have been reported in patients receiving octreotide.(3)
                    Hypoglycemia or hyperglycemia, suppression of thyroid-stimulating hormone resulting
                    in hypothyroidism, and cardiac conduction abnormalities have been reported in patients
                    receiving octreotide. These effects may be seen more frequently in patients with
                    acromegaly.(3)


Infusion-related    Allow solution to come to room temperature prior to sub-Q injection.(3) Should avoid
cautions            sub-Q injection at the same site over a short period of time; hence, sub-Q administration
                    site should be rotated. Two weeks after the initial episode of pancreatitis, the 12-year-
                    old patient developed recurrent pancreatitis that was previously treated with octreotide
                    (6 mcg/kg/day). He was started on octreotide (1.3 mcg/kg/dose q 8 hr). During admin-
                    istration of the tenth dose, he immediately experienced flushing, erythema, periorbital,
                    and perioral angioedema, difficulty breathing, cough, and abdominal pain. Octreotide was
                    stopped and pheniramine was given. His physical findings improved within 5 minutes and
                    he was back to baseline within 1 hour. He developed this reaction upon rechallenge and
                    a desensitization protocol was instituted for the duration of this octreotide treatment.(5)


Dosage              Chylothorax/chyloperitoneum: 0.3–4 mcg/kg/hr IV. Dose should be gradually
                    increase to desired effect.(6-16) Some suggest increasing the dose by 5–10 mcg/kg/day
                    at 48- to 96-hour intervals to desired effect.(18,20) Titrations up to 6–12 mcg/kg/hr have
                    been reported in infants and children.(12-17)
                    Ultimate sub-Q doses range from 10–40 mcg/kg/day in 3 divided doses sub-Q.(18-21)
                    A systematic review of 20 reports of octreotide therapy for chylothorax recommended
                    larger doses (80–100 mcg/kg/day) earlier rather than starting with a low dose and titrating
                    gradually upward.(22) Treatment duration may vary but generally occurs for 1–3 weeks.
                    Diarrhea/increased GI output: 13–200 mcg/day sub-Q or IV in 2 divided doses.(23-28)
                    Doses of 1.4–20 mcg/kg/day divided q 12 hr have been used.(23,24,26-28) Begin at the low
                    end of the dosage range and increase by 0.3 mcg/kg/dose q 3 days.(23) An alternative
                    method is 1 mcg/kg loading dose followed by a continuous infusion of 1 mcg/kg/hr.(29)
                    A recent study showed better response rate to octreotide in patients with chemotherapy-
                    associated diarrhea (92%) compared to patients with acute graft versus host disease-
                    associated diarrhea (45%). Mean starting dose in these patents was 4 mcg/kg/day (range:
                    1–23 mcg/kg/day) with a maximum dose of 9 mcg/kg/day (range: 1–49 mcg/kg/day).(30)
                    Fistula closure
                        Neonates: 1.4 mcg/kg/day sub-Q divided BID; gradually increased up to 5 mcg/kg/day
                        sub-Q divided BID.(31)
                        Children: 50–300 mcg/day sub-Q divided in 2–3 doses. Start at lower end of dosage
                        range and increase gradually.(32)




