Dosage (cont.)         GI bleeding: Bolus of 1–2 mcg/kg(33) followed by IV infusion of 1–2 mcg/kg/hr.(33,34) Older
                       children have received an adult dose (50 mcg over 5 minutes followed by 50 mcg/hr).
                       Continuous infusion can be increased every 8 hours if there is no decrease in bleeding.
                       Once there is no active bleeding, the dose is tapered by 50% every 12 hours and can be
                       stopped when the dose is 25% of initial dose.(33-35) For chronic GI bleeding, 4–8 mcg/kg/day
                       sub-Q has been given with concurrent iron therapy.(36)
                       Hyperinsulinemia (congenital)/hypoglycemia of infancy: Initial doses of 2–10 mcg/
                       kg/day in 2–6 doses sub-Q.(37-44) Doses are titrated up to 40 mcg/kg/day based on glucose
                       concentrations. Total daily dose may be given sub-Q in divided doses or continuous infu-
                       sion via a sub-Q or IV infusion pump.(28,37,40)
                       Pancreatitis/pancreatic tumors: A 10-year-old received somatostatin 1.5 mcg/kg/hr
                       IV continuous infusion for pancreatic pseudocyst.(45) A 15-month-old infant received
                       2 mcg/kg sub-Q q 6 hr that was increased up to 20 mcg/kg for recurrent acute pancre-
                       atitis and ascites.(46) A 12-year-old received a titrated dose (maximum of 75 mcg/kg/
                       dose) of octreotide sub-Q in combination with chemotherapy for a malignant pancreatic
                       carcinoid tumor.(47) Four patients with acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) who developed
                       asparaginase-associated pancreatitis were effectively treated with octreotide continuous IV
                       infusion. Octreotide was initiated at 7.2 mcg/kg/day and titrated down to 3.6 mcg/kg/day
                       for 2 days and 1.8 mcg/kg/day for 2 days once abdominal pain improved.(48) Three
                       patients with ALL who had a history of asparaginase-associated pancreatitis were treated
                       with octreotide once daily sub-Q (2.5–4 mcg/kg/day) for prevention of pancreatitis after
                       retreatment with asparaginase.(49)
                       Sulfonylurea poisoning: In children, a single dose of 25 mcg can be given IV. Dose and
                       duration vary depending on amount of sulfonylurea ingested and half-life of the agent.(50-53)
                       Continuous infusion, sub-Q, and IV are appropriate routes, and 2 mcg/kg/day sub-Q divided
                       q 12 hr has also been cited as appropriate.(50) 2 mcg/kg followed by 2 mcg/kg/hr has been
                       used in a toddler with hypoglycemia unresponsive to dextrose infusion and glucagon.(54)
                       Tall stature/excessive growth hormone (GH) secretion (use not established):
                       100–500 mcg 2–3 times daily up to 1500 mcg/day has been used but has not fully sup-
                       pressed GH levels(28,55)
                           Children: 300–600 mcg/day in 3 divided doses(28,55)
                           Adolescents: 500–1500 mcg/day in 2–3 divided doses(28)
                       One study found 37.5 mcg (prepubertal) or 50 mcg (pubertal) given via sub-Q injection
                       1 hour before bedtime was effective in children.(56) Continuous sub-Q infusion with the
                       bulk of the total daily dose given overnight has been found to be superior to sub-Q or
                       depot injection therapy.(55)


Dosage adjustment      Dosage adjustment is needed for depot injection in patients with dialysis-dependent
in organ dysfunction   renal impairment and with established cirrhosis of the liver. In adults, the adjusted dose
                       is 10 mg IM q 4 wk (50% reduction) and titrated based on response.


Maximum dosage         No data for maximum dosage in children. (See the Comments section.) In adults, the
                       maximum dose is 1500 mcg/day divided q 8 hr.(3)


Additives              Sandostatin products contain lactic acid, mannitol, phenol (in multidose vials but not
                       ampuls), and sodium bicarbonate (for pH adjustment).(3)
                       The Bedford formulation contains lactic acid, sodium chloride and sodium hydroxide (for pH
                       adjustment), and phenol (multidose vial).(4)


Suitable diluents      D5W,(3) NS(4)


Maximum                In emergency situations, octreotide may be administered undiluted.(3)
