Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(4)
                        Stability: Clear solution should be stored under refrigeration. Allow solution to come to
                        room temperature before administration. Can be stored at room temperature for up to
                        14 days when protected from light.(4)
                        Compatibility: Octreotide should not be added to PN because of the formation of glycosyl
                        octreotide conjugate(3); however, this is controversial.(4)
                        Photosensitivity: Protect from light.(4) When protected from light octreotide acetate can
                        be stored at room temperature for up to 14 days.(4)


IV push                 In emergency situations, may be administered over 3 minutes.(3,4)


Intermittent infusion   Dilute with 50–200 mL and infuse over 15–30 minutes.(3,4)


Continuous infusion     Dilute and infuse over 24 hours.(3,4)


Other routes of         Usually given by sub-Q injection.(3) Sub-Q injection sites should be rotated.(3) Use the
administration          concentration with the smallest volume to reduce injection site pain.
                        IM administration with depot form only (Sandostatin LAR Depot).(3,4) Reconstitute with
                        provided diluent and administer immediately after reconstitution into gluteal area (avoid
                        deltoid injections due to pain and discomfort at injection site).


Comments                Significant adverse effects: A systematic review of somatostatin or octreotide in infants
                        and young children reported side effects of cutaneous flush, nausea, loose stools, transient
                        hypothyroidism, elevated liver function tests, strangulation ileus (with somatostatin) and
                        transient abdominal distention, temporary hyperglycemia, and necrotizing enterocolitis
                        (NEC) (with octreotide).(6)
                        Severe paradoxical hyperglycemia and bradycardia has been reported in an infant
                        receiving octreotide 0.5 mcg q 6 hr sub-Q during subtotal pancreatectomy for congenital
                        hyperinsulinism.(58)
                        A 6-month-old infant died of fulminant colitis after receiving somatostatin analogue in
                        a dose of 18 mcg/kg/day. Prior to the infant’s death, the dose had been decreased to
                        3.5 mcg/kg/day.(59)
                        A 16-month-old with enterocutaneous fistula developed sudden abdominal pain and
                        increased nasogastric drainage and died 8 hours after receiving a single dose of 100 mcg
                        sub-Q of somatostatin analogue.(3)
                        Several reports in neonates and infants have associated the use of octreotide to the
                        development of NEC.(8,60,61)
                        Asymptomatic gallstones were found in an infant 1 year after being started on octreotide.(37)
                        Other: Tachyphylaxis is a common occurrence, requiring larger doses to maintain similar
                        effect, especially when used in patients with hyperinsulinism.(38,40,41)
                        Octreotide has been used to improve GI symptoms (abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting)
                        in a 12-year-old with bowel obstruction due to progression of adenocarcinoma despite
                        surgery and chemotherapy.(62)
                        May decrease vitamin B 12 concentrations and alter dietary fat absorption. (3) Fat
                        malabsorption documented by fecal fat testing was observed in one infant on prolonged
                        octreotide therapy.(44)




