Brand names         Zofran


Medication error    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
potential
                    USP reports that ondansetron has been confused with dolasetron, granisetron, and olan-
                    zapine.(1) No patient harm occurred. Zofran was confused with Reglan, Zantac, Zocor,
                    Zoloft, Zometa, and Zosyn and no patient harm resulted. However, Zofran was confused
                    with Zanaflex and patient harm did occur.


Contraindications   Contraindications: Patients with known hypersensitivity to the drug.(2) Concomitant
and warnings        use of apomorphine with ondansetron is contraindicated based on reports of profound
                    hypotension and loss of consciousness.(2)
                    Warnings: Hypersensitivity reactions may occur in patients with hypersensitivity to other
                    5-hydroxytryptamine 3 (5-HT3) antagonists.(2) An anaphylactoid reaction occurred in an
                    11-year-old girl.(3) Postmarketing cases of torsades de pointes have been reported. Avoid
                    use of ondansetron in patients with congenital long QT syndrome. Use ECG monitoring in
                    patients with hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, congestive heart failure, bradyarrhythmias,
                    or patients receiving medications that lead to QT prolongation.(2) Serotonin syndrome has
                    been reported with ondansetron both alone and in combination with other serotonergic
                    drugs.(2)
                    Can mask ileus and/or gastric distension in patients being treated for CINV.(2)


Infusion-related    Blood pressure changes and increased QTc interval have been reported during infusion
cautions            in adults.(4)


Dosage              Prevention of chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, ages ≥6 months:
                    0.15 mg/kg (maximum dose 16 mg) over 15 minutes given 30 minutes before chemo-
                    therapy with 2 or 3 additional doses given 4 and 8 hours after the first dose.(2,5-10) A single
                    dose of 0.6 mg/kg (≤32 mg/dose) was no more effective than 0.15 mg/kg (≤8 mg)
                    q 4 hr for 4 doses.(9) Similarly, no difference in efficacy was noted between 5 (≤8 mg)
                    and 10 mg/m2 (≤16 mg).(11) The inclusion of dexamethasone is recommended when the
                    patient is receiving moderately to highly emetogenic chemotherapy.(12)
                    In 26 children (18 months to 15 years) undergoing IT chemotherapy who received
                    0.45 mg/kg given 30 minutes prior to treatment, vomiting was decreased compared to
                    those who received 0.15 mg/kg.(11)
                    In adults, 3 doses of 0.15 mg/kg up to a maximum of 16 mg/dose. First dose should be
                    infused 15–30 minutes prior to the start of emetogenic chemotherapy, followed by sub-
                    sequent doses at 4 and 8 hours after the first ondansetron dose.(2,6) Use of single doses
                    of 32 mg is no longer recommended.(2)
                    Prevention of postoperative nausea and vomiting (PONV)
                        ≥1 month old to ≤40 kg: 0.1 mg/kg given once(2,14)
                        >40 kg to 12 years of age: ≤4 mg given once.(2,14) Others report infusing 0.025–0.15
                        mg/kg immediately before or immediately after induction of anesthesia or postop-
                        eratively in symptomatic patients.(15-23) One study noted that 0.075 mg/kg was as
                        effective as 0.1–0.15 mg/kg.(21) Administration of a second dose in patients who fail
                        to respond does not improve control.(2)
                    ED treatment of acute gastroenteritis: 0.15 mg/kg (maximum dose of 16 mg/dose),
                    infused over 2 minutes in children 1 month to 22 years, reduced the need for hospital
                    admission and decreased the number of vomiting episodes in the acute period of illness.(24,25)
                    Treatment of postanesthesia shivering: In a study with 59 children, 4 mg ondanse-
                    tron given IVP once upon completion of intravenous anesthesia was effective at treating
                    shivering.(26)




