Medication error    Use tall man letters to decrease confusion between PENTobarbital and PHENobarbital.(1)
potential
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Confusion with PENTobarbital, Phenergan, and phe-
                    nytoin sodium has been noted. Confusion with phenytoin has resulted in patient harm
                    and death.(2)

Contraindications   Contraindications: In patients with known hypersensitivity to barbiturates or any com-
and warnings        ponent of the formulation.(3) Should not be used in patients with a history of manifest or
                    latent porphyria, marked hepatic impairment, dyspnea or airway obstruction, nephritic
                    patients (large doses) or in those with a history of sedative/hypnotic addiction.(3) Should
                    not be given intra-arterially or subcutaneously.(3)
                    Warnings: Rapid administration may cause respiratory depression, apnea, laryngospasm,
                    or hypotension.(3) Paradoxical excitement may occur or important symptoms could be
                    masked when given to patients with acute or chronic pain. (See Rare Adverse Effects in
                    the Comments section for information on suicide.) Should not be used in patients with a
                    known hypersensitivity (i.e., Stevens–Johnson syndrome) reaction to other anticonvul-
                    sants.(39) May be habit forming.(3) Should be withdrawn gradually if large doses have been
                    used for prolonged periods.(3)

Infusion-related    May cause respiratory depression or apnea if administered too rapidly or when combined
cautions            with other sedatives.(3) Extravasation may cause tissue necrosis. Inadvertent intra-arterial
                    injection can cause spasms, severe pain, and other symptoms (e.g., discolored skin or
                    white hand with cyanosed skin) along the involved artery, which can result in local reac-
                    tions varying from transient pain to gangrene.(3)

Dosage              Hyperbilirubinemia: Studies evaluating the use to prevent or treat neonatal hyperbiliru-
                    binemia and decrease the need for phototherapy and exchange transfusion are mixed.(5-8)
                    5 mg/kg/day within 6 hours of life for 5 days reduced the duration of phototherapy(6);
                    however, 5 mg/kg/day for 3 days did not decrease the need for phototherapy or exchange
                    transfusion.(7) 12 mg/kg as a single dose within hours of birth significantly increased
                    the rate of bilirubin disappearance, but the effect was not noted until day 7.(8) A dose of
                    20 mg/kg followed by 5 mg/kg/day for 1 week did not produce a clinically important dif-
                    ference when used with phototherapy.(5)
                    Neonatal abstinence syndrome (NAS): An opiate is the preferred initial therapy;
                    however, addition of phenobarbital may decrease severity.(9,10) One study involving data
                    from 1424 patients with NAS from 14 children’s hospitals noted that patients treated with
                    phenobarbital had increased length of treatment, length of stay, and hospital charges when
                    compared to morphine and methadone.(40) Another randomized, open-labeled, control study
                    noted no difference in the length of stay, duration of therapy, and need for adjunctive
                    when phenobarbital and morphine were compared.(41) If symptoms persist after an initial
                    loading dose of 20 mg/kg, an additional 5 mg/kg up to a total loading dose of 40 mg/
                    kg can be given.(41) This is followed by 5–6 mg/kg/day.(11,40,41) If the severity score is <8,
                    then a serum concentration of 20 mg/L is adequate and the dose should be maintained for
                    72 hours.(11) If the score is >8, give 10 mg/kg q 12 hr until the syndrome is better or
                    serum phenobarbital concentrations reach 70 mg/L.(11,41) Once the infant is stable for 72
                    hours, the dose should be decreased 15% per day.(11) When the severity score is <8 and
                    serum phenobarbital concentration is <15 mg/L, phenobarbital should be stopped.(11)
                    Perinatal asphyxia: 40 mg/kg/dose over 60 minutes given as soon as possible has been
                    associated with a 27% reduction in seizures and significant improvement in neurological
                    outcome at 3 years of age.(13) However, a meta-analysis, from randomized controlled tri-
                    als, found little evidence to support the use of phenobarbital as prophylaxis therapy for
                    neonatal seizures.(12) Another report noted no difference in risk of death, neurodisability,
                    or subsequent seizures when phenobarbital was given.(43) Term infants with asphyxia had
                    higher trough serum concentration than those without asphyxia(14) and require about half
                    the maintenance dose of nonasphyxiated neonates.(15)




