                                                          PHENobarbital Sodium
Dosage (cont.)         Seizures—nonstatus epilepticus
                           Neonates: Loading dose of 15–20 mg/kg.(16) If there is no clinical response an
                           additional 10 mg/kg may be given. Maintenance doses of 3.5–4.5 mg/kg/day if ≤35
                           weeks and 4–5 mg/kg/day if >35 weeks gestational age.(15) Dose >5 mg/kg/day are
                           generally associated with toxicity.(16) Serum phenobarbital concentrations should be
                           >40 mg/L before an additional anticonvulsant is used.(17)
                           Infants and children: Loading dose of 20 mg/kg(25-27,48) up to 1000 mg.(26,44,47,48)
                           Some advocate an additional 20 mg/kg/dose if no response within 15 minutes.(26)
                           Maintenance doses of ≤5 mg/kg/day.(18-20) If 1–5 years of age then 6–8 mg/kg/day,
                           if 5–12 years of age then 4–6 mg/kg/day, and if 12 years of age give 1–3 mg/kg/
                           day.(19) All doses may be given BID or once daily.(18-20)
                       Seizures—status epilepticus (persistent or recurrent)
                           Neonates: Phenobarbital is the preferred first drug of choice for acute neonatal sei-
                           zureseizures,(21,5) and has been noted to be more effective than phenytoin.(46) 15–20
                           mg/kg,(16,23) or 30 mg/kg(24) has been suggested. If the clinical response is inadequate
                           give an additional 10 mg/kg. Maintenance dose of ≤5 mg/kg/day divided BID or given
                           once daily.(25)
                           Infants and children: Some suggest that phenobarbital should only be used when
                           maximum doses of benzodiazepine and hydantoin have failed.(24) Loading dose of 20
                           mg/kg(25-27,48) up to 1000 mg.(26,44,47,48) Some advocate an additional 20 mg/kg/dose if
                           no response within 15 minutes.(26)
                       Seizures—status epilepticus (refractory or superrefractory): At this point may use
                       a continuous infusion of a short-acting barbiturate. (See the Pentobarbital monograph.)(48)
                       Alternatively, given 10 mg/kg/dose q 30 min until seizures stop (maximum 80–120 mg/kg
                       in 24 hours).(28,30,47,48) (See the Maximum Dosage section.)
                           Maintenance dose: See Seizures—nonstatus epilepticus above.

Dosage adjustment      Adjust dosage using serum concentrations in patients with severe renal dysfunction.(29)
in organ dysfunction   If CrCl <10 mL/min, give 50% of normal dose q 24 hr.(29) Larger dosages are necessary
                       in children undergoing continuous cycling peritoneal dialysis,(30) CRRT(42) and in neonates
                       on extracorporeal membrane oxygenation.(31,32)

Maximum dosage         <30(24)–40(21,26) mg/kg as a loading dose. Up to 300 mg/dose(3) or 1 g/dose(22) for status
                       epilepticus. Total doses of 70–120 mg/kg were given over 24 hours to children with refrac-
                       tory status epilepticus.(28,33) Because of interpatient variability in phenobarbital elimination,
                       individualize dosage based on serum phenobarbital concentrations.

Additives              May contain benzyl alcohol and/or propylene glycol.(3) (See Appendix C for specific infor-
                       mation about benzyl alcohol propylene glycol potential for toxicity.)

Suitable diluents      May be diluted with an equal volume of D2.5W, D5W, D10W, R, LR, NS, ½NS, D-R, D-LR,
                       D-saline combinations, dextran 6%-D5W, and dextran 6%-LR.(34)

Maximum                Most note 10 mg/mL,(47) but 130 mg/mL has been reported.(34)
concentration

Preparation and        Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery               before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                       other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                       and extended stability.(34)
                       Storage/stability: Store at controlled room temperatures.(34) There was not loss of
                       drugs when phenobarbital was stored for 4 weeks under simulated summer conditions
                       (26 °C to 38 °C) in paramedic vehicle.(34)
                       Compatibility: See Appendix D or other appropriate resources for PN compatibility information.
                       Photosensitivity: Protect from light.



