IV push                              Not recommended

Intermittent infusion                <30, 60, 65, or 130 mg/mL undiluted (available commercially)(3) and infuse over 3–5
                                     minutes, not to exceed 30 mg/min(5) or 50–75 mg/min(3,25) or 1 mg/kg/min(47) to 2 mg/
                                     kg/min.(24) Large loading doses (e.g., 40 mg/kg) may be infused over 60 minutes.(13)

Continuous infusion                  No information available

Other routes of                      May be given IM deep into muscle at a volume <5 mL.(3)
administration

Comments                             Rare adverse effects
                                          Anticonvulsant hypersensitivity syndrome: An acute, life-threatening, idiosyn-
                                          cratic reaction has been reported in patients receiving phenytoin, phenobarbital,
                                          carbamazepine, primidone, and lamotrigine.(35-37) Symptoms generally develop within
                                          1–12 weeks following initiation and include a classic triad of fever, rash, and lymphade-
                                          nopathy.(35-37) Peripheral blood leucocytosis and eosinophilia and internal organ involve-
                                          ment may also be noted. Immediate discontinuation of the suspected anticonvulsant is
                                          essential for good outcome.(35-37) Cross-reactivity among the aromatic anticonvulsants
                                          has been noted; hence, these should not be used as alternative agents.(35-37,39)
                                          Suicidal behavior/ideation: The incidence of this adverse effect has been noted
                                          to be greater than placebo (0.43% versus 0.24% risk) in patients receiving 11 anti-
                                          convulsants (carbamazepine, divalproex sodium, felbamate, gabapentin, lamotrigine,
                                          levetiracetam, oxcarbazepine, pregabalin, tiagabine, topiramate, zonisamide) as
                                          either monotherapy or as adjuvant therapy for the treatment of epilepsy, psychiatric
                                          disorders, and other conditions.(38) The FDA(4) will require that the product labeling of
                                          the entire class of antiepileptics include a warning concerning the risk of suicidality.
                                     Monitoring: Heart rate, blood pressure, and EEG. Also assess for respiratory depression
                                     and sedation. Large variability exists in patient response to initial and maintenance dos-
                                     ages; individualize dosage based on serum concentration and clinical response. Thera-
                                     peutic serum concentration is 15–40 mg/L. Significantly higher serum concentrations are
                                     required if used for medically induced coma (>100 mg/L with no reflexes).
                                     Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                                     combining any drug with phenobarbital. Phenobarbital is a substrate for CYP2C9, CYP2C19,
                                     and CYP2E1.(4) Because phenobarbital induces the CYP1A2; CYP2A6, CYP2C8, CYP2C9 and
                                     CYP3A4, and UGT, it is associated with numerous drug interactions.


