Preparation and                     Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                            before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                                    other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                                    and extended stability.(9)
                                    Preparation: Must be diluted prior to administration. The addition of concentrated potas-
                                    sium solution to a maintenance fluid requires careful mixing including inversion, agitation,
                                    and kneading of the container to ensure a uniform potassium concentration throughout
                                    the fluid. Inadequate mixing may result in inconsistent potassium concentrations in the
                                    solution and deliver potassium at a rate that may exceed recommendations.(3,13)
                                    Delivery system issues: Potassium chloride injection 80 mEq/L added to dextrose 5%
                                    contained in glass bottles results in a leaching of precipitates consisting of silica and
                                    alumina.(14)
                                    Compatibility: See Appendix D and other appropriate resources for PN compatibility
                                    information.

IV push                             Contraindicated(3)

Intermittent infusion               Must be diluted prior to administration.(3) ≤0.5–1 mEq/kg/hr.(4-8)

Continuous infusion                 Usual route of administration as a component of maintenance fluids

Other routes of                     None(3)
administration

Comments                            Precautions: Use with caution in patients with renal and/or cardiac disease and in patients
                                    receiving digoxin and medications that can increase serum potassium concentration.(3)
                                    Aluminum may reach toxic concentrations following prolonged parenteral administration
                                    if kidney function is impaired. Premature neonates are particularly at risk due to kidney
                                    immaturity and their concurrent need for large amounts of calcium and phosphate solutions,
                                    which also contain aluminum.(15,16) Patients with impaired kidney function, including prema-
                                    ture neonates, who receive parenteral levels of aluminum at greater than 4–5 mcg/kg/day
                                    accumulate aluminum at levels associated with CNS and bone toxicity.(3,15)
                                    Monitoring: Clinical status, including urine output and periodic serum laboratory test (e.g.,
                                    potassium, creatinine, BUN) should be monitored.(3) ECG monitoring should accompany
                                    infusion of individual doses >0.5 mEq/kg/hr.(3)
                                    Miscellaneous: In adults, the addition of lidocaine (50 mg/65 mL of infusate) decreased
                                    the pain associated with infusion.(17)


