Medication error       High-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm if it
potential              is used in error.(1) Confusion can be associated with the use of millimoles (mmol) versus
                       milliequivalents (mEq). The most reliable method of ordering IV phosphate is by milli-
                       moles. It is important to consider the amount of potassium provided when determining
                       phosphate replacement.
                       Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Potassium phosphates have been confused with
                       potassium chloride and sodium phosphates, and patient harm did not occur.(2)


Contraindications      Administration of potassium at excessive rates may result in fatal dysrhythmias.(3) ECG
and warnings           monitoring should accompany large-dose potassium infusions.


Infusion-related       Potassium infusions may cause concentration-related phlebitis. Extravasation may cause
cautions               tissue sloughing and necrosis.(4) (See Appendix E for management of extravasation.)
                       Potassium phosphates should not be administered with calcium-containing solutions.(5)


Dosage                 Potassium phosphates are used to replace phosphate and not to replace potassium. The
                       valence of phosphate changes with pH; therefore, the preferred units in which to express
                       the amount of phosphate are mmol.(3)
                       4.4 mEq of potassium = 3 mmol of phosphate.(3,5)
                       Daily PN requirements (see the Comments section)
                           Neonates and infants: 1–2 mmol/kg/day
                           Phosphate dosing is linked to calcium dosing. In neonates, the appropriate ratio is
                           2.5 mEq calcium: 1 mmol of phosphates. The ratio gradually decreases to 1:1 as the
                           infant approaches childhood.(6,7)
                           Older infants and children: 1.5–2 mmol/kg/day(3)
                           Older children and adolescents: 15 mmol/L or per 1000 kilocalories
                       Diabetic ketoacidosis: If the phosphate concentration is low, one-third to one-half the
                       potassium deficit can be replaced as phosphates.(8) These patients should be monitored
                       for hypocalcemia and hypomagnesemia.(8,9)
                       Hypophosphatemia in critically ill adults receiving PN(10,11)
                           Mild (serum phosphate concentration of 2.3–3 mg/dL): 0.16–0.32 mmol/kg
                           over 4–6 hours
                           Moderate (1.6–2.2 mg/dL): 0.32–0.64 mmol/kg over 4–6 hours
                           Severe (<1.5 mg/dL): 0.64–1 mmol/kg over 8–12 hours


Dosage adjustment      Potassium and phosphate ions are renally eliminated; therefore, extreme caution is
in organ dysfunction   required when dosing patients with renal insufficiency.(3)


Maximum dosage         Infants receiving PN may require daily doses of up to 2 mmol/kg for optimal bone growth.(6,7)


Additives              Contains contaminant aluminum that may be toxic. If renal function is impaired, aluminum
                       may accumulate following prolonged administration. Premature neonates are particularly
                       at risk.(3)


Suitable diluents      D-LR, D-R, D-S, D5LR, D5NS, D5W, D10W, LR, NS, ½NS, or R(5)


Maximum                Peripheral IV: 40 mEq potassium/L = 27 mmol phosphates/L
