Brand names         Protopam Chloride, generic


Medication error    None reported
potential

Contraindications   Contraindications: In those with a known hypersensitivity to the drug.(1)
and warnings
                    Warnings: Should not be used for phosphorus, inorganic phosphate, or organophosphate
                    poisoning not associated with anticholinesterase activity or in the case of carbamate class
                    pesticide poisoning (may increase toxicity of carbaryl).(1)
                    Use with caution in patients with myasthenia gravis because drug may precipitate a
                    myasthenic crisis.(1)


Infusion-related    Side effects (e.g., tachycardia, laryngospasm, and muscle rigidity) may be more common
cautions            after rapid IV administration of doses larger than 30 mg/kg.(1,2) Weakness, blurred vision,
                    dizziness, headache, nausea, and tachycardia have also been noted during infusion.(1)


Dosage              Most effective if administered within 24–48 hours of exposure, but patients presenting
                    late (i.e., 2–6 days) may still benefit for use.(3,4)
                    Should be administered in combination with atropine(1); however, atropinization (e.g.,
                    flushing, mydriasis, tachycardia, dryness of the mouth and nose) may occur earlier than
                    expected when compared to the use of atropine alone. This is especially true if the total
                    dose of atropine has been large and the administration of pralidoxime chloride has been
                    delayed.
                    Chemical warfare: The dose and route of administration are based on severity of symp-
                    toms and treatment setting.(1)
                        Severe symptoms: In the outpatient setting (usually given IM) administer 25 mg/kg.
                        In the ED give 15 mg/kg as slow IV injection for both mild-to-moderate or severe
                        symptoms (e.g., strange or confused behavior, severe difficulty breathing or respiratory
                        secretions, severe muscular twitching and general weakness†, involuntary urination
                        and defecation*, convulsions, and unconsciousness).(1)
                    Organophosphate poisoning: For CNS and nicotinic (e.g., fasciculation, muscle weak-
                    ness, paralysis, and decreased respiratory effort) symptoms. A Cochrane review of available
                    randomized control trials found insufficient evidence to support benefit or harm.(11) An IV
                    loading dose followed by a continuous infusion may maintain therapeutic concentrations
                    longer than traditional intermittent administration.(1) Pralidoxime chloride is most effective
                    when administered within the first 24–36 hours of organophosphate ester ingestion.(3)
                    One study in adults noted that continuous micropump of atropine and pralidoxime chloride
                    combined is more effective than the use of repeated-bolus injection in the treatment of
                    severe acute organophosphorus insecticide poisoning.(12) Another retrospective study of
                    152 cases compared repeated pulse IM to IV of pralidoxime chloride. The group receiving
                    IM administration for severe poisoning used less atropine, had fewer atropine adverse
                    effects, and had a shorter ICU stay.(13)
                    20–50 mg/kg IV loading dose (up to 2 g/dose) over 15–30 minutes; repeat dose after
                    1–2 hours if cholinergic signs recur and then q 10–12 hr as needed.(1,3-6)
                    Follow loading dose with a continuous infusion of 10–20 mg/kg/hr for ≤60 hours while
                    symptoms persist.(1,2,6) If IV access is not possible, give 15 mg/kg (maximum 600 mg)
                    IM and repeat every 15 minutes as needed to a total dose of 45 mg/kg (2000 mg).(1)
                    *These symptoms are less reliable as they can be observed in healthy infants and young
                    children. Symptoms must be considered collectively when nerve agent or pesticide expo-
                    sure is known or suspected.
                    †Infants may become drowsy or unconscious, with muscle floppiness rather than muscle
                    twitching, soon after exposure to nerve agents or pesticides.




