Propofol
Preparation and                     Delivery system issues: To ensure proper delivery of propofol, in-line IV filters should
delivery (cont.)                    be >5 microns.(4) Do not administer through the same IV catheter with blood or plasma.
                                    Tubing and any unused portions of propofol vials should be discarded after 12 hours.(4,27)

IV push                             10 mg/mL(4) given over 20–30 seconds.(4,7-10,13) Has been given safely over 10 seconds.(9)

Intermittent infusion               Not routinely used

Continuous infusion                 2–10 mg/mL in D5W.(4) Do not dilute <2 mg/mL due to emulsion instability.(4)

Other routes of                     IM is not recommended.(4)
administration

Comments                            Rare adverse effects
                                          Cardiovascular: A 9-month-old who received large doses of propofol for 5 days for
                                          status epilepticus developed first and secondary degree atrioventricular block, widened
                                          QRS, and ventricular tachycardia.(40)
                                          Pancreatitis: Acute pancreatitis has been reported.(37-39)
                                          Propofol-related infusion syndrome (PRIS): The syndrome is characterized by
                                          severe metabolic acidosis, hyperkalemia, lipemia, dysrhythmia, rhabdomyolysis,
                                          hepatomegaly, and cardiac and renal failure. Although prolonged administration of more
                                          than 4 mg/kg/hr for >48 hours increases the likelihood, the syndrome has also been
                                          reported with large-dose, short-term infusions during surgical anesthesia.(25) Several
                                          practitioners have successfully managed PRIS with venovenous hemodiafiltration and
                                          charcoal hemofiltration or extracorporeal membrane oxygenation.(25) Plasmapheresis
                                          was not beneficial.(25)
                                          Seizures: Acute dystonia and seizures have also been reported in those with and
                                          without a history of epilepsy.(32-36) Subtherapeutic doses or abrupt withdrawal may
                                          contribute to this effect; hence, increasing the dose may terminate seizure activity.
                                    Withdrawal: The patient should be slowly weaned/tapered from propofol. Abrupt
                                    discontinuation may result in rapid awakening and/or resistance to mechanical ventila-
                                    tion. Patients may also experience, anxiety, agitation, hyperirritability, tremors, jitteriness,
                                    bardycardia and/or flushing of the hands and feet. Before weaning propofol discontinue
                                    neuromuscular blockers and opioids.(4)
                                    Monitoring: While on propofol, the patient should be monitored for clinical features (e.g.,
                                    hypotension) and laboratory findings that have been attributed to the PRIS syndrome.
                                    Should symptoms occur propofol should be discontinued. Arterial blood gases, potassium,
                                    creatine phosphokinase, aspartate aminotransferase (AST) and alanine aminotransferase
                                    (ALT), BUN, and creatinine. Serum triglyceride levels should be obtained prior to initia-
                                    tion of therapy and every 3–7 days thereafter, especially if receiving for >48 hours with
                                    doses exceeding 50 mcg/kg/min.
                                    Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations before
                                    combining any drug with propofol. Propofol is a substrate of CYPB6 (major) and a minor
                                    substrate for CYP1A2, 2A6, 2C9, 2C19, 2D6, 2E1, and 3A4. In pediatric patients, admin-
                                    istration of fentanyl concomitantly with propofol may result in serious bardycardia.(4)


