Comments                             Beginning October 8, 2009, the reference standard for heparin products changed to the
                                     World Health Organization’s International Standards (IS) unit dose definition. This is about
                                     10% less potent than the previous USP unit.(9)
                                     Adverse effects: Following cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB), a 6-week-old infant expe-
                                     rienced pulmonary hypertension and impending respiratory failure immediately after
                                     receiving protamine.(10) This infant was treated with high-frequency oscillation ventilation,
                                     inhaled nitric oxide (iNO), and prostacyclin and recovered. Following CPB, a 3-year-old child
                                     developed pulmonary hypertension after protamine was given.(11) She failed to respond to
                                     conventional treatments and was started on iNO after which the pulmonary artery pres-
                                     sures decreased. She was subsequently weaned from iNO and mechanical ventilation and
                                     was discharged on postoperative day 4.
                                     One study in adults reported that when given prior to protamine, IV famotidine, but not
                                     diphenhydramine, prevented hypotension.(12)
                                     Monitoring: Coagulation studies including activated partial thromboplastin time or acti-
                                     vated clotting time should help to guide therapy. Blood pressure should be monitored
                                     during infusion.(3,6)



