Brand names            Ceprotin


Medication error       None reported by ISMP or USP(1-3)
potential

Contraindications      Protein C concentrate is made from human plasma. While the risk of transmission of
and warnings           infectious agents is low, the product may still transmit disease.(4) Consider vaccination
                       for hepatitis A and B in patients receiving repeated doses of protein C concentrate.(4)
                       Concurrent administration with t-PA or other anticoagulants may increase bleeding risk.(4)
                       Protein C concentrate contains heparin: if heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is suspected,
                       discontinue.(4) Contains sodium >200 mg. Patients with renal impairment or on a low-
                       sodium diet should be informed.(4)


Infusion-related       The drug product may contain traces of mouse protein and/or heparin. If allergic/hyper-
cautions               sensitivity symptoms occur, the infusion should be discontinued.(4)


Dosage                 Dosage regimen (dose, frequency, duration) should be tailored to individual patient based
                       on severity of protein C deficiency, age, patient’s clinical condition, and protein C plasma
                       concentrations.(4,5) The manufacturer reports that pediatric patients from 2 days of age
                       to adolescence have been included in retrospective and prospective trials.(4) In general,
                       dosing recommendations for pediatric patients and adults are as follows:
                       Acute episodes/short-term prophylaxis(4,5)
                           Initial dose: 100–120 international units/kg
                           Subsequent 3 doses: 60–80 international units/kg q 6 hr
                           Maintenance dose: 45–60 international units/kg q 6–12 hr
                       Long-term prophylaxis(4,5): 45–60 international units/kg q 12 hr
                       After the initial dose, subsequent doses should be adjusted to maintain peak protein C
                       plasma activity of 100%. After the acute episode has subsided, continue same dose to
                       maintain trough protein C plasma activity above 25%.(4,5)
                       Purpura fulminans secondary to meningococcemia(6,7)
                           Neonates: 100 international units/kg/day divided q 4–6 hr
                           Infants, children, and adolescents: 100–150 international units/kg q 6 hr for 72
                           hours, then 50–150 international units/kg q 12 hr until target symptom resolution
                           or 7 days of treatment


Dosage adjustment      No information is known regarding dosage adjustment in organ dysfunction.(4)
in organ dysfunction

Maximum dosage         In clinical trials, doses up to 600 international units/kg/day (i.e., 150 international units/
                       kg q 6 hr) for acute episodes/short-term prophylaxis and 291.7 international units/kg/day
                       for long-term prophylaxis have been used.(4)


Additives              Contains 8 mg/mL human albumin and 8.8 mg/mL sodium; thus, the total sodium content
                       in the maximum daily dose is greater than 200 mg.(4) Monitor patients on a low sodium
                       diet and/or with renal impairment closely.(4)
                       Contains heparin; if heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is suspected, check platelets and
                       discontinue therapy.(4)


Suitable diluents      SW(4)




752
                                                                                                                  
                                                  Protein C Concentrate (Human)
Maximum                            100 international units/mL(4)
concentration

Preparation and                    Product available in 500 international units/vial and 1000 international units/vial to be
delivery                           reconstituted with 5 mL and 10 mL SW, respectively (final concentration of 100 interna-
                                   tional units/mL). Filter with filter needle provided prior to administration. Dose should be
                                   given within 3 hours of reconstitution.(4)


IV push                            Not indicated


Intermittent infusion              For patients ≥10 kg, infuse at 2 mL/min; for pediatric patients <10 kg, do not exceed an
                                   infusion rate of 0.2 mL/kg/min.(4)


Continuous infusion                An initial dose of 100 international units/kg, followed by continuous IV infusion with initial
                                   rate of 10 international units/kg/hr adjusted daily to keep plasma protein C concentration
                                   80–120 international units/mL, has been used for purpura fulminans.(8)


Other routes of                    Sub-Q infusions have been used in two children with severe protein C deficiency at doses
administration                     of 250–350 international units/kg to maintain trough protein C levels of >25 international
                                   units/dL.(9)


Comments                           Monitoring: Protein C activity (using a chromogenic assay) should be measured to tailor
                                   dosage regimen.(4) (See the Dosage section.)
                                   Drug interactions: Concomitant administration with thrombolytics or anticoagulants
                                   may increase the risk of bleeding.(4)
                                   Pharmacokinetic considerations: Protein C half-life may be decreased with acute
                                   thrombosis, purpura fulminans, and skin necrosis.(4)
                                   Pharmacokinetic parameters have been reported in a case report of two sisters who were
                                   both initiated on protein C concentrate therapy as infants for severe congenital protein
                                   C deficiency.(10)
                                   Other: Two infants with severe congenital protein C deficiency have received initial doses
                                   of 550 units (first infant) and 156 international units/kg q 12 hr (other infant) then 85–90
                                   international units/kg/dose given 3 times per week.(10)


