cautions
                       Infusions of 50 mg over <5 minutes have resulted in transient hypotension in critically
                       ill adults.(4)
                       Pain at the injection site (IV and IM administration) may occur. Burning and itching may
                       also occur with IV administration.(3)


Dosage                 Active GI bleeding, peptic ulcer disease, or hypersecretory conditions: Doses of
                       2.5–3.8 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr(5) or 3.6–4.8 mg/kg/day via continuous infusion have
                       been suggested.(6,7)
                       Prophylaxis against dexamethasone-associated ulceration in premature infants
                       with bronchopulmonary dysplasia: An infusion of 0.0625 mg/kg/hr was reported suf-
                       ficient to increase and maintain gastric pH >4.(8)
                       Prophylaxis against stress ulceration
                           Premature neonates: 1 mg/kg/day divided q 12 hr(9)
                           Term neonates: Although one study recommended 4.5 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr,(9)
                           another study reported that term neonates receiving 2 mg/kg/dose did not require
