administration

Comments                             Significant adverse effects: Bradycardia began 2 hours after ranitidine was infused in
                                     a neonate and gradually resolved within 24 hours of discontinuation.(26) No other cause
                                     of bradycardia could be determined.
                                     Ranitidine has produced a negative chronotropic effect in children receiving concomitant
                                     tolazoline.(27)
                                     Rare adverse effects: May precipitate acute porphyric attacks; do not use in patients
                                     with a history of acute porphyria.(3) H2-blocker therapy has been associated with the
                                     incidence of necrotizing enterocolitis in very low birth weight infants.(28,29)
                                     Monitoring: Increases in alanine aminotransferase (ALT) may occur when given at higher
                                     doses for 5 days or more; monitor ALT beginning at day 5 of therapy.(3)
                                     Pharmacodynamic considerations: Several critically ill children have failed to respond
                                     to 6 mg/kg/day.(12,16) Tolerance developed in five of six infants who received continuous
                                     infusion ranitidine in PN solutions for 2–6 weeks.(18)
                                     Drug interactions: Ranitidine inhibits a variety of CYP isoenzymes and is associated with
                                     numerous drug interactions.(3) Consult appropriate resources for dosing recommendations
                                     before combining any drug with ranitidine.
                                     Laboratory interference: May result in a false-positive urine protein test; testing with
                                     sulfosalicylic acid recommended.(3)


