Infusion-related    Hypersensitivity reactions have been reported.(3) (See the Dosage section.)
cautions
                    Give concomitant IV hydration.(1)
                    Anaphylaxis may occur at any point during infusion. Immediately and permanently dis-
                    continue rasburicase should anaphylaxis occur.(1)


Dosage              Indicated for the initial management of plasma uric acid levels in pediatric patients who
                    are expected to develop tumor lysis syndrome and subsequent elevations in uric acid
                    as a result of their disease (lymphoma, leukemia, and solid tumor malignancies) and/or
                    treatment regimen.(1)
                    Rasburicase may elicit an antibody response that may increase hypersensitivity reactions
                    and decrease efficacy.(3) For this reason, the manufacturer recommends that it should be
                    used for a single course of therapy only.(1) Rasburicase has been administered safely and
                    effectively to pediatric and adult patients who had received prior therapy.(3,4)
                    Hyperuricemia associated with malignancy: 0.1–0.2 mg/kg/day for up to 5 days(1-10)
                        Alternate dosing: Limited data suggest that a single dose administered prior to
                        the initiation of chemotherapy may be sufficient. Monitoring of electrolytes, uric acid
                        levels and hydration status is necessary to determine the need for additional doses.(10)
                        High risk and baseline uric acid level >7.5 mg/dL: 0.2 mg/kg once daily
                        Intermediate risk and baseline uric acid level <7.5 mg/dL: 0.15 mg/kg once daily
                        Low risk and baseline uric acid level <7.5 mg/dL: 0.1 mg/kg or 0.05 mg/kg
                        once daily
                    Chemotherapy should begin 4–24 hours after administration of rasburicase.(1)
                    Some trials have given 0.15–0.2 mg/kg q 12 hr for the first 48–72 hours in patients at
                    greatest risk of developing hyperuricemia.(3,4,6-9)
                    A single dose of 0.15 mg/kg was effective at decreasing uric acid levels in patients
                    (18 months to 72 years) with bulky tumor.(11)
                    A single dose of 6 mg has been found to be effective in adults.(12)
                    Tumor lysis syndrome (TLS) treatment: 0.15–0.2 mg/kg/dose with or without allo-
                    purinol for 1–7 days with other supportive care.(14,15) In a retrospective case series, seven
                    children with ALL and TLS received a single dose of 0.15 mg/kg, along with hyperhydra-
                    tion, antihypertensives and allopurinol. All patients had rapid and sustained lowering of
                    their serum uric acid to <4 mg/dL.(15)




