  ($teddy_207_id$rituximab$teddy_207_id$, $teddy_207_name$RiTUXimab$teddy_207_name$, $teddy_207_source$Teddy bear.pdf$teddy_207_source$, 2877421, $teddy_207$RiTUXimab
Brand names         Rituxan


Medication error    ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error.(1)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
                    ISMP and USP report that riTUXimab has been confused with InFLIXimab; patient harm
                    resulted.(2,3)
                    USP reports that Rituxan has been confused with riTUXimab; no patient harm resulted.(3)


Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Rituximab carries black box warnings for fatal infusion reactions
and warnings        (see the Infusion-Related Cautions section), hepatitis B reactivation, severe mucocutane-
                    ous reactions, and progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy.(4)
                    Hepatitis B virus reactivation can occur in patients treated with rituximab, in some cases
                    leading to fulminant hepatitis, hepatic failure, and death. All patients should be screened
                    for hepatitis B prior to treatment initiation and should be closely monitored. Discontinue
                    rituximab and concomitant medications in the event of hepatitis B reactivation.(4)
                    Severe mucocutaneous reactions with fatal outcomes have been reported. Onset is vari-
                    able but may occur as quickly as the first day of exposure to rituximab.(4)
                    Other warnings: May cause increased risk of infection, life-threatening cardiac arrhyth-
                    mias, renal toxicity, bowel obstruction, and bowel perforation.(4)
                    Tumor lysis syndrome (hyperkalemia, hypocalcemia, hyperuricemia, hyperphosphatemia,
                    and acute renal failure) necessitating dialysis to prevent renal failure has been reported
                    within 12–24 hours after the first rituximab infusion in patients with non-Hodgkin lym-
                    phoma. Treatment should include supportive care with electrolyte correction and dialysis,
                    as needed. Prophylaxis may be warranted in patients with a high tumor burden.


Infusion-related    Rituximab has been associated with the development of a fatal infusion reaction complex
cautions            (hypoxia, pulmonary infiltrates, respiratory distress, myocardial infarction, ventricular
                    fibrillation or cardiogenic shock). About 80% of these fatal reactions occur with the first
                    dose.(4) Monitor patients carefully during infusions, and discontinue infusion for severe
                    reactions.
                    Severe infusion reactions (hypotension, hypoxia, bronchospasm, or angioedema) are most
                    common with the first dose and generally occur within 30–120 minutes after beginning the
                    infusion. Treatment should include interruption of the infusion and the use of supportive
                    care measures, including oxygen, epinephrine, corticosteroids, bronchodilators, and IV
                    fluids. Often the infusion can be resumed with a 50% reduction in the infusion rate. Close
                    monitoring in subsequent courses should occur.(4)
                    Other less serious hypersensitivity reactions are common and can be treated with a 50%
                    reduction in infusion rate and acetaminophen and diphenhydramine. Bronchodilators and
                    IV saline may also be used as needed.(4)
                    Premedication with acetaminophen and diphenhydramine is recommended to reduce the
                    incidence of hypersensitivity reactions (e.g., fevers, chills, rigors).(4)
                    Patients at risk for tumor lysis syndrome should receive a uricostatic agent and aggres-
                    sive hydration.(4)


Dosage              Consult institutional protocols for complete dosing information.
                    Rituximab is part of combination therapy to treat newly diagnosed, relapsed, or refrac-
                    tory CD20 positive B-cell, non-Hodgkin lymphoma, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia
                    (ALL), as well as other hematologic disorders.




