Brand names         Naropin


Medication error    ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error.(1)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
                    USP reports that ropivacaine has been confused with bupivacaine; no patient harm
                    resulted.(2)


Contraindications   Contraindications: Ropivacaine is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity
and warnings        to the drug or any amide anesthetic.(3)
                    Warnings: Ropivacaine should only be administered by persons specifically trained in
                    the use of local anesthetics.(3)
                    The FDA notified healthcare professionals in November 2009 of the risk of chondrolysis
                    following continuous intra-articular infusion of local anesthetics, including ropivacaine, via
                    elastomeric infusion devices. The FDA has received 35 reports of chondrolysis; some were
                    in previously healthy young adults, and most following shoulder surgery. The cases had
                    received the local anesthetics for postoperative pain for periods of 48–72 hours. Chon-
                    drolysis symptoms (joint pain, stiffness, loss of motion) occurred as early as 2 (median 5)
                    months following therapy with the local anesthetic.(3,4)


Infusion-related    Not for IV infusion or IM administration. Administer only by lumbar epidural injection or
cautions            infusion, by thoracic epidural infusion, by injection for nerve block, and by infiltration.(3)
                    Accidental IV injection may result in cardiac arrhythmia or cardiac arrest, seizures, coma,
                    and respiratory arrest.(3,5)
                    Oxygen, CPR, intubation equipment, medications, and trained personnel in emergency
                    management should be immediately available when using ropivacaine.(3)


Dosage              Dose varies with the anesthetic procedure, the area to be anesthetized, the vascularity
                    of the tissues, the number of neuronal segments to be blocked, the depth and duration
                    of anesthesia required, as well as individual response.
                    A test dose of epinephrine with or without lidocaine has been used to verify that the needle
                    is not in the vascular space.(3,6,7) Once the catheter is placed, negative aspiration of blood
                    or cerebrospinal fluid(3,8,9-11) and the absence of cardiovascular changes following a test
                    dose indicates correct position of the catheter.(3,11,12) (See Significant Adverse Effects in
                    the Comments section.)
                    The manufacturer does not recommend the use of ropivacaine in pediatric patients.(3)
                    Caudal/epidural block: Children 1.25–6.5 mg/kg(6,8,9,11-14) or 1 mL/kg of 0.2% solution(5)
                    Epidural continuous infusion
                        Children 4 months to 7 years: A loading dose of 1–1.4 mg/kg followed by 0.2–0.4
                        mg/kg/hr for 48 hours.(10,13) One group of investigators reported continuous infusion
                        up to 96 hours without adverse events.(10)
                        Children from 7–12 years: Twenty-four children assigned to receive a patient-
                        controlled epidural infusion consisting of bolus doses of 4 mg (lockout 10 minutes) with
                        a continuous infusion of 3.2 mg/hr (total hourly ropivacaine could equal 27.2 mg, if
                        needed) required less drug than 24 children assigned to receive a continuous epidural
                        infusion of 0.4 mg/kg/hr (239 mg/48 hr compared to 576 mg/48 hr, respectively).(7)
                        Adults: A loading dose of 20–40 mg (10–20 mL of 0.2% solution) followed by 12–28
                        mg/hr (6–14 mL/hr of 0.2% solution) for up to 72 hours(3)
                    Local anesthesia: 16 children ages 9.9 ± 2.9 years undergoing maxillar alveolar graft-
                    ing received 0.4–0.8 mg/kg followed by 0.2 mg/kg/hr through a catheter positioned at
                    an iliac crest donor site for up to 48 hours for postoperative analgesia.(15)




