                    5% saline = 855 mEq/L


Medication error    ISMP high-alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error (hypertonic solutions [greater than 0.9% saline]).(1)
                    USP reports that sodium chloride has been confused with potassium chloride. Sodium
                    chloride has been confused with sodium bicarbonate and patient harm resulted.(2)
                    ISMP reports that use of hypotonic saline solutions postoperatively resulted in acute
                    hyponatremia and death in two previously healthy children.(3)


Contraindications   A variety of sodium concentrations are available. The bulk packages are concentrated and
and warnings        are not intended for direct infusion.(4) Choose products carefully.


Infusion-related    During chronic hyponatremia, shifts of water and sodium occur to maintain normal cel-
cautions            lular volume in the brain.(5) Too rapid correction of hyponatremia can result in osmotic
                    demyelination syndromes (central pontine myelinolysis; extrapontine myelinolysis).(5-10)
                    Similarly, during chronic hypernatremia the brain generates idiogenic osmoles to maintain
                    cerebral intracellular osmolality. Too rapid decreases in serum sodium may cause water
                    to move into the brain cells resulting in cerebral edema, seizures, and coma.(5,6)
                    In general, the serum sodium should not be increased or decreased ≥10–12 mEq/L per
                    day in these chronic conditions.(5,6)


Dosage              Maintenance IV fluids: A Cochrane review concluded that isotonic IV maintenance fluids
                    with sodium concentrations similar to plasma decrease risk of hyponatremia compared to
                    hypotonic fluids (in first 24 hours of administration) in primarily surgical populations age
                    >3 months.(11-13,25,26) Subsequently published trials also support the use of isotonic fluids
                    in critically ill patients(27) as well as a general pediatric (medical, not critically ill) popula-
                    tion.(28) In a neonatal study, term newborns with hyperbilirubinemia experienced a higher
                    incidence of hyponatremia when receiving D5W 0.2% NaCl but also a higher incidence of
                    hypernatremia when receiving D5W NS.(14)
                    Hypotension/shock: 20 mL/kg of NS over 5–10 minutes that may be repeated. Up to
                    60–80 mL/kg (or more) may be required during the first hour; however, the child should
                    continuously be assessed for fluid overload.(15,16) In one study, hypertonic (3%) NaCl 15
                    mL/kg over 30 minutes compared to NS 20 mL/kg (× 1 or 2 boluses) as the initial fluid
                    bolus regimen in pediatric patients with septic shock resulted in less total volume required
                    for resuscitation (23 versus 29 mL/kg) but also a significantly higher increase in serum
                    sodium concentration (10 versus 5 mEq/L).(17)
                    Dehydration: 20 mL/kg of NS over 20 minutes; may be repeated to restore intravascular
                    volume.(6) In severe dehydration, multiple boluses that are infused rapidly may be required
                    to prevent shock.(6) After intravascular volume is restored, correction of the fluid deficit
                    should begin along with providing maintenance fluids.
                    Symptomatic hyponatremia: The initial goal of sodium replacement is symptom reso-
                    lution and not correction of the serum sodium to normal. As an estimate, 1 mL/kg of
                    3% NaCl will raise the serum sodium by 1 mEq/L.(5) More aggressive replacement may
                    be required in patients with severe symptoms such as seizures.(5) Following resolution of
                    symptoms, the infusion should be adjusted to increase serum sodium concentration by no
                    more than 10–12 mEq/L per day.(5,6) Frequent monitoring of serum sodium concentrations
                    is essential. (See the Comments section.)




