Sodium Nitroprusside
Comments (cont.)                    Thiocyanate is a product of cyanide metabolism and a route for cyanide elimination.
                                    Sodium thiosulfate has been administered with sodium nitroprusside at infusion rates of
                                    5–10 times that of nitroprusside to accelerate the metabolism of cyanide to thiocyanate;
                                    however, further study is needed before this can be recommended.(3)
                                    Rare adverse effects: A single case report describes the development of rash leading to
                                    toxic epidermal necrolysis within 3 minutes of starting a sodium nitroprusside infusion.(26)
                                    Monitoring: Acid-base balance and venous oxygen concentration may suggest cyanide
                                    toxicity. Blood pressure should be continuously monitored, preferably intra-arterially.(3)
                                    Cyanide and thiocyanate serum concentrations may be warranted in patients receiving
                                    extended therapy (>24–72 hours),(8,21) doses >2 mcg/kg/min, or with renal(29) or hepatic
                                    dysfunction.(3)


