Preparation and         Protect from excessive room light and sunlight during preparation. Use two-step process
delivery                for preparation: (1) dilute vial of temsirolimus with 1.8 mL of supplied diluent to produce
                        30 mg/3 mL (mix well and allow air bubbles to subside); (2) withdraw the required amount
                        of concentrate-diluent mixture as prepared in step 1 and further dilute into a 250-mL bag
                        of NS (mix by inversion and avoid excessive shaking; protect from excessive room light
                        and sunlight; administer within 6 hours).(1)
                        Administration materials should be glass, polyolefin, or polyethylene to avoid excessive
                        loss of product and diethylhexyl phthalate (DEHP) extraction.(1) When PVC administration
                        has to be used, it should not contain DEHP.(1)
                        Use with an in-line polyethersulfone filter with pore size <5 microns.(1)


IV push                 Not recommended


Intermittent infusion   Infuse over 30–60 minutes weekly via infusion pump.(1)


Continuous infusion     Not recommended


Other routes of         Not recommended
administration

Comments                Significant adverse effects: Hyperglycemia, immunosuppression, interstitial lung
                        disease, hyperlipidemia, bowel perforation, renal failure, wound healing complications,
                        intracerebral hemorrhage have been reported.(1) Patients should be closely monitored.
                        The most common adverse reactions (≥30%) are rash, asthenia, mucositis, nausea,
                        edema, and anorexia.(1)
                        There is a low risk for emesis.
                        The most common laboratory abnormalities (≥30%) are anemia, hyperglycemia, hyper-
                        lipidemia, hypertriglyceridemia, lymphopenia, elevated alkaline phosphatase, elevated
                        SCr, hypophosphatemia, thrombocytopenia, elevated AST, and leukopenia.(1)
                        Significant drug interactions: Strong inducers of CYP3A4/5, such as dexamethasone,
                        carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, rifampin, and rifampicin, may decrease expo-
                        sure of the active metabolite (sirolimus). A dose adjustment should be considered.(1,6)
                        Concomitant use of St. John’s Wort should be avoided due to unpredictable temsirolimus
                        concentrations.(1)
                        Strong inhibitors of CYP3A4 such as atazanavir, clarithromycin, indinavir, itraconazole, keto-
                        conazole, nefazodone, nelfinavir, ritonavir, saquinavir, and telithromycin may increase con-
                        centrations of the active metabolite sirolimus. A dose adjustment should be considered.(1,7)
                        Concomitant use of temsirolimus and sunitinib has resulted in dose-limiting toxicity requir-
                        ing hospitalization.(1)
                        Concomitant use of temsirolimus and valproic acid has been shown to significantly decrease
                        the MTD of temsirolimus, with mucositis as the major dose-limiting toxicity.(8)
                        Other: Live virus vaccines (measles, mumps, rubella; varicella; rotavirus) should be
                        avoided during treatment.(1)




