Comments                          Adverse effects: Increased heart rate,(3,6,8,15,16,23-25) increased systolic and decreased dia-
                                  stolic blood pressure,(8,15) tremor,(3,8,17,23-25) headache,(3,8,23-25) and hypokalemia(3,16,23-25) have
                                  been reported. An 11-year-old who received 10 mcg/kg/min for 6 hours had no evidence
                                  of arrhythmias, and creatine phosphokinase MB isoenzyme (CPK-MB) was normal.(15) How-
                                  ever, in that same study three children receiving a lower terbutaline dose (two were also
                                  receiving aminophylline) did have an elevated CPK-MB concentration. Two of 18 children
                                  developed ST segment depression following terbutaline and epinephrine. There was no
                                  correlation between CPK values and arrhythmias or ST changes. Elevated troponin levels
                                  have also been reported.(11,27) A prospective evaluation of IV terbutaline’s effect on cardiac
                                  parameters found no clinically significant cardiac toxicity; patients in this study received
                                  a mean maximum 2.4 mcg/kg/min for a mean duration of 53 hours.(13)
                                  The incidence of tremor and tachycardia may be increased in those receiving theophylline
                                  concomitantly.(8)
                                  Bruising, tenderness, and site infection have been reported with continuous sub-Q
                                  infusion.(17)
                                  Pharmacokinetics: Although larger doses are used in children than in adults, there is
                                  no pharmacokinetic basis to support the need for larger doses.(21)
                                  Drug interactions: Terbutaline may increase theophylline clearance.(22) (See Adverse
                                  Events above.)
                                  Monitoring: Blood pressure should be monitored frequently. ECG and oxygen saturation
                                  should be monitored continuously. Consider monitoring serum potassium due to the
                                  potential risk of hypokalemia.(7)


