Dosage (cont.)          Glaucoma or cataract surgery complicated by hyphema and/or fibrin: Intracam-
                        eral injection of 5–25 mcg postoperatively.(45-47) An additional dose may be required.(47)
                        Myocardial infarction: A 7-year-old child with a thrombosed coronary aneurysm was
                        given 5 mg IV push <1.5 hours after arrival, followed by 0.75 mg/kg over 0.5 hour, then
                        0.5 mg/kg over 1 hour and symptoms resolved.(48)
                        Intrapleural fibrinolysis: For the treatment of complicated parapneumonic effusions
                        or empyemas in infants as young as 3 months and children, the most common regimens
                        included 0.1 mg/kg up to a max of 6 mg diluted in 25–100 mL NS(49) or alternatively a
                        standard dose of 4 mg diluted in 30–50 mL NS.(50,51) The dose was instilled into the chest
                        tube and the tube was clamped for 1 hour then returned to suction.(49-51) Multiple doses
                        were often required, ranging from 1–7 doses per patient.(49-51)
                        Intrapericardial fibrinolysis: In a 4-month-old with a fibropurulent pericardial effusion
                        due to Staphylococcus aureus, 1 mg in 5 mL NS was instilled via the pericardial drain,
                        clamped for 2 hours (during which the patient was rotated side-to-side q 15 min), then
                        was unclamped and allowed to drain. The dose was repeated 12 hours after the initial dose
                        and again the next day. Of note, the initial dose was intended to be a higher volume, but
                        the patient experienced significant hemodynamic instability after 8 mL had been instilled;
                        2 mL were withdrawn thereby alleviating the symptoms. Caution must be exercised when
                        administering doses via this method. This patient experienced complete resolution of the
                        effusion 48 hours after starting therapy.(52)
                        Infective endocarditis: Seven high-risk infants with overwhelming sepsis were treated
                        with 0.2–0.3 mg/kg/hr × 6 hours with the dose then increased to 0.5 mg/kg/hr and con-
                        tinued until vegetations were no longer visible by echocardiogram.(53)


Dosage adjustment       None needed for renal dysfunction.(54) However, caution should be exercised in patients
in organ dysfunction    with hemostatic defects that might be related to renal or hepatic disease.(4)


