 Brand names         Generic

 Medication error    None reported
 potential

 Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning: Patients with impaired renal function and those receiving large
 and warnings        doses or prolonged therapy have an increased risk for nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity
                     (i.e., vestibular and auditory), of which ototoxicity may be irreversible.(2) The risk of
                     toxicity can also be increased by prolonged elevations in serum concentrations; concur-
                     rent use of medications known to be nephro-, neuro-, or oto-toxic; dehydration; and
                     advancing age.(2) (See Rare Adverse Effects and Monitoring in the Comments section.)
                     Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity reaction to tobramycin or other
                     aminoglycosides.(2)
                     Other warnings: Aminoglycosides can cause neuromuscular blockade and potentiate
                     the effects of neuromuscular blockers.(2,4,5) If neuromuscular blockade occurs, calcium
                     salts may reverse it.(2) (See Appendix C for specific information.)

 Infusion-related    None
 cautions

 Dosage              Although use has been questioned in pediatric patients, the following equation is recom-
                     mended in adult obese patients receiving aminoglycosides.(2,6,7,75)
                     Dosing weight = IBW + 0.4 (TBW – IBW). (See Appendix B.)
                     Loading dose: Limited data are available, but some advocate an initial 3–5 mg/kg dose
                     in neonates and infants.(8-13)
                     Neonates
                         Maintenance dose: Many studies using various dosing methods for aminoglycosides
                         (mostly studied with gentamicin) have been reported in neonates. Most reports have
                         based recommendations on either weight and postnatal age (PNA)(15-21) or gesta-
                         tional age.(13,14,22-36) Use of dose and interval equations based on AUC have also been
                         recommended in the preterm neonate.(37,38) Traditional dosing (2.5 mg/kg/dose) in
                         premature neonates has become less commonly recommended with large dose,
                         extended interval regimens becoming accepted practice. PNA at which any of these
                         regimens are applicable has not been well studied, but the few studies delineating
                         a PNA have generally been ≤7 days of age.(13,19,22,23,25-28,31,34,37)
                         Based on age and weight
                         Traditional dosing: 2–2.5 mg/kg q 8–24 hr. Interval based on PNA and weight.(2,15,16)

                                                PNA <7 days                             ≥7 days
                                    <1200 g                  ≥1200 g                   >1200 g
                                    5 mg/kg               4.5–5 mg/kg                  5 mg/kg
                                 q 48 hr(17-19,21)       q 24–48 hr(17-21)         q 24–36 hr(17,18,20)
                         Based on weeks gestational age (WGA)

                                     <30 WGA                 30–34 WGA                >34 WGA
                                    2.5–3 mg/kg             2.5–3 mg/kg               2.5 mg/kg
                                  q 18–36 hr(14,31-35)   q 16–24 hr(14,31-34,36)   q 12–24 hr(14,31-34)
                                                                  OR
                                     4–5 mg/kg               4–5 mg/kg                  4 mg/kg
                                  q 36–48 hr(25,28-30)    q 24–48 hr(25,29,30)       q 24 hr(13,22-27)
                     Infants and children: 6–7.5 mg/kg/day divided q 8 hr(2,17) or 5–7.5 mg/kg q 24 hr(17,76)
                     Cystic fibrosis: 10 mg/kg q 24 hr(2,54,55,77-79)




830
                                                                                                             
                                                                 Tobramycin Sulfate
Dosage adjustment     Based on traditional dosing: If CrCl is >50 mL/min, give a normal dose q 8 hr; if CrCl is
in organ              between 30–50 mL/min, give a normal dose q 12–18 hr; if CrCl is between 10–29, give
dysfunction           normal doses q 18–24 hr; and if CrCl is <10 mL/min, give a normal dose q 48–72 hr.(56)

Maximum dosage        200 mg/dose in adults.(2) Individualize dosage based on serum concentrations.(2)

Additives             The premixed product contains 15.4 mEq of sodium/100 mL.(59) The 1.2 g/30 mL
                      vials contain 23.5 mg (1.02 mEq) sodium and the 80 mg/2 mL vials contain 1.6 mg
                      (0.07 mEq) of sodium.(2) Contains sodium bisulfite.(2,59) (See Appendix C for specific
                      information about sulfite hypersensitivity.)

Suitable diluents     D5W, NS(2,59)

Maximum               Dose in 50–100 mL of diluent or a volume allowing accurate measurement and delivery
concentration         over 20–60 minutes.(2,59)

Preparation and       Delivery system issues: The mixing of tobramycin with a beta-lactam antibiotic in
delivery              vitro can result in substantial inactivation of the aminoglycoside.(59) (See Appendix C for
                      more specific information.)
                      Compatibility: See Appendix D for PN compatibility information.(60)

IV push               Not recommended. Although aminoglycosides have been administered over 15 seconds,(63)
                      1 minute,(62) and 3–5 minutes,(61,64) it is not recommended.

Intermittent          Over 20–60 minutes(59)
infusion

Continuous infusion   Not recommended. Aminoglycosides have been given by continuous infusion,(66,67) but
                      nephrotoxicity may occur more frequently.(67)

Other routes of       40 mg/mL may be given IM.(2,59) Solutions prepared from or commercially available in bulk
administration        packages, ADD-Vantage vials, and premixed solutions in NS should not be given IM.(2,59)

Comments              Rare adverse effects: Aminoglycosides accumulate in renal cortical tissue and may
                      damage proximal tubule cells leading to oliguric renal failure. Risk of nephrotoxicity may
                      be influenced by type of aminoglycoside, dose, duration (cumulative dose), frequency
                      of therapy and elevated serum concentration.(2,3) It is also increased by advancing age,
                      preexisting renal or hepatic dysfunction, decreased renal perfusion, hypoalbuminemia,
                      and dehydration.(2,3) Current administration of nephrotoxic medications may increase
                      the risk of toxicity and should be avoided.(2)
                      Cochlear and/or vestibular ototoxicity has been associated with all aminoglycoside anti-
                      biotics.(4) Total AUC may be a better indicator of ototoxic risk than either peak or trough
                      serum concentration.(68,69) Use cautiously when giving with other drugs known to cause
                      ototoxicity.
                      Aminoglycosides may potentiate the curare-like effects on the neuromuscular junction
                      and should be used cautiously in patients with neuromuscular disorders since they may
                      aggravate muscle weakness.(2,4) Aminoglycosides may enhance the respiratory depres-
                      sant effect of neuromuscular-blocking agents and may prolong blockade.(2,5)
                      Monitoring: Because large variability exists in patient response to therapy, individualize
                      dosage based on serum concentrations, clinical response, and renal function. Recom-
                      mended peak and trough serum tobramycin concentrations are 4–12 mg/L and <2 mg/L,
                      respectively.(2)
                      Although serum concentration monitoring has become routine practice in many institu-
                      tions, not all patients require monitoring.(72,73)
                      Drug interactions: Consult appropriate resources before combining any drug with
                      tobramycin.
                      Laboratory interference: Serum concentrations may be falsely elevated when blood
                      samples are collected through central venous Silastic catheters.(74)


