Brand names         Cyklokapron, generic

Medication error    Cyklokapron may be confused with cycloSPORINE.(1)
potential

Contraindications   Contraindications: Patients with acquired defective color vision (due to inability to moni-
and warnings        tor for toxicity), patients with subarachnoid hemorrhage, patients with active intravascular
                    clotting, and patients with hypersensitivity to the drug or any ingredient.(2)
                    Warnings: Focal retinal degeneration has developed in animals at doses ranging from
                    3–40 times the recommended human dose. Although retinal changes have not been
                    reported in humans, vision changes have been reported. The manufacturer recommends
                    ophthalmological exam before and regularly during treatment for patients who are to
                    receive tranexamic acid continually for more than several days.(2)
                    Convulsions have been reported.(2) Venous and arterial thrombosis or thromboembolism
                    has been reported.(2)

Infusion-related    Hypotension may occur with rapid infusion; administer at a maximum rate of 100 mg/min.(2)
cautions

Dosage              Reduction of blood loss during and after congenital heart disease repair
                        Neonates: Bolus dose of 100 mg/kg after induction of anesthesia,(3,4) either with(3)
                        or without(4) an additional dose of 100 mg/kg into the cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB)
                        circuit, followed by a continuous infusion of 10 mg/kg/hr during the operation(3,4)
                        has been used in neonates. A lower dose (50 mg/kg as a slow infusion just prior to
                        surgical incision)(5) has also been studied in neonates but was found to reduce blood
                        loss only in patients with cyanotic heart disease or those undergoing a second tho-
                        racotomy. A pharmacokinetic study performed in 55 patients ages 2 days to 4 years
                        old recommends the following regimens to achieve targeted tranexamic acid serum
                        concentrations throughout surgery(24):
                            Target 20 mcg/mL: Loading dose, 15 mg/kg over 15 minutes; infusion, 2.5 mg/
                            kg/hr during surgery; CPB prime dose, 20 mcg per mL of CPB prime volume.
                            Target 60 mcg/mL: Loading dose, 50 mg/kg over 15 minutes; infusion, 7 mg/
                            kg/hr during surgery; CPB prime dose, 60 mcg per mL of CPB prime volume.
                            Target 150 mcg/mL: Loading dose, 120 mg/kg over 15 minutes; infusion, 17 mg/
                            kg/hr during surgery; CPB prime dose, 150 mcg per mL of CPB prime volume.
                        Infants, children, and adolescents: In patients undergoing repair of cyanotic heart
                        defects, the following regimens have been reported. Initial doses (after anesthesia
                        induction) of 10 mg/kg,(6-8) 50 mg/kg(5,9) or 100 mg/kg,(4,10,11) with a dose into the
                        cardiopulmonary bypass circuit of 10 mg/kg(6-8) or 100 mg/kg,(10,11) then followed
                        either by a continuous infusion of 1 mg/kg/hr for 8 hours(6) or 10 mg/kg/hr during the
                        operation.(4,11) Instead of a continuous infusion, some centers reported an additional
                        bolus dose at the end of bypass (10–20 mg/kg,(6-8) 50 mg/kg,(9) or 100 mg/kg).(10)
                        Each of these studies reported efficacy versus placebo, or similar efficacy compared
                        to aprotinin (no longer available on the U.S. market.)
                        In patients undergoing repeat thoracotomy, initial dose of 100 mg/kg with 100 mg/
                        kg into the cardiopulmonary bypass circuit and 10 mg/kg/hr until transfer to the ICU
                        reduced blood loss and transfusion requirements compared to placebo.(11)
                        A pharmacokinetic study in patients 1–12 years old undergoing CPB recommended the
                        following to achieve serum concentrations of 20 mcg/mL throughout surgery: (weight
                        5 to 40 kg) loading dose 6.4 mg/kg over 5 minutes followed by infusion of 2 to 3.1
                        mg/kg/hr (see the Comments section for specific infusion recommendations in 5-kg
                        weight increments).(25)
                        A pharmacokinetic study performed in 55 patients ages 2 days to 4 years old recom-
                        mended the following regimens to achieve targeted tranexamic acid serum concen-
                        trations throughout surgery(24):
                            Age <2 months: See Neonates in the Dosing section.




