                         14 mg/kg/hr during surgery; CPB prime dose, 150 mcg per mL of CPB prime volume.
                 Reduction of blood loss during and after craniofacial surgery: Loading dose 15–100
                 mg/kg over 15–20 minutes followed by 5–10 mg/kg/hr during surgery was associated
                 with reduced blood loss and/or reduced transfusion requirements in two randomized,
                 double-blind trials(12,13) and one retrospective study.(14) Authors of one of these random-
                 ized, controlled trials recommend, based on pharmacokinetic analyses, a loading dose of
                 10 mg/kg over 15 minutes followed by 5 mg/kg/hr to achieve steady-state concentra-
                 tions above 16 mcg/mL (presumed therapeutic target to eliminate plasminogen-induced
                 platelet activity in vitro) without producing high peak concentrations such as those that
                 occur after loading doses of 50 mg/kg.(26)
                 Reduction of blood loss during and after spine surgery: Loading dose 10–100 mg/
                 kg (one study limited the loading dose to 2 g in adults)(15-18) over 15 minutes(17,18) fol-
                 lowed by 1–10 mg/kg/hr (one study limited the infusion to 100 mg/hr in adults) during
                 surgery(16-18) or continued 5 hours after surgery(15) was associated with reduced blood loss
                 and/or reduced transfusion requirements in three randomized, double-blind trials(15,17,18)
                 and one retrospective study.(16)
                 Reduction of blood loss during surgery in heparinized patients: One study of
                 10 patients reported the use of tranexamic acid 4–10 mg/kg initial dose with 2 mg/kg/
                 hr for 24–48 hours (one patient received 15 mg/kg/dose q 8 hr for 48 hours) in patients
                 undergoing surgical repair of congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) while heparinized
                 (activated clotting times 180–200 seconds) and receiving extracorporeal membrane
                 oxygenation (ECMO). Authors reported reduced blood loss and transfusion requirement
                 compared to historical patients who did not receive tranexamic acid.(19) Another study also
                 reported on the use of tranexamic acid during and after surgical repair of CDH in patients
                 on or off ECMO. The typical regimen was a loading dose of 4 mg/kg 1 hour before surgery
                 followed by 1 mg/kg/hr for 24 hours postoperatively.(20)
                 Tooth extraction in patients with hemophilia: 10 mg/kg immediately before extrac-
                 tion (together with replacement therapy), and 10 mg/kg/dose q 6–8 hr short-term
                 (2–8 days) to reduce or prevent hemorrhage and reduce the need for replacement therapy
                 during and following tooth extraction.(2)
                 Traumatic injury with significant bleeding: Consensus recommendation by Royal
                 College of Paediatrics and Child Health: 15 mg/kg (max 1 g) over 10 minutes followed by
                 2 mg/kg/hr for 8 hours or until bleeding stops. Initiate within first 3 hours after trauma. (27)
                 In a military setting, 1 g bolus doses within the first 3 hours after trauma and repeated
                 at the discretion of the physician have been used in injured children; tranexamic acid
                 was independently associated with reduced mortality (odds ratio 0.27, 95% confidence
                 interval 0.85–0.89).(28) In adults with trauma and significant hemorrhage, 1 g over
                 10 minutes followed by 1 g over 8 hours, starting within 8 hours of trauma, reduced all
                 cause mortality (CRASH-2 trial).(29)




