administration

Comments                Significant adverse effects: Whether tranexamic acid, when used to reduce blood loss
                        in pediatric cardiac surgery, is more prone to adverse effects including renal insufficiency
                        or seizures compared to similar agents such as aminocaproic acid or aprotinin (no longer
                        available on the U.S. market), is controversial. A large multicenter analysis including over
                        20,000 patients(22) suggested that tranexamic acid had more favorable outcomes compared
                        to aprotinin (in-hospital mortality, bleeding requiring surgical intervention, ICU length of
                        stay, and the requirement for dialysis). Conversely, a single-center study reported better
                        clinical outcomes for aprotinin compared to tranexamic acid (better hemostasis, reduced
                        inotropic requirement, and shorter duration of mechanical ventilation) without any increase
                        in thrombotic events or acute kidney injury.(3) One group found a trend toward increased
                        seizures in patients receiving tranexamic acid compared to aprotinin (n = 4 patients versus
                        0 patients, respectively; p = 0.14).(9) The same group reported a retrospective analysis
                        that found no difference in safety outcomes (renal failure or seizures) between patients
                        receiving tranexamic acid or aminocaproic acid.(23)
                        Pharmacokinetic considerations: Molecular weight 157.2(2)
