Brand names             THAM (no longer available in the United States)


Medication error        Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
potential
                        ISMP reports that tromethamine may be confused with Trophamine.(1)


Contraindications       Contraindications: Uremia and anuria.(2) Also contraindicated in neonates with chronic
and warnings            respiratory acidosis and salicylate intoxication.(2)
                        Warnings: Hypoglycemia can occur in preterm and term neonates.(2)


Infusion-related        Extravasations may result in tissue necrosis, severe inflammation, and sloughing.(2-5)
cautions                Extravasations may be treated with procaine 1% added to hyaluronidase or with phentol-
                        amine infiltrated around the site.(3) For infusion by peripheral vein, an indwelling catheter
                        in a large vein in an elevated limb is recommended to minimize irritation.(2)
                        Infusion via low-lying umbilical catheters may result in hepatocellular necrosis.(2) (See
                        the Comments section.)
                        Rapid infusion may result in prolonged hypoglycemia.(2) Three of four adults given tro-
                        methamine to evaluate its effects on blood chemistries experienced decreases in blood
                        glucose, which resulted in persistent symptoms for 24 hours in one adult.(6)
                        Too rapid administration may decrease the respiratory drive resulting in respiratory
                        depression and apnea.(7)


Dosage                  Doses (mL) are based on 0.3 M solution; 100 mL = 3.6 g, 30 mEq, 30 mmol.(2)
                        Neonates with acidosis associated with respiratory distress syndrome: 1 mL/kg for
                        each pH unit <7.4.(2,8) One study reported using doses of 1.8–4.7 mmol/kg in six neonates
                        (26–37 weeks gestational age [WGA]).(9)
                        Infants and children: Dose (mL) = base deficit (mEq/L) × body weight (kg) × 1.1(2)


Dosage adjustment       Contraindicated in patients who are anuric or uremic(2)
in organ dysfunction

Maximum dosage          In neonates, the maximum dose is 5-7 mmol/kg/day.(4)
                        In adults, 35 g (291 mmol)/hr(2) or 15 mmol/kg/day.(4,10)


Additives               Glacial acetic acid is added to decrease the pH to 8.6.(2) The buffering capacity is decreased
                        about 10% with this addition.(2)


Suitable diluents       None noted


Maximum                 Undiluted(2)
concentration

Preparation and         Vials do not contain preservatives and are intended for single use.(2)
delivery

IV push                 Not indicated


Intermittent infusion   Over >1 hour.(2) In acute acidosis, 25% to 50% of the calculated deficit can be given over
                        5–10 minutes with the remainder infused over >1 hour.(4,5)




