Brand names         Depacon, generic


Medication error    None
potential

Contraindications   U.S. boxed warning
and warnings
                        Hepatic failure: Fatalities have occurred in patients receiving valproic acid and its
                        derivatives and usually occurs during the first 6 months of treatment.(2) Serious or
                        fatal hepatotoxicity may be preceded by nonspecific symptoms such as malaise,
                        weakness, lethargy, facial edema, anorexia, and vomiting. Because of their increased
                        risk for hepatotoxicity, valproate should be used cautiously in children <2 years of
                        age, especially if they are receiving multiple anticonvulsants, have congenital meta-
                        bolic disorders, severe seizures accompanied by mental retardation, or organic brain
                        disease.(3-6)
                        Mitochondrial disease: An increased risk of valproate-induced acute liver failure
                        and subsequent death has been reported in patients with hereditary neurometabolic
                        syndromes caused by DNA mutations of the mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma
                        (POLG) gene (e.g., Alpers-Huttenlocher syndrome). Valproate sodium injection is
                        contraindicated in patients known to have mitochondrial disorders caused by POLG
                        mutations and children under 2 years of age who are clinically suspected of having a
                        mitochondrial disorder. In patients over 2 years of age who are clinically suspected
                        of having a hereditary mitochondrial disease, valproate sodium injection should only
                        be used after other anticonvulsants have failed.(2)
                        Pancreatitis: Life-threatening pancreatitis has been reported in both children and
                        adults and has occurred shortly after initial use and after several years of use.(2,7-9) Some
                        patients have developed hemorrhagic pancreatitis that progressed rapidly from onset
                        of symptoms to death. If pancreatitis is diagnosed, valproate should be discontinued.
                        Teratogenicity: Congenital malformations such as neural tube defects (e.g., spina
                        bifida) have been reported following chronic therapy to the mother.(2)
                    Contraindications: Should not be administered to patients with hepatic disease or sig-
                    nificant hepatic dysfunction. It is contraindicated in patients with known hypersensitivity
                    to the drug and should not be given to patients with known urea cycle disorders.(2)
                    Other warnings: Thrombocytopenia, hypothermia, and hyperammonemia may occur.(2)
                    (See Rare Adverse Effects for information on suicide in the Comments section.)


Infusion-related    Severe vein irritation and pain at the injection site may occur following administration of
cautions            undiluted solution.(2) Compartment syndrome has been reported in an adult.(10) Hypotension
                    and arrhythmia have been reported following rapid administration.(2) (See Hypotension
                    and Arrhythmia in the Comments section.)


Dosage              Generally should not be used in infants <2 years of age unless other alternatives have
                    failed and the benefits clearly outweigh the risk.(2) IV use for >14 days has not been
                    studied(2); convert to oral administration as soon as possible.
                    Migraine headache: IV valproate has been safely and effectively used in adults(11-13) and
                    adolescents.(14) Initial doses of 1000 mg were infused at 50 mg/min in 31 adolescents.(14)
                    When response was not sufficient, a second 500 mg/dose was given.(14) In another study,
                    patients were given a loading dose of 20–40 mg/kg followed by a continuous infusion of
                    1–1.5 mg/kg/hr.(15)
                    Seizures (acute, nonstatus epilepticus)
                        Replacement therapy: Individuals chronically receiving oral valproate may be con-
                        verted to IV valproate using the same total daily dose (1:1 conversion); however, it
                        should be administered q 6 hr or by continuous infusion.(2)




846
                                                                                                                  
                                                                        Valproate Sodium
Dosage (cont.)             Children (≥2 years) and adolescents(56): A loading dose of 20–40 mg/kg followed
                           by 10–15 mg/kg administered q 6 hr; repeat loading dose and increase maintenance
                           dose until seizures are controlled or the patient exhibits unacceptable toxicities.(2,16-20)
                           Maintenance dose in children ≥2 years: 30–60 mg/kg/day divided q 6 hr(15,17-20,22,23)
                       Seizures (refractory generalize convulsive status epilepticus): Many guidelines
                       recommend valproate as a second-line agent in refractory status epilepticus.(53-55)
                           Neonates and infants: Although IV valproate has been used safely in neonates to
                           treat acute seizures and status epilepticus,(24,25) it should not be used unless all other
                           options have failed. A single dose of 10–25 mg/kg was given and an apparent Vd of
                           0.245 L/kg and clearance of 25 mL/hr/kg/hr were estimated.(24) The elimination of val-
                           proate is significantly reduced in neonates and infants; therefore, doses may need to
                           be administered less frequently than in older children. If used, it should be given with
                           levocarnitine. (See Other in the Comments section and the Levocarnitine monograph.)
                           Children (≥2 years) and adolescents(56,57): A loading dose of 20–40 mg/kg up to
                           500 mg/dose. If seizures continue after 15 minutes, a second loading doses should
                           be given. Loading dose should be followed by 10–15 mg/kg administered q 6 hr.(2,16-21)
                           Some have given a loading dose of 20–40 mg/kg followed by a continuous infusion
                           of 1–1.5 mg/kg/hr.(15)
                           Maintenance dose in children ≥2 years: 30–60 mg/kg/day divided q 6 hr(15,17-20,22,23)
                       If the patient is on valproate monotherapy or other anticonvulsants that do not induce
                       the cytochrome P450 system, begin with 1 mg/kg/hr; if the patient is on one or more
                       inducers (e.g., phenobarbital, phenytoin, rifampin), begin with 2 mg/kg/hr; if the
                       patient is receiving inducers and is in pentobarbital coma, begin with 4–6 mg/kg/hr.(25)
                       One group recommends beginning at 5 mg/kg/hr; once the patient is seizure free for
                       6 hours, the dose should be reduced by 1 mg/kg/hr q 2 hr.(27)
                       Seizures (nonconvulsive status epilepticus): Children ≥2 years and adolescents
                       should be given a loading dose of 13.4–25 mg/kg(22,26) followed by 4 mg/kg q 6 hr.(26)
                       Seizures (prophylaxis for post-traumatic epilepsy): Because a clinical study in adults
                       with head trauma showed a higher mortality rate than that observed for phenytoin, IV
                       valproate is not recommended for seizure prophylaxis following head trauma.(2,31)


Dosage adjustment      No dosage adjustment necessary in renal dysfunction.(2) However, renal failure may cause
in organ dysfunction   a disproportionate increase in free-to-total valproate ratios, causing total serum concen-
                       trations to be misleading.(2,33) Patients on intermittent hemodialysis should be given a
                       normal dose post dialysis.(32) Should not be administered to patients with hepatic disease
                       or significant hepatic dysfunction.(2)


Maximum dosage         Has not been established. A loading dose of 40 mg/kg(24) and a continuous infusion of 6 mg/
                       kg/hr have been used in children.(23) A dose of 240 mg/hr has been given to a 67-year-old
                       adult.(34) Children receiving one or more medications known to increase the metabolism of
                       valproate may require doses up to 100 mg/kg/day, while children in pentobarbital coma
                       may require larger doses (150 mg/kg/day).(25,35)


Additives              None


Suitable diluents      Has been given undiluted; however, severe vein irritation occurred. Administer in a 1:1
                       or 2:1 dilution in at least 50 mL of D5W, NS, or LR.(2)


Maximum                25–50 mg/mL(2)
concentration




