Valproate Sodium
Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(50)
                        Stability: When stored in glass or PVC bags at room temperature (15°C to 30°C) the
                        product is stable for at least 24 hours.(2) Any unused portion of a vial should be discarded.(2)


IV push                 Although not recommended by the manufacturer,(2) has been given as a bolus without
                        adverse effects: loading dose of 20–40 mg/kg over 1–5 minutes.(31) One report gave
                        7.5–41.5 mg/kg at a rate of 1.5–11 mg/kg/min(36) and 10 mg/kg/min up to 30 mg/kg.(37)
                        (See Rare Adverse Effects in the Comments section.)


Intermittent infusion   60 minutes (but not more than 15–20 mg/min)(2,15) or 1–6 mg/kg/min.(24,30,38) (See Rare
                        Adverse Effects in the Comments section.)


Continuous infusion     2–4 mg/mL in D5W or NS at a rate of 1–6 mg/kg/hr(2,25)


Other routes of         None reported
administration

Comments                Rare adverse effects
                            Hepatic failure: See U.S. Boxed Warning in the Contraindications and Warnings sec-
                            tion. Some have advocated that the concomitant administration of L-carnitine may
                            improve survival of those with severe valproate-induced hepatotoxicity. L-carnitine
                            may enhance beta-oxidation of valproate, thereby limiting cytosolic omega-oxidation
                            and the production of toxic metabolites that have been implicated in liver toxicity and
                            ammonia accumulation.(49)
                            Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS)(51-53): DRESS
                            is an acute, life-threatening, idiosyncratic reaction that’s includes a classic triad of
                            fever, rash, and lymphadenopathy and may involve multisystem organ failure (e.g.,
                            liver, kidney, heart, lungs). Historically, DRESS has been noted in patients receiving
                            anticonvulsants containing an aromatic structure (i.e., phenytoin, phenobarbital,
                            carbamazepine, primidone, and lamotrigine). Although valproate is structurally unre-
                            lated, as it is a nonaromatic drug, cases of DRESS have been reported following oral
                            therapy. Median time to develop of dermatologic symptoms (SJS, TEN) is 21 days
                            following initiation, but cases have occur with 1–40 days.(2) Immediate discontinuation
                            of valproate is essential for good outcome and high-dose steroids may be indicated.
                            Although the existence of cross sensitivity with other drugs know to cause DRESS has
                            not been established it would be best to avoid these agents if possible.(2)
                            Hypotension and arrhythmia: The manufacturer’s recommendation to infusion over
                            60 minutes is predicated on concerns about hemodynamic instability. An 11-year-old
                            girl who received 30 mg/kg (480 mg) over 20 minutes (0.5 mg/kg/hr) developed
                            significant, but reversible, hypotension 12 minutes into the infusion.(38) Another
                            11-year-old girl developed hypotension 20 minutes after receiving 30 mg/kg (960 mg)
                            over 60 minutes.(30) Others have noted that 11 mg/kg/min(14) and 1.5–11 mg/kg/
                            min(29,30,39-41) did not produce hypotension, bradycardia, or arrhythmias. In one clini-
                            cal safety study, about 90 patients with epilepsy were given a single infusions of (up
                            to 15 mg/kg and mean dose of 1184 mg) over 5–10 minutes (1.5–3 mg/kg/min).(2)
                            Patients generally tolerated the more rapid infusions.(2)
                            Thrombocytopenia: About 25% of those given 50 mg/kg/day developed thrombo-
                            cytopenia (platelet counts <75 × 109/L).(2,42,43) The likelihood increases when total
                            serum valproate concentrations exceed 100 mg/L.




848
                                                                                                                     
                                                                                                 Valproate Sodium
Comments (cont.)                    Hyperammonemia: Despite normal liver function tests, hyperammonemic encepha-
                                    lopathy accompanied by unexplained acute alterations in level of consciousness and/
                                    or cognitive function with lethargy or vomiting have been reported.(2) An ammonia level
                                    should be measured and valproate should be stopped if the level is elevated. Concomitant
                                    administration of topiramate and valproic acid has been associated with hyperammone-
                                    mia with or without encephalopathy in patients who have tolerated either drug alone.(2,44)
                                          Hypothermia (unintentional drop to <35°C): Has occurred in patients receiving
                                          valproate and those given concomitant topiramate with valproate.(2,45,46) It has been
                                          noted in patients with and without hyperammonemia.(2)
                                          Suicidal behavior/ideation: The incidence of this adverse effect has been noted
                                          to be greater than placebo (0.43% versus 0.24% risk) in patients receiving 11 anti-
                                          convulsants (carbamazepine, divalproex sodium, felbamate, gabapentin, lamotrigine,
                                          levetiracetam, oxcarbazepine, pregabalin, tiagabine, topiramate, zonisamide) as
                                          either monotherapy or as adjuvant therapy for the treatment of epilepsy, psychiatric
                                          disorders, and other conditions.(2) The FDA will require that the product labeling of
                                          the entire class of antiepileptics include a warning concerning the risk of suicidality.
                                    Monitoring: Liver function test should be performed before initiation of valproate and
                                    frequently during therapy.(2) Screening for POLG mutation should be performed in accor-
                                    dance with current clinical practice.(2) Although dosing is often guided by total serum val-
                                    proate concentrations (50–150 mg/L), the relationship between serum concentration and
                                    clinical response is not well documented. The variability in serum concentrations may be
                                    attributed to the nonlinear, concentration-dependent protein binding of valproate, which
                                    affects clearance. Patients planning surgery should have platelet count and coagulation
                                    parameters monitored before surgery.(2)
                                    Pharmacokinetics: Infants ≤2 months of age have a decreased ability to eliminate
                                    valproate compared to older children and adults. This is attributed to a combination of
                                    immature development of glucuronosyltransferase and other hepatic enzyme and an
                                    increased volume of distribution due to decreased plasma protein binding.(47) Pediatric
                                    patients (i.e., between 3 months and 10 years) have 50% higher clearances expressed
                                    on weight (i.e., mL/min/kg) than do adults.(18,19) Over the age of 10 years, children have
                                    pharmacokinetic parameters that approximate those of adults.(18,19)
                                    Drug interactions: Because valproate inhibits CYP2C9, epoxide hydroxylase, and beta-
                                    oxidation, it is associated with numerous drug interactions.(48) Valproate can also serve as
                                    a substrate and can be inhibited or induced by other drugs. Consult appropriate resources
                                    for dosing recommendations before combining any drug with valproate.


