Dosage (cont.)          Bacterial endocarditis (prophylaxis): A single dose of vancomycin 30–60 minutes
                        before the procedure may be indicated in the highest risk patients.(18) (See the Com-
                        ments section.)
                        Bacterial endocarditis (treatment): 60 mg/kg/day divided q 6 hr, up to 2 g/dose.
                        Duration is based on the source of infection.(3,19)
                        Ventricular shunt infection: ISMP lists intraventricularly administered medications as
                        high-alert and have an increased risk of causing significant patient harm if it is used in
                        error.(26)
                        5–10 mg/dose intraventricularly q 24 hr(27-29,61) may be administered in conjunction with
                        60 mg/kg/day divided q 6 hr by IV administration.(28,29)


Dosage adjustment       Adjust dosage in renal dysfunction.(30) Initial dose should be at least 15 mg/kg even in
in organ dysfunction    mild-to-moderate renal impairment.(2) If CrCl is 30–50 mL/min, give a normal dose q 12 hr;
                        if CrCl is 10–29 mL/min, give normal dose q 18–24 hr; and if CrCl is <10 mL/min, adjust
                        dosage based on serum concentrations.(30)
                        Infants on extracorporeal membrane oxygenation have an increased circulating volume
                        and transiently altered renal function; therefore, a suggested dose is 20 mg/kg q 24 hr.(31)
                        Patients with malignancy (mean dose of 71.5, up to 90 mg/kg/day)(32,33,63) and burns
                        (up to 90–100 mg/kg/day)(62) may have increased clearance and require larger doses.
                        Although serum vancomycin concentration should not be routinely monitored, trough
                        concentration may be helpful in patients who are not clinically responding or are receiv-
                        ing unusually high doses.


Maximum dosage          Although higher doses have been used, 60 mg/kg/day (not to exceed the adult dose of
                        4g/day) is still recommended.(3,15)


Additives               None


Suitable diluents       NS, LR, D5W, D5NS, D5LR(2,34)


Maximum                 <5 mg/mL.(2,34) May use 10 mg/mL in fluid-restricted patients, but the risk of infusion
concentration           reactions increase.(2)


Preparation and         Delivery system issues: Vancomycin PVC containers have been reported to leach less
delivery                than detectable concentrations.(34)
                        Compatibility: See Appendix D for PN compatibility information.(36)


IV push                 Not recommended(2)


Intermittent infusion   Over ≥60 minutes or ≤10 mg/min in adults.(2) (See the Infusion-Related Cautions section;
                        see Rare Adverse Effects in the Comments section.)


Continuous infusion     May be given by continuous IV infusion.(34) Vancomycin has slow bactericidal activity and
                        exhibits time-dependent killing. Some have proposed that administration by continuous infu-
                        sion would maximize the time that vancomycin serum concentrations exceed the minimum
                        inhibitory concentration for the suspected organism and thereby enhance efficacy, prevent
                        bacterial regrowth, and perhaps decrease toxicity.(38,39) Data for the administration of vanco-
                        mycin by continuous infusion are extremely limited and have focused on adults(37,38,40-42) and
                        neonates.(39,43,64) Neonates have received a loading dose(44,65) followed by 10–50 mg/kg/day
                        by continuous infusion. (39,43,44) A pediatric study reported using 40 mg/kg/day
                        in adolescents and infants and 55–60 mg/kg/day in other children by continuous infu-
                        sion.(66) A higher rate of nephrotoxicity occurred in adult patients receiving continuous
                        infusions producing steady state concentrations ≥28 mg/mL.(42)




