Brand names         Vasostrict


Medication error    ISMP high alert medication that has an increased risk of causing significant patient harm
potential           if it is used in error.(24)
                    Look-alike, sound-alike drug names
                    USP reports that vasopressin has been confused with desmopressin and oxytocin. Pitressin
                    has been confused with pitocin.(1)


Contraindications   Contraindications: Anaphylaxis or hypersensitivity to vasopressin or any of its
and warnings        components.(2)
                    Warnings: May worsen cardiac output in patients with altered cardiac response.(2)


Infusion-related    Extravasation of IV infusion may result in tissue necrosis.(3) (See Appendix E for extrava-
cautions            sation treatment.)


Dosage              Note that dosing UNITS are often different among indications (e.g., unit/kg/min versus
                    unit/kg/hr) and may or may not be based on patient weight.
                    Catecholamine-refractory shock: 0.0002–0.004 unit/kg/min (0.2–4 milliunit/kg/min)
                    titrated up to a usual dose of 0.0005–0.002 unit/kg/min (0.5–2 milliunit/kg/min) and
                    maximum reported doses of 0.008–0.02 unit/kg/min (8–20 milliunits/kg/min) based on
                    desired hemodynamic response.(2,7-9,28,29) In an adolescent, 0.04–0.06 unit/min has been
                    used.(8) In a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial, 69 pediatric patients
                    with vasodilatory shock received a median 0.0011 unit/kg/min (1.1 milliunit/kg/min)
                    with interquartile range 0.0007–0.0018 unit/kg/min (0.7–1.8 milliunits/kg/min). This
                    study found no difference (vasopressin versus placebo) in time to achieve hemodynamic
                    stability, vasoactive-free days, organ failure-free days, ventilator-free days or ICU dura-
                    tion. Additionally, mortality was 30% in the vasopressin group compared to 15.6% placebo
                    (p = NS).(10) In adults with shock, continuous low-dose infusion of 0.01–0.1 unit/min in
                    conjunction with other pressors is supported.(2,11,12) However, the VASST trial, a multi-
                    center, randomized, double-blind trial comparing vasopressin 0.01–0.03 unit/min to nor-
                    epinephrine for patients with septic shock already receiving norepinephrine, found that
                    vasopressin did not reduce 28-day mortality.(13)
                    Diabetes insipidus
                        Continuous IV infusion: Initially 0.0005 unit/kg/hr (0.5 milliunit/kg/hr) doubling
                        the dose q 30 min(4) or increasing by 0.0005 unit/kg/hr (0.5 milliunit/kg/hr) every
                        5–10 minutes(33) until urine output is <2 mL/kg/hr(4,33) and urine osmolality is twice
                        that of plasma osmolality.(4) The dosage required rarely exceeds 0.01 unit/kg/hr
                        (10 milliunit/kg/hr).(4,34) A 3-day-old baby and a 3-year-old child were started on
                        0.003 unit/kg/hr (3 milliunit/kg/hr). Polyuria resolved and the drug was weaned over
                        several days.(5)
                        IM or sub-Q injection: Previous manufacturer (no longer producing vasopressin)
                        prescribing information included IM and sub-Q dosing. In adults, 5–10 unit BID–
                        TID as needed. The manufacturer recommends that doses for pediatric patients be
                        proportionately reduced.(6,30) Desmopressin, a synthetic analog of ADH with potent
                        antidiuretic but no vasopressor activity, has reduced the need to use vasopressin for
                        this indication.
                    Hypotension (as first-line therapy): A small randomized, blinded pilot study compared
                    the use of vasopressin (n = 10) to dopamine (n = 10) as the first-line treatment option
                    of hypotension in extremely low birth weight infants and found no difference in percent
                    responders to therapy and time to adequate mean blood pressure.(31)
                    Hemodynamic support after cardiac surgery: Patients (n = 19) receiving low-dose
                    vasopressin (initial dose 0.0003 unit/kg/min then titrated to hemodynamic goals) starting
                    in the operating room after a Norwood or arterial switch procedure received decreased
                    fluid resuscitation and catecholamines in the first 24 hours postoperatively.(32)




