Dosage (cont.)         Pulmonary hypertension: 0.02 unit/kg/hr increased daily by 0.01 unit/kg/hr to a
                       maximum of 0.04 unit/kg/hr was started in a 5-month-old infant with bronchopulmo-
                       nary dysplasia and pulmonary arterial hypertension unresponsive to inhaled nitric oxide
                       resulting in improvement in oxygenation index and blood pressure.(25) A case series of 10
                       neonates with refractory persistent pulmonary hypertension and systemic hypotension
                       demonstrated an improvement in oxygenation index, blood pressure, and urine output
                       with vasopressin use (0.0002 unit/kg/min ± 0.0002 unit/kg/min increased by 0.0001
                       unit/kg/min every hour as needed for clinical response, maximum dose of 0.0012 unit/
                       kg/min; median duration of 49 hours).(26)
                       Gastrointestinal (GI) bleeding (other therapies have decreased the use of vaso-
                       pressin for this indication)
                           Age-based(14)
                               0.1 unit/min as a continuous infusion; increased by 0.05 unit/min each hour up
                               to a maximum of
                               <5 years: 0.2 unit/min
                               5–12 years: 0.3 unit/min
                               >12 years: 0.4 unit/min
                           Weight-based
                               An initial infusion of 0.3 unit/kg (up to 20 units) infused over 20 minutes may
                               be used prior to a continuous infusion of 0.2–0.4 unit/1.73 m2/min. The infusion
                               is maintained for 12 hours after bleeding stops and then tapered to off over the
                               next 24–36 hours.(15)
                               0.002–0.005 unit/kg/min up to a maximum of 0.01 unit/kg/min was studied in
                               17 children (mean age 7.2 years).(16) Doses higher than 0.01 unit/kg/min were
                               no more efficacious at controlling bleeding but resulted in significantly more
                               complications (electrolyte disturbances, fluid overload, hypertension, cardiac
                               dysrhythmias).
                       Cardiac arrest: 0.4–1 unit/kg bolus (maximum 40 units/dose),(3) although the Pediatric
                       Advanced Life Support (PALS) authors found insufficient evidence to recommend for or
                       against the use of vasopressin.(17) A four-patient case series found evidence that vasopres-
                       sin administration may be beneficial during prolonged pediatric cardiac arrest.(18) Results
                       from the American Heart Association National Registry of Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation
                       demonstrate an association with vasopressin use and worse return of a sustained circu-
                       lation for pediatric in-hospital cardiac arrest; however, several limitations of the registry
                       have been identified allowing for a potential patient selection bias.(19) The 2015 American
                       Heart Association (AHA) Guidelines Update for Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation and Emer-
                       gency Cardiovascular Care, recommend against using vasopressin over epinephrine or
                       with epinephrine in adult cardiac arrest.(35)


Dosage adjustment      Vasopressin is primarily metabolized by the kidney and liver. No specific dosage adjust-
in organ dysfunction   ment recommended in renal(20) and liver dysfunction; titrate to effect.


Maximum dosage         Shock: 0.02 unit/kg/min was used in extremely low birth weight infants.(29) 0.1 unit/min
                       reported in adults.(2)
                       Diabetes insipidus (IV): 0.01 unit/kg/hr (10 milliunits/kg/hr)(4,34)
                       GI bleeding: ≥0.01 unit/kg/min has been used but resulted in significantly more adverse
                       effects.(16)


Additives              Contains chlorobutanol as a preservative.(2)


Suitable diluents      D5W, NS(2)




