Brand names            Calan, generic


Medication error       High-alert medication associated with an increased risk of significant patient harm should
potential              an error occur.(1)


Contraindications      Contraindications: Known hypersensitivity to verapamil or any of components of the IV
and warnings           formulation.(2) Severe hypotension or cardiogenic shock, second- or third-degree atrioven-
                       tricular (AV) block and/or sick sinus syndrome (unless patient has a pacemaker), severe
                       coronary heart failure, treatment with beta blockers (should be administered hours apart),
                       atrial flutter or atrial fibrillation with an accessory bypass tract (Wolff-Parkinson-White
                       syndrome,(2-4) and/or ventricular tachycardia.
                       Use should be restricted to facilities that are prepared to continuously monitor ECG and
                       perform resuscitation including direct-current cardioversion.(2,6)
                       Continuous ECG monitoring should be performed during infusion.(6,7)


Infusion-related       Hypotension, bradycardia, tachycardia, asystole, and arrhythmias may occur, particularly
cautions               in patients with sinus node dysfunction or impaired AV conduction.(2)
                       Calcium chloride 10 mg/kg should be at the bedside in the event of hypotension. (See
                       the Calcium Chloride monograph.)(2)


Dosage                 The 2010 PALS guidelines state that verapamil should not be used in infants during CPR
                       because it may cause refractory hypotension and cardiac arrest.(7) The 2015 Neonatal(28) and
                       PALS(29) guidelines do not contain any information on varapamil. Verapamil is not included
                       in the 2015 American Heart Association Guidelines for Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation and
                       Emergency Cardiovascular Care.(30)
                       Calcium chloride 10 mg/kg should be at the bedside in the event of significant hypoten-
                       sion. (See the Calcium Chloride monograph.)(2)
                       Tachyarrhythmias (SVT, atrial fibrillation/flutter)
                           Neonates and infants (0–1 year): Although verapamil has been used in this age
                           group, severe hemodynamic side effects including death have been reported(9-12);
                           hence, it should not be used unless an ICU physician or cardiologist is present and
                           continuous ECG monitoring is used.Doses of 0.1–0.2 mg/kg (usual single dose of
                           0.75–2 mg).(2,13-17) If necessary, the dose can be repeated 30 minutes after the initial
                           dose.(2,15)
                           Children 1–15 years: Initially given IV/IO at doses of 0.1–0.3 mg/kg(2,6,7,13-16,18)
                           (usual single dose of 2–5 mg; maximum 5 mg).(2) If necessary, the dose (≤10 mg)
                           can be repeated one time 30 minutes after the initial dose.(2)


Dosage adjustment      Only 3% to 4% pf verapamil is excreted unchanged in the urine, and only one metabolite
in organ dysfunction   (i.e., norverapamil) retains 20% of the vasodilating activity of the parent drug. Most refer-
                       ences suggest there is no need to modify dose in those with renal dysfunction. Use with
                       caution and monitor ECG (for PR prolongation) and blood pressure for signs of toxicity in
                       patients with renal insufficiency.(32)
                       A report has suggested the need to decreased the dose by 50% in adults with cirrhosis.(19)


Maximum dosage         Neonates and infants: 0.2 mg/kg and ≤2 mg/dose(2)
                       1–15 years: 0.3 mg/kg and ≤10 mg/dose(2)
                       An 18-year-old with anticholinergic-induced torsades de pointes and prolonged QT interval
                       unresponsive to standard therapies was successfully treated with 3 doses of 5 mg (0.1 mg/kg)
                       verapamil followed by an infusion of 5 mcg/kg/min.(20)




