                           for 2 weeks
                       Decrease morbidity from bronchopulmonary dysplasia/chronic lung disease in
                       preterm infants: 2000 units (n = 20) or placebo (n = 20) IM was given to very low birth
                       weight (VLBW; 700–1300 g) neonates with increased risk for bronchopulmonary dysplasia
                       (BPD)/chronic lung disease (CLD) on day 4 of life and then every other day for 28 days.
                       Vitamin A status was improved and morbidity associated with BPD/CLD was decreased.(3)
                       In a larger, multicenter study, either 5000 units (n = 405) or placebo (n = 402) was given
                       IM 3 times weekly for 4 weeks in extremely low birth weight (ELBW; 401–1000 g) infants
                       beginning on day of life 1–4. Compared to controls, the treated infants had improved
                       vitamin A serum concentrations, and the combined incidence of death or CLD at 36 weeks
                       postmenstrual age was decreased in treated infants versus controls (55% versus 62%,
                       respectively).(4) A follow-up study evaluating the long-term outcome when these infants
                       were 18–22 months adjusted age found no differences in neurodevelopmental outcomes
                       or pulmonary morbidity between the vitamin A group and controls.(5)
                       One group recommended the following regimen for VLBW infants at high risk of BPD/CLD(6):
                       2000 units/kg/dose IM on day of life 1 and every other day until enteral feedings account
                       for 75% of total energy intake, at which time the route is changed to enteral and the dose
                       increased to 4000 units/kg/dose given until discharge from the neonatal ICU. Should the
                       patient be placed NPO, the IM dose and route are resumed. Vitamin A and retinol binding
                       protein concentrations should be measured weekly. (See the Comments section.)
                       Retinopathy of prematurity (prevention): A randomized, double-blinded, placebo-
                       controlled trial in 89 infants <32 WGA and/or <1501 g birth weight received 10,000 units
                       IM 3 times a week starting on day 2 until oral supplements started (or until a max of
                       12 doses) compared to placebo. Treated infants were not less likely to develop retinopa-
                       thy of prematurity but did have improved retinal sensitivity, suggesting potential longer
                       term benefit.(9)


