Brand names            Mephyton, generics


Medication error       Look-alike, sound-alike drug names. Vitamin K has been confused with Urocit-K and that
potential              phytonadione has been confused with Coumadin and phenytoin sodium.(1)


Contraindications      U.S. boxed warning: Severe reactions, including fatalities, have been reported with
and warnings           IV administration even when the product is diluted and infused slowly. Severe reactions
                       have also been noted following IM administration.(23) Usually these resemble anaphylaxis
                       or hypersensitivity type reactions.(23) Restrict IV and IM use to situations when the sub-Q
                       route is not feasible.(2)
                       Contraindications: Hypersensitivity to phytonadione or any of its components.(2)


Infusion-related       IV use should be restricted to times when sub-Q or IM administration in not feasible. If
cautions               used, it is imperative to weigh the risk. (See U.S. Boxed Warning in the Contraindications
                       and Warnings section.)(2)


Dosage                 Sub-Q or intramuscular administration is preferred to IV.(16) Vitamin K should not be given
                       intramuscularly to children on anticoagulants because of the risk of intramuscular hemor-
                       rhage; may be given IV.(13)
                       Maintenance nutritional requirements: For IV administration as part of parenteral
                       nutrition, infants should receive 10 mcg/kg/day and children should be given 200 mcg/
                       day.(19) Rate of administration should not exceed 1 mg/min.(2)
                       Prothrombin deficiency (e.g., malabsorption syndromes, broad spectrum
                       antibiotics)
                           Older children and adolescents: 2 mg(11,12)
                       Reversal of vitamin K antagonist (e.g. warfarin): Limited data are available in children.
                       30 mcg/kg IV.(13,14,20) In adults, if major bleeding is present, regardless of INR, administer
                       prothrombin complex concentrate, human and large doses of vitamin K (5–10 mg IV).(20)
                       Vitamin K deficiency (neonatal hemorrhagic disease)
                           Prophylaxis: Given IM within 1 hour of birth(2,3)
                               ≤1500 g: 0.2–1 mg IM as a single dose(2-6,24)
                               >1500 g: 1 mg IM as a single dose(2-6)
                           An initial prophylactic dose of 0.2 mg IM maintained vitamin K sufficiency in preterm
                           infants 32 weeks’ gestation until at least the 4th postnatal week and did not cause
                           overload of the hepatic vitamin K1-recycling pathway in the first week of life.(24)
                           Treatment: 1 mg sub-Q; however, larger doses may be needed if the mother was
                           receiving anticoagulant therapy.(2,4,7-10)
                       Vitamin K deficiency (non-neonatal)
                           Biliary atresia: If INR >1.5, give 5 mg IM and follow with oral therapy.(21)
                           Chronic cholestasis: Normally treated with oral vitamin K, but 5 mg has been given
                           IV in a 5-year-old with severe bleeding diathesis.(22)


