Maximum                 10 mg/mL (undiluted). Generally given IM when used in neonates.(2)
concentration

Preparation and         Parenteral products should be visually inspected for particulate matter and discoloration
delivery                before use. Refer to appropriate references for more information on compatibility with
                        other drugs and solutions; compatibility following Y-site delivery, and suggested storage
                        and extended stability.(16)
                        Stability: Available as a clear yellow liquid. Administration should begin immediately after
                        dilution and unused product should be discarded.(16)
                        Compatibility: See Appendix D and other appropriate resources for PN compatibility
                        information.
                        Photosensitivity: Protect from light at all times.(2) Infusion solutions containing phyto-
                        nadione should be wrapped in an opaque material (e.g., aluminum foil) as significant loss
                        has been noted within hours.(16)


IV push                 Not used


Intermittent infusion   Over 15–30 minutes at a rate not exceed 1 mg/min.(2,13,16) IV administration should be
                        restricted due to the potential for severe adverse reactions.(2)


Continuous infusion     Specified dose can be added to PN solutions.(16,17)


Other routes of         The preferred route of administration is sub-Q,(2) while the IM route should be restricted
administration          to occasions where the sub-Q route cannot be used.(2)


Comments                Monitoring: Improvement in hemostasis may not be detectable until 1–2 hours after
                        administration.(2,13,22) Some recommend measurement of international normalized ratio
                        4–6 hours after the dose in those at high risk of bleeding or 12 hours after the dose in
                        less severe cases of over-warfarinization.(13)
                        When administered intravenously, it is generally detectable within 1 or 2 hours, hemor-
                        rhage is controlled within 3–6 hours, and a normal prothrombin level is often obtained
                        within 12–14 hours.(22)
                        Use of large doses of vitamin K may cause warfarin resistance for ≥1 week.(13)
                        Hyperbilirubinemia and severe hemolytic anemia have occurred in neonates receiving
                        larger doses.(2)
                        Two neonates who received IV phytonadione for prophylaxis developed late-onset hemor-
                        rhagic disease.(18)




