APPENDIX C: Additives and Antibiotic Considerations


Additives        Benzyl alcohol: Preservative-free products should be used in neonates and infants
                 when possible.(21) A 3-week-old, very low birth weight (710 g) infant experienced a pro-
                 found oxygen desaturation that required resuscitation after the third and fourth doses of
                 clindamycin that was subsequently related to the benzyl alcohol preservative.(2) Admin-
                 istration of benzyl alcohol preserved flush solutions was associated with a fatal gasping
                 syndrome, intraventricular hemorrhage, metabolic acidosis, and increased mortality in
                 preterm infants.(3) Hypersensitivity reactions to benzyl alcohol in parenteral products have
                 been reported in adults.(4,5)
                 Paraben: Paraben preservatives may cause hypersensitivity reactions that are more
                 common with cutaneous exposure.(6) However, one case of pruritus and bronchospasm
                 following infusion of hydrocortisone that contained a paraben has been reported.(7)
                 Propylene glycol: Propylene glycol is added to parenteral drugs as a solubilizer. Rapid
                 infusion of medications that contain propylene glycol has resulted in cardiac dysrhyth-
                 mias.(8) Its half-life is three times longer in neonates than in adults(1) and has caused
                 hyperosmolality(9) and seizures(10) in preterm neonates receiving 3 g/day.
                 Sulfites: Sulfites may cause hypersensitivity reactions that are more common in those
                 with asthma. Most reactions are mild but can include anaphylactic symptoms and life-
                 threatening or less severe asthma episodes.(1,11,12) Epinephrine may be required in severe
                 cases, and if the sulfite-free product is not available, the sulfite-preserved epinephrine
                 should be used.(1)


Antibiotic       Type I reaction to antibiotics: Death from anaphylaxis is usually due to respiratory and
considerations   cardiovascular arrest. Epinephrine should be immediately given followed by a vasopressor
                 agent. Large volumes of crystalloid fluids may be necessary when epinephrine’s effect is
                 not immediate or sustained.(22)
                 Desensitization protocols may be necessary when a patient with a history of a type 1
                 reaction requires the drug for cure of the infection. Once desensitized, the patient can
                 typically tolerate the course of therapy. Sensitivity to the drug returns after up to 4 half-
                 lives requiring further desensitization regimens for subsequent courses.(22)
                 Penicillins, cephalosporins, carbapenems, and monobactams all produce similar allergic
                 reactions and cross-reactivity may occur. The rate of cross-reactivity is related to the
                 drug’s molecular structure and may vary even within a drug class.(22,23)
                 Although higher numbers have previously been reported, the true incidence of patients
                 allergic to penicillin who cross-react to cephalosporins may be as low as 2%.(14)
                 Inactivation aminoglycoside/penicillin: The beta-lactam ring of penicillins can link
                 with an amino sugar of the aminoglycoside and inactivate the aminoglycoside.(15,16) To
                 avoid this potential interaction, administer penicillins 1 hour before or after an aminogly-
                 coside, adequately flush the infusion line between each infusion, or infuse them through
                 separate lines. In vivo inactivation that is dose dependent(18) can also occur particularly
                 in patients with renal failure.(15,18) In patients with end-stage renal failure, gentamicin
                 half-life was decreased by 25% to 74% after carbenicillin or ticarcillin was added to the
                 drug regimen.(19)
                 Aminoglycosides/neuromuscular blockade: Aminoglycosides may cause neuromus-
                 cular blockade, and this effect is pronounced in patients with renal insufficiency, neuro-
                 muscular disease, and hypocalcemia.(15,20)
                 The effects of nondepolarizing neuromuscular blockers may be prolonged during amino-
                 glycoside use.(20)
                 Cephalosporins and bleeding: The N-methyl-thiotetrazole (NMTT) side chain may inhibit
                 vitamin K-dependent clotting factors resulting in a prolonged PTT time.(15) The coagulopa-
                 thy is more pronounced in patients with renal insufficiency.(15) The non-NMTT side chain
                 antibiotics have also been associated with bleeding in patients with renal insufficiency.
                 The NMTT side chain causes a disulfiram-like reaction (e.g., flushing, sweating, headache,
                 and tachycardia) when combined with alcohol.


